const DATA = {"FACTS": "\nHARD MEASURED FACTS (computed directly from the local data \u2014 do NOT contradict, do NOT re-derive, do NOT restate back):\n\nSCALE\n- MAVEN-Arg: 4,480 docs; 80,479 events; 236,937 arguments; 143 argument roles; 168 event types; 7 entity types (Location, Other, Organization, Person, Product, Building, Art).\n- train+valid = 3,623 docs with gold arguments. test.jsonl is BLIND ('event_mentions', no gold arguments).\n- MAVEN-ERE covers the SAME documents: doc-id overlap 4,480/4,480 = 100%. Shared event ids = 79,740.\n- MAVEN-ERE temporal relations: BEFORE 1.04M, CONTAINS 152K, SIMULTANEOUS 9.9K, OVERLAP 9.9K, BEGINS-ON 639, ENDS-ON 380.\n\nENTITIES / ARGUMENTS\n- Only 40.1% of arguments carry 'entity_id'; 59.9% are bare {content, offset} spans with no coreference.\n- ZERO entities appear in >1 document (0 of 68,348). Entities are strictly document-local; no cross-doc linking exists.\n- Entities (ids, 7 types, mention offsets) ARE PROVIDED as input in EVERY split including blind test.\n- 16,284 entities participate in >=2 events; mean 3.15 events/entity, max 59.\n- Shared-participant event pairs: 274,525; of those 139,093 (50.7%) also carry a MAVEN-ERE temporal relation.\n\nTIME\n- TIMEX spans ARE PROVIDED in every split including blind test. TIMEX DETECTION IS NOT A TASK.\n- TIMEX has NO normalised value field, only surface strings. Normalisation is unbuilt, zero gold supervision.\n- 20,827 gold TIMEX spans (9,287 distinct surfaces) in train+valid. Types: DATE, DURATION, TIME, PREPOSTEXP.\n- MEASURED normalisation difficulty: 62.1% deterministic regex (bare year 29.9%, day-month-year 9.1%, month+year 6.9%, N+unit 6.1% (a DURATION - constrains width not position), month+day+year 4.6%, ranges/decades/centuries/deictic 5.6%). 37.9% needs document context, dominated by four tractable families: relative dates (\"the next day\" x91, \"the following day\" x62, \"the same day\" x21), year-less dates (\"September 11\" x24, \"August 31\" x17), named periods (\"World War II\" x70, typed DURATION), weekdays (\"Sunday\" x21). Realistic build: regex (~2d) + TimeML-style document reference-time propagation (~2d) + ~50-entry gazetteer (~0.5d) => ~85-90% coverage in about a week.\n- Event-TIMEX anchors: CONTAINS 66,418; SIMULTANEOUS 496; BEGINS-ON 122; ENDS-ON 39. CONTAINS is ~99% of anchoring mass; BEGINS-ON/ENDS-ON too rare to learn.\n- Events with a TIGHT TIMEX anchor: 37,852 = 44.9% (10.4/doc). Loose bound only: 27.2%. No TIMEX link: 27.9%.\n- Events with >=2 CONTAINS anchors: 17,102 (2 anchors: 10,653; 3: 3,707; >=4: 2,742). Usually NESTED granularity (\"in 1778\" + \"on November 11\").\n- MAVEN-Arg's 143 roles contain NO Time/Date/When role. Only 'Duration': 33 instances, 1 event type (Prison), values like \"life\", \"21 years\".\n- Only 4.97% of argument spans overlap any TIMEX; only 13.8% of TIMEX are covered by any argument span. EAE structurally cannot supply event time.\n\nGOLD CONSISTENCY AUDIT (critical)\n- Gold temporal graph is essentially CONFLICT-FREE: 0 BEFORE cycles across 843,808 pairs; 0 symmetric violations; BEFORE is 100.0% transitively closed (7 missing of 6,718,279 chains).\n- CAUSE contradicted by BEFORE: 11/46,014 (0.02%). subevent contradicted: 61/12,019 (0.5%). subevent lacking implied CONTAINS: 1,638/12,019 (13.6%, incompleteness not contradiction).\n- TOTAL genuine contradictions in the whole gold corpus: ~72. Mining conflicts FROM gold has almost no target.\n\nMINING POWER\n- (EventType1, EventType2) signatures with support >=10: 2,708 (81% instance coverage). (T1,role1,T2,role2) >=10: 2,688 (59%).\n- Projected property vocabulary \"EventType#Role\" = 652 distinct (623 with >=10). PaTeCon's |R|^2 index = 425,104 entries ~0.1 GB. Scales fine.\n\nPATECON REFERENCE (AAAI 2023 + arXiv 2312.11053 PaTeCon+; github.com/JianhaoChen-nju/PaTeCon)\n- REIFIED statement-node graph: eVertex (entity) + sVertex (statement carrying start/end/weight/truth). Indexes: eVertexList (subject->statements), sVertexList (property->statements), temporalRelationList, entityType.\n- Two hand-fixed structural patterns: SP(a) one subject two temporal statements; SP(b) two subjects joined by an atemporal property.\n- Three constraint families: temporal disjointness, temporal ordering, mutual exclusion. Head predicates: start, finish, before, disjoint, include (+ 'false' for mutex, + span bounds).\n- TRIVALENT logic over uncertain time: positive/negative/UNKNOWN; unknown discarded from support and confidence.\n- Fact-level vs ENTITY-level support/confidence (entity positive only if ALL its subgraphs positive) - their headline contribution.\n- PaTeCon+ adds two-stage pruning via law of large numbers. Defaults: support=10, candidate_conf=0.5, conf=0.9.\n\nTHE PROJECT (TempEKG, URA Lab, HCMUT)\n- Target COLING 2027 (CORE B). Deadline 12 Oct 2026. Today 28 Aug 2026 => 45 DAYS.\n- Team of 3: Vinh (Phase 3 Graph Projection + annotation tooling), Nhut (Phase 2 GRAPH CONSTRUCTION - THIS IS THE USER), Nhan (Phase 4 Constraint Mining).\n- Phases: P1 Event Extraction (PAIE/SCPRG/DEEIA on MAVEN-Arg, DONE); P2 Graph Construction from GOLD labels, events with time anchors, split train/test, build ECKG; P3 Projection ECKG->Temporal KG to reuse PaTeCon; P4 Constraint Mining per PaTeCon + FAKE conflicts by perturbing times; P5 Conflict Detection on test graph.\n- Two targeted conflict types: (1) ORDERING CONFLICT - contradictory temporal ordering between related event pairs, relations {start, finish, before, disjoint, include}; (2) GRANULARITY CONFLICT - inconsistent temporal precision for the SAME event.\n- PROBLEMS ALREADY DIAGNOSED: (a) the fake-conflict evaluation is CIRCULAR (conflicts generated by violating mined constraints then detected with those same constraints); (b) Phase 1 is orphaned - extractors never feed the graph since P2 uses gold; (c) projecting event-as-subject makes all predicates degenerate (all statements of one event share one interval) so projection MUST be entity-as-subject: (entity_x, \"EventType#Role\", event_e, T_e); (d) train/test must split BY DOCUMENT or entity-sharing leaks across the split.\n- Their repos (PAIE_Maven, DEEIA_Maven, SCPRG, TSAR_Maven, MAVEN_Arg_Pipeline) contain NO temporal component whatsoever.\n", "RESEARCH_DIGEST": "### Sweep consistency-benchmark\nFINDINGS:\n- The strongest 2025 precedent for consistency-aware temporal RE with LLMs is Fan & Strube, 'Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction Using Large Language Models' (Findings of EMNLP 2025, pp. 18605-18622). Their pipeline is exactly three stages: (1) context selection, (2) prompts inspired by Allen's interval algebra, (3) reflection-based consistency learning. This is the canonical citation for 'Allen-algebra prompting + reflection' and is a directly runnable LLM baseline for a temporal-conflict-detection benchmark. [WHY: Gives TempEKG a mandatory non-symbolic baseline (LLM + Allen prompting + reflection) to contrast with PaTeCon-style mined constraints. Reviewers at COLING will ask 'why not just prompt an LLM'; this paper is the strongest form of that question and must be run, not just cited.]\n- Consistency has already been used as a LABEL-FREE evaluation signal for temporal RE: Kougia et al., 'Analysing zero-shot temporal relation extraction on clinical notes using temporal consistency' (BioNLP 2024 / arXiv 2406.11486) score five LLMs (GPT-3.5, Mixtral, Llama 2, Gemma, PMC-LLaMA) on two temporal properties computed purely from the model's own outputs: UNIQUENESS (each event pair must receive exactly one relation) and TRANSITIVITY. They then study whether repairing inconsistencies improves accuracy, and find LLMs violate both properties substantially. [WHY: This is the single most important methodological precedent for escaping circularity: consistency violation rate is measurable WITHOUT gold conflict labels. TempEKG can report 'violation rate of a predicted graph' as a primary, gold-free metric on the blind MAVEN-Arg test split, where no gold arguments/relations exist at all.]\n- The KG-error-detection field has already published the exact critique of TempEKG's Phase 4: standard practice corrupts triples by replacing an entity with a RANDOM one, and such synthetic errors are qualitatively unlike real errors, where the wrong element is highly semantically related. Reported consequence: recall drops roughly 30% when detectors validated on synthetic noise are moved to real errors. Recommended fix is semantically-similar (confusable) noise rather than uniform replacement. [WHY: Quantifies the penalty TempEKG will pay if it ships only rule-negating fake conflicts: expect a ~30%-scale optimism gap. Turn this into a headline result by reporting BOTH regimes and the delta (the 'circularity gap').]\n- Jarnac, Chabot & Couceiro's WikiConflict (K-CAP 2025, 13th Knowledge Capture Conference, Dayton, Dec 12 2025) builds a conflicting-data dataset from a Wikipedia category plus WIKIDATA EDIT HISTORY, and is integrated into the TrustFuse data-fusion testbed. Separately, 'Leveraging Wikidata's edit history in knowledge graph refinement tasks' (arXiv 2210.15495) formalises the key idea: use triples the community actually DELETED as the pool of realistic corruptions, instead of inventing corruptions. [WHY: Establishes 'errors sampled from an independent, human-generated error process' as an accepted methodology with a citable lineage. MAVEN has no edit history, but the structural analogue exists: errors sampled from the Phase-1 extractors' own confusion distribution. Cite these to justify that design as standard, not improvised.]\n- PaTeCon+ (arXiv 2312.11053) already solves the recall-measurement problem the way TempEKG must: conflict detection is run at scale on WD27M/FB37M, but RECALL is computed against two SEPARATELY human-annotated wrong-fact sets, WD-411 and FB-128. Precision-side quality is reported as human judgements of the mined constraints themselves (86% of WD27M and 96% of FB37M constraints judged 'Correct' or 'has Merit'). PaTeCon+ with fact-level confidence produced ~1.8x constraints and ~38x conflicts on WD27M vs entity-level. [WHY: The parent paper's own protocol is: constraint precision by human rating + conflict recall on a small independently-annotated wrong-fact set. TempEKG's ~72 genuine gold contradictions plus a small human-authored error set are the direct analogue of WD-411/FB-128. Also note the 38x conflict blow-up: fact-level confidence will flood the top-k, so precision@k discipline is essential.]\n- Under extreme imbalance, ROC-AUC is actively misleading because a large true-negative pool absorbs false positives with negligible FPR movement; AUPRC / Precision@k are the field-standard replacements, and 2025-2026 metric audits identify Standard-F1, AUC-PR, Range-AUC-PR and VUS-PR as the reliable family. [WHY: With ~72 true conflicts in 843,808 pairs the base rate is ~8.5e-5. Any ROC-AUC number would look near-perfect and mean nothing. TempEKG must report AUPRC alongside the base rate as the random-baseline precision, plus P@50/100/500/1000.]\n- There is an established, citable method for evaluating detectors when exhaustive labelling is impossible and positives are ultra-rare: 'Needle in a Haystack: Label-Efficient Evaluation under Extreme Class Imbalance' (arXiv 2006.06963), plus 'Adaptive Stratified Sampling for Precision-Recall Estimation' (UAI 2018). Both estimate precision/recall from an adaptively chosen, importance-weighted sample with confidence intervals; the standard label-efficient families are model-based, stratified sampling, and importance sampling. [WHY: This is how to report a defensible RECALL on the natural (uninjected) test graph without annotating 843k pairs. Stratify by detector score band, annotate a fixed budget per stratum, Horvitz-Thompson-weight, report CIs. It converts 'we cannot measure recall' into a methodological contribution.]\n- Holdout rule-mining evaluation is a recognised protocol: split into an exploratory set and an evaluation set, mine rules (with acceptable p-values) on the former, and validate them ONLY on the latter, explicitly to avoid evaluating rules on the data that produced them (see 'Controlling False Positives in Association Rule Mining', arXiv 1110.6652; AMIE/AMIE3 use held-out fact sets to count predicted facts). [WHY: Directly supplies the citation for the document-disjoint mine/eval split, and for a leave-one-pattern-out ablation. Note this fixes only HALF of TempEKG's circularity (data provenance); the other half (error provenance) needs the injector firewall below.]\n- LLM-as-judge validation practice hardened sharply in 2026 and the naive protocol is now explicitly attacked. 'Reliability without Validity' (arXiv 2606.19544) ran 21 models from 9 providers, 3 benchmarks, 3 protocols, 541,000 judgments over 118 runs, and argues that judge validation by EXACT-MATCH agreement does not correct for chance and systematically overstates discriminative ability. 'The Coin Flip Judge?' (arXiv 2606.13685) and a RAND-cited study report no judge is uniformly reliable and frontier models exceed 50% error on hard bias benchmarks. Operational thresholds in current practice: human-human Cohen's kappa typically 0.5-0.8; judge-human kappa <0.5 means the rubric must be rewritten; >=0.6 acceptable, >=0.8 strong; re-measure monthly for judge drift against a ~200-item hand-labelled gold set. [WHY: If TempEKG uses an LLM to adjudicate flagged conflicts (tempting, given 3 people and 45 days), reviewers in 2026-2027 will demand a chance-corrected judge-vs-human kappa on a human gold subset. Budget ~200-300 human-adjudicated items purely to validate the judge, and never report LLM-only labels as gold.]\n- For extraction-type tasks specifically, LLM-as-judge agreement with humans lands around Cohen's kappa 0.72-0.80 (biomedical relation extraction, arXiv 2506.00777), and hybrid pipelines (LLM drafts, expert corrects; e.g. DeepSeek-V3 draft annotations guiding human experts) are now the accepted cost-efficient protocol rather than LLM-only labelling. Annotation-guideline quality is itself a measured lever on LLM extraction performance (Frontiers in AI 2026, 'Improving LLM-based event extraction with annotation guidelines'). [WHY: 0.72-0.80 is the number to target and to compare against. It also justifies the LLM-draft/human-adjudicate design for Vinh's annotation tooling, which is the only way 3 people annotate a credible conflict pool in 45 days.]\n- IAA metric choice is a live 2026 topic, not a formality: 'Counting on Consensus: Selecting the Right Inter-annotator Agreement Metric for NLP Annotation and Evaluation' (arXiv 2603.06865) organises agreement measures by task type and shows how LABEL IMBALANCE and MISSING DATA distort reliability estimates; Krippendorff's alpha is the safest choice under incomplete annotation, >2 coders, or mixed measurement levels. [WHY: A conflict-adjudication pool is by construction extremely imbalanced and incompletely annotated (pooled top-k, different annotators see different items). Fleiss kappa will be unstable and easy to attack; report Krippendorff's alpha (plus per-class alpha and the undecidable rate).]\n- Human-authored errors are now treated as a distinct, higher-value error class than model-injected ones. ReFACT (arXiv 2509.25868 / EACL 2026 long 381) is positioned as the only benchmark with fully human-verified SPAN-LEVEL and ERROR-TYPE annotations for long-form scientific QA, contrasted against FavaBench and HALoGEN which have rich taxonomies but rely on model-generated outputs. A 2026 study of human-induced factual errors reports even GPT-5.4 reaching only 52% word-level F1 on synthesized realistic cases, and evaluates on BOTH synthesized realistic test cases AND real corrections. [WHY: Two transferable requirements: (a) annotate LOCALISATION (which fact in the conflicting set is the culprit), not just binary detection; (b) always report the same detector on synthetic-realistic AND real-error tracks side by side. The 52% figure also shows a hard task is publishable - low scores are not a reason to hide the real-error track.]\n- Reasoning-aware error injection is the current best-practice recipe for making synthetic errors non-trivial: inject at the level of a single reasoning step, ONE step at a time, to preserve semantic coherence and avoid cascading inconsistencies; substitute shared entities with semantically plausible but incorrect alternatives, or negate/rewrite salient factual units to create contradictions (CECOR, arXiv 2605.02277 and related). [WHY: Gives the concrete injection discipline for temporal perturbation: change exactly ONE date/interval per document, keep granularity and surface form plausible (sample the replacement from the TIMEX-surface distribution actually observed in the corpus), and never cascade - otherwise the injected conflict is detectable from surface statistics rather than temporal logic.]\n- MAVEN-ERE temporal RE performance context for 2025-2026: a graph propagation model with rich event structures reports 60.7 F1 on MAVEN-ERE temporal (vs 37.4 causal, 32.9 subevent). Zero-shot ChatGPT temporal RE is up to ~30 F1 points below supervised methods; on the TIMELINE test set ChatGPT scores P 31.11 / R 35.67 / F1 33.24. EventRelBench (Findings EMNLP 2025) is 35K event-relation questions over coreference/temporal/causal/supersub with a 48K instruction-tuning set (EventRelInst), and concludes current LLMs still fall short. [WHY: Sets the realistic ceiling for any predicted-graph track: a state-of-the-art temporal extractor is only ~60 F1 on these documents, so a graph built from PREDICTIONS will contain abundant, naturally distributed temporal errors. That is the error source that makes the benchmark non-circular AND un-orphans Phase 1.]\n- Logic-constraint enforcement in temporal/subevent RE has a standard differentiable formulation to cite and reuse: Joint Constrained Learning (Wang et al., EMNLP 2020, arXiv 2010.06727) converts declarative constraints into differentiable objectives and names three consistency types - ANNOTATION consistency, SYMMETRY consistency, CONJUNCTION consistency. Descendants include LogicERE (high-order reasoning over a logic-constraint-induced graph), PIPER (logic-driven deep contrastive optimization), and CERMiner (weighted double consistency constraint learning, Symmetry 2025, 17(11):1910). [WHY: The 'annotation / symmetry / conjunction' triple is the accepted vocabulary for temporal consistency violations and maps cleanly onto TempEKG's ordering-conflict family. Use these three as named, separately reported violation classes rather than inventing terminology - and note the audit already shows gold has 0 symmetry violations, so symmetry is a sanity check, not a task.]\n- Global inference for temporal RE is standardly formulated as ILP over the whole temporal graph, with a global objective from local scorers plus constraints for one-label assignment, event-relation consistency, symmetry and transitivity; ILP with hard constraints is explicitly described as only approximating the optimum because the underlying problem is NP-hard. 2025 work continues in this direction (Global Zero-shot Temporal Graph Generation, EMNLP 2025 main, pp. paper 1601, applies ILP optimization to temporal graph predictions). [WHY: Global ILP repair is the obvious 'strong symbolic baseline' for conflict detection: an unsatisfiable or high-cost ILP/MaxSAT relaxation IS a conflict signal, requiring no mined constraints at all. This is the cheapest constraint-independent detector and therefore the fairest non-circular comparison point for PaTeCon-style mining.]\n- Weighted MaxSAT is a viable and precedented alternative to ILP for this class of problem: Deep Weighted MaxSAT was used for aspect-based opinion extraction under noisy data (EMNLP 2020), MaxSAT is used for inferring LTL formulas from noisy data (arXiv 2104.15083), and standard SMT solvers such as Z3 handle MaxSAT directly. Timeline construction is standardly framed as translating temporal constraints into a temporal constraint graph over primitive relations and solving it, with the timeline existing only if the links are temporally consistent. [WHY: Partial-weighted MaxSAT gives a principled CULPRIT-LOCALISATION mechanism the rule-based pipeline lacks: soft-clause weights come from extractor confidences, and the falsified clauses in the optimal assignment are exactly the ranked conflict candidates. One Z3 call per document; implementable inside 45 days.]\n- The Vague/UNKNOWN problem has a modern solution to align with PaTeCon's trivalent logic: METRE ('Only One Relation Possible?', arXiv 2408.07353, Hu, Huang & Feng) reframes ETRE as MULTI-LABEL, inferring each relation's possibility independently and treating Vague as 'more than one relation possible', with a speculation mechanism to recover the latent relation behind Vague; evaluated on TB-Dense, MATRES and UDS-T. Dataset geometry to cite: TB-Dense has 6 relations (Before/After/Includes/Is_Included/Simultaneous/Vague), MATRES reduces to 4 (Before/After/Equal/Vague) annotated on start points only; TBD and MATRES are dominated by same/adjacent-sentence pairs, TDDMan is exclusively long-distance, TDDAuto mixes both. [WHY: PaTeCon discards UNKNOWN from support and confidence; METRE shows the discarded mass is recoverable and informative. For conflict detection, 'possibly-conflicting' must be a first-class output class, and the annotation scheme must offer an explicit UNDECIDABLE label whose rate is reported (otherwise annotators force-choose and IAA collapses).]\n- The granularity-conflict type TempEKG targets is named but undetected in the literature. KONTRAST (arXiv 2607.25959, Wei, Ehrhart & Troncy) proposes a taxonomy of cross-modal knowledge inconsistencies over text/tables/KGs with exactly four classes - DIRECT CONFLICTS, INFORMATION GRANULARITY DIFFERENCES, TEMPORAL CHANGES, and KG INCOMPLETENESS - and explicitly distinguishes granularity mismatch from direct contradiction and from structural incompleteness, using Text-to-SPARQL plus LLM reasoning. Granularity elsewhere appears only as a modelling device for TKG completion (hierarchical year/month/day representations, arXiv 2408.15293; quadruplet networks, Sci Rep 2025). [WHY: KONTRAST's four-way taxonomy should become TempEKG's annotation label set almost verbatim - it is the citable justification for separating 'incomplete' from 'contradictory', which the gold audit already proves is essential (13.6% subevent gaps are incompleteness). And no one has built a granularity-conflict DETECTION benchmark: that is the clearest novelty claim available.]\n- Venue-level requirements are now hard gates, not soft advice. ARR/EACL 2026 and ACL 2026 require a dedicated 'Limitations' section - papers without one are rejected WITHOUT REVIEW. ARR reviewer guidelines explicitly instruct reviewers to value a high-quality resource for an under-resourced domain even when it does not sound novel. NeurIPS 2026's Evaluations & Datasets track (the current high-water mark for resource-paper rigour) requires persistent public hosting (Dataverse/Kaggle/HuggingFace/OpenML), mandatory Croissant metadata, a small inspectable sample for datasets >4GB, and - most relevant - that authors clearly articulate the dataset's relationship to evaluative claims: what kinds of claims an assessment on it can and cannot support. [WHY: The NeurIPS E&D 'what claims can this support' clause is precisely the circularity question in reviewer language. Writing an explicit 'Claims Supported / Claims Not Supported' subsection pre-empts the strongest possible rejection reason, and costs one page.]\n- Annotation-quality management is itself an audited dimension: 'Analyzing Dataset Annotation Quality Management in the Wild' (arXiv 2307.08153) finds excellent projects do most of: iterative annotation process, annotator training, computed agreement AND error rate, extensive validation, and continuous human inspection; guidelines must be released for reproducibility and for judging annotation quality. Datasheets for Datasets (arXiv 1803.09010) is the expected documentation format (motivation, composition, collection process, recommended uses). [WHY: A 3-person team can hit every item on that list cheaply if it is planned now: pilot round, guideline v1->v2 with disagreement analysis, alpha on a fixed pilot batch, and a released datasheet. Retrofitting it in October is impossible.]\n- Timeline-construction framing supplies a second, orthogonal evaluation axis. TimeSET uses single-document timelines with document-level order annotation, saliency-based event selection and PARTIAL ordering explicitly to bound annotation workload; prior timeline datasets annotate either event-to-time anchoring or event-to-event pairwise ordering, and both suffer missing temporal information. Formulation comparison work (arXiv 2403.00990) finds an NLI formulation with Flan-T5 strongest, with few-shot LLMs still weak. Clinical timeline evaluation decomposes into EVENT RECOVERY (event match rate) plus TEMPORAL LOCALIZATION. [WHY: Saliency-based event selection + partial ordering is the annotation-budget trick TempEKG needs: do not annotate all 843k pairs or even all events per document - annotate a salient subset with partial order, and report event-recovery and temporal-localization separately, mirroring accepted timeline metrics.]\n- A concrete, citable warning on where extraction error stops mattering: 'Understanding the Effect of Knowledge Graph Extraction Error on Downstream Graph Analyses' (arXiv 2506.12367) finds a band of extraction performance in which bias across most downstream graph metrics is near zero, with biases becoming pronounced only as extraction quality declines further. [WHY: Motivates an extrinsic arm of the protocol (does removing flagged conflicts improve a downstream temporal QA/ordering task?) while warning that the effect may be null in the high-quality regime. Run it, but pre-register it as a secondary claim, not the headline.]\nKEY PAPERS:\n- Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction Using Large Language Models (Fan & Strube) (Findings of EMNLP 2025, pp. 18605-18622) https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1010/ - The 2025 reference method for LLM temporal consistency: context selection + Allen-interval-algebra prompting + reflection-based consistency learning. Must be a baseline in TempEKG's conflict-detection comparison.\n- Analysing zero-shot temporal relation extraction on clinical notes using temporal consistency (Kougia, Sedova, Stephan, Zaporojets, Roth) (BioNLP @ ACL 2024) https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11486 - Defines gold-free consistency scores (uniqueness + transitivity) over LLM outputs across 5 LLMs and studies whether repairing inconsistency improves accuracy. The template for measuring conflict detection with no gold conflicts.\n- Conflict Detection for Temporal Knowledge Graphs: A Fast Constraint Mining Algorithm and New Benchmarks (PaTeCon+) (arXiv 2023 / journal 2025) https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11053 - The parent method AND the parent evaluation protocol: constraint precision by human rating (86% WD27M / 96% FB37M valuable), conflict recall against separately annotated wrong-fact sets WD-411 / FB-128, fact- vs entity-level confidence contrast (~38x conflict blow-up).\n- Detecting Knowledge Inconsistencies Across Text, Tables, and Knowledge Graphs (KONTRAST; Wei, Ehrhart, Troncy) (arXiv, July 2026) https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25959 - Four-class inconsistency taxonomy - direct conflict / granularity difference / temporal change / KG incompleteness - the closest existing conceptualisation of TempEKG's two conflict types, and the citation that legitimises granularity conflict as a category.\n- WikiConflict: A New Dataset for Conflicting Data Reconciliation in Knowledge Graph Construction (Jarnac, Chabot, Couceiro) (K-CAP 2025 (13th Knowledge Capture Conference, Dayton, Dec 2025)) https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3731443.3771371 - Conflict dataset built from Wikipedia category + Wikidata edit history, integrated into the TrustFuse testbed. Precedent for sourcing conflicts from a real human error process rather than injection.\n- Leveraging Wikidata's edit history in knowledge graph refinement tasks (arXiv 2022 (Journal of Web Semantics line of work)) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.15495 - Formalises realistic corruption: sample negatives from triples the community actually deleted. The methodological anchor for 'errors from an independent error process'.\n- Knowledge Graph Error Detection with Contrastive Confidence Adaption / Contrastive Knowledge Graph Error Detection (AAAI 2024 / CIKM 2022) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.12108 - States the random-replacement critique explicitly and reports the ~30% recall drop from synthetic to real errors. Also gives a strong non-rule-based detector baseline TempEKG should run.\n- Needle in a Haystack: Label-Efficient Evaluation under Extreme Class Imbalance (KDD 2020) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.06963 - Adaptive, importance-weighted estimation of precision/recall when positives are vanishingly rare and full labelling is impossible. The tool for reporting recall with CIs on an 843k-pair graph.\n- Adaptive Stratified Sampling for Precision-Recall Estimation (UAI 2018) https://www.cs.purdue.edu/homes/yexiang/publications/uai-18-adaptive-final.pdf - Score-stratified sampling estimator for PR curves under budget; the concrete recipe for the recall-estimation arm.\n- Reliability without Validity: A Systematic, Large-Scale Evaluation of LLM-as-a-Judge Models Across Agreement, Consistency, and Bias (arXiv 2026) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.19544 - 541k judgments / 21 models / 118 runs; shows exact-match judge validation is not chance-corrected and overstates ability. Required citation if any LLM judge appears in the adjudication loop.\n- The Coin Flip Judge? Reliability and Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation (arXiv 2026) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.13685 - Companion evidence that judges are not uniformly reliable across benchmarks; use for the limitations section on LLM-assisted adjudication.\n- Improving Automatic Evaluation of LLMs in Biomedical Relation Extraction via LLMs-as-the-Judge (arXiv 2025) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.00777 - Reports Cohen's kappa 0.72-0.80 between LLM judge and human for extraction-style judgements - the concrete target number for a TempEKG conflict-adjudication judge.\n- Counting on Consensus: Selecting the Right Inter-annotator Agreement Metric for NLP Annotation and Evaluation (arXiv 2026) https://arxiv.org/html/2603.06865 - Task-type-indexed guidance on IAA, with explicit treatment of label imbalance and missing data; justifies Krippendorff's alpha over Fleiss kappa for an incomplete, imbalanced conflict pool.\n- ReFACT: A Benchmark for Scientific Confabulation Detection with Positional Error Annotations (arXiv 2025 / EACL 2026 long paper) https://arxiv.org/html/2509.25868v3 - Human-authored, span-level, error-typed benchmark positioned against model-generated alternatives (FavaBench, HALoGEN). Model for annotating culprit LOCALISATION, not just detection.\n- Joint Constrained Learning for Event-Event Relation Extraction (Wang, Chen, Zhang, Roth) (EMNLP 2020) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06727 - Canonical differentiable-constraint formulation and the annotation / symmetry / conjunction consistency vocabulary that TempEKG should adopt for its violation classes.\n- Global Zero-shot Temporal Graph Generation (EMNLP 2025 (main)) https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1601.pdf - 2025 evidence that ILP global optimization over predicted temporal graphs is still the live approach; supplies a constraint-mining-free symbolic detector baseline.\n- Only One Relation Possible? Modeling the Ambiguity in Event Temporal Relation Extraction (METRE; Hu, Huang, Feng) (arXiv 2024) https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.07353 - Multi-label treatment of Vague with a speculation mechanism on TB-Dense/MATRES/UDS-T; the modern counterpart to PaTeCon's discarded UNKNOWN class.\n- EventRelBench / EventRelInst (Findings of EMNLP 2025) https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.482/ - 35K event-relation QA benchmark + 48K instruction-tuning set covering temporal relations; the 2025 measuring stick for LLM event-relation competence and a ready comparison resource.\n- A graph propagation model with rich event structures for joint event relation extraction (IP&M 2024/2025) https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306457324001705 - Reports 60.7 F1 temporal / 37.4 causal / 32.9 subevent on MAVEN-ERE - the ceiling number that makes a predicted-graph error track credible.\n- NeurIPS 2026 Evaluations & Datasets Track: Call for Papers and Reviewing Guidelines (2026) https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026/EvaluationsDatasetsReviewerGuidelines - Current strictest resource-paper bar: persistent hosting, Croissant metadata, inspectable sample, and an explicit statement of which evaluative claims the dataset can support - i.e. the circularity question in reviewer language.\n- ACL Rolling Review CFP + Reviewer Guidelines; EACL 2026 Call for Papers (2026) https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines - Mandatory Limitations section (auto-desk-reject without), and explicit reviewer instruction to value high-quality resources for under-resourced domains even when novelty seems low.\n- Analyzing Dataset Annotation Quality Management in the Wild (arXiv 2023 / TACL-line) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2307.08153 - Empirical checklist of what 'excellent' annotation QA looks like (iteration, training, agreement + error rate, validation, continuous inspection) - directly convertible into a 45-day annotation plan.\n- Controlling False Positives in Association Rule Mining (VLDB 2012 / arXiv 1110.6652) https://arxiv.org/pdf/1110.6652 - Holdout rule-mining protocol (exploratory set mines, evaluation set validates) - the citation for TempEKG's document-disjoint mine/eval split and for statistical control of spurious mined constraints.\n- Understanding the Effect of Knowledge Graph Extraction Error on Downstream Graph Analyses (arXiv 2025) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.12367 - Shows downstream bias is near zero in a band of extraction quality; calibrates expectations for an extrinsic 'does cleaning help' experiment.\nREUSABLE: PROTOCOL STEP 0 - Freeze-and-hash the constraint set before any error exists. Mine constraints on the document-disjoint MINE split, serialise the full constraint set (pattern, head predicate, support, confidence), commit its SHA-256 to the repo, and state the commit in the paper. Every subsequent evaluation number is reported against this frozen set. This is the single cheapest, most legible anti-circularity artefact: it makes 'the errors were designed to violate the constraints' falsifiable rather than a matter of trust. Cite holdout rule mining (arXiv 1110.6652) and AMIE held-out fact counting.; PROTOCOL STEP 1 - Constraint/error PROVENANCE MATRIX as the paper's central table. Rows = error sources (S1 system-predicted graph, S2 confusion-sampled injection, S3 human-authored perturbation, S4 native gold contradictions, S5 rule-negating injection). Columns = detectors (frozen mined constraints, leave-family-out constraints, MaxSAT/ILP global inference, embedding-based KGED, LLM+Allen prompting+reflection, random). Circularity is then a MEASURED quantity - the gap between the S5 column-max and the S1 column-max - not a reviewer's suspicion. Report that gap as 'the circularity gap' and expect it to be large (the KGED literature's synthetic->real recall drop is ~30%).; PROTOCOL STEP 2 (PRIMARY, non-circular, zero label cost) - System-error track. Run the already-trained Phase-1 extractors (PAIE / DEEIA / SCPRG) plus a TIMEX normaliser plus a temporal-relation classifier trained ONLY on the mine split over the eval-split documents; build the ECKG from PREDICTIONS; diff against the gold ECKG. The diff gives thousands of naturally distributed, automatically labelled temporal errors whose generating process is provably independent of the constraint miner (the extractors have no temporal component at all). This simultaneously un-orphans Phase 1 - the extractors become the benchmark's error generator, which is a legitimate and defensible role for them.; PROTOCOL STEP 3 - Confusion-sampled (not rule-negating) injection. For each injected error, perturb exactly ONE temporal fact per document, and draw the replacement from an EMPIRICAL confusion distribution estimated from an independent model: the TIMEX-normaliser's own error modes (year-less date resolved to the wrong reference year, 'the following day' propagated from the wrong anchor, DURATION read as DATE, nested-granularity anchor collapsed to the coarser one), or a masked-LM over the 9,287 observed TIMEX surfaces. Never cascade perturbations. Report the injection distribution as a table so reviewers can audit that it does not mirror the constraint heads {start, finish, before, disjoint, include}.; PROTOCOL STEP 4 - Injector firewall (dual control). The injection code is written and run by a team member with no read access to the frozen constraint file, and the injected-error manifest is sealed until detection results are produced. Report an explicit 'blindness audit': the fraction of injected conflicts that any single mined constraint fires on, and the fraction detectable only via multi-hop closure. Mitigation of circularity by using different systems for generation, detection and judging is the accepted framing in 2026 benchmark-validity discussions.; PROTOCOL STEP 5 - Leave-one-family-out (LOFO) and leave-one-pattern-out. For each of PaTeCon's three constraint families (temporal disjointness, temporal ordering, mutual exclusion) plus a new granularity family, evaluate detection of errors that violate ONLY other families' semantics; and re-mine with each structural pattern (SP(a) one-subject-two-temporal-statements, SP(b) two-subjects-atemporal-link) removed. LOFO converts 'detected by the constraint that generated it' into a controlled ablation and is the analogue of held-out rule validation.; PROTOCOL STEP 6 - Metric suite mandated by the base rate. Base rate on the natural graph is ~1e-4. Report: (a) random-baseline precision = base rate, stated explicitly; (b) Precision@k for k in {50,100,500,1000} - the operationally meaningful numbers since a human can only review k items; (c) R-Precision; (d) AUPRC; (e) precision at fixed recall levels. Do NOT report accuracy or ROC-AUC anywhere - 2025-2026 metric audits are unambiguous that AUROC is over-optimistic when the true-negative pool dominates.; PROTOCOL STEP 7 - Two-tier recall. Tier A: EXACT recall on the injected / system-error sets, where every positive is known by construction. Tier B: ESTIMATED recall on the natural, uninjected graph via score-stratified sampling with Horvitz-Thompson / importance weighting and reported confidence intervals (Needle in a Haystack, KDD 2020; Adaptive Stratified Sampling for PR Estimation, UAI 2018). Stratify by detector score decile, fix a per-stratum annotation budget, and report the estimator and its variance. Never present Tier A recall as corpus recall.; PROTOCOL STEP 8 - Pooled, blinded adjudication (TREC pooling). Pool the top-k flagged items from ALL detectors including a random baseline, shuffle, strip system identity, and annotate the union. Precision per system is then computed on identically-sourced judgements; pool-relative recall is reported and explicitly labelled an upper-bound-of-coverage underestimate. Pooling is the standard answer to 'you only annotated what your own system flagged', and it makes the annotation budget shared across all baselines instead of per-system.; PROTOCOL STEP 9 - Four-way label set, not binary. Every pooled item is labelled {GENUINE CONFLICT | SPURIOUS: constraint is wrong | SPURIOUS: graph is incomplete, not contradictory | UNDECIDABLE}. The incompleteness class is non-negotiable given the measured 13.6% subevent-without-CONTAINS rate; KONTRAST's taxonomy (direct conflict / granularity difference / temporal change / KG incompleteness) is the citable justification. The UNDECIDABLE class operationalises PaTeCon's trivalent UNKNOWN as an annotation option and its rate must be reported, not hidden by forced choice.; PROTOCOL STEP 10 - Localisation, not just detection. For every genuine conflict, annotate WHICH fact in the conflicting set is the culprit. Report set-level detection F1 AND culprit-accuracy@1 separately (ReFACT's positional/span-level annotation is the precedent). A detector that flags the right pair but blames the right fact only 40% of the time is useless downstream, and reviewers will notice if this is not measured.; PROTOCOL STEP 11 - Annotation reliability done to 2026 standards. 3 independent annotators on a common overlap batch (>=300 items), 4th-person adjudication, Krippendorff's alpha reported overall AND per class (alpha, not Fleiss kappa, because the pool is imbalanced and incompletely annotated - arXiv 2603.06865). Run a pilot round, revise guidelines from the disagreement analysis, report guideline v1->v2 diffs, and release the guidelines. Report annotator error rate on a seeded gold subset, not only agreement (arXiv 2307.08153).; PROTOCOL STEP 12 - LLM judge only as a validated pre-filter. If an LLM adjudicates, first hand-label ~200-300 items, then report chance-corrected judge-vs-human Cohen's kappa (target >=0.6, the extraction-domain reference band is 0.72-0.80) plus position/order and verbosity bias probes and a re-run for drift. Explicitly note that exact-match agreement is not acceptable validation (arXiv 2606.19544). LLM labels never enter the gold set; they only reduce the human pool.; PROTOCOL STEP 13 - MaxSAT/ILP as the constraint-mining-free strong baseline AND as the localiser. Encode each document's temporal facts as partial weighted MaxSAT: hard clauses = Allen-algebra composition/symmetry axioms, soft clauses = extracted relations weighted by extractor confidence. Falsified soft clauses under the optimal assignment are the ranked conflict candidates with culprit identity for free. One Z3 call per document. This detector uses zero mined constraints, so its performance is by construction non-circular and forms the fairest floor for the mined-constraint claim.; PROTOCOL STEP 14 - Gold-free consistency metrics as an auxiliary headline. Report uniqueness-violation rate, symmetry-violation rate, conjunction/transitivity-violation rate (the Joint Constrained Learning vocabulary) on every predicted graph, following Kougia et al.'s consistency-score methodology. These require NO conflict labels at all, and can therefore be reported on the BLIND MAVEN-Arg test split - the one place where no circularity objection is even formulable.; PROTOCOL STEP 15 - Human-authored realistic perturbation set (300-500 items). Have annotators EDIT temporal facts in eval documents to create a plausible error they would expect a human or a system to make, without ever seeing the constraint set. This is the highest-credibility injected track (ReFACT's human-authorship argument) and is cheap: it is an annotation task Vinh's tooling can serve directly, and 300 items at ~2 min each is one person-day.; PROTOCOL STEP 16 - Report a 'Claims Supported / Claims Not Supported' subsection. State plainly which of these the benchmark licenses: ranking detectors by top-k precision on realistic errors (yes); absolute corpus-level recall (only as an interval estimate); generalisation beyond news-domain MAVEN documents (no); detection of errors whose generating process resembles the constraint language (measured and reported as the circularity gap, not claimed as capability). NeurIPS 2026 E&D reviewing guidelines make articulating exactly this the central resource-paper requirement, and it directly answers the circularity reviewer.; PROTOCOL STEP 17 - Resource-paper hygiene checklist to satisfy before submission: mandatory Limitations section (ARR/EACL 2026 auto-reject without it); a Datasheet for Datasets; persistent hosting on HuggingFace with Croissant metadata; released annotation guidelines; released frozen constraint set with its hash; per-split document IDs proving the document-level (entity-leak-free) split; annotator recruitment and compensation statement; and at minimum five baselines (random, frequency/prior, embedding-based KGED, MaxSAT/ILP, LLM+Allen+reflection).; PROTOCOL STEP 18 - Granularity conflicts get their own protocol arm, because the substrate is already measured. The 17,102 events with >=2 CONTAINS anchors (mostly nested granularity such as 'in 1778' + 'on November 11') are a natural positive-free consistency test: a granularity-conflict detector must accept NESTED anchors and reject INCOMPATIBLE ones. Build the eval by perturbing one anchor of a nested pair so the fine anchor falls outside the coarse one - a perturbation that is defined purely by interval containment arithmetic, independent of any mined constraint, hence structurally non-circular.; PROTOCOL STEP 19 - Optional extrinsic arm, pre-registered as secondary. Measure whether deleting flagged facts improves a downstream temporal ordering / timeline task, and follow timeline-evaluation practice by decomposing into event recovery (event match rate) and temporal localisation. Pre-register it as secondary and expect a possibly null result: extraction-error impact on downstream graph metrics is near zero within a band of extraction quality (arXiv 2506.12367).\nNOVELTY GAPS: No event-centric temporal conflict-detection benchmark exists. PaTeCon/PaTeCon+ operate on entity-centric encyclopaedic KGs (Wikidata WD27M, Freebase FB37M) where every statement has an explicit validity interval; MAVEN-ERE-style event graphs have qualitative relations, no normalised times, and document-local entities. Porting constraint-based conflict detection from the encyclopaedic regime to the document/event regime is genuinely unoccupied territory - and the entity-as-subject projection (entity_x, EventType#Role, event_e, T_e) is the non-obvious technical move that makes it possible.; GRANULARITY CONFLICT is named but never detected. KONTRAST (arXiv 2607.25959) lists 'information granularity differences' as one of four cross-modal inconsistency classes; the rest of the granularity literature is TKG *completion* (hierarchical year/month/day embeddings, quadruplet networks). Nobody has published a detection task, a dataset, or a metric for temporal-granularity inconsistency. This is the clearest available novelty claim and has a ready-made substrate in nested CONTAINS anchors.; Non-circular evaluation of MINED-constraint conflict detection is an open methodological gap, not just TempEKG's local problem. PaTeCon+ measures conflict recall on separately annotated wrong-fact sets (WD-411, FB-128) but does not control the PROVENANCE OVERLAP between the constraint language and the error-generating process, and does not report any circularity diagnostic. A 'provenance matrix + circularity gap' methodology would be a citable contribution independent of the dataset.; TIMEX normalisation with zero gold supervision is an unaddressed setting. Every temporal-consistency paper assumes normalised times (TimeML TIMEX3 values) or works on qualitative relations only. A weakly-supervised normaliser evaluated by DOWNSTREAM CONSISTENCY rather than by gold values - i.e. consistency as the supervision signal, following the Kougia et al. consistency-score idea in reverse - is unpublished and fits the 45-day budget.; Symbolic mined constraints have never been benchmarked head-to-head against 2025-generation LLM temporal reasoning (Allen-algebra prompting + reflection, Fan & Strube 2025) on a conflict-DETECTION task at document scale. The existing LLM temporal work is all extraction or QA; conflict detection under extreme class imbalance is a different and unmeasured regime, and current numbers suggest LLMs will do poorly (zero-shot temporal RE is ~30 F1 below supervised; ChatGPT F1 33.24 on TIMELINE).; Nobody has unified PaTeCon's TRIVALENT (positive/negative/UNKNOWN) treatment of uncertain time with METRE's multi-label treatment of Vague. PaTeCon discards UNKNOWN from support and confidence; METRE shows the Vague mass is recoverable and informative. A conflict detector with a calibrated 'possibly conflicting' output class, evaluated with an explicit UNDECIDABLE annotation label, does not exist.; The incompleteness-vs-contradiction distinction is systematically conflated in KG error-detection evaluation. The measured 13.6% subevent-without-implied-CONTAINS rate is a concrete, quantified instance of a distinction the literature names (KONTRAST) but no benchmark isolates or scores. A benchmark that scores the two separately - and penalises detectors that flag incompleteness as conflict - would be methodologically novel by itself.; Localisation of the culprit fact within a detected conflict set is unmeasured in the temporal-KG conflict-detection line. ReFACT establishes span/positional annotation as the standard in factual-error detection; no temporal conflict-detection paper reports culprit-identification accuracy. MaxSAT falsified-clause weighting gives a principled way to produce and evaluate it.; Evaluation of error detectors on graphs built from REAL SYSTEM OUTPUT (rather than injected noise) is rare in the temporal/event space, despite the ~30% synthetic-to-real recall drop being documented in the entity-KG space. A benchmark whose primary error track is the diff between a predicted ECKG and a gold ECKG - errors with a fully natural distribution and free labels - would be the first of its kind for event temporal graphs, and doubles as the justification for keeping the Phase-1 extractors in the pipeline.\n\n### Sweep time-granularity\nFINDINGS:\n- Multi-granularity temporal constraint networks are a fully worked-out, 25-year-old formalism. Bettini, Wang & Jajodia extend Dechter/Meiri/Pearl TCSP with constraints of the form \"T2 - T1 in [m,n] granules of granularity G\", show that consistency of a network with multiple granularities is NP-hard in general (unlike single-granularity STP, which is O(n^3)), and give both a sound-and-complete algorithm and approximate (arc/path-consistency-style) algorithms plus the minimal-network problem. The hardness comes from having to manipulate periodic sets representing granularities, not from disjunction. [WHY: This is the formalism the team should CITE as its substrate, not reinvent. It also dictates the key engineering decision: if you resolve every underspecified TIMEX to a concrete granule BEFORE consistency checking, you stay in single-granularity STP (polynomial, complete). If you keep yearless/periodic TIMEXes as periodic sets, you are in NP-hard multi-granularity TCSP. Given the 45-day deadline, resolve-then-STP is the only viable path; the periodic-set version is future work.]\n- Euzenat (Computational Intelligence 2001) is the closest formal ancestor of a \"granularity conflict\" notion. He explicitly frames the problem as: the relation between two objects may differ depending on the granularity considered, and \"when merging representations of different granularity, this may raise problems\". He defines upward and downward granularity-conversion operators over relation algebras, imposes six independent constraints they must satisfy, proves existence/uniqueness results, and derives the unique operator pairs for qualitative temporal relations. Two constructors (product, weakening) generate new qualitative systems preserving conversion properties. [WHY: A blanket claim of \"we are the first to formally define conflicts arising from multiple temporal granularities\" is FALSE and will be caught by any reviewer from the TIME / temporal-DB community. Euzenat's upward/downward conversion + consistency conditions IS a formal theory of cross-granularity conflict. The team must position as operationalisation-in-an-event-KG, not as formal novelty.]\n- The granularity vocabulary the team needs already has canonical formal definitions in the Bettini/Dyreson/Evans/Snodgrass/Wang \"Glossary of Time Granularity Concepts\" (LNCS 1399, 1998): granule, chronon, anchored vs unanchored, finer-than, groups-into, groups-periodically-into, subgranularity, shifting, covers (G ~ H iff image of G is contained in image of H), and partitions (G partitions H iff G covers H and G groups into H). Granularity conversion is decomposed into scaling / casting / regularization. [WHY: Use these exact terms in the paper's Definition section. In particular \"month-day-without-year\" (XXXX-11-11) is NOT a partition of the timeline: it covers days and groups periodically into years but does not partition. Calling it a granularity loosely will draw a reviewer objection; calling it a periodic subgranularity is correct and citable.]\n- Yearless dates are periodic sets, not intervals, and there is a dedicated tractable representation for them: Bettini & Mascetti map calendar/granularity expressions to MINIMAL PERIODIC SETS, giving canonical forms plus algorithms for equivalence and set operations. This is the correct representation for TIMEX3 values containing X-placeholders. [WHY: Directly answers the \"contained in November 11\" half of the question. \u27e6XXXX-11-11\u27e7 = {y-11-11 : y in Z}, a minimal periodic set with period 1 year. Intersection with \u27e61778\u27e7 = [1778-01-01, 1778-12-31] is computed by periodic-set intersection and yields the singleton {1778-11-11} \u2014 i.e. NOT a conflict but a refinement. Emptiness of that intersection is the conflict test.]\n- ISO-TimeML / TIMEX3 already has a standard underspecification syntax for exactly this case: the value attribute is an ISO-8601 profile with x/X placeholders for unspecified components \u2014 spec examples include value=\"xxxx-09\" for a bare month, value=\"xxxx-wxx-1\" for \"every Monday\", value=\"XXXX-WXX\" for \"last week\". Relative/underspecified timexes additionally carry temporalFunction, anchorTimeID (the TIMEX3 to which the denotation is applied) and valueFromFunction. The spec states that TIMEX3s treated as temporal functions \"typically appear in an underspecified form\" pending later resolution. [WHY: The team should emit TIMEX3-conformant values with X placeholders plus an explicit granularity field, NOT collapse everything to a date. The placeholder pattern is the machine-readable carrier of granularity, and anchorTimeID/temporalFunction is exactly the field needed for the 37.9% context-dependent cases. Free standards compliance = a reviewer-pleasing paragraph.]\n- HeidelTime's normalisation pipeline is the concrete engineering precedent for the team's ~1-week normaliser. It builds a context-independent representation (CIR) with UNDEF markers \u2014 UNDEF-year-12-25 for a yearless date, UNDEF-last-day for \"yesterday\", UNDEF-REF for referential cases \u2014 then a separate disambiguation phase fully specifies them. Crucially it has a distinct DOMAIN STRATEGY: for news, reference time = document creation time; for NARRATIVE documents (explicitly Wikipedia articles), reference time = the last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE. [WHY: MAVEN is Wikipedia = narrative domain, and MAVEN docs have NO document creation time. So DCT-based propagation is unavailable and the last-mentioned-DATE heuristic is the right (and citable) default. Also: the UNDEF-* vocabulary gives a ready-made two-stage design (CIR then disambiguation) which is exactly what makes the granularity-conflict detector possible \u2014 the conflict is only decidable after stage 2.]\n- The four-endpoint imprecise-interval representation exists in BOTH the temporal-DB and the temporal-IE literature. Dyreson & Snodgrass (TODS 1998, ICDE'93 best paper) formalise indeterminate instants as (lower chronon, upper chronon, probability mass) with query-time credibility and plausibility parameters, and note that even a determinate instant is only known to within a chronon. In temporal IE, Reimers et al. (ACL 2016) anchor TimeBank events at day granularity splitting single-day vs multi-day events, and follow-up work introduces a UNIFIED QUADRUPLE representation of time anchors covering Single-Day/Multi-Day x Certain/Uncertain, and allows tags coarser than day (e.g. month). [WHY: The team's event representation should be the quadruple \u27e8[b-,b+],[f-,f+]\u27e9 over a day-level chronon, which subsumes both PaTeCon's (start, finish, weight) statement node and MAVEN's CONTAINS anchors, and makes 'loose bound only' (27.2% of events) and 'tight anchor' (44.9%) a single uniform type rather than two cases. Cite Dyreson & Snodgrass for the semantics, Reimers/quadruple work for the IE-side precedent \u2014 this kills a 'why not just a timestamp?' reviewer question.]\n- Adding a `disjoint` head predicate is precisely what breaks polynomial-time complete detection. Nebel & B\u00fcrckert (JACM 1995) show ORD-Horn is a maximal tractable subclass of Allen's algebra, strictly containing the pointisable relations, on which path consistency is a COMPLETE decision procedure in cubic time; Krokhin, Jeavons & Jonsson (JACM 2003) give the complete classification into exactly 18 maximal tractable subalgebras. Interval disjointness translates to (end1 < start2) OR (end2 < start1) \u2014 a genuine disjunction of strict inequalities that is not ORD-Horn. [WHY: Actionable for Nhut (P2/P3) and Nhan (P4). PaTeCon's head predicate set {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} is NOT inside a tractable subalgebra because of `disjoint`. Options: (a) drop disjoint and get a complete O(n^3) Floyd-Warshall/path-consistency detector as an exact, non-mined BASELINE \u2014 a strong paper asset; (b) keep disjoint and route to SAT/ILP. Either way the paper should state which subalgebra its language sits in; almost no KG-conflict paper does, and it is cheap rigor.]\n- The most dangerous prior art is a 2026 preprint, 'Detecting Knowledge Inconsistencies Across Text, Tables, and Knowledge Graphs' (the Kontrast framework, Text-to-SPARQL + LLM reasoning). It defines a taxonomy of cross-modal inconsistencies whose FIRST category is 'information granularity differences' (alongside direct conflicts, temporal changes, KG incompleteness), and reports a measured breakdown of flagged conflicts: 74.2% genuine (e.g. incompatible birth dates), 18.6% granularity mismatch (year-only vs full date), 7.2% parsing/normalisation error. Its explicit recommendation is that contradiction benchmarks should 'either normalize values to a common granularity before labeling or focus on conflict cases where both sides share the same datatype and precision metadata'. [WHY: This paper names the phenomenon and quantifies it \u2014 so 'nobody has noticed granularity mismatch' is dead. BUT its stance is the exact opposite of the team's: it treats granularity mismatch as a FALSE POSITIVE to be normalised away and excluded from benchmarks. That is a positioning gift, not a blocker: TempEKG can claim the first work to treat granularity as a signal to be ADJUDICATED (conflict vs benign refinement vs unknown) rather than noise to be normalised, and can cite Kontrast's own 18.6% as the prevalence justification. This citation is mandatory in related work; omitting it is the single biggest novelty risk.]\n- No existing temporal-KG conflict-detection system defines a precision/granularity constraint family. PaTeCon (AAAI 2023) and PaTeCon+ mention granularity only as a DEFECT of hand-enumerated constraints ('labor-intensive and may have granularity issues'; 'constraints with only property restrictions may be too coarse'), and their three families are temporal disjointness, temporal ordering, mutual exclusion \u2014 none about precision. TeCre resolves conflicts via TKG embedding (no granularity notion). TISCO (JWS 2019) infers temporal scopes from the Web by matching/selection/merging with no precision reasoning. Soulard, Sa\u00efs & Raad (ESWC 2025) discover simple and complex temporal constraints over an entity timeline for explainable temporal fact validation \u2014 the nearest 2025 competitor \u2014 but again ordering/validity, not precision. [WHY: The defensible novelty slot is precisely 'a precision/granularity constraint family added to the mined-constraint taxonomy, with a detector and a benchmark'. PaTeCon's own text supplies the motivating quote: they diagnose granularity as a problem and do not solve it. Frame TempEKG as closing that stated gap in the paper the project already builds on.]\n- Wikidata already carries first-class precision metadata (precision 6=millennium, 7=century, 8=decade, 9=year, 10=month, 11=day, 12-14 hour/min/sec, with >day effectively unsupported), and the Wikidata data-quality discussions explicitly accept multiple mutually incompatible dates as separate statements for the same entity+property (Jesus's birth, Mallory's death) rather than as errors, and warn that 'too much precision is not accuracy, but false precision'. [WHY: Two consequences. (1) A granularity field on every anchor is standard practice, so adding one is not itself a contribution \u2014 the contribution must be the conflict SEMANTICS over it. (2) Wikidata's stance shows that multiple incompatible anchors can be legitimate epistemic disagreement, not corpus error. TempEKG must therefore distinguish 'incompatible because sources disagree' (disjunctive, benign, needs provenance) from 'incompatible within one document/statement' (conjunctive, a real conflict). Without that distinction reviewers will call the conflict class ill-posed.]\n- Semantic-web temporal validation confirms that OWL cannot express these checks and that SHACL is the current mechanism. Robaldo & Batsakis show OWL-Time's axiomatisation 'cannot enforce several validation constraints on temporal knowledge that can be expressed using the Time Ontology vocabulary' because OWL lacks operators to compare temporal data, and use SHACL shapes + SHACL-SPARQL rules to invalidate KGs OWL cannot; their worked failure case is precisely two instants attached to the same temporal entity carrying different dateTime values. [WHY: Gives an independent, non-embedding, non-mining baseline the team can implement in a day: express the granularity-conflict rule as a SHACL-SPARQL shape over the projected TKG. Having a declarative-standards baseline alongside the mined-constraint detector materially strengthens a COLING submission, and their 'two instants, different dateTime' example is a one-granularity special case of the team's rule \u2014 good contrast to cite.]\n- Document-level temporal consistency via ILP/structured prediction is the established repair mechanism in temporal IE: global inference imposing symmetry + transitivity (plus linguistic and temporal-causal constraints) over the whole temporal graph, with the caveat that ILP is NP-hard and needs an external solver or relaxation. 2025 work (Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction, Findings of EMNLP 2025) continues this line for LLM outputs, motivated by inconsistent model outputs introducing contradictions that impair document-level understanding. [WHY: Reusable directly, and it also reframes the orphaned-Phase-1 problem: the natural home for extracted (non-gold) events is a PREDICTED graph whose conflict rate is high \u2014 unlike gold's ~72 contradictions. Running the same detector on predicted vs gold graphs turns Phase 1 from an orphan into the source of real, non-synthetic conflicts, and gives a non-circular evaluation.]\n- Fuzzy/vague interval algebras are a mature alternative to crisp four-endpoint bounds: Schockaert & De Cock ('Imprecise Temporal Interval Relations', 2006; 'Qualitative Temporal Reasoning about Vague Events', IJCAI 2007) and Ohlbach ('Relations Between Fuzzy Time Intervals', TIME 2004, with the FuTI library) generalise Allen's 13 relations to intervals with gradual beginnings/endings, allowing NON-CONVEX fuzzy intervals; later work extends 4D-fluents and Allen's algebra with possibility degrees inside Fuzzy-OWL 2. [WHY: Recommendation: DO NOT use fuzzy intervals. They introduce membership functions with no supervision available (MAVEN has zero gold normalisation), destroy the crisp emptiness test that makes granularity conflict decidable, and buy nothing for the CONTAINS-dominated (~99%) anchoring mass. Cite them in related work as the deliberately-rejected alternative \u2014 a one-paragraph inoculation against a 'why not fuzzy?' review.]\n- 2025 TKG literature independently identifies multigranularity as an unsolved modelling problem: methods 'often assume unified temporal element standards and simplified temporal structures' whereas real data 'records multigranular temporal information, ranging from macro-level intervals (e.g., centuries) to fine-grained timestamps'. Alignment work (Towards Temporal Knowledge Graph Alignment in the Wild, 2025) and granularity-aware completion (LGRe / quadruplet networks, Sci Rep 2025) attack it with representation learning per granularity level and adaptive fusion, not with logical conflict detection. [WHY: Confirms the topic is live and rising in 2025-2026 (good for a COLING 2027 pitch) while showing the whole cluster treats granularity as a REPRESENTATION-LEARNING problem, leaving the symbolic detect-and-explain framing open. Explicitly contrast: they fuse granularities to improve link prediction; TempEKG adjudicates them to flag errors.]\nKEY PAPERS:\n- A general framework for time granularity and its application to temporal reasoning (Bettini, Wang, Jajodia \u2014 Annals of Mathematics and AI, 1998) https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1018938007511 - Defines granularity systems and the notion of a temporal constraint network with MULTIPLE granularities; proves consistency NP-hard in general; proposes approximate consistency and minimal-network algorithms. The substrate formalism for granularity conflict \u2014 cite as the definition, do not reinvent.\n- Solving multi-granularity temporal constraint networks (Bettini, Wang, Jajodia \u2014 Artificial Intelligence 140(1-2):107-152, 2002) https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0004370202002230 - The complete algorithmic treatment: sound-and-complete consistency/solution algorithm over constraints \u27e8[m,n] G\u27e9, complexity analysis, and multi-granularity-specific optimisations via periodic-set manipulation. This is the exact algorithm TempEKG's detector should be described as a restriction of.\n- Granularity in Relational Formalisms \u2014 With Application to Time and Space Representation (Euzenat \u2014 Computational Intelligence 17(4), 2001) https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/0824-7935.00170 - THE novelty threat on the formal side. Upward/downward granularity-conversion operators over relation algebras, six independence-proved constraints, uniqueness results, and the explicit statement that merging representations of different granularity 'raises problems'. Must be cited; the team's claim must be narrower than this paper's scope.\n- A Glossary of Time Granularity Concepts (Bettini, Dyreson, Evans, Snodgrass, Wang \u2014 Temporal Databases: Research and Practice, LNCS 1399, 1998) https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~will/papers/glossary.pdf - Canonical definitions of granule, chronon, finer-than, groups-into, groups-periodically-into, covers, partitions, subgranularity, and granularity conversion (scaling/casting/regularization). Supplies the exact terminology for the paper's Definitions section.\n- Supporting Temporal Reasoning by Mapping Calendar Expressions to Minimal Periodic Sets (Bettini & Mascetti (JAIR-era / arXiv 1110.2213)) https://arxiv.org/pdf/1110.2213 - Canonical minimal-periodic-set representation with equivalence and set operations \u2014 the correct data structure for TIMEX3 X-placeholder values like XXXX-11-11, and the machinery behind the intersection-emptiness conflict test.\n- Supporting Valid-Time Indeterminacy (Dyreson & Snodgrass \u2014 ACM TODS 23(1), 1998 (extends ICDE'93 best paper)) https://www2.cs.arizona.edu/~rts/pubs/TODS98.pdf - Indeterminate instants (lower/upper chronon + probability mass), query-time credibility/plausibility, compact 32-bit encodings, and the argument that no instant is known below chronon resolution. The database-side semantics for TempEKG's four-endpoint event representation.\n- Reasoning about Temporal Relations: A Maximal Tractable Subclass of Allen's Interval Algebra (Nebel & B\u00fcrckert \u2014 JACM 42(1), 1995) https://gki.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/nebel-burckert-jacm95.pdf - ORD-Horn maximality; path consistency is complete in cubic time inside ORD-Horn and strictly contains the pointisable relations. Determines whether PaTeCon's head-predicate language admits a polynomial complete detector (it does not, because of `disjoint`).\n- Reasoning about temporal relations: The tractable subalgebras of Allen's interval algebra (Krokhin, Jeavons & Jonsson \u2014 JACM 50(5), 2003) https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/876638.876639 - Complete classification into exactly 18 maximal tractable subalgebras. Lets the paper state precisely which subalgebra its relation language sits in \u2014 cheap rigor that essentially no KG-conflict paper provides.\n- Temporal Constraint Networks (Dechter, Meiri & Pearl \u2014 Artificial Intelligence 49, 1991) https://philpapers.org/rec/DECTCN-2 - STP (single interval per edge, polynomial via all-pairs shortest paths, conflict = negative cycle) vs TCSP (disjunctive, NP-hard). The dividing line that decides whether TempEKG's detector is exact-and-cheap or heuristic.\n- TimeML Specification 1.1/1.2 and ISO-TimeML (Pustejovsky et al.; ISO 24617-1) https://timeml.github.io/site/publications/timeMLdocs/timeml_1.1b.htm - TIMEX3 value as an ISO-8601 profile with x/X placeholders (xxxx-09, XXXX-WXX, xxxx-wxx-1) plus temporalFunction / anchorTimeID / valueFromFunction. The standard-compliant carrier of granularity and underspecification for TempEKG's normaliser output.\n- Increasing Informativeness in Temporal Annotation (narrative containers) (Stubbs & Pustejovsky \u2014 LAW V, 2011) http://amberstubbs.net/docs/LAW_2011_Stubbs_Pustejovsky_narrative_containers.pdf - Narrative containers / Narrative Time: TIMEXes act as containers that anchor nearby events, with annotation reduced to placing events into containers. This is EXACTLY MAVEN-ERE's CONTAINS-dominated (~99%) anchoring structure \u2014 the theoretical justification for treating multi-anchor events as nested containers, and for calling a non-nested anchor set anomalous.\n- Multilingual and cross-domain temporal tagging (HeidelTime) (Str\u00f6tgen & Gertz \u2014 LRE, 2013) https://ds.ifi.uni-heidelberg.de/files/Team/jannik/publications/StroetgenGertz_MultilingualCrossdomainTemporalTagging.pdf - UNDEF-* context-independent representation then disambiguation; and the NARRATIVE-domain strategy (reference time = last mentioned DATE) which is the right default for MAVEN's Wikipedia documents that lack a creation time.\n- Temporal Anchoring of Events for the TimeBank Corpus (Reimers, Dehghani & Gurevych \u2014 ACL 2016) https://aclanthology.org/P16-1207.pdf - Day-granularity event anchoring split into single-day vs multi-day events; the IE-side precedent for anchoring events to time rather than to each other, and for admitting coarser-than-day tags.\n- Predicting Event Time by Classifying Sub-Level Temporal Relations Induced from a Unified Representation of Time Anchors (arXiv 2008.06452 (follows Inducing Temporal Relations from Time Anchor Annotation, NAACL 2018)) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.06452 - Unified QUADRUPLE representation of time anchors spanning Single-Day/Multi-Day x Certain/Uncertain \u2014 the temporal-IE analogue of the four-endpoint bound, and the closest existing 'multi-precision event anchor' representation.\n- PaTeCon: A Pattern-Based Temporal Constraint Mining Method for Conflict Detection on Knowledge Graphs (Chen et al. \u2014 AAAI 2023) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.09015 - The paper TempEKG builds on. Its own framing supplies the motivating gap quote: hand-enumerated constraints 'may have granularity issues' and property-only constraints 'may be too coarse'. Its three constraint families contain NO precision/granularity family.\n- Conflict Detection for Temporal Knowledge Graphs: A Fast Constraint Mining Algorithm and New Benchmarks (PaTeCon+) (Chen, Ren, Ding, Ouyang, Hu, Qu \u2014 arXiv 2312.11053) https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11053 - Two-stage pruning and the new benchmarks. Benchmark-construction methodology to differentiate from \u2014 and the direct precedent for what a granularity-conflict benchmark contribution would look like.\n- Detecting Knowledge Inconsistencies Across Text, Tables, and Knowledge Graphs (Kontrast) (arXiv 2607.25959, 2026) https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25959 - THE critical novelty threat. Names 'information granularity differences' as a taxonomy category and reports 74.2% genuine / 18.6% granularity mismatch (year-only vs full date) / 7.2% normalisation error, then recommends normalising granularity away before labelling contradictions. Cite it, adopt its 18.6% as motivation, and invert its stance.\n- Explainable Temporal Fact Validation Through Constraints Discovery in Knowledge Graphs (Soulard, Sa\u00efs & Raad \u2014 ESWC 2025, LNCS 15718) https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-94575-5_13 - Nearest 2025 competitor: discovers simple and complex temporal constraints capturing consistency of facts within an entity's timeline, for explainable temporal validation. Entity-timeline-centric like TempEKG's mandated entity-as-subject projection \u2014 must be positioned against.\n- On the interplay between validation and inference in SHACL \u2014 an investigation on the Time Ontology (Robaldo & Batsakis \u2014 Semantic Web Journal / 2026) https://www.semantic-web-journal.net/content/interplay-between-validation-and-inference-shacl-investigation-time-ontology - Shows OWL-Time cannot enforce temporal validation constraints expressible in its own vocabulary and gives SHACL + SHACL-SPARQL shapes that can, with a worked 'two instants, different dateTime' case. A cheap declarative baseline for the granularity-conflict rule.\n- Towards Temporal Knowledge Graph Alignment in the Wild (arXiv 2507.14475, 2025) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.14475 - States plainly that TKG methods assume unified temporal standards while real data is multigranular (centuries to timestamps). Best 2025 citation that the problem is real and unsolved \u2014 and that the field's answer is representation learning, not detection.\n- Imprecise Temporal Interval Relations / Qualitative Temporal Reasoning about Vague Events (Schockaert & De Cock \u2014 2006 / IJCAI 2007) https://www.ijcai.org/Proceedings/07/Papers/090.pdf - Fuzzy generalisation of Allen's 13 relations to intervals with gradual boundaries and possibly non-convex support. Cite as the deliberately rejected alternative: no supervision exists for membership functions and it destroys the crisp emptiness test.\n- Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction with LLMs (Findings of EMNLP 2025) https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1010.pdf - Current state of global temporal consistency enforcement (symmetry/transitivity, ILP-style global inference) over document-level graphs \u2014 the repair-side technique and the argument that PREDICTED graphs, unlike gold, are rich in real conflicts.\n- TISCO: Temporal scoping of facts (Rula, Palmonari, Rubinacci, Ngonga Ngomo, Lehmann, Maurino, Esteves \u2014 Journal of Web Semantics 54, 2019) https://boa.unimib.it/bitstream/10281/211376/2/Rula-2019-J%20Web%20Semantics-AAM.pdf - Matching/selection/merging of temporal-scope evidence from Web + RDF for DBpedia facts (F1 up to ~80%). Evidence that even the dedicated temporal-scoping line does no precision reasoning \u2014 supports the novelty slot.\nREUSABLE: Two-stage normalisation, HeidelTime-style: emit a context-independent representation with explicit UNDEF/X markers (UNDEF-year-11-11, TIMEX3 value XXXX-11-11) in stage 1, then disambiguate in stage 2 using the NARRATIVE reference-time rule (last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE), since MAVEN's Wikipedia docs have no creation time. Keep the stage-1 output persisted \u2014 it is the only place granularity is observable, and the conflict detector needs it.; Represent every event as a four-endpoint quadruple over a day chronon: E(e) = \u27e8[b-,b+],[f-,f+]\u27e9. Cite Dyreson & Snodgrass for semantics and the unified-quadruple time-anchor work for the IE precedent. This uniformly subsumes tight-anchored, loose-bound-only, and unanchored events instead of special-casing them, and maps cleanly onto PaTeCon's (start, finish) statement fields.; Represent underspecified TIMEXes as MINIMAL PERIODIC SETS (Bettini & Mascetti) rather than intervals, and compute anchor compatibility by periodic-set intersection with a canonical-form emptiness test. This is what makes yearless dates ('November 11', 'every Sunday') first-class instead of discarded.; Down-cast then STP: convert all resolved anchors to the bottom granularity (days), giving only constraints of the form b >= x, f <= y, b <= f. That is a Simple Temporal Problem \u2014 Floyd-Warshall in O(n^3) decides consistency COMPLETELY, with conflict witnessed by a negative cycle. Use this as an exact, non-mined, fully explainable baseline detector alongside the mined constraints. Cite Dechter/Meiri/Pearl and Nebel & B\u00fcrckert for completeness.; Declare the relation language's position in the tractability classification. Dropping `disjoint` keeps the language inside ORD-Horn where path consistency is complete; keeping it forces SAT/ILP. State this explicitly \u2014 it is one paragraph and almost no KG-conflict paper does it.; Trivalent evaluation carried through granularity, matching PaTeCon's positive/negative/UNKNOWN: return CONFLICT only when the intersection is provably empty, CONSISTENT when nonempty, and UNKNOWN when an anchor's underspecification could not be resolved. Never let an unresolved yearless date produce a conflict \u2014 that is precisely the 7.2% normalisation-error bucket Kontrast measured.; Euzenat's upward/downward conversion operators for the case where two anchors are incomparable but intersecting: the intersection of two calendar granules (e.g. 1778 x November) may not itself be a granule of any single calendar granularity, so an explicit conversion/anchoring step is needed rather than naive min/max on endpoints.; SHACL-SPARQL shapes as a second, declarative detector (Robaldo & Batsakis): encode the granularity-conflict rule as a shape over the projected entity-as-subject TKG. Gives a standards-based baseline in roughly a day and pre-empts 'why not just constraints in the ontology?'.; ILP / global-inference repair with symmetry + transitivity over the document-level graph (Findings of EMNLP 2025 and the structured-prediction line) as the resolution stage after detection, and as the mechanism for turning detected conflicts into a corrected graph \u2014 turning detection into a usable contribution rather than a report.; Non-circular evaluation via normaliser-level perturbation rather than constraint-level perturbation: generate granularity conflicts by shifting a granule at ONE precision level in the TIMEX normaliser's output (e.g. year 1778 -> 1779 while leaving the day anchor) and detect them with the granularity-lattice / STP checker, which was never fit to any mined constraint. This breaks the diagnosed circularity that afflicts the ordering-conflict evaluation, and is the strongest single argument for prioritising granularity conflicts over ordering conflicts under the 45-day budget.; Run the SAME detector on the gold graph and on the Phase-1 extractor's predicted graph. Gold is near-conflict-free, so gold cannot evaluate a detector; the predicted graph supplies real, non-synthetic conflicts and simultaneously de-orphans Phase 1 without changing the Phase-2 gold-based construction.\nNOVELTY GAPS: VERDICT, UNAMBIGUOUS: 'Granularity conflict' is NOT available as a formal-definition contribution. Formalising inconsistency across multiple temporal granularities was done by Bettini/Wang/Jajodia (AMAI 1998, AI 2002 \u2014 multi-granularity TCSP consistency, NP-hard, with algorithms) and by Euzenat (Computational Intelligence 2001 \u2014 granularity conversion operators with proved upward/downward consistency constraints, explicitly motivated by problems when merging representations of different granularity). Any sentence of the form 'we are the first to formally define conflicts arising from multiple temporal precisions' is false and will be rejected by a TIME/temporal-DB-literate reviewer.; VERDICT, SECOND HALF: the phenomenon is also no longer un-noticed empirically. The 2026 Kontrast preprint (arXiv 2607.25959) names 'information granularity differences' as the first category of its inconsistency taxonomy and measures it at 18.6% of flagged conflicts (vs 74.2% genuine, 7.2% normalisation error). This paper MUST be cited; discovering it at review time would be fatal.; WHAT IS STILL OPEN (the defensible claim): granularity has never been operationalised as a first-class, adjudicated ERROR TYPE inside a KG conflict-detection pipeline. Kontrast treats it as a false positive to normalise away and explicitly recommends excluding it from benchmarks; PaTeCon/PaTeCon+ name granularity only as a defect of hand-written constraints and have no precision constraint family; TeCre, TISCO, Soulard et al. (ESWC 2025), and the Wikidata quality framework have no precision reasoning. Claim the INVERSION: granularity divergence is a signal to be adjudicated into {benign refinement, genuine conflict, unknown}, not noise to be normalised \u2014 and cite Kontrast's own 18.6% as the prevalence justification.; SECOND OPEN SLOT: event-centric rather than entity-attribute. All prior granularity-mismatch discussion is about entity attributes (birth dates, year-only vs full date). Nobody has defined granularity conflict over EVENTS carrying multiple text-derived containment anchors at differing precisions, nor connected it to Stubbs & Pustejovsky's narrative containers. This is a genuinely unoccupied intersection of temporal IE and KG quality.; THIRD OPEN SLOT: a benchmark. There is no dataset of labelled temporal-granularity conflicts. Given that the local gold corpus contains only ~72 genuine contradictions, the benchmark itself (perturbation protocol + human-verified precision study + the benign/conflict/unknown three-way labels) is plausibly a larger contribution than the detector.; CRITICAL DESIGN WARNING: nesting is the NORMAL case, not a conflict. Multi-anchor events form a subset-chain (year contains month contains day) in the overwhelming majority of cases \u2014 a detector that flags 'event has >=2 anchors at different precisions' would be almost entirely false positives, exactly the failure mode Kontrast quantified. The conflict definition must be intersection-emptiness (or provable non-chain structure), never precision-difference.; POSITIONING WARNING: distinguish CONJUNCTIVE incompatibility (multiple anchors within one document/statement must all hold; empty intersection is a real error) from DISJUNCTIVE disagreement (independent sources give rival dates; legitimate epistemic disagreement \u2014 Wikidata explicitly permits multiple incompatible dates as separate statements for the same entity+property). Without this split the conflict class is ill-posed, and the disjunctive reading additionally pushes the problem into NP-hard disjunctive TCSP.; GAP THE TEAM CAN ALSO CLAIM CHEAPLY: no KG temporal-conflict paper states which tractable subalgebra its relation language occupies. PaTeCon's {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} sits outside ORD-Horn because interval disjointness is a genuine disjunction of strict inequalities, so its detection problem is NP-hard while an exact O(n^3) complete detector exists for the disjoint-free sublanguage. Stating and exploiting this is a small, safe, defensible technical contribution.\n\n### Sweep constraint-mining\nFINDINGS:\n- The single strongest 'formal footing' replacement for PaTeCon's ad-hoc support/confidence is the soundness/atomicity criterion from Martin, de Almeida, Romero & Queralt, 'How and Why False Denial Constraints are Discovered' (PVLDB 18, 2025, pp. 3477-...). They show empirically that across FastDC, Hydra, DCFinder, ADCMiner and FastADC the proportion of discovered DCs that are NOT golden 'is rarely below 95%', and that over 95% of discovered DCs have >=5 predicates on half the datasets. Their diagnosis is that the standard validity definition (satisfiability + minimality + non-triviality) accepts any conjunction of INDEPENDENT predicates whose joint probability of co-occurrence happens to be below the approximation factor. Proof sketch in the paper: if p(phi) (joint probability under independence) decreases at the same rate as 1/(n-1) as tuples n grow, 'the algorithm is simply agglomerating independent predicates'. [WHY: This is exactly PaTeCon's failure mode, stated as a theorem with a fix, and it is the citable stick to beat PaTeCon with. PaTeCon's 'temporal disjointness' / 'mutual exclusion' constraints with support>=10 and conf>=0.9 are precisely conjunctions of independent conditions: a constraint like (EventType A #Role r1) disjoint (EventType B #Role r2) can reach conf 0.9 simply because A and B rarely share a participant at all, not because any temporal relationship holds. With 2,708 (T1,T2) signatures at support>=10 and 652 EventType#Role properties, TempEKG will mine thousands of such constraints. Adopting their ATOMICITY test turns 'we picked conf=0.9' into 'we reject any constraint whose satisfaction is not attributable to a dependence between its predicates', which is a defensible COLING contribution rather than a threshold.]\n- Their atomicity test is cheap and fully specified: for predicate set phi={p1..pn}, phi is NOT atomic if it can be reduced by dropping some p_i without changing the conditional behaviour of any remaining p_j, i.e. if P(p_j | phi\\{p_i,p_j}) = P(p_j | phi\\{p_j}). They estimate each conditional as Bernoulli with a Beta-distributed population parameter, update by Bayesian learning to Beta(a+1,b+1) from a successes / b failures, and compute the probability that the log-odds ratio between the two conditionals is not smaller than zero; the equality is 'not fulfilled' (i.e. real dependence) if that probability falls below a constant alpha. They report that 'probabilities for uncorrelated predicates never fell below 0.01, meaning using any reasonable alpha value would lead to similar results'. They also split triviality into SYNTACTIC (always satisfied from the DC's structure alone, known before seeing data) and SEMANTIC (always satisfied because the joint probability of the predicates is minuscule) and show semantic triviality is a subset of non-soundness. Result: sound DCs have 'consistently reasonable lengths', the count of false DCs drops by over 95%, and they recover true DCs on Tax that domain experts had missed; no golden DC was lost to atomicity testing except where data was too small. [WHY: Implementable in well under a week: two Beta counters per (predicate, conditioning-set) pair over MAVEN's 274,525 shared-participant event pairs, one alpha=0.01 threshold, no new theory. It gives Nhan a principled pruner that sits on top of the existing PaTeCon pipeline instead of replacing it, and it produces a headline number of the same shape as the PVLDB paper's ('X% of PaTeCon-style temporal constraints on an event KG are unsound'), which is a publishable finding on its own.]\n- The current work closest to TempEKG's target is Soulard, Sais & Raad, 'Explainable Temporal Fact Validation Through Constraints Discovery in Knowledge Graphs', ESWC 2025 (LNCS 15718, pp. 227-244). They discover both SIMPLE and COMPLEX temporal constraints capturing temporal consistency of facts within an ENTITY'S TIMELINE, and \u2014 critically \u2014 express them 'through an extension to time sequences of Allen's temporal algebra', then validate facts with both symbolic and ML-based classifiers on Wikidata. The same authors followed up with 'When Facts Expire: Benchmarking Temporal Validity in Knowledge Graphs', CIKM 2025. [WHY: This paper already occupies two positions the project assumed were free: (a) constraints anchored to an entity timeline (which is exactly the 'entity-as-subject' projection the project decided it must use), and (b) an Allen-algebra formalisation replacing PaTeCon's five ad-hoc head predicates {start, finish, before, disjoint, include}. It must be cited and differentiated. The available differentiator is that they work on Wikidata entity-attribute facts, not on EVENTS with 168 types and 143 roles, and not on a document-local graph with no cross-doc entity linking. Adopting Allen's 13 relations as the constraint head vocabulary also gives free formal machinery: closure under composition, path consistency, and the ORD-Horn tractable subalgebra of Krokhin/Jeavons/Jonsson (JACM 50(5), 2003) for polynomial consistency checking.]\n- Allen's interval algebra is already the operative formalism in 2025 event-relation NLP: Fan & Strube, 'Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction Using Large Language Models', Findings of EMNLP 2025 (pp. 18605-18622), predict temporal relations by comparing the start time and end time of two events under Allen's interval algebra to enforce consistency at the discourse level. Separately, IA-RAG (2026) represents events as explicit temporal intervals and performs retrieval under Allen's Interval Algebra. [WHY: Two uses. First, it makes the Allen reformulation of the ordering-conflict head predicates a natural fit for a COLING audience rather than a DB-venue import. Second and more importantly, Fan & Strube's setting is the honest fix for the circular fake-conflict evaluation: run a temporal relation extractor over the blind/held-out MAVEN documents, and detect conflicts in the PREDICTED relation graph. The measured gold graph has ~72 genuine contradictions and 0 BEFORE cycles, so gold offers no target \u2014 but predicted graphs from LLM/classifier ERE systems are known to be globally inconsistent, and those inconsistencies were generated by a process entirely independent of the mined constraints. That breaks circularity without inventing a new benchmark, and it also un-orphans Phase 1.]\n- MiniAMIE is real, brand-new, and squarely about making rule mining cheap: 'MiniAMIE: Quick and Dirty Rule Mining on Knowledge Bases' by Luis Galarraga, Julianne Guerbette, Isseinie Sinouvassane and Paul Viallard, The Web Conference (WWW) 2026, shipped as the -mini flag in dig-team/AMIE. It (1) restricts AMIE's language bias to CLOSED PATHS only, (2) approximates support and confidence using independence assumptions instead of computing them exactly, and (3) consequently drops several of AMIE's indexes. Reported effect: mining only closed paths incurs little penalty in downstream prediction and can even help; full MiniAMIE (approximated metrics) has a dataset-dependent penalty but gave a slight hits@10 boost on nations. Benchmark code at gitlab.inria.fr/vguerbet/benchmark-miniamie. AMIE 3's own documented defaults are PCA confidence 0.1 and head coverage 0.01. [WHY: Note the irony worth exploiting: MiniAMIE's independence approximation of support/confidence is the SAME independence assumption that Martin et al. show produces false constraints. For TempEKG the operative lesson is the opposite of PaTeCon+'s: PaTeCon's 'law of large numbers' two-stage pruning and MiniAMIE's independence approximation are both efficiency hacks that trade away exactly the dependence signal needed to tell a real constraint from an artefact. Since the projected index is only ~425K entries / ~0.1 GB, TempEKG does not need the approximation at all, and can afford exact counts plus the atomicity test \u2014 which is a clean positioning statement: 'at event-KG scale, efficiency is not the bottleneck; validity is.']\n- PCA confidence is not a safe upgrade over PaTeCon's plain confidence and should not be adopted uncritically. The PCA assumes that if a subject has at least one object for a predicate, no further objects exist in the real world; it is sensible for functional/quasi-functional relations (place of birth, nationality) but 'clearly less sound' for n-to-n relations (friendships, co-authorships). Under random fact missingness PCA confidence performs WORSE than standard confidence, while completeness confidence (Tanon, Stepanova, Razniewski, Mirza, Weikum, 'Completeness-aware Rule Learning from Knowledge Graphs', ISWC 2017 / IJCAI 2018) significantly outperforms both. The broader landscape of measures and their weaknesses is catalogued in Kalo et al./Barba et al., 'Neurosymbolic Methods for Rule Mining' (arXiv:2408.05773) and in Razniewski et al., 'Completeness, Recall, and Negation in Open-world Knowledge Bases: A Survey' (ACM Computing Surveys 56, 2024). [WHY: The relation being mined here, entity -> 'EventType#Role' -> event, is emphatically n-to-n (16,284 entities in >=2 events, mean 3.15, max 59), which is the exact regime where PCA confidence is unsound. So 'we swapped in PCA confidence' would be a weak and attackable move. Completeness confidence is also awkward because it needs completeness oracles/cardinality statements that MAVEN does not have. The defensible line is: standard confidence + explicit trivalent treatment of unknown (which PaTeCon already has) + atomicity soundness, and an explicit paragraph explaining why PCA is inapplicable. That paragraph is itself a citable contribution to an NLP venue that has never had this discussion.]\n- For EVENT-level constraints specifically, declarative process mining is a far better-fitting formal footing than KG rule mining, and it already has exactly the three measures PaTeCon lacks a grounding for. DECLARE/MINERful defines, per constraint template over an event log: SUPPORT = number of fulfilments divided by number of traces (for existence constraints) or by number of occurrences of the ACTIVATION (for relation constraints); CONFIDENCE = support scaled by the fraction of traces where the constrained activity / activation occurs; plus INTEREST FACTOR. The template catalogue is ordering-native: response, precedence, succession, alternate and chain variants, not-co-existence, plus negative templates. MINERful was 'the most efficient algorithm to discover control-flow constraints using the Declare language', ships redundancy AND inconsistency simplification, and was re-released as 'Declarative Process Mining with MINERful, Reloaded' (CEUR Vol-4032, paper-43, 2025). Declare constraints reduce to LTLf formulae, and there is a direct ASP encoding (arXiv:2412.10152, 2024). Related 2025 work mines declarative best-practice constraints from reference models and checks logs for violations (arXiv:2407.02336). [WHY: This is the cheapest high-value adaptation available in 45 days. A MAVEN document with its events ordered by the (essentially conflict-free, 100% transitively closed) BEFORE relation IS a trace; 4,480 documents = 4,480 traces; the 168 event types are the activity alphabet. Mining Declare templates over event-type traces gives ordering constraints ('Prison never precedes Arrest' as not-succession) with a 20-year-old citable definition of support/confidence/interest factor and a battle-tested activation-based denominator \u2014 which is precisely the denominator question PaTeCon fudges. MINERful is off-the-shelf Java, so this is days not weeks, and it yields a second constraint family for free, giving the paper two independently-grounded mining tracks to compare against PaTeCon's.]\n- The graph-constraint literature has purpose-built temporal formalisms that already solve TempEKG's projection problem, so the project does not need to bend an event graph into a Wikidata-shaped statement graph. Temporal Graph Functional Dependencies (TGFDs) impose topological AND attribute-dependency constraints over a PERIOD OF TIME across a sequence of graph snapshots induced by time intervals; satisfiability, implication and validation for k-bounded TGFDs are in PTIME, and TGFDMiner discovers MINIMAL, FREQUENT TGFDs (Alipourlangouri, Mansfield, Chiang, Zhu, arXiv:2108.08719; Noronha & Chiang, 'Discovery of Temporal Graph Functional Dependencies', CIKM 2021). The static siblings are Graph Entity Dependencies (GEDs = graph pattern + attribute dependency, unifying keys and FDs; Fan & Lu, TODS 2019), with discovery as a MINIMAL COVER problem (arXiv:2301.06264 / Information Systems 2024), Graph Differential Dependencies (GDDs, ADC 2023), and Graph Generating Dependencies (GGDs, arXiv:2403.17082) which can enforce EXISTENCE of a node/edge/pattern. [WHY: PaTeCon's two hand-fixed structural patterns SP(a)/SP(b) are the weakest part of the whole method and the hardest thing to defend to a reviewer ('why these two?'). A GED/TGFD framing replaces 'two hand-drawn patterns' with 'graph pattern + dependency, discovered as a minimal cover', which is a standard, cited, and complexity-characterised formulation. The GGD ability to enforce EXISTENCE also maps directly onto the 1,638/12,019 (13.6%) subevent pairs lacking an implied CONTAINS: those are not contradictions but missing-edge violations, which GEDs/DCs cannot express and GGDs can. That distinction (contradiction vs incompleteness) is worth a subsection.]\n- Approximate-DC machinery gives the exact vocabulary for tolerating noise without hand-picking a confidence threshold. A-FastDC (Chu, Ilyas & Papotti, VLDB 2013) introduced approximate DCs and C-FastDC introduced constants in predicates; Pena, de Almeida & Naumann, 'Discovery of Approximate (and Exact) Denial Constraints' (PVLDB 13, 2020, DCFinder) and 'Fast Algorithms for Denial Constraint Discovery' (PVLDB 16, 2023) scaled it; Xiao/Tan et al., 'Fast Approximate Denial Constraint Discovery' (PVLDB 16, 269-...) reports its ClueToEvi being 7.5x-10.1x faster than EviBuild and AEI 8.9x-1237x faster than SearchMC; Hydra (Bleifuss, Kruse & Naumann, PVLDB 11, 2017) achieves near-linear rather than FastDC's quadratic scaling (Tax: FastDC exceeds a 15h limit; Hydra ~40 min). Rapidash (arXiv:2309.12436) pursues constraint discovery via rapid verification. Incremental DC discovery exists (VLDB Journal 2023). Deep-RL-based DC discovery appeared at CIKM 2024. [WHY: TempEKG does NOT need any of these algorithms \u2014 evidence-set construction over 274,525 event pairs is trivial versus the millions-of-tuples regime these target. What it needs is their LANGUAGE: an approximate DC with a violation-rate/g1-style error bound is the same object as a PaTeCon constraint with confidence < 1, but stated in a form a reviewer recognises. Cite Hydra/DCFinder for the evidence-set formulation (the set of predicate-satisfaction bitvectors over tuple pairs), then note that PaTeCon's confidence is a per-constraint g1 error. Warning from the same literature, though: Martin et al. found approximation itself 'sometimes results in longer DCs and increases the number of false DCs', and that it 'introduces underfitting, causing algorithms to identify subsets of true rules as valid DCs, which then leads to the rejection of these true DCs due to minimality' \u2014 so minimality + approximation interact badly and PaTeCon inherits this.]\n- The granularity-conflict half of the project has no good match in DC/rule mining but does have one in numeric conformance constraints and in temporal-ontology validation. Conformance Constraint Discovery (Fariha, Nath, Meliou et al., SIGMOD 2021) defines constraints over arithmetic PROJECTIONS of numerical attributes and is explicitly motivated by the fact that 'existing data profiling primitives such as functional dependencies and denial constraints cannot model' them. On the temporal side, Robaldo & Batsakis (2026) show the OWL axiomatisation of the W3C Time Ontology 'cannot enforce several validation constraints on temporal knowledge that can be expressed using its vocabulary', and give a SHACL formalisation that does. The metric-vs-qualitative distinction is standard: metric constraints restrict the DISTANCE between points ('five time units after'), qualitative constraints restrict relative position. [WHY: 'Granularity conflict' as currently defined (inconsistent temporal precision for the SAME event) is a METRIC/numeric property \u2014 the width of the anchoring interval \u2014 not a qualitative Allen relation, and PaTeCon has no head predicate for it. The 17,102 events with >=2 CONTAINS anchors, which are usually NESTED granularity ('in 1778' + 'on November 11'), are a numeric-containment phenomenon: |I_coarse| >> |I_fine| and I_fine subset-of I_coarse. Framing them as (a) an Allen 'during' constraint plus (b) a conformance constraint on log-width ratio gives a defensible two-part formalisation, and the Time Ontology SHACL paper supplies the vocabulary (xsd date granularity, time:unitType) plus a citable statement that OWL/DL cannot express it \u2014 which is the justification for mining constraints at all.]\n- The SHACL side is now genuinely usable for both induction and validation. Induction: SHACLearner (Rabbani, Lissandrini, Hose / Omran, Taylor, Rodriguez Mendez, Haller; Semantic Web Journal 13(4), 2022, doi:10.3233/SW-223063) adapts an embedding-based Open Path Rule Learner to mine Inverse Open Path (IOP) rules, augmented with minimum-cardinality constraints and quality measures IOP Standard Confidence, IOP Head Coverage and Cardinality, plus a numerical confidence measure; evaluated on YAGO2s (4M facts), DBpedia 3.8 (11M), Wikidata (8M). SHACTOR (SIGMOD 2023 demo; VLDB 16 'Extraction of Validating Shapes from Very Large Knowledge Graphs') takes a KG plus a support threshold omega and confidence threshold epsilon and returns node shapes with supp(s)>omega and property shapes with supp(phi_s)>omega and conf(phi_s)>epsilon, explicitly to isolate SPURIOUS shapes caused by erroneous data, and auto-generates SPARQL to retrieve the offending nodes/facts. Validation: UpSHACL (Zacouris, Ke, Acosta; ISWC 2025) gives a formal model of the subgraph AFFECTED BY AN UPDATE, builds a reduced subgraph validated by off-the-shelf SHACL engines, and reports up to 10x speedup with no missed results; 'SHACL Validation Under Graph Updates' (ISWC 2025) is a companion. Caveats are documented: 'Is SHACL Suitable for Data Quality Assessment?' (arXiv:2507.22305 / CEUR Vol-4093) enumerates limits, and violation reports are routinely too large to interpret (SHACLens, Frontiers in Bioinformatics 2026; xpSHACL, arXiv:2507.08432). [WHY: Three concrete uses. (1) SHACTOR's supp/conf-over-shapes formulation is the closest published precedent to PaTeCon's thresholds and, unlike PaTeCon, it is framed as SPURIOUSNESS DETECTION \u2014 cite it as the honest interpretation of what low-support constraints mean. (2) SHACLearner supplies IOP Head Coverage as an already-named measure for the entity-as-subject projection, so head coverage does not have to be reinvented. (3) UpSHACL is directly reusable for Phase 5: conflict detection on the test graph is exactly 'validate only the subgraph affected by an update', and adopting it turns a naive re-scan into a citable incremental algorithm. But do NOT propose emitting the constraints as SHACL shapes: plain SHACL-core cannot compare interval endpoints across two nodes, which is the whole ordering-conflict family.]\n- The fake-conflict circularity problem is a recognised methodological failure in the data-cleaning community, with 2025 papers to cite. 'Towards Practical Benchmarking of Data Cleaning Techniques: On Generating Authentic Errors via Large Language Models' (arXiv:2507.10934, 2025) exists precisely because synthetic injected errors misrepresent real ones. The REIN benchmark (EDBT 2023, arXiv:2302.04702) is the standard harness covering Raha (error detection via automated configuration of many detection strategies), Baran (error correction), HoloDetect, ED2 and HoloClean (probabilistic inference unifying integrity constraints, external data and statistical co-occurrence for holistic repair); reported example figures include Raha and ED2 at avg F1 0.80 and 0.78 on Adult. 'Systematic Evaluation of Knowledge Graph Repair with Large Language Models' (arXiv:2507.22419, 2025) addresses the KG-side version. A recent KG-construction benchmark states explicitly that noise patterns from text extraction 'are complex and domain-dependent, and therefore differ substantially from the simplified synthetic noise typically considered in controlled experimental studies' (arXiv:2605.05476). Even PaTeCon's own successor is caught here: PaTeCon+ (arXiv:2312.11053) had to build WD-Confl and YG-Confl benchmarks rather than find real conflicts. [WHY: Gives the project language and citations to convert its worst weakness into a stated contribution. The defensible design: (i) NEVER generate conflicts by violating a mined constraint; (ii) generate perturbations from an INDEPENDENT generator \u2014 TIMEX re-normalisation errors from the measured normalisation difficulty distribution (37.9% context-dependent, with the four named families), or granularity coarsening/refinement drawn from the observed nested-anchor distribution \u2014 and report per-family recall; (iii) add a genuinely independent test set from a temporal-relation extractor's predictions. Point (ii) is strong because the perturbation distribution is empirically measured from the corpus rather than invented, which is exactly what arXiv:2507.10934 argues for.]\n- Statistically-principled alternatives to fixed support/confidence thresholds exist and are cheap for this data size. Tatti's minimal-window significance for episodes deems an episode significant if the average length of its MINIMAL WINDOWS deviates strongly from the expectation under an independence model ('Significance of Episodes Based on Minimal Windows', arXiv:1902.02755); related work summarises episode sets (arXiv:1902.02834) and ranks episodes by a partition model (arXiv:1902.01002). Significance-based discriminative sequential pattern mining is formulated under MULTIPLE HYPOTHESIS TESTING (Expert Systems with Applications 2019), building on Webb's 'Multiple Hypothesis Testing in Pattern Discovery' (arXiv:0906.5263) and swap-randomisation for assessing data-mining results. Time-constrained sequential pattern mining has a settled semantics \u2014 mingap, maxgap and sliding window (swin), with adjacent-element time differences required to lie in [min-gap, max-gap] \u2014 realised in constraint-programming form (Constraints 2018) and in gapped/window-constrained algorithms (Springer 2020); negative sequential patterns with gap constraints reached TKDD 2025. [WHY: With 652 candidate properties the candidate constraint space is roughly 10^5-10^6 hypotheses, small enough that Holm-Bonferroni or Benjamini-Hochberg FDR control over per-constraint exact binomial/Fisher tests is computationally free and replaces support=10 / conf=0.9 with a reported FDR level. That single change ('we report constraints at FDR <= 0.05 instead of at a hand-tuned confidence') is the cheapest way to claim a stronger formal footing than PaTeCon, and it composes with the atomicity test (atomicity kills dependence-free constraints; FDR kills small-sample flukes \u2014 the two failure modes are different).]\n- Rule confidence over temporal KGs has moved past TLogic/TILP in ways relevant to how a temporal constraint's confidence should be defined. TLogic (Liu, Ma, Hildebrandt, Joblin, Tresp, AAAI 2022, arXiv:2112.08025) mines temporal logical rules via temporal random walks; TILP (Xiong, Yang et al., ICLR 2024, arXiv:2402.12309) is differentiable, uses a Markov-constrained random walk and explicit temporal operators, and handles TIME INTERVALS rather than timestamps; StreamLearner was the earliest extension of static rule learning to TKGs. TempValid (Huang et al., ACL 2024 long, 'Confidence is not Timeless: Modeling Temporal Validity for Rule-based Temporal Knowledge Graph Forecasting') makes the key conceptual move: it treats a rule's confidence as a learnable TIME FUNCTION rather than a static count ratio, with rule-adversarial and time-aware negative sampling, and beats prior rule-based methods on six TKGF datasets. CountTRuCoLa (arXiv:2509.09474, 2025) continues on rule confidence learning for TKG forecasting. NeuSTIP (arXiv:2305.11301) does neuro-symbolic link AND time prediction using Allen-relation-based rule templates. [WHY: Two caveats worth stating in the paper. First, all of these optimise FORECASTING (predict a future fact), not CONSISTENCY CHECKING, so their confidence semantics (a scoring function for ranking) is not interchangeable with a constraint's confidence (a bound on violation rate) \u2014 saying so explicitly pre-empts an obvious reviewer question about why TLogic/TILP are not baselines. Second, NeuSTIP is the exception worth mining for design: it uses Allen-relation rule templates over intervals, which is the template vocabulary TempEKG needs, and it is the natural bridge citation between the NLP/TKG literature and the Allen-algebra constraint framing.]\n- There is an explicit 2025 survey to anchor the whole 'conflict detection' framing: 'Dealing with Inconsistency for Reasoning over Knowledge Graphs: A Survey' (arXiv:2502.19023, 26 Feb 2025) organises the field into (a) detecting the parts of the KG that cause inconsistency, (b) fixing an inconsistent KG, and (c) inconsistency-tolerant reasoning. Detection splits into EXACT methods (recursive hitting-set enumeration over minimal explanations, with modularisation over independent subsets for efficiency; complexity from NExpTime down to PTime) and APPROXIMATE methods (ML classifiers predicting inconsistency from learned ABox patterns). Repair uses distance functions weighting alterations to find minimal fixes, assertion-reliability queries, and human-in-the-loop tools (OntoDebug). Tolerant reasoning covers repair-based AR/CAR semantics and many-valued/paraconsistent logics with multiple truth values plus probabilistic weighting. Stated open challenges: scalability, expressiveness-vs-efficiency, and integrating ML with algorithmic approaches. Companion: Akasiadis, Nentidis, Charalambidis, Artikis, 'Detecting and Fixing Inconsistencies in Large Knowledge Graphs' (SETN/Hellenic AI 2024; journal version 2025), and 'Fast Computation of Explanations for Inconsistency in Large-Scale Knowledge Graphs' (WWW 2020). [WHY: PaTeCon's trivalent positive/negative/UNKNOWN logic is, in this survey's terms, an ad-hoc many-valued inconsistency-tolerant semantics, and the survey provides the standard names (AR/CAR semantics, paraconsistent logics) to describe it properly. More practically, the survey's 'minimal explanation / hitting set' framing is what the project should use for OUTPUT: rather than flagging a conflicting pair, return the minimal set of statements whose removal restores consistency. That is a strictly better deliverable than a binary conflict flag, it is what makes the result 'explainable' in the ESWC-2025 sense, and on a document-local graph (max ~59 events per entity, no cross-doc entities) the hitting-set computation is trivially small.]\n- MAVEN-ERE's own annotation-completeness literature bears directly on why gold contains almost no contradictions. MAVEN-ERE (Wang et al., EMNLP 2022) contains 103,193 coreference chains, 1,216,217 temporal relations, 57,992 causal and 15,841 subevent relations, with a TIMELINE annotation scheme yielding 95.3 temporal relations per 100 words versus 14.3 in MATRES. EventFull (arXiv:2412.12733) targets COMPLETE AND CONSISTENT event relation annotation, i.e. it treats completeness and consistency of the relation set as the annotation problem itself. 'Only One Relation Possible? Modeling the Ambiguity in Event Temporal Relation Extraction' (arXiv:2408.07353) argues the single-label assumption is wrong and models multi-label ambiguity. [WHY: Explains the measured conflict-free gold graph as a consequence of the annotation scheme (timeline-based annotation enforces global consistency by construction), which is the honest reason gold offers no mining target \u2014 not a claim that Wikipedia event text is consistent. Citing EventFull and the ambiguity paper also opens the more interesting reframing: the actionable defect in MAVEN-ERE is INCOMPLETENESS and AMBIGUITY, not contradiction, so mined constraints are better evaluated as (a) predictors of missing relations (e.g. the 1,638 subevent pairs lacking implied CONTAINS, and the 7 missing BEFORE transitive chains) and (b) detectors of inconsistency in PREDICTED graphs. Both targets are real and neither is circular.]\nKEY PAPERS:\n- How and Why False Denial Constraints are Discovered (PVLDB Vol. 18, 2025 (Martin, de Almeida, Romero, Queralt; HPI/UPC). Code: github.com/nosocalgroc/DCValidity) https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p3477-martin.pdf - THE key paper. >95% of discovered DCs are false; introduces SOUNDNESS via ATOMICITY, tested with a Bayesian Beta-Bernoulli log-odds conditional-independence test at alpha~0.01; splits triviality into syntactic vs semantic; shows succinctness/coverage/interestingness ranking metrics all score underfit constraints HIGH and therefore fail. Directly portable to PaTeCon-style temporal constraints in a few days and gives the project both its critique of PaTeCon and its own methodological contribution.\n- Explainable Temporal Fact Validation Through Constraints Discovery in Knowledge Graphs (ESWC 2025, LNCS 15718, pp. 227-244 (Soulard, Sais, Raad; LISN/Paris-Saclay)) https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-94575-5_13 - Nearest competitor. Discovers simple and complex temporal constraints over an ENTITY'S TIMELINE expressed as an extension of Allen's temporal algebra to time sequences; symbolic + ML validation on Wikidata. Occupies both the entity-as-subject projection and the Allen formalisation the project was planning; must be cited and differentiated on the event-level / 168-type / document-local axis.\n- Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction Using Large Language Models (Findings of EMNLP 2025, pp. 18605-18622 (Yi Fan, Michael Strube; HITS Heidelberg)) https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1010/ - Uses Allen's interval algebra (comparing event start and end times) to enforce consistency in discourse-level temporal relation extraction. Provides both the NLP-venue precedent for the Allen framing and the non-circular conflict source: predicted temporal graphs are globally inconsistent in ways independent of any mined constraint.\n- MiniAMIE: Quick and Dirty Rule Mining on Knowledge Bases (The Web Conference (WWW) 2026 (Galarraga, Guerbette, Sinouvassane, Viallard). In dig-team/AMIE via -mini) https://luisgalarraga.de/docs/MiniAMIE___WWW.pdf - Closed-path language bias + independence-based approximation of support and confidence + dropped indexes. Establishes the state of the art in cheap rule mining AND, read against Martin et al., shows why that specific approximation is the source of false constraints. Ammunition for arguing that at event-KG scale exact counting is affordable and validity, not efficiency, is the bottleneck.\n- Declarative Process Mining with MINERful, Reloaded (CEUR-WS Vol. 4032, paper 43, 2025 (MINERful: Di Ciccio & Mecella, orig. ProM/CEUR Vol-1418)) https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-4032/paper-43.pdf - Formal, long-established support / confidence / interest-factor definitions for DECLARE ordering templates over event logs, with ACTIVATION-based denominators \u2014 exactly the denominator PaTeCon fudges. Ships redundancy and inconsistency simplification. A MAVEN document is a trace; this is the cheapest second mining track to build in 45 days.\n- Temporal Graph Functional Dependencies [Extended Version] / Discovery of Temporal Graph Functional Dependencies (arXiv 2108.08719 (Alipourlangouri, Mansfield, Chiang, Zhu); CIKM 2021 (Noronha & Chiang), McMaster) https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.08719 - Topological + attribute constraints over a period of time across interval-induced snapshots; k-bounded satisfiability/implication/validation in PTIME; TGFDMiner discovers minimal, frequent TGFDs. Replaces PaTeCon's two hand-drawn structural patterns SP(a)/SP(b) with a principled graph-pattern + dependency formulation with known complexity.\n- Learning SHACL Shapes from Knowledge Graphs (SHACLearner) (Semantic Web Journal 13(4), 2022, doi:10.3233/SW-223063 (Omran, Taylor, Rodriguez Mendez, Haller et al.)) https://www.semantic-web-journal.net/system/files/swj3063.pdf - Inverse Open Path rules with minimum-cardinality constraints, scored by IOP Standard Confidence, IOP Head Coverage and Cardinality plus a novel numerical confidence; scaled to YAGO2s/DBpedia/Wikidata. Supplies ready-named head-coverage and cardinality measures for the entity-as-subject projection.\n- SHACTOR: Improving the Quality of Large-Scale Knowledge Graphs with Validating Shapes / Extraction of Validating Shapes from Very Large Knowledge Graphs (PVLDB 16 (2023) + SIGMOD 2023 demo (Rabbani, Lissandrini, Hose; AAU)) https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.14778/3579075.3579078 - supp(s)>omega for node shapes, supp(phi_s)>omega and conf(phi_s)>epsilon for property shapes, framed explicitly as isolating SPURIOUS constraints caused by erroneous data, with auto-generated SPARQL to retrieve offending facts. The closest published precedent for PaTeCon-style thresholds, and a better interpretation of what low support means.\n- UpSHACL: Targeted Constraint Validation for Updates over Knowledge Graphs (ISWC 2025, LNCS (Zacouris, Ke, Acosta; TUM). Companion: 'SHACL Validation Under Graph Updates', ISWC 2025) https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-09527-5_7 - Formal model of the subgraph affected by an update, reduced-subgraph validation on stock SHACL engines, up to 10x speedup with no missed results. Directly reusable framing for Phase 5 incremental conflict detection on the test graph.\n- Dealing with Inconsistency for Reasoning over Knowledge Graphs: A Survey (arXiv 2502.19023, 26 Feb 2025 (Nentidis et al.)) https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19023 - Anchor survey. Detection (exact hitting-set over minimal explanations, with modularisation; vs approximate ML) / fixing (distance-weighted minimal repairs, reliability queries, OntoDebug) / inconsistency-tolerant reasoning (AR & CAR repair semantics, paraconsistent many-valued, probabilistic). Names PaTeCon's trivalent logic properly and motivates outputting minimal explanations instead of binary conflict flags.\n- Conflict Detection for Temporal Knowledge Graphs: A Fast Constraint Mining Algorithm and New Benchmarks (PaTeCon+) (arXiv 2312.11053 (extension of PaTeCon, AAAI 2023, arXiv 2304.09015; Chen et al., Nanjing Univ.)) https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11053 - The baseline. Its own need to construct WD-Confl / YG-Confl benchmarks is documentary evidence that real temporal conflicts are scarce, which is the citable justification for TempEKG changing the evaluation protocol rather than repeating it.\n- Confidence is not Timeless: Modeling Temporal Validity for Rule-based Temporal Knowledge Graph Forecasting (TempValid) (ACL 2024 (long) (Huang et al.); follow-on CountTRuCoLa, arXiv 2509.09474, 2025) https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.580/ - Treats rule confidence as a learnable time function rather than a static count ratio, with time-aware and rule-adversarial negative sampling. Best available argument that static confidence is inadequate for temporal rules \u2014 but note the objective is forecasting, not consistency checking, which is why it is related work and not a baseline.\n- Towards Practical Benchmarking of Data Cleaning Techniques: On Generating Authentic Errors via Large Language Models (arXiv 2507.10934, 2025) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.10934 - Cite alongside REIN (EDBT 2023, arXiv 2302.04702) and 'Systematic Evaluation of KG Repair with LLMs' (arXiv 2507.22419) to justify replacing constraint-derived fake conflicts with an independently-derived, empirically-calibrated perturbation generator.\n- Conformance Constraint Discovery (SIGMOD 2021 (Fariha, Nath, Meliou et al.)) https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3448016.3452795 - Constraints over arithmetic projections of numerical attributes, explicitly motivated by the fact that FDs and DCs cannot express them. The right formal home for GRANULARITY conflicts, which are metric (interval width / nesting ratio), not qualitative Allen relations.\n- On the Interplay Between Validation and Inference in Shapes Constraint Language - An Investigation on the Time Ontology (Applied Ontology / SAGE, 2026 (Robaldo, Batsakis)) https://doi.org/10.1177/22104968261440710 - Shows the OWL axiomatisation of the W3C Time Ontology cannot enforce several temporal validation constraints expressible in its own vocabulary, and gives a SHACL formalisation that can. Provides both the granularity vocabulary and a citable statement that ontology reasoning alone cannot do this job.\n- Completeness-aware Rule Learning from Knowledge Graphs (IJCAI 2018 / ISWC 2017 (Tanon, Stepanova, Razniewski, Mirza, Weikum); survey: ACM CSUR 56, 2024, doi 10.1145/3639563) https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2018/0749.pdf - Documents that PCA confidence performs WORSE than standard confidence under random missingness and is unsound for n-to-n relations. Since (entity, EventType#Role, event) is strongly n-to-n, this is the citation that justifies NOT adopting PCA confidence.\n- Efficient Denial Constraint Discovery with Hydra / Discovery of Approximate (and Exact) Denial Constraints (DCFinder) / Fast Approximate DC Discovery (PVLDB 11 (2017) Bleifuss/Kruse/Naumann; PVLDB 13 (2020) & PVLDB 16 (2023) Pena/de Almeida/Naumann; PVLDB 16 p269 Xiao/Tan) https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol11/p311-bleifub.pdf - Source of the evidence-set formulation and of approximate-DC violation-rate semantics. Needed as vocabulary and related work, NOT as algorithms: TempEKG's ~275K event pairs are far below the scale these target.\n- Significance of Episodes Based on Minimal Windows / Multiple Hypothesis Testing in Pattern Discovery (Tatti (arXiv 1902.02755); Webb (arXiv 0906.5263); significance-based discriminative SPM, ESWA 2019) https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.02755 - Statistical-significance and FDR-controlled alternatives to fixed support/confidence thresholds. With ~652 candidate properties the hypothesis space is small enough that Holm-Bonferroni or Benjamini-Hochberg is free, letting the paper report constraints at a controlled FDR instead of at conf=0.9.\n- An Efficient Approach for Discovering Graph Entity Dependencies (GEDs) (arXiv 2301.06264 / Information Systems, 2024; GEDs orig. Fan & Lu, ACM TODS 44(2), 2019) https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.06264 - GED = graph pattern + attribute dependency, unifying keys and FDs; discovery as MINIMAL COVER with graph-partitioning-based scope discovery and pruning over the candidate space. Together with GDDs (ADC 2023) and GGDs (arXiv 2403.17082, which can enforce EXISTENCE of a node/edge/pattern) this covers the missing-CONTAINS-edge case that DCs cannot express.\n- EventFull: Complete and Consistent Event Relation Annotation (arXiv 2412.12733 (2024/2025); with 'Only One Relation Possible?' arXiv 2408.07353) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.12733 - Explains why MAVEN-ERE gold is near-consistent by construction (timeline annotation enforces global consistency) and reframes the real defects as incompleteness and ambiguity \u2014 both of which give non-circular evaluation targets.\nREUSABLE: ATOMICITY / SOUNDNESS TEST (Martin et al. PVLDB 2025) \u2014 3-5 days. For each mined temporal constraint with predicate set phi, test for every p_i in phi whether P(p_j | phi\\{p_i,p_j}) = P(p_j | phi\\{p_j}). Estimate each conditional as Beta(successes+1, failures+1) from counts over the 274,525 shared-participant event pairs; compute P(log-odds ratio >= 0) and require it below alpha=0.01. Reject non-atomic constraints. Reported effect in the source domain: false constraints down by >95% with essentially no loss of true ones. This is the single highest-value 45-day addition and it plugs into PaTeCon's existing counting pass without touching the graph representation.; FDR-CONTROLLED THRESHOLDS \u2014 1-2 days. Replace support=10 / conf=0.9 with a per-constraint exact binomial or Fisher test against a null built from marginal predicate frequencies, then Benjamini-Hochberg (or Holm-Bonferroni for strict FWER) across the ~10^5-10^6 candidate space. Report 'constraints at FDR <= 0.05' instead of hand-tuned thresholds. Composes with atomicity: atomicity removes dependence-free constraints, FDR removes small-sample flukes.; ALLEN'S 13 RELATIONS AS THE CONSTRAINT HEAD VOCABULARY \u2014 2-3 days. Replace {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} with Allen relations plus their composition table. Free wins: composition-based closure gives constraint propagation; the ORD-Horn tractable subalgebra (Krokhin/Jeavons/Jonsson, JACM 2003) gives polynomial-time consistency checking; path consistency gives a standard conflict detector. Precedent at an NLP venue via Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025) and at a Semantic Web venue via Soulard et al. (ESWC 2025) and NeuSTIP.; DECLARE TEMPLATE MINING OVER DOCUMENT TRACES \u2014 4-6 days including MINERful integration. Treat each of the 4,480 documents as a trace, event types as the activity alphabet, and BEFORE-order as the trace order (safe: the gold BEFORE relation has 0 cycles and is 100% transitively closed). Mine response / precedence / succession / alternate and chain variants / not-co-existence with MINERful's activation-based support, confidence and interest factor, then apply MINERful's redundancy and inconsistency simplification. Yields an ordering-constraint family with 20-year-old citable measure definitions and a second, independent mining track to compare against the PaTeCon track.; MINIMAL-EXPLANATION (HITTING SET) OUTPUT \u2014 2-3 days. Instead of flagging conflicting pairs, return the minimal set of statements whose removal restores consistency, per the exact-detection branch of the 2025 inconsistency survey. Tractable here because entity neighbourhoods are tiny (max 59 events per entity, no cross-document entities). Turns 'conflict detected' into 'explainable conflict', matching the framing of the ESWC 2025 competitor.; INCREMENTAL VALIDATION VIA AFFECTED SUBGRAPH (UpSHACL, ISWC 2025) \u2014 2-3 days. For Phase 5, formalise the subgraph affected by adding/perturbing an event's time anchor and validate only that reduced subgraph. Up to 10x speedup with no missed results in the source paper; makes the detection stage a citable algorithm rather than a re-scan.; GRANULARITY CONFLICTS AS METRIC/CONFORMANCE CONSTRAINTS \u2014 3-4 days. Model each granularity relation as (a) an Allen 'during' constraint on the nested anchors plus (b) a conformance constraint (SIGMOD 2021) on an arithmetic projection such as log width ratio of the coarse to fine interval, learned from the observed distribution over the 17,102 multi-anchor events. Explicitly note that neither DCs nor plain SHACL-core can express cross-node interval-endpoint comparisons, which justifies the custom formalism.; SPURIOUSNESS FRAMING FOR LOW-SUPPORT CONSTRAINTS (SHACTOR) \u2014 1 day of writing. Adopt SHACTOR's stance that constraints below the support/confidence thresholds are evidence of ERRONEOUS DATA to be surfaced, and emit a query/pointer per spurious constraint identifying the offending events. Converts a discarded set into a reported artefact.; INDEPENDENTLY-CALIBRATED PERTURBATION GENERATOR \u2014 3-4 days. Derive conflict injection from the measured TIMEX normalisation-difficulty distribution (62.1% regex-deterministic vs 37.9% context-dependent across the named families: relative dates, year-less dates, named periods, weekdays) and from the observed nested-anchor granularity distribution, NOT from the mined constraints. Report per-family recall. Cite arXiv 2507.10934 / REIN for why constraint-derived synthetic errors invalidate a benchmark.; PREDICTED-GRAPH EVALUATION SET \u2014 4-6 days, and it un-orphans Phase 1. Run a temporal-relation extractor (or an LLM prompted in the Fan & Strube setting) over held-out documents, then apply the mined constraints to its predicted relation graph. Inconsistencies there arise from a process wholly independent of the constraints. Report both conflict-detection precision and whether flagged relations correlate with extractor errors against gold.; MISSING-EDGE (EXISTENCE) CONSTRAINTS VIA GGD-STYLE HEADS \u2014 2-3 days. The 1,638 subevent pairs lacking an implied CONTAINS and the 7 missing BEFORE transitive chains are incompleteness, not contradiction; DCs and GEDs cannot express 'an edge must exist' but GGDs can. Split the reported taxonomy into contradiction vs incompleteness violations and evaluate each separately.\nNOVELTY GAPS: No published work applies DC/rule-mining SOUNDNESS criteria (atomicity, conditional-independence testing) to TEMPORAL constraints, in any graph. Martin et al. (PVLDB 2025) do it for relational DCs; PaTeCon, PaTeCon+ and Soulard et al. (ESWC 2025) all still use bare support/confidence. 'The first soundness-tested temporal constraint miner' is an open, cheap, and defensible claim.; No work mines temporal constraints at the EVENT level with a rich event-type/role vocabulary. PaTeCon and Soulard et al. both operate on entity-attribute facts in Wikidata/YAGO. A 168-event-type x 143-role x 652-property event KG is genuinely new territory, and event types give a semantic class hierarchy that Wikidata properties do not.; GRANULARITY conflicts are essentially unstudied as a constraint-mining problem. PaTeCon+ mentions manual constraints 'may have granularity issues' but does not mine granularity constraints; the multi-granularity TKG literature (Learning Granularity Representation, arXiv 2408.15293; quadruplet networks, Sci Rep 2025; MULTIQ) treats granularity as a representation-learning input, never as a consistency property to be constrained. Nobody has defined a granularity constraint with a violation semantics.; DOCUMENT-LOCAL constraint mining is unexplored. Every existing system assumes globally-linked entities. A corpus where 0 of 68,348 entities appear in more than one document forces per-document constraint checking with cross-document generalisation only through TYPES, which changes the support semantics (support must be counted over documents, not over entity instances \u2014 this is precisely the DECLARE trace-vs-activation denominator distinction, and no KG constraint miner has confronted it).; No bridge exists between declarative process mining (DECLARE/MINERful/LTLf) and temporal KG constraint mining, despite both discovering ordering constraints over timestamped events with support/confidence measures. The two literatures do not cite each other. Building that bridge with a head-to-head comparison on the same event graph is a clean, self-contained contribution, and it is achievable in 45 days because both toolchains already exist.; Constraint-mining evaluation methodology is a live open problem specifically because gold graphs are conflict-free by annotation construction. The data-cleaning community has moved to authentic-error generation (arXiv 2507.10934) and the KG community has not. A protocol that evaluates on (a) extractor-predicted graphs, (b) independently-calibrated perturbations, and (c) missing-edge prediction, with per-family breakdown, would be a methodological contribution reusable beyond this paper \u2014 and it is the direct answer to the diagnosed circularity.; The interaction between MINIMALITY and APPROXIMATION is a known but unfixed defect: Martin et al. observe that approximation 'introduces underfitting, causing algorithms to identify subsets of true rules as valid DCs, which then leads to the rejection of these true DCs due to minimality'. PaTeCon inherits this and nobody has characterised it for temporal constraints. Even a measured demonstration of the effect on an event KG is publishable.; No temporal constraint miner distinguishes CONTRADICTION from INCOMPLETENESS in its output taxonomy, though the distinction is sharp in the graph-dependency literature (DC/GED cannot express existence; GGD can). With measured incompleteness (13.6% of subevent pairs) dwarfing measured contradiction (~72 corpus-wide), this distinction is the empirical backbone of an honest paper.; Rule-confidence work over temporal KGs (TLogic, TILP, TempValid, CountTRuCoLa) is entirely forecasting-oriented; nobody has asked what a rule's confidence should MEAN for consistency checking as opposed to ranking. TempValid's insight that confidence decays with time has no counterpart in the constraint literature \u2014 a time-dependent violation bound for temporal constraints is unclaimed conceptual ground, though probably too ambitious for 45 days and better flagged as future work.\n\n### Sweep representation\nFINDINGS:\n- The reification-vs-RDF-star-vs-qualifiers-vs-n-ary question has been settled empirically, and the answer is consumer-dependent: Iglesias-Molina, Ahrabian, Ilievski, Pujara & Corcho (ISWC 2023) compared Standard Reification, N-Ary Relationships, Wikidata Qualifiers and RDF-star across three consumer scenarios (human knowledge exploration via a user study; systematic SPARQL querying measured in time and query complexity; graph completion via embedding models). Qualifiers and RDF-star win for exploration and querying; STANDARD REIFICATION performed most consistently for embedding/inference consumers while being cumbersome for humans. [WHY: This is the single most useful citation for defending your architecture: it licenses a TWO-SERIALISATION design instead of one compromise. Keep PaTeCon's reified statement-node graph (eVertex/sVertex) as the INTERNAL mining representation \u2014 the paper says reification is the right choice for exactly this class of consumer \u2014 and release the public artifact as qualifier-style or RDF 1.2 reified triples for human/SPARQL consumption. You no longer have to argue reification is 'better'; you cite that it is better FOR MINING, which pre-empts the obvious reviewer objection that reification is dated.]\n- 'RDF-star' as a citable object no longer exists in the form most 2020-2023 papers describe. RDF 1.2 replaced quoted/embedded triples with TRIPLE TERMS plus an explicit reification vocabulary: a reifying triple has predicate rdf:reifies and a triple term as object; its subject is called the REIFIER; the set of triples sharing one reifier as subject is a TRIPLE ANNOTATION. RDF 1.2 Concepts and RDF 1.2 Semantics reached Candidate Recommendation (charter target Q2 2025), with Working Drafts still being issued in July 2026. [WHY: A COLING 2027 paper that writes 'we use RDF-star quoted triples' will read as two years stale to any semantic-web-adjacent reviewer. Use the RDF 1.2 vocabulary (triple term / rdf:reifies / reifier / triple annotation) if you touch RDF at all, and note that the reifier IS a statement node \u2014 i.e. RDF 1.2's own official design has converged on precisely PaTeCon's sVertex. That is a free, strong argument that your reified statement-node model is the standards-endorsed one, not a legacy workaround.]\n- Keeping n-ary event structure intact is empirically worth a large amount, not just aesthetically preferable: StarE (Galkin et al., EMNLP 2020) reports that leveraging qualifiers yields gains up to 25 MRR points over triple-only (binarised) representations of the same facts. Follow-up work (Egami, Matsushita, Ugai & Fukuda, 2025/2026) shows the choice among Reification (REF), Singleton Property (SGP) and RDF-star (RDR) itself materially changes KG-embedding and link-prediction results, so metadata representation model is a confound that must be reported. [WHY: Justifies refusing to flatten MAVEN-Arg's 143 roles into binary (event, role, arg) triples as the PRIMARY store, and gives you a number to quote for it. It also warns you: if you ever report any learned-model number on the projected graph, the projection choice (entity-as-subject vs event-as-subject) is a confound you must state, because Egami et al. show representation alone moves LP metrics.]\n- Hyper-relational TEMPORAL KGs are an established named data structure with released benchmarks: HTKG (Ding et al., Findings of EMNLP 2024), with datasets Wiki-hy and YAGO-hy, where every fact is a primary triple + qualifier key-value pairs + an explicit timestamp indicating time validity of the WHOLE fact, and where the paper explicitly separates time-invariant (TI) qualifiers from time-dependent ones. VITA (2025) extends this with versatile time representation for temporal hyper-relational KGs. [WHY: This gives you a citable, off-the-shelf name and format for what you are building \u2014 'a document-grounded HTKG' \u2014 instead of inventing terminology. The TI/time-dependent qualifier split is directly applicable: an event's EventType is time-invariant, its participants' role-fillers are time-invariant within the event, but the anchoring TIMEX is time-dependent. Also note the contrast that is your novelty: Wiki-hy/YAGO-hy are distilled from Wikidata/YAGO, so no HTKG benchmark is grounded in raw text with gold argument roles. Yours would be the first.]\n- Time sits in three different places depending on which literature you are in, and each has a canonical citation. (1) Time on the EDGE: TKG quadruples (s,r,o,t) or interval-labelled edges [t_start,t_end], possibly left-/right-open \u2014 the default of all TKGC/TKG-reasoning work. (2) Time on the EVENT NODE: EventKG builds on the Simple Event Model (SEM) and puts sem:hasBeginTimeStamp / sem:hasEndTimeStamp on the event resource itself, and additionally reifies event-entity relations so counts and provenance can hang off them. (3) Time on the STATEMENT: Wikidata qualifiers P580/P582 on a statement node; HTKG's per-fact timestamp; PaTeCon's sVertex start/end. [WHY: The defensible choice for you is (2) as the source of truth and (3) as a DERIVED view, and you can say so with precedent. Rationale you can state as a design principle: because every argument statement of one event shares that event's single interval (the degeneracy you already diagnosed), time is a property OF THE EVENT NODE, not of any participation edge; the Phase-3 projection then materialises node time onto statements so PaTeCon can consume it. Do NOT choose (1): edge-labelled quadruples would force you to replicate one interval across all of an event's up-to-many argument edges, which is both redundant and the direct cause of the degenerate-predicate problem.]\n- Granularity must be stored as an explicit precision code on the time value, not inferred from an interval. Wikibase's 'time' datatype carries an integer precision field alongside the value (9 = year, 10 = month, 11 = day, up to 14 = second), which is why Wikidata can hold '1778' and '11 November 1778' as distinct-precision assertions about the same thing rather than as an interval containment. OWL-Time separately provides the interval/instant, duration and 13 Allen-style topological relations vocabulary that the ECTKG survey uses as its temporal backbone. [WHY: This is the make-or-break representation decision for your GRANULARITY CONFLICT type. If you normalise 'in 1778' to the interval [1778-01-01, 1778-12-31] and stop there, granularity conflict becomes indistinguishable from ordinary interval containment and your second conflict type collapses into the first. Store (value, precision) pairs Wikidata-style and a granularity conflict becomes definable with zero extra machinery: two same-event anchors whose precision codes differ and whose precision-expanded intervals are NOT nested. Your 17,102 events with >=2 CONTAINS anchors are then a naturally-occurring, non-circular test set for granularity conflict \u2014 which also gives you a partial escape from the fake-conflict circularity problem, because those cases are found in the data rather than injected by you.]\n- Bitemporal graph models are now mature and 2024-2025-citable: AeonG (PVLDB 17(6):1515-1527, 2024) gives built-in temporal support with a dedicated storage+query engine; 'Bitemporal Property Graphs: Dealing with Both Valid and Transaction Time' (ADBIS 2025) extends AeonG to carry BOTH valid time and transaction time, noting that bitemporality is consolidated in the relational world but implemented by very few graph databases; Gradoop's TPGM (VLDB Journal 2021) is bitemporal over vertices, edges AND properties with composable snapshot/difference/grouping operators; BiTRDF (Mathematics 13(13):2109, 2025) adds both axes to RDF. [WHY: You do not need transaction time for world-time facts, but you DO need a second axis, and bitemporality is the citable frame for it. Repurpose the transaction/system axis as a DERIVATION axis: every statement carries (valid interval from the TIMEX, derivation layer = gold | rule-normalised | model-predicted, confidence). This single change fixes your orphaned-Phase-1 problem without new work: the same graph holds a gold layer and a Phase-1-extractor layer, and 'do mined constraints transfer from gold to predicted graphs?' becomes a real experiment and arguably a better contribution than the fake-conflict evaluation. Cite ADBIS 2025 for the model, not for an implementation \u2014 do not adopt AeonG as your engine.]\n- Kuzu is dead as an infrastructure choice. The GitHub repository was archived on 10 October 2025 and the company website taken down following Apple's acquisition of the Ontario-based company (disclosed to the EU, publicly reported February 2026); maintenance is left to community forks. Meanwhile DuckDB's graph path is the DuckPGQ community extension (CWI Database Architectures group, VLDB 2023), implementing SQL:2023 SQL/PGQ with bounded/unbounded path search, reachability, shortest and cheapest path \u2014 explicitly documented as still under active development with incomplete features. [WHY: If your plan named Kuzu, drop it now \u2014 an artifact whose engine was archived a year before submission is a reproducibility liability, and a reviewer can check the archive banner in ten seconds. With 45 days and ~2-3M edges, the defensible engine story is: Parquet as the canonical release format (columnar, projection-pushdown, zero server, standard for dataset releases) + DuckDB for the relational/aggregation work + DuckPGQ only for the path queries you actually need (BEFORE-closure reachability). Report it as an engineering choice justified by scale, and state the scale \u2014 that is a stronger paper sentence than naming a graph database.]\n- Concrete numbers exist for RDF-store choice at your exact scale. A May 2026 benchmark of 11 RDF frameworks at ~100K / 1M / 10M triples reports, at 10M triples: Turtle load of 7.6s (QLever), 9.4s (maplib), 19-24s (Jena, RDF4J) versus ~240s (rdflib); and aggregation-query latency of 2ms (QLever), 17.9ms (maplib) versus ~43s (rdflib). It also observes that Neo4j + neosemantics is workable for one-way migration but involves significant semantic loss in RDF-to-property-graph conversion. [WHY: Your reified graph will land in the low millions of triples \u2014 the exact band where rdflib is a 43-second-per-query trap and where a Rust/C++ embedded store (Oxigraph, QLever, maplib) is 3-4 orders of magnitude faster with no server. If you want an RDF release alongside Parquet, use Oxigraph/pyoxigraph (embedded, RocksDB-backed, quad store so named graphs come free for your derivation layers). Blog-benchmark provenance is weak for citation, so cite the ACM Computing Surveys RDF-stores survey for the claim and use the blog only to pick the tool.]\n- The closest existing work to your ORDERING CONFLICT target is already published on your exact dataset: Fan & Strube, 'Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction Using Large Language Models', Findings of EMNLP 2025, pp. 18605-18622, which compares events' start and end times per Allen's interval algebra, checks predictions for consistency, and recycles consistency-filtered predictions as new training data, evaluated with MAVEN-ERE. Related 2026 work (IA-RAG) represents events as explicit intervals and retrieves under Allen's Interval Algebra, and EventFull (2024) targets complete and consistent event-relation annotation. [WHY: This is a live novelty threat you must position against explicitly, and also a gift: Fan & Strube do consistency checking as a decoding/training constraint over PREDICTED relations, whereas you mine statistical constraints over event-type/role signatures. State the difference in one sentence (local logical closure over pairs vs. learned type-level constraints) or a reviewer will state it for you. The gift: their start/end-point decomposition is exactly the reduction that lets your {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} head predicates be checked by point-algebra path consistency, and Allen's tractable subalgebras (Jonsson & Krokhin, JACM 50(5)) give you a polynomial-time consistency checker you can cite rather than build.]\n- Two 2025-2026 papers give you formal vocabulary for the provenance/uncertainty layer. 'Provenance-Enhanced Statements in Knowledge Graphs' (arXiv 2606.15246, June 2026) argues current models record who asserted what but treat provenance as semantically neutral, and proposes DEC, which groups provenance-homogeneous statement sets into 'cognitive worlds' with a distinguished factual core plus locality/rationality/permeation/intensionality principles \u2014 and is explicitly agnostic to whether the underlying encoding is named graphs, RDF-star or classical reification. Separately, 'The Equivalence Theorem' (arXiv 2603.13603, March 2026) argues n-ary relations with attributes, temporal validity, uncertainty quantification and causal relations between relations are mutually entailing capabilities, realised in an Attributed Temporal Causal Hypergraph, with a claimed hierarchy SQL < LPG < TypeDB < ATCH. [WHY: DEC's 'agnostic to named graphs / RDF-star / reification' point is your license to pick reification purely on engineering grounds without a semantics argument \u2014 quote it. Its cognitive-worlds idea is a clean formalism for gold-layer vs predicted-layer coexistence with an inconsistent layer that does not destabilise the factual core, which is precisely what you need when injecting perturbed times. The Equivalence Theorem is a single-author unrefereed preprint \u2014 do NOT cite it as a result \u2014 but its four-capability list (n-ary + attributes, temporal validity, uncertainty, relations-between-relations) is a one-sentence justification of why your model needs statement nodes, since MAVEN-ERE's CAUSE/SUBEVENT are literally relations between relations.]\n- The event-centric KG survey landscape has a named gap that your dataset fills. 'Event-Centric Temporal Knowledge Graph Construction: A Survey' (Mathematics 2023, 11(23), 4852) decomposes the problem into (a) event extraction, (b) extraction of temporal relations and attributes, (c) creation of event-based KGs and timelines, builds its temporal layer on OWL-Time's four components (time of event, duration, interval, temporal relation), and concludes that despite many datasets there is a NOTICEABLE GAP in annotated data that could support comprehensive END-TO-END models. The event-KG taxonomy that separates event-centric KGs from event-schema KGs and event-logic/eventuality graphs (ASER, ATOMIC, GLUCOSE, ELG) is from 'What is Event Knowledge Graph: A Survey' (TKDE 2022). [WHY: Two things. First, the survey's stated gap is your motivation paragraph, written for you by someone else: MAVEN-Arg + MAVEN-ERE on identical documents is exactly the end-to-end-capable annotation the survey says is missing. Second, the TKDE taxonomy tells you which box to claim: you are building an event-centric KG (instance-level, document-grounded), NOT an event logic graph / eventuality KG (abstract generalised verb-phrase nodes, ASER/ELG style). Say this explicitly in Section 2 \u2014 it cheaply forecloses 'why not ASER/ATOMIC' from a reviewer, since those have abstract nodes and no anchorable calendar time.]\n- 2025 event-centric KG construction work builds graphs by traversing EXISTING KGs, not from raw text. ChronoGrapher (Blin, Tiddi, van Trijp, ten Teije; Semantic Web Journal / SAGE 2025, doi 10.1177/22104968251377247) retrieves event subgraphs from DBpedia/Wikidata via pruned semantically-informed best-first search then enriches with structured and text-based triples; EventRAG (ACL 2025 long, pp. in 2025.acl-long.830) shows event-KG-derived triples in prompts yield more source-grounded answers than generic triples; ECS-KG (Data & Knowledge Engineering, 2025) builds an event-centric semantic KG from news using contextual embeddings plus a temporal GNN and GAT. [WHY: These are your 2025 related-work anchors and none of them competes with you: all three start from an existing KG or unlabelled news, so none has gold n-ary argument roles or a million gold temporal relations. That contrast is the cleanest available statement of your contribution. EventRAG additionally gives you a downstream utility argument if a reviewer asks 'what is the ECKG for' \u2014 event-KG triples beat generic triples for grounded generation.]\n- Standard reification's cost is a known, quotable constant, and the classic empirical study of the alternatives on Wikidata predates all of them: Hernandez, Hogan & Krotzsch, 'Reifying RDF: What Works Well With Wikidata?' (SSWS 2015) evaluates standard reification, n-ary relations, singleton properties and named graphs, and standard reification multiplies triple count by roughly 4x (one statement node plus subject/predicate/object triples per original triple). [WHY: Lets you state your storage budget as a derived number rather than a guess, and pre-empts 'reification will blow up'. At your scale the 4x factor is irrelevant \u2014 that is the point worth making in the paper: representation fidelity is free here, so choose the most expressive model, and say explicitly that the scale argument that normally motivates RDF-star does not apply.]\n- Temporal-expression normalisation has 2024-2026 alternatives to hand-written rules, including one aimed exactly at event-anchored expressions. Automatic rule generation for time-expression normalisation learns normalisation rules rather than hand-authoring them (arXiv 2108.13658); MLM-based multilingual normalisation combines fine-tuned masked LMs for detection with grammar-based normalisers and reports best results on GOLD-TIMEX normalisation (arXiv 2205.10399); generative-LLM normalisation across five open models is evaluated in ECAI 2024 (doi 10.3233/FAIA240940); EventTempEx (FIRE 2025, doi 10.1145/3777867.3777876) is a tool specifically for interpreting event-anchored temporal expressions. A 2025 review of transformer-based temporal information extraction (arXiv 2504.07470) covers the timex/event/relation task family. [WHY: Your normalisation component has zero gold supervision, which normally means it is unevaluable and therefore attackable. The gold-TIMEX-normalisation setting in arXiv 2205.10399 is the right framing (spans given, only value predicted \u2014 identical to your situation since TIMEX spans are provided in every split), and EventTempEx is direct prior art for the relative-date family. Cite these and describe your rules+reference-time-propagation+gazetteer pipeline as a reimplementation of an established recipe rather than an ad-hoc hack; that converts a weakness into a documented design with a baseline to compare against.]\n- Cross-document event and entity coreference is an active 2025-2026 subfield (xCoRe, EMNLP 2025 main; discourse-coherence-enhanced CD-ECR, ACL 2025 long; argument-centric causal intervention, Scientific Reports 2025/2026), evaluated on ECB+, GVC, FCC and WEC-Eng \u2014 none of which is MAVEN. [WHY: Gives you a principled scope exclusion. Because MAVEN entities are strictly document-local, your ECKG is a UNION OF DOCUMENT-LOCAL GRAPHS, and constraint mining therefore operates over event-TYPE/role signatures rather than over recurring individuals \u2014 the opposite of PaTeCon, which mines over Wikidata entities that recur globally. That difference is not a limitation to hide, it is the most interesting thing about your setting and should be Section 3's framing; cite this literature to show cross-doc linking was considered and deliberately deferred.]\n- Property-graph representation gained formal standing that RDF-only reviewers may not know: GQL was published as an ISO/IEC standard on 12 April 2024, the first new ISO database query language certified since SQL in 1987, defining data structures and basic operations over property graphs; SQL/PGQ (SQL:2023) is its relational sibling from the same ISO committee, and their theoretical models and relative expressive power have been formally analysed (arXiv 2409.01102). [WHY: If you release the graph as a property graph / SQL-PGQ-queryable relational schema instead of RDF, this is the sentence that makes it defensible rather than lazy. It also removes any need to justify DuckPGQ as exotic: it implements a 2023 ISO standard.]\nKEY PAPERS:\n- Comparison of Knowledge Graph Representations for Consumer Scenarios (ISWC 2023 (Iglesias-Molina, Ahrabian, Ilievski, Pujara, Corcho)) https://iswc2023.semanticweb.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/142650263.pdf - THE citation for your representation choice. Empirically compares Standard Reification, N-Ary Relationships, Wikidata Qualifiers and RDF-star across knowledge exploration (user study), systematic SPARQL querying, and graph completion (embeddings). Conclusion \u2014 reification is most consistent for embedding/inference consumers, qualifiers/RDF-star for querying \u2014 licenses your reified internal store + qualifier-style release.\n- Consistent Discourse-level Temporal Relation Extraction Using Large Language Models (Findings of EMNLP 2025, pp. 18605-18622 (Fan & Strube)) https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1010/ - Closest published competitor to your ORDERING CONFLICT contribution and it uses MAVEN-ERE. Decomposes temporal relations into event start/end comparisons per Allen's algebra, checks prediction consistency, recycles consistent predictions as training data. You must cite and differentiate (pairwise logical closure vs type-level statistical constraints).\n- Temporal Fact Reasoning over Hyper-Relational Knowledge Graphs (Findings of EMNLP 2024 (Ding et al.); arXiv 2307.10219) https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-emnlp.20/ - Names and benchmarks the data structure you are building: primary triple + qualifiers + explicit fact-level timestamp, with time-invariant vs time-dependent qualifiers separated. Datasets Wiki-hy and YAGO-hy. Establishes that all existing HTKG benchmarks are KG-distilled, so a text-grounded one is a gap.\n- Message Passing for Hyper-Relational Knowledge Graphs (StarE) (EMNLP 2020 (Galkin, Trivedi, Maheshwari, Usbeck, Lehmann)) https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.596/ - Quantified argument against binarising n-ary events: qualifiers yield up to +25 MRR over triple-only representations of the same facts. Also the origin of the hyper-relational (primary triple + qualifier set) formalism your event-argument structure instantiates.\n- EventKG: A Multilingual Event-Centric Temporal Knowledge Graph / EventKG - the Hub of Event Knowledge on the Web (ESWC 2018 and Semantic Web Journal 2019 (Gottschalk & Demidova)) https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.04526 - Canonical event-as-node schema with time on the event resource (SEM: sem:hasBeginTimeStamp / hasEndTimeStamp), reified event-entity relations carrying counts, and named-graph provenance per source. The direct precedent for 'time lives on the event node, not the participation edge'.\n- Event-Centric Temporal Knowledge Graph Construction: A Survey (Mathematics 2023, 11(23), 4852) https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/11/23/4852 - Your motivation paragraph: decomposes ECTKG construction into event extraction / temporal relation & attribute extraction / KG-and-timeline creation, grounds the temporal layer in OWL-Time's four components, and explicitly identifies the lack of annotated data supporting end-to-end models \u2014 the gap MAVEN-Arg+MAVEN-ERE closes.\n- What is Event Knowledge Graph: A Survey (IEEE TKDE 2022 (Guan et al.); arXiv 2112.15280) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2112.15280 - Taxonomy paper that lets you place your artifact precisely as an event-centric KG rather than an event-schema KG or an event logic / eventuality graph (ELG, ASER, ATOMIC, GLUCOSE), foreclosing 'why not ASER' objections.\n- ChronoGrapher: Event-Centric Knowledge Graph Construction via Informed Graph Traversal (Semantic Web Journal / SAGE 2025 (Blin, Tiddi, van Trijp, ten Teije)) https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/22104968251377247 - Most recent event-centric KG construction paper (2025). Traverses existing KGs (DBpedia/Wikidata) with pruned best-first search plus structured and text-based enrichment \u2014 i.e. the opposite starting point from yours, which makes it the ideal contrast for related work.\n- Bitemporal Property Graphs: Dealing with Both Valid and Transaction Time (ADBIS 2025) https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-05281-0_15 - The bitemporal model to cite when you add a derivation/provenance axis (gold vs rule-normalised vs model-predicted) alongside valid time. Extends AeonG with both time axes; notes bitemporality is rare in graph DBs. Pair with AeonG (PVLDB 17(6):1515-1527, 2024) and Gradoop TPGM (VLDB Journal 2021).\n- Provenance-Enhanced Statements in Knowledge Graphs (DEC framework) (arXiv 2606.15246, June 2026) https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15246 - Formal semantics for layering gold, rule-derived and model-predicted statements in one graph with an inconsistent layer that does not destabilise the factual core; explicitly agnostic to named graphs vs RDF-star vs classical reification, which is your license to choose reification on engineering grounds.\n- RDF 1.2 Concepts and Abstract Data Model (W3C Candidate Recommendation, 2025-2026) https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-concepts/ - Current W3C reality for RDF reification: triple terms, rdf:reifies, reifiers, triple annotations. Use this vocabulary instead of 'RDF-star quoted triples', and note that the standard's own reifier IS a statement node, matching PaTeCon's sVertex.\n- Reifying RDF: What Works Well With Wikidata? (SSWS @ ISWC 2015 (Hernandez, Hogan, Krotzsch)) https://aidanhogan.com/docs/reification-wikidata-rdf-sparql.pdf - Classic empirical comparison of standard reification vs n-ary relations vs singleton properties vs named graphs on Wikidata; source of the ~4x triple-count factor for standard reification.\n- Comparison of Metadata Representation Models for Knowledge Graph Embeddings (arXiv 2503.21804 (2025); Springer chapter 10.1007/978-3-032-13109-6_4 (2026) (Egami, Matsushita, Ugai, Fukuda)) https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.21804 - Shows the metadata representation model (Reification / Singleton Property / RDF-star) itself changes KG-embedding and link-prediction results in hyper-relational KGs \u2014 a confound to report if you run any learned model on the projected graph.\n- DuckPGQ: Bringing SQL/PGQ to DuckDB (PVLDB 2023; extension status pages 2025-2026) https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.14778/3611540.3611614 - Engine justification: SQL/PGQ (SQL:2023) in DuckDB with bounded/unbounded paths, reachability, shortest/cheapest path \u2014 embedded, no server, standards-based. Read alongside the DuckDB Oct 2025 graph-queries post and the official Graph Queries guide; note the extension is documented as incomplete.\n- A Survey of RDF Stores & SPARQL Engines for Querying Knowledge Graphs (VLDB Journal / arXiv 2102.13027) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2102.13027 - Standard survey to cite for RDF-store/SPARQL-engine selection rather than a blog benchmark, when justifying an embedded store (Oxigraph/QLever) over rdflib at low-millions triple scale.\n- Reasoning about temporal relations: The tractable subalgebras of Allen's interval algebra (Journal of the ACM 50(5), 2003 (Jonsson & Krokhin)) https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/876638.876639 - Gives polynomial-time consistency checking for your {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} predicate set once relations are reduced to start/end point comparisons \u2014 cite instead of building an ad-hoc checker. OWL-Time supplies the 13-relation vocabulary itself.\n- Multilingual Normalization of Temporal Expressions with Masked Language Models (arXiv 2205.10399) https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.10399 - Right evaluation framing for your unsupervised TIMEX normaliser: gold-span, value-only normalisation (exactly your setting, since TIMEX spans are provided in every split), MLM detection + grammar-based normaliser. Pair with automatic rule generation (arXiv 2108.13658) and EventTempEx (FIRE 2025) for event-anchored relative dates.\n- EventRAG: Enhancing LLM Generation with Event Knowledge Graphs (ACL 2025 (long paper 830)) https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.830.pdf - Downstream-utility argument for an event KG if a reviewer asks what the artifact is for: event-KG-derived triples in prompts produce more source-grounded answers than generic KG triples.\n- The Equivalence Theorem: First-Class Relationships for Structurally Complete Database Systems (arXiv 2603.13603, March 2026 (Alford)) https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.13603 - Position preprint whose four mutually-entailing capabilities (n-ary relations with attributes, temporal validity, uncertainty, causal relations between relations) exactly describe your requirements \u2014 MAVEN-ERE CAUSE/SUBEVENT are relations between relations. Single-author, unrefereed: cite as motivation only, never as a result.\nREUSABLE: Two-serialisation architecture, justified by ISWC 2023: reified statement-node graph internally (mining/inference consumer, where reification measured best) + qualifier-style / RDF 1.2 reified release (exploration and SPARQL consumer). Removes the need to argue any single representation is universally superior.\nNOVELTY GAPS: No hyper-relational temporal KG benchmark is grounded in raw text with gold n-ary argument roles: Wiki-hy and YAGO-hy (Findings EMNLP 2024) are distilled from Wikidata/YAGO, ChronoGrapher (2025) traverses DBpedia/Wikidata, EventKG integrates existing KGs, ECS-KG (DKE 2025) uses unlabelled news. A document-grounded HTKG from MAVEN-Arg + MAVEN-ERE would be the first, and the ECTKG survey (Mathematics 2023) names exactly this missing resource.; Granularity as a first-class, precision-coded property of event time is essentially unclaimed. TKGC work on 'temporal granularity' (e.g. LTGQ, Scientific Reports 2025; LGRe) means multi-scale time ENCODERS for embeddings, not detection of inconsistent temporal PRECISION as a data-quality defect. Wikidata's precision field exists but is not treated as a conflict surface. Your 17,102 multi-anchor events are a naturally-occurring, non-injected test set \u2014 this is the strongest available escape from the fake-conflict circularity problem.; Constraint mining over document-local graphs is unexplored. PaTeCon and all its successors mine over globally-recurring Wikidata/Freebase entities; with entities strictly document-local, constraints must be typed at the (EventType, Role) level rather than the individual level. Nobody has characterised whether temporal constraints are learnable at type level only, and that question is a paper-sized contribution independent of the conflict-detection application.; Transfer of mined temporal constraints from a gold-annotated layer to a model-extracted layer of the SAME graph has no prior work, and a bitemporal/derivation-axis model (ADBIS 2025) makes it cheap to run. This is a non-circular evaluation that simultaneously reconnects your orphaned Phase 1, and is a better headline experiment than perturbation-injected conflicts.; Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025) already do Allen-algebra consistency over MAVEN-ERE temporal predictions, so 'temporal consistency on MAVEN-ERE' is no longer novel by itself. The unclaimed space is type-level statistical constraints over event-type/role signatures versus their instance-level logical closure \u2014 you must state this contrast explicitly, and ideally report both so the comparison is yours rather than a reviewer's objection.; No event-centric KG release provides a per-statement (valid interval, precision code, derivation layer, confidence) tuple. EventKG carries provenance and counts but not precision or extraction confidence; HTKG carries timestamps but not provenance. A release combining all four would be a distinguishing artifact contribution even if the mining results are modest.; Storage/engine choices in event-KG papers are almost never justified against scale, and the ground has shifted (Kuzu archived Oct 2025; DuckPGQ implementing ISO SQL/PGQ; GQL an ISO standard since Apr 2024). A short, honest engine-justification paragraph with your measured scale is cheap novelty-adjacent credibility that most comparable papers lack.", "DESIGNS_FULL": {"D-provenance-multiversion": "DESIGN: PROV-ECKG: a three-layer, provenance-first multi-version Event-Centric Knowledge Graph over MAVEN-Arg + MAVEN-ERE\nOne physical store holding the SAME 4,480 documents at three measured extraction-noise levels (L0 gold, L1 gold-trigger+predicted-argument, L2 fully-predicted) with a (valid interval, precision code, derivation layer, confidence) tuple on every statement, so that conflict detection is a differential, confidence-weighted query across layers instead of an injected benchmark \u2014 and the noise dial is literally `WHERE layer_id = :L`.\n\nCORE IDEA: Repurpose the transaction-time axis of a bitemporal property graph (Bitemporal Property Graphs, ADBIS 2025; Gradoop TPGM, VLDBJ 2021; AeonG, PVLDB 17(6) 2024 \u2014 cited as models, NOT adopted as engines) as a DERIVATION axis. Valid time stays what it always was (the TIMEX-derived interval of the event in the world); the second axis records HOW the row came to exist: gold annotation, rule-normalised time, or model prediction, each with a calibrated confidence. Because MAVEN-Arg and MAVEN-ERE annotate the SAME 4,480 documents (100% doc overlap, 79,740 shared event ids), and because Phase 1's extractors are already trained, the same document can be materialised three times inside one store and joined row-for-row on a canonical event key.\n\nThat single change buys four things Phase 2 currently cannot deliver:\n\n(1) It de-orphans Phase 1 structurally, not rhetorically. The extractors stop being a dead branch and become the graph's error generator. L0-vs-L2 diff on a shared canonical key yields thousands of naturally distributed, automatically labelled temporal/argument errors (SPURIOUS / MISSING / ROLE_SWAP / TIME_SHIFT / GRAN_DROP) whose generating process \u2014 a PAIE/DEEIA/SCPRG argument decoder with no temporal component whatsoever \u2014 is provably independent of anything Nhan mines. Cite Contrastive KG Error Detection (AAAI 2024 / CIKM 2022) for the measured ~30% recall drop when detectors validated on synthetic noise meet real errors, and WikiConflict (K-CAP 2025) + Wikidata-edit-history refinement (arXiv 2210.15495) as the accepted precedent for sourcing errors from an independent human/system error process.\n\n(2) It kills the circularity as a *measured quantity* rather than an argument. The paper's central table becomes a provenance matrix: rows = error sources (S1 L2-predicted diff, S2 confusion-sampled TIMEX perturbation, S3 human-authored, S4 the ~72 native gold contradictions, S5 rule-negating injection), columns = detectors. \"Circularity gap\" = S5-column-max minus S1-column-max. Constraints are mined on L0 restricted to the MINE split, serialised, SHA-256'd and committed before any error exists (holdout rule mining, arXiv 1110.6652).\n\n(3) Gold's near-emptiness stops being a problem. 0 BEFORE cycles / 0 symmetric violations across 843,808 pairs is not a shortage of targets once L0 is a *reference layer* rather than the mining target: any symmetry violation or BEFORE cycle appearing in L2 is, by construction, an extraction error with a free label, because gold has exactly zero of them.\n\n(4) Granularity becomes attributable. A precision code lives on the TIMEX value (Wikibase-style integer: 9=year, 10=month, 11=day) and a time-derivation code lives beside it (regex / reference-time-propagation / gazetteer / LLM-tail / unresolved). A granularity conflict is then not just \"these two anchors disagree\" but \"these two anchors disagree AND the blame falls on tlayer=refprop with norm_conf 0.55\" \u2014 which is what makes it a data-quality finding rather than a curiosity.\n\nThe representation is deliberately two-serialisation, licensed by Iglesias-Molina et al. (ISWC 2023): a normalised columnar store + a reified statement-node VIEW (PaTeCon's sVertex, which is also exactly RDF 1.2's *reifier*) for the mining/inference consumer where reification measured best, and an optional qualifier-style / RDF 1.2 (`rdf:reifies`, triple terms, triple annotations) release for the human/SPARQL consumer. Time lives on the EVENT NODE (EventKG/SEM precedent, `sem:hasBeginTimeStamp`) and is *materialised onto statements* only at Phase-3 projection time \u2014 because every argument statement of one event shares that event's one interval, which is precisely the degeneracy already diagnosed. DEC (arXiv 2606.15246) supplies the semantics for layer coexistence: L0 is the factual core, L1/L2 are cognitive worlds that can be internally inconsistent without destabilising it, and DEC is explicitly agnostic to named graphs vs RDF-star vs reification \u2014 quote that as the licence to choose reification on engineering grounds alone.\n\nNODE/EDGE MODEL: NODES (every node row is keyed by (layer_id, node_id) and carries ckey_id, prov_json, conf):\n- Document. Layer-invariant (the text is the text). Carries ere_split and tempekg_split (MINE|EVAL, document-disjoint).\n- Event. Per layer. In L0/L1 the id is the gold `EVENT_*` (event coreference already collapsed by MAVEN-Arg/ERE). In L2 the id is minted by the ED model and aligned to a ckey by trigger character-offset match. Carries ev_type (168), type_conf, trigger_conf. TIME LIVES HERE (interval4), following EventKG/SEM, not on any edge.\n- EventMention. Per layer. The offset-bearing node; the only place char/token offsets are authoritative.\n- Entity. Layer-invariant in practice: entities (ids, 7 types, mention offsets) are PROVIDED as input in every split including blind test, so entity_src = 'gold_given' in all three layers. This is a large gift and the design leans on it: the subject side of the entity-as-subject projection is noise-free even in L2, which isolates the noise dial to arguments/triggers/relations/time.\n- PseudoEntity. Rule-derived (its own derivation code), minted to give the 59.9% of arguments that are bare {content, offset} spans a subject. Without this the entity-as-subject projection discards ~142K of 236,937 argument statements.\n- Timex. Per layer (the SPANS are given in every split, so timex nodes are identical across L0/L1/L2; what differs per layer is the *normalised value*, its precision, and its derivation code). 20,827 gold spans / 9,287 distinct surfaces in train+valid.\n\nSTATEMENT NODES (the reified sVertex analogue; one row = one argument participation):\n- stmt(stmt_id) : (event_id, role, subject) + property = ev_type||'#'||role (652 distinct, 623 with support >=10) + arg_conf + ent_link_conf + truth \u2208 {1 asserted, 0 retracted-by-diff, -1 unknown}. This is the row Phase 4 counts, and the row RDF 1.2 would call a reifier. Hernandez/Hogan/Kr\u00f6tzsch (SSWS 2015) put reification's cost at ~4x triples; at 236,937 statements that factor is irrelevant \u2014 say so in the paper, because it is the point: representation fidelity is free at this scale, so choose the most expressive model.\n\nEDGES (all per layer, all confidence-bearing):\n- anchor(event_id, timex_id, anchor_rel, rel_conf) \u2014 event-TIMEX. CONTAINS is ~99% of anchoring mass (66,418 of 67,075); BEGINS-ON (122) and ENDS-ON (39) are stored but never learned.\n- trel(head_id, tail_id, rel, rel_conf) \u2014 the 6 MAVEN-ERE temporal relations over event-event and event-TIMEX pairs, head_kind/tail_kind explicit because direction is convention-bearing.\n- crel(CAUSE | PRECONDITION), subevent(parent, child). These are relations BETWEEN relations, which is the one-sentence justification for statement nodes (the four-capability list in arXiv 2603.13603 \u2014 cite as motivation only, single-author unrefereed preprint, never as a result).\n- pointrel(x, y, op, w) \u2014 derived. Endpoint variables `ev:<id>.s`, `ev:<id>.f`, `tx:<id>.s`, `tx:<id>.f`; op \u2208 {<, <=, =}; w = confidence weight. This is the layer the ordering detector actually runs on.\n\nCROSS-LAYER SPINE (the piece no other angle has):\n- ckey(ckey_id, doc_id, ckey_kind) \u2014 canonical cross-layer event identity.\n- node_align(layer_id, node_id, ckey_id, align_method, align_conf) \u2014 the ONLY sanctioned way to join across layers.\n\nMEASURED, this project, not from the brief: the alignment key differs by split and both cases are exact.\n  * train/valid: MAVEN-Arg event ids \u2286 MAVEN-ERE event ids and the mention ids agree too \u2014 sampled docs gave argEV/ereEV 20/20 shared 20, 15/15 shared 15, 39/42 shared 39; mention-level 20/20, 20/20, 40/43. So ckey_kind='gold_event', ckey_id = `EVENT_*`.\n  * blind test (857 docs, doc overlap 857/857): MAVEN-Arg exposes `event_mentions` with NO event grouping, so `EVENT_*` ids do not exist there. Its mention ids are a strict subset of MAVEN-ERE test mention ids \u2014 sampled docs gave 30/30, 16/16, 31/31 shared, with ERE carrying more mentions (49, 38, 61). So ckey_kind='ere_mention', ckey_id = the ERE mention id. Any design that assumes a gold event id on the blind test breaks; this one does not.\n\nBLOCKING JOIN, budget it explicitly: MAVEN-Arg offsets are CHARACTER offsets into the `document` string; MAVEN-ERE offsets are TOKEN offsets plus sent_id. A `tokmap(doc_id, sent_id, tok_idx) -> (char_start, char_end)` bridge is required before a single argument can be attached to a temporally anchored event. This is the real critical path of Phase 2 and it is ~2 days, not an afternoon.\n\nSCHEMA: Root: C:\\Reseach_Quang\\tempekg\\  (new; sibling of EE\\MAVEN-Arg and MAVEN_ERE)\n  build\\eckg_v1\\eckg.duckdb                      -- working store\n  build\\eckg_v1\\parquet\\layer_id=0\\<table>.parquet ... layer_id=3\\   -- canonical release\n  build\\eckg_v1\\frozen\\constraints_L0_MINE.jsonl + .sha256\n  build\\eckg_v1\\manifest\\split_MINE.txt, split_EVAL.txt              -- doc ids, one per line\nSources: C:\\Reseach_Quang\\EE\\MAVEN-Arg\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl ; C:\\Reseach_Quang\\MAVEN_ERE\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl\n\n-- ===================== 0. PROVENANCE DIMENSIONS =====================\nCREATE TABLE layers (                 -- the DERIVATION axis (bitemporal 2nd axis, repurposed)\n  layer_id     SMALLINT PRIMARY KEY,\n  layer_name   VARCHAR,   -- 'L0_gold' | 'L1_trig' | 'L2_pred' | 'L3_blind'\n  trigger_src  VARCHAR,   -- 'gold' | 'ed_model'\n  arg_src      VARCHAR,   -- 'gold' | 'PAIE' | 'DEEIA' | 'SCPRG'\n  entity_src   VARCHAR,   -- 'gold_given' in ALL layers (entities are input in every split)\n  timex_span_src VARCHAR, -- 'gold_given' in ALL layers (TIMEX spans are input in every split)\n  trel_src     VARCHAR,   -- 'gold_ere' | 'trel_clf'\n  model_ckpt   VARCHAR,\n  calib_json   VARCHAR,   -- temperature-scaling params fitted on MINE split ONLY\n  git_sha      VARCHAR,\n  built_at     TIMESTAMP\n);\n-- seed: (0,'L0_gold','gold','gold','gold_given','gold_given','gold_ere',...)\n--       (1,'L1_trig','gold','PAIE','gold_given','gold_given','gold_ere',...)\n--       (2,'L2_pred','ed_model','PAIE','gold_given','gold_given','trel_clf',...)\n--       (3,'L3_blind','ed_model','PAIE','gold_given','gold_given','trel_clf',...)  -- 857 blind docs\n\nCREATE TABLE time_layers (            -- derivation axis for TIME, orthogonal to layers\n  tlayer_id SMALLINT PRIMARY KEY,     -- 0 regex | 1 refprop | 2 gazetteer | 3 llm_tail | 4 unresolved\n  method VARCHAR, expected_cov DOUBLE, notes VARCHAR\n);\n-- 0 regex  -> 62.1% of TIMEX deterministic (bare year 29.9, dmy 9.1, month+year 6.9, N+unit 6.1, mdy 4.6, ranges 5.6)\n-- 1 refprop-> narrative reference time = last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE (HeidelTime narrative domain\n--             strategy; MAVEN is Wikipedia and has NO document creation time, so DCT propagation is unavailable)\n-- 2 gazetteer -> ~50 entries ('World War II' x70, 'the Second World War' x32, 'World War I' x30; typed DURATION)\n-- 4 unresolved -> val_lo/val_hi stay NULL. NEVER guessed. Feeds the trivalent UNKNOWN.\n\nCREATE TABLE docs (\n  doc_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, title VARCHAR, n_sent INT, n_char INT,\n  ere_split VARCHAR,            -- 'train' | 'valid' | 'test'\n  tempekg_split VARCHAR         -- 'MINE' | 'EVAL' | 'BLIND'   (document-disjoint, seed 20261012)\n);\nCREATE TABLE tokmap (           -- the blocking bridge: ERE token offsets <-> Arg char offsets\n  doc_id VARCHAR, sent_id INT, tok_idx INT, char_start INT, char_end INT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (doc_id, sent_id, tok_idx)\n);\n\n-- ===================== 1. CROSS-LAYER SPINE =====================\nCREATE TABLE ckey (\n  ckey_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  ckey_kind VARCHAR             -- 'gold_event' (train/valid) | 'ere_mention' (blind test)\n);\nCREATE TABLE node_align (\n  layer_id SMALLINT, node_kind VARCHAR,   -- 'event' | 'entity' | 'timex'\n  node_id VARCHAR, ckey_id VARCHAR,       -- NULL => spurious node, no gold counterpart\n  align_method VARCHAR,  -- 'id' | 'trigger_offset_exact' | 'trigger_offset_overlap' | 'unaligned'\n  align_conf DOUBLE,\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, node_kind, node_id)\n);\n\n-- ===================== 2. NODES (per layer) =====================\nCREATE TABLE events (\n  layer_id SMALLINT, event_id VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR, ckey_id VARCHAR,\n  ev_type VARCHAR, ev_type_id SMALLINT,          -- 168 types\n  type_conf DOUBLE, trigger_conf DOUBLE,          -- 1.0 in L0; calibrated prob in L1/L2\n  n_mentions SMALLINT, prov_json VARCHAR,\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, event_id)\n);\nCREATE TABLE event_mentions (\n  layer_id SMALLINT, mention_id VARCHAR, event_id VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  trigger VARCHAR, sent_id INT, tok_start INT, tok_end INT, char_start INT, char_end INT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, mention_id)\n);\nCREATE TABLE entities (\n  layer_id SMALLINT, entity_id VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  ent_type VARCHAR,              -- Location|Other|Organization|Person|Product|Building|Art\n  entity_kind VARCHAR,           -- 'gold' | 'pseudo'\n  cluster_conf DOUBLE,           -- 1.0 gold; string/head-noun cluster score for pseudo\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, entity_id)\n);\nCREATE TABLE entity_mentions (\n  layer_id SMALLINT, mention_id VARCHAR, entity_id VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  surface VARCHAR, char_start INT, char_end INT, PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, mention_id)\n);\n\n-- ===================== 3. STATEMENT NODES (the sVertex / RDF 1.2 reifier) =====================\nCREATE TABLE stmt (\n  stmt_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,\n  layer_id SMALLINT, doc_id VARCHAR, event_id VARCHAR, ckey_id VARCHAR,\n  role VARCHAR,        -- one of 143: 'Agent','Victim','Location_original','Location_final','Duration',...\n  property VARCHAR,    -- ev_type||'#'||role  e.g. 'Killing#Victim'   (652 distinct; 623 with supp>=10)\n  subj_kind VARCHAR,   -- 'entity' | 'pseudo' | 'span'\n  entity_id VARCHAR,   -- NULL iff subj_kind='span'\n  surface VARCHAR, char_start INT, char_end INT,     -- NULL when the source gave only entity_id\n  arg_conf DOUBLE,     -- 1.0 in L0; temperature-calibrated decoder prob in L1/L2\n  ent_link_conf DOUBLE,-- 1.0 for gold entity_id (40.1% of args); cluster score for pseudo; NULL for span\n  truth SMALLINT,      -- 1 asserted | 0 retracted-by-diff | -1 unknown   (DEC factual-core discipline)\n  prov_json VARCHAR    -- {\"src\":\"PAIE\",\"ckpt\":\"...\",\"raw_logit\":...,\"decode_rank\":1}\n);\n\n-- ===================== 4. TIME =====================\nCREATE TABLE timex (\n  layer_id SMALLINT, timex_id VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  tx_type VARCHAR,           -- DATE | DURATION | TIME | PREPOSTEXP\n  surface VARCHAR, sent_id INT, tok_start INT, tok_end INT, char_start INT, char_end INT,\n  -- STAGE 1: context-independent representation (HeidelTime CIR / ISO-TimeML TIMEX3)\n  cir_value VARCHAR,         -- '1815' | 'XXXX-08-29' | 'UNDEF-next-day' | 'P14Y'\n  precision_s SMALLINT,      -- Wikibase precision: 6 mill,7 cent,8 dec,9 year,10 month,11 day\n  is_periodic BOOLEAN,       -- TRUE for X-placeholder values (yearless dates, weekdays)\n  is_anchorable BOOLEAN,     -- FALSE for pure DURATION: constrains WIDTH, not POSITION\n  anchor_timex_id VARCHAR, temporal_fn BOOLEAN,   -- ISO-TimeML anchorTimeID / temporalFunction\n  -- STAGE 2: resolved\n  val_lo DATE, val_hi DATE,  -- day-chronon expansion; NULL if tlayer_id=4 (unresolved)\n  mps_period_days INT, mps_offset INT,            -- minimal periodic set (Bettini & Mascetti)\n  tlayer_id SMALLINT, norm_conf DOUBLE,\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, timex_id)\n);\nCREATE TABLE anchor (\n  layer_id SMALLINT, doc_id VARCHAR, event_id VARCHAR, ckey_id VARCHAR, timex_id VARCHAR,\n  anchor_rel VARCHAR,        -- CONTAINS | SIMULTANEOUS | BEGINS-ON | ENDS-ON | BEFORE | OVERLAP\n  rel_conf DOUBLE, tlayer_id SMALLINT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, event_id, timex_id, anchor_rel)\n);\nCREATE TABLE interval4 (      -- the four-endpoint quadruple, per event per layer\n  layer_id SMALLINT, event_id VARCHAR, ckey_id VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  b_lo DATE, b_hi DATE, f_lo DATE, f_hi DATE,   -- <[b-,b+],[f-,f+]> over a DAY chronon\n  precision_eff SMALLINT,     -- finest precision_s among contributing anchors\n  precision_set VARCHAR,      -- JSON multiset e.g. '[9,11]' -- kept for granularity queries\n  n_anchors SMALLINT,\n  anchor_tier VARCHAR,        -- 'tight' | 'loose' | 'none'  (L0: 44.9% / 27.2% / 27.9%)\n  width_days INT,\n  time_conf DOUBLE,           -- MIN over contributing (rel_conf * norm_conf)\n  tlayer_mask SMALLINT,       -- bitmask of contributing time_layers -> blame attribution\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, event_id)\n);\n\n-- ===================== 5. EVENT-EVENT EDGES + POINT GRAPH =====================\nCREATE TABLE trel (layer_id SMALLINT, doc_id VARCHAR, head_id VARCHAR, tail_id VARCHAR,\n  head_kind VARCHAR, tail_kind VARCHAR, rel VARCHAR, rel_conf DOUBLE, prov_json VARCHAR,\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, doc_id, head_id, tail_id, rel));\nCREATE TABLE crel (layer_id SMALLINT, doc_id VARCHAR, head_id VARCHAR, tail_id VARCHAR,\n  rel VARCHAR, rel_conf DOUBLE, PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, doc_id, head_id, tail_id, rel));\nCREATE TABLE subevent (layer_id SMALLINT, doc_id VARCHAR, parent_id VARCHAR, child_id VARCHAR,\n  rel_conf DOUBLE, implied_contains_present BOOLEAN,   -- L0: FALSE for 1,638 of 12,019 (13.6%)\n  PRIMARY KEY (layer_id, doc_id, parent_id, child_id));\nCREATE TABLE pointrel (layer_id SMALLINT, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  x VARCHAR, y VARCHAR,       -- 'ev:EVENT_x.s' | 'ev:EVENT_x.f' | 'tx:TIME_y.s' | 'tx:TIME_y.f'\n  op VARCHAR,                 -- '<' | '<=' | '='\n  w DOUBLE,                   -- weight = product of source confidences (MaxSAT soft-clause weight)\n  src_kind VARCHAR, src_id VARCHAR);\n\n-- ===================== 6. DIFFERENTIAL + CONFLICT OUTPUT =====================\nCREATE TABLE layer_diff (      -- generated, not annotated: the free error labels\n  diff_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY, ref_layer_id SMALLINT, cmp_layer_id SMALLINT,\n  doc_id VARCHAR, ckey_id VARCHAR, property VARCHAR, subject_id VARCHAR,\n  diff_kind VARCHAR,   -- 'MATCH'|'SPURIOUS'|'MISSING'|'ROLE_SWAP'|'TYPE_SWAP'\n                       -- |'TIME_SHIFT'|'GRAN_DROP'|'GRAN_REFINE'|'ANCHOR_LOST'|'TREL_FLIP'\n  delta_days INT, delta_precision SMALLINT, cmp_conf DOUBLE\n);\nCREATE TABLE conflict (\n  conflict_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY, layer_id SMALLINT, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  ctype VARCHAR,   -- ORDERING: 'ORD_NEGCYCLE'|'ORD_SYM'|'ORD_ENTAIL'|'ORD_TRANS'\n                   -- GRANULARITY: 'GRAN_EMPTY'|'GRAN_NONNEST'|'GRAN_DRIFT'|'GRAN_DROP'\n  scope VARCHAR,   -- 'intra_layer' | 'differential'\n  ref_layer_id SMALLINT,        -- non-NULL only when scope='differential' (normally 0)\n  ckey_set VARCHAR,             -- JSON list of ckey_ids\n  witness_json VARCHAR,         -- the pointrel cycle, or the two anchors + their precision codes\n  verdict VARCHAR,              -- 'CONFLICT' | 'CONSISTENT' | 'UNKNOWN'  (trivalent, PaTeCon-compatible)\n  weight DOUBLE,                -- confidence-weighted severity = 1 - PROD(conf of witness rows)\n  culprit_stmt_id BIGINT, culprit_anchor VARCHAR, culprit_tlayer_id SMALLINT, culprit_conf DOUBLE,\n  detector VARCHAR,             -- 'stp_negcycle'|'periodic_intersect'|'maxsat'|'mined:<tc_id>'|'shacl'\n  gold_status VARCHAR           -- 'gold_clean'|'gold_conflict'|'no_gold'  <- the free label\n);\nCREATE TABLE constraints_frozen (   -- the anti-circularity artefact\n  tc_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, family VARCHAR, head_pred VARCHAR, body_json VARCHAR,\n  support INT, conf DOUBLE, atomicity_p DOUBLE, bh_qvalue DOUBLE,\n  mined_on_layer SMALLINT, mined_on_split VARCHAR, set_sha256 VARCHAR\n);\n\n-- ===================== 7. THE INTERFACE VIEWS (see interface field) =====================\nCREATE VIEW v_projection_input AS\nSELECT s.layer_id, s.doc_id, d.tempekg_split, s.ckey_id, s.stmt_id,\n       COALESCE(s.entity_id, 'SPAN:'||s.stmt_id) AS subject_id,\n       s.subj_kind, e.ent_type AS subject_type,\n       s.property, s.role, ev.ev_type, s.event_id AS object_event_id,\n       i.b_lo, i.b_hi, i.f_lo, i.f_hi, i.precision_eff, i.precision_set,\n       i.anchor_tier, i.width_days, i.tlayer_mask,\n       s.arg_conf * COALESCE(s.ent_link_conf,1.0) * COALESCE(i.time_conf,1.0) AS stmt_conf\nFROM stmt s\n  JOIN events ev USING (layer_id, event_id)\n  JOIN docs d USING (doc_id)\n  LEFT JOIN interval4 i USING (layer_id, event_id)\n  LEFT JOIN entities e USING (layer_id, entity_id)\nWHERE s.truth = 1;\n\nCREATE VIEW v_noise_dial AS       -- the dial, as a reportable table\nSELECT layer_id, tempekg_split,\n       COUNT(*) AS n_stmt, COUNT(DISTINCT ckey_id) AS n_events,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT property) AS n_properties,\n       AVG(stmt_conf) AS mean_conf,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN anchor_tier='tight' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)::DOUBLE/COUNT(*) AS tight_frac\nFROM v_projection_input GROUP BY 1,2;\n\nINTERVALS: Four things per event, all persisted, none inferred at query time:\n\n1. QUADRUPLE over a day chronon: interval4 = <[b_lo, b_hi], [f_lo, f_hi]>. Semantics from Dyreson & Snodgrass (TODS 23(1), 1998) indeterminate instants; IE-side precedent from Reimers et al. (ACL 2016) and the unified quadruple time-anchor representation (arXiv 2008.06452). This uniformly subsumes L0's three measured tiers instead of special-casing them: tight anchor (37,852 events, 44.9%, 10.4/doc) \u2192 narrow b/f boxes; loose bound only (27.2%) \u2192 one side open; no TIMEX link (27.9%) \u2192 all four NULL with anchor_tier='none'. It also degrades to PaTeCon's exact (start, finish) when b_lo=b_hi and f_lo=f_hi, so Nhan's sVertex fields fill with a straight rename.\n\n2. PRECISION CODE, Wikibase-style, on the TIMEX value AND aggregated onto the event (precision_eff plus the full precision_set multiset). 9=year, 10=month, 11=day. This is the make-or-break decision: if '1815' is normalised straight to [1815-01-01, 1815-12-31] and nothing else is kept, granularity conflict collapses into ordinary interval containment and the project's second conflict type disappears. precision_set='[9,11]' is what survives that collapse.\n\n3. MINIMAL PERIODIC SET for underspecified values (Bettini & Mascetti, arXiv 1110.2213), carried as (cir_value, is_periodic, mps_period_days, mps_offset). '29 August' with no year is \u27e6XXXX-08-29\u27e7 = {y-08-29 : y \u2208 Z}, period 365/366 days \u2014 a periodic subgranularity that COVERS days and GROUPS PERIODICALLY INTO years but does NOT PARTITION the timeline (Bettini/Dyreson/Evans/Snodgrass/Wang glossary, LNCS 1399, 1998 \u2014 use these exact words or a TIME-community reviewer will object to calling it a granularity). Compatibility is periodic-set intersection with a canonical-form emptiness test.\n\n4. is_anchorable FALSE for DURATION. Real case found in the data: doc 0371bbf116422f8e3a0a853bdd1962aa (\"Murder of Leigh Leigh\") has TIME_7ad0f1d2 with surface '14 years' typed DATE in gold \u2014 a duration mistyped as a date. It constrains WIDTH not POSITION, so it must never enter b/f as a position bound. MAVEN-Arg's 143 roles confirm the same asymmetry from the other side: no Time/Date/When role exists at all, and 'Duration' has 33 instances on exactly one event type (Prison), values like \"life\" and \"21 years\". Only 4.97% of argument spans overlap any TIMEX and only 13.8% of TIMEX are covered by any argument, so EAE structurally cannot supply event time \u2014 the anchor table is the only route and it must be built, not extracted.\n\nRESOLVE-THEN-STP, deliberately. All resolved anchors are down-cast to the day chronon before consistency checking, which keeps the network a Simple Temporal Problem (Dechter/Meiri/Pearl, AI 1991) \u2014 Floyd-Warshall decides consistency COMPLETELY in O(n^3), conflict witnessed by a negative cycle, documents averaging 23.3 events so it is milliseconds. Keeping periodic sets live inside the network would put us in multi-granularity TCSP, which Bettini/Wang/Jajodia (AMAI 1998; AI 140, 2002) prove NP-hard. Periodic-set arithmetic is therefore used ONLY inside the granularity detector's intersection test, never inside the ordering solver. With 45 days that is the only viable split, and it is stateable in one paragraph with citations.\n\nTRIVALENT THROUGHOUT: unresolved (tlayer_id=4) yields val_lo/val_hi NULL, which yields verdict='UNKNOWN', never 'CONFLICT'. That is exactly the 7.2% normalisation-error bucket Kontrast measured; letting an unresolved yearless date fire a conflict would reproduce their worst failure mode.\n\nORDERING CONFLICT: Three detectors over pointrel, all confidence-weighted, run per (doc_id, layer_id). Ordering conflict here means: the point-algebra closure of one document-layer entails both p and \u00acp for some pair, i.e. the STP is unsatisfiable.\n\nTRANSLATION (fixed, layer-independent): BEFORE(a,b) \u2192 a.f < b.s ; CONTAINS(a,b) \u2192 a.s <= b.s AND b.f <= a.f ; SIMULTANEOUS \u2192 a.s = b.s AND a.f = b.f ; OVERLAP(a,b) \u2192 a.s < b.s < a.f < b.f ; BEGINS-ON(e,tx) \u2192 e.s = tx.s ; ENDS-ON(e,tx) \u2192 e.f = tx.f. Anchors add absolute bounds b_lo <= e.s <= b_hi, f_lo <= e.f <= f_hi. Every pointrel row carries w = product of the confidences that produced it (rel_conf * norm_conf * type_conf).\n\nD1 \u2014 STP negative-cycle (exact, mining-free, the honest floor). Floyd-Warshall over the document-layer's point graph. Unsatisfiable \u21d2 ctype='ORD_NEGCYCLE', witness = the cycle's pointrel rows. This detector uses ZERO mined constraints, so its numbers are non-circular by construction and it is the fairest baseline for Nhan's mined-constraint claim. Cite Dechter/Meiri/Pearl for completeness and Nebel & B\u00fcrckert (JACM 42(1), 1995) plus Krokhin/Jeavons/Jonsson (JACM 50(5), 2003) for the tractability position. STATE THE POSITION EXPLICITLY, because almost no KG-conflict paper does: PaTeCon's head predicate set {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} is NOT inside ORD-Horn, because interval disjointness is (a.f < b.s) OR (b.f < a.s), a genuine disjunction of strict inequalities. Drop `disjoint` and path consistency is a complete cubic decision procedure; keep it and you must route to MaxSAT. This design does both: D1 runs on the disjoint-free sublanguage, D3 handles disjoint.\n\nD2 \u2014 cheap structural checks that are free labels because gold has exactly zero of them. Symmetry violation (ctype='ORD_SYM') and transitivity gap (ctype='ORD_TRANS'), using the Joint Constrained Learning vocabulary (Wang et al., EMNLP 2020: annotation / symmetry / conjunction consistency). L0 measured: 0 symmetric violations, 0 BEFORE cycles across 843,808 pairs, BEFORE 100.0% transitively closed (7 missing of 6,718,279 chains). Therefore any ORD_SYM or ORD_NEGCYCLE row with layer_id=2 is an extraction error with certainty and needs no annotation. Consequence for engineering: do NOT recompute closure over L0 \u2014 it is already closed and recomputing double-counts support. Compute closure only for layer_id \u2208 {2,3}.\n\nD3 \u2014 partial weighted MaxSAT for CULPRIT LOCALISATION. Hard clauses = Allen/point composition and symmetry axioms; soft clauses = the extracted relations and anchors with weight w from pointrel. One Z3 call per document-layer (23.3 events/doc; trivial). The falsified soft clauses in the optimal assignment ARE the ranked conflict candidates, and their identity fills culprit_stmt_id / culprit_anchor. This gives what the temporal-KG conflict line has never reported: culprit-accuracy@1 alongside set-level detection F1 (ReFACT, arXiv 2509.25868 / EACL 2026, is the precedent for demanding positional annotation, not just binary detection). Equivalently expressible as minimal-explanation / hitting-set output per the 2025 inconsistency survey (arXiv 2502.19023) \u2014 say that, since it makes the output \"explainable\" in the ESWC-2025 sense rather than a binary flag.\n\nTHE DIFFERENTIAL PART \u2014 this is what the multi-version store adds and single-version designs cannot express. Every conflict row carries scope, ref_layer_id and gold_status. For a conflict found at layer_id=2 on ckey set K:\n  gold_status = 'gold_clean'    if the same K in layer 0 is satisfiable  \u2192 the conflict IS an extraction error, free positive label\n  gold_status = 'gold_conflict' if layer 0 is also unsatisfiable on K    \u2192 one of the ~72 genuine gold contradictions, or an annotation gap\n  gold_status = 'no_gold'       if K has no layer-0 counterpart (spurious event, or layer 3 blind docs)\nThat column is the whole non-circular evaluation. It turns \"did the detector find the conflict I injected?\" into \"did the detector find the conflict a temporal-relation classifier actually made?\", labelled for free.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE, real ids. Doc 364ed14fc610df6e25a2f446e2b2d2ab (\"Expedition of the Thousand\", 21 events, 2 TIMEX). L0 holds BEFORE(EVENT_8d2fbddb\u2026, EVENT_f535eb21\u2026 [Motion, trigger \"Expedition\"]), subevent(EVENT_f535eb21\u2026, EVENT_d2587256\u2026), CONTAINS(TIME_c61b2c2b\u2026 \"1860\", EVENT_d2587256\u2026), and OVERLAP(EVENT_e2cbbfb4\u2026 [Control, \"ruled\"], TIME_c61b2c2b\u2026). D1 on layer 0: satisfiable, no cycle \u2014 as expected. Now L2's trel_clf, run on the EVAL split, emits both BEFORE(EVENT_f535eb21, EVENT_8d2fbddb) and BEFORE(EVENT_8d2fbddb, EVENT_f535eb21) with rel_conf 0.61 and 0.58. D2 fires ORD_SYM; D1 fires ORD_NEGCYCLE with witness the two-arc cycle; D3's optimal assignment falsifies the 0.58 clause and writes culprit_stmt_id pointing at it. gold_status='gold_clean' because layer 0 on that pair is satisfiable. weight = 1 - (0.61*0.58) = 0.646. One row, one free label, one localised culprit, zero annotation.\n\nSCALE CHECK: mean 23.3 events/doc gives ~254 pairs/doc, ~1.1M candidate pairs corpus-wide for the L2 classifier \u2014 the same order as gold's 1.22M temporal relations, so the predicted layer is genuinely comparable in density and not a toy.\n\nPOSITIONING, mandatory: Fan & Strube (Findings of EMNLP 2025, pp. 18605-18622) already do Allen-algebra consistency over MAVEN-ERE temporal predictions. Say the difference in one sentence or a reviewer will: they enforce instance-level logical closure over pairs at decode/training time in a single-version graph; here consistency is a differential query between co-resident gold and predicted layers of the same document with per-statement provenance and confidence, which is what makes the violation attributable to a named model and checkpoint rather than merely detected.\n\nGRANULARITY CONFLICT: Granularity conflict is defined on the ANCHOR SET of one canonical event key, and the definition is INTERSECTION EMPTINESS, never precision difference. That distinction is the single most important guard in the design: 17,102 L0 events carry >=2 CONTAINS anchors (2 anchors: 10,653; 3: 3,707; >=4: 2,742) and they are overwhelmingly NESTED (\"in 1778\" + \"on November 11\"), i.e. narrative containers in the Stubbs & Pustejovsky (LAW V, 2011) sense. A detector that flagged \"event has >=2 anchors at differing precision\" would be almost entirely false positives \u2014 exactly the failure Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959) quantified at 18.6% of its flagged conflicts.\n\nFOUR SUBTYPES, all confidence- and provenance-attributed:\n\nGRAN_EMPTY (intra-layer, conjunctive). Two or more anchors of the same event in the same layer whose precision-expanded / periodic-set-expanded denotations have EMPTY intersection. Verdict CONFLICT only when provably empty; CONSISTENT when non-empty; UNKNOWN when any contributing anchor has tlayer_id=4. Worked example, real: doc 79e2767b814f136745e35123957316b3 (\"1815 North Carolina hurricane\"), EVENT_a0b68c8ac4f38c6bcfafdab518b24bd8 (type Arriving, trigger \"arriving\"), anchors TIME_689b44ba\u2026 surface '29 August' (yearless \u2192 cir_value 'XXXX-08-29', precision_s 11, is_periodic TRUE) and TIME_fe849f87\u2026 surface '1815' (cir_value '1815', precision_s 9). \u27e6XXXX-08-29\u27e7 \u2229 \u27e61815\u27e7 = {1815-08-29}: singleton, non-empty \u21d2 BENIGN REFINEMENT, and interval4 collapses to b_lo=b_hi=f_lo=f_hi=1815-08-29 with precision_set='[9,11]'. Now perturb the coarse anchor to 1816 in the normaliser output: intersection is empty \u21d2 GRAN_EMPTY, weight 1-(1.0*0.72), culprit_anchor='TIME_fe849f87\u2026', culprit_tlayer_id=0. That perturbation is defined purely by interval/periodic-set arithmetic and touches no mined constraint, so it is structurally non-circular \u2014 this is the strongest single reason to prioritise granularity over ordering under a 45-day budget.\n\nGRAN_NONNEST (intra-layer, structural). Anchor set is non-empty pairwise but does not form a subset chain \u2014 a provable non-container structure. Reported separately from GRAN_EMPTY because it is a weaker, more debatable signal and belongs in the annotation pool, not in the automatic-label pool.\n\nGRAN_DRIFT (differential, cross-layer \u2014 only this design can express it). Same ckey_id, layer 0 precision_eff = 11 with val = 1815-08-29; layer 2 precision_eff = 9 with val \u2286 1815 but the two denotations are NOT nested (e.g. the L2 reference-time propagation resolved the yearless '29 August' against the wrong last-mentioned DATE and produced 1816-08-29). delta_precision and delta_days go into layer_diff; conflict row gets scope='differential', ref_layer_id=0, culprit_tlayer_id=1 (refprop). This is the provenance-attributed granularity error: the graph does not just say \"wrong\", it says \"wrong, by the reference-time propagator, by +366 days, at confidence 0.55\".\n\nGRAN_DROP (differential, incompleteness NOT contradiction). Same ckey has a tight anchor in layer 0 and anchor_tier='none' in layer 2, because trel_clf failed to predict the CONTAINS edge. This is precision LOSS, and it must be scored separately from contradiction. The L0 audit already proves why the distinction is the empirical backbone of an honest paper: measured incompleteness (1,638 of 12,019 subevent pairs, 13.6%, lack the implied CONTAINS) dwarfs measured contradiction (~72 corpus-wide). DCs and GEDs cannot express \"an edge must exist\"; GGDs (arXiv 2403.17082) can \u2014 cite that for the existence-head framing and keep the two classes in separate columns of every results table.\n\nCONJUNCTIVE vs DISJUNCTIVE, stated explicitly or the class is ill-posed. Within one document-layer, all anchors of an event are asserted together, so empty intersection is a genuine error (conjunctive). Across layers, rival anchors are independent assertions from different derivation processes, so an empty intersection is disagreement to be adjudicated, not error (disjunctive) \u2014 which is precisely Wikidata's own stance that multiple mutually incompatible dates may legitimately coexist as separate statements. The scope column carries this distinction, and it also keeps the disjunctive reading out of the solver, where it would push us into NP-hard disjunctive TCSP.\n\nTHE DURATION TRAP, with a real instance. Doc 0371bbf116422f8e3a0a853bdd1962aa, EVENT_afb6ae79\u2026 (Criminal_investigation, \"investigation\") has anchors TIME_d9e52a90 '1996' and TIME_7ad0f1d2 '14 years' \u2014 the latter typed DATE in gold but actually a duration. is_anchorable=FALSE routes it to width-only, and the detector returns verdict='UNKNOWN' rather than a spurious GRAN_EMPTY. Nine other events in that same document share the identical anchor pair, so getting this wrong would manufacture ~10 false conflicts from one annotation slip. Report the count of such cases as a finding; it is evidence for the honest position, not an embarrassment.\n\nSECOND, DECLARATIVE DETECTOR, ~1 day: encode the GRAN_EMPTY rule as a SHACL-SPARQL shape over the projected entity-as-subject TKG (Robaldo & Batsakis: OWL-Time's axiomatisation provably cannot enforce constraints expressible in its own vocabulary; their worked failure case, two instants on one temporal entity with different dateTime values, is the one-granularity special case of this rule). Having a standards-based baseline alongside the mined-constraint detector pre-empts \"why not just constraints in the ontology?\" for the price of a day.\n\nPOSITIONING, mandatory and non-negotiable. Do NOT claim formal novelty: Bettini/Wang/Jajodia (AMAI 1998; AI 140, 2002) formalised multi-granularity temporal constraint networks and proved consistency NP-hard, and Euzenat (Computational Intelligence 17(4), 2001) gave upward/downward granularity-conversion operators with proved consistency conditions, explicitly motivated by problems when merging representations of different granularity. Do NOT claim the phenomenon is unnoticed: Kontrast names \"information granularity differences\" as the first category of its taxonomy and measures it at 18.6%. The defensible claim is the INVERSION plus the attribution: Kontrast treats granularity mismatch as a false positive to normalise away and recommends excluding it from contradiction benchmarks; here it is adjudicated into {benign refinement, genuine conflict, unknown}, at event level rather than entity-attribute level, with the blame assigned to a named derivation layer and confidence. Cite Kontrast's own 18.6% as the prevalence justification.\n\nINTERFACE TO P3/P4: Phase 2 ships ONE artefact directory and ONE contract document. Nobody reads Phase 2's Python.\n\nDELIVERABLE: C:\\Reseach_Quang\\tempekg\\build\\eckg_v1\\ containing eckg.duckdb, parquet\\layer_id={0,1,2,3}\\*.parquet, manifest\\split_{MINE,EVAL}.txt, frozen\\constraints_L0_MINE.jsonl(+.sha256), and CONTRACT.md with the table list, the six guarantees, and a `pytest` contract-test file that both consumers run before filing a bug.\n\nTO PHASE 3 (Vinh \u2014 projection ECKG -> Temporal KG):\nSingle entry point `v_projection_input`. It is already entity-as-subject, which is the direction the audit says is mandatory (event-as-subject makes all predicates degenerate because every statement of one event shares one interval). Vinh selects layer and gets the quadruple form his projection needs:\n    SELECT subject_id, subject_type, property, object_event_id,\n           b_lo, b_hi, f_lo, f_hi, precision_eff, precision_set,\n           stmt_conf, ckey_id, doc_id\n    FROM v_projection_input\n    WHERE layer_id = :L AND tempekg_split = :S;\n  * property is pre-materialised as ev_type||'#'||role, e.g. 'Killing#Victim', 'Attack#Attacker', 'Military_operation#Location', 'Hostile_encounter#Side_A', 'Process_start#Agent' \u2014 652 distinct values, 623 with support >=10. Vinh does not build this string; he consumes it, so the vocabulary can never drift between phases.\n  * The noise dial is the `layer_id` parameter. Nothing else in his code changes between G_gold, G_trig and G_pred. That is the whole point of the angle.\n  * subject_id is never NULL: gold entity_id where present (40.1% of args), PSEUDO_* where the pseudo-entity pass clustered a bare span, 'SPAN:<stmt_id>' as an unlinkable singleton fallback. Vinh must never see a subjectless statement.\n  * Vinh additionally gets `v_reified` (stmt_id, subject_id, property, object_event_id, b/f, precision, layer_id, conf), the sVertex/RDF-1.2-reifier shape, if he wants to emit a qualifier-style or RDF 1.2 (`rdf:reifies`, triple term, reifier, triple annotation) release. Two serialisations, licensed by Iglesias-Molina et al. (ISWC 2023): reified internally for the mining/inference consumer where it measured best, qualifier-style externally. Warning to pass on: Egami et al. (arXiv 2503.21804) show the metadata representation model itself moves link-prediction metrics, so if any learned number is reported on the projected graph, the projection choice is a confound that must be stated.\n\nTO PHASE 4 (Nhan \u2014 constraint mining):\nFour PaTeCon-shaped indexes, materialised per layer, plus exact counting views. No approximation: the |R|^2 index at 652 properties is ~425,104 entries / ~0.1 GB, so exact counts are affordable and PaTeCon+'s two-stage pruning and MiniAMIE's independence approximation are both unnecessary here. Say that in the paper as a positioning line: at event-KG scale efficiency is not the bottleneck, validity is.\n  * v_eVertexList(layer_id, subject_id) -> LIST(stmt_id)          [PaTeCon eVertexList; the SP(a) star]\n  * v_sVertexList(layer_id, property)   -> LIST(stmt_id)          [PaTeCon sVertexList]\n  * v_temporalRelationList(layer_id, doc_id, head_id, tail_id, rel, rel_conf)\n  * v_entityType(layer_id, entity_id, ent_type)                    [7 types]\n  * v_type_pair(layer_id, doc_id, e1, e2, t1, t2, shared_role_sig) [the index PaTeCon does NOT have and Nhan needs: instances are document-local, so the aggregation key is the TYPE SIGNATURE, not the entity. 2,708 (T1,T2) signatures at support>=10 covering 81% of instances; 2,688 (T1,r1,T2,r2) at 59%. 274,525 shared-participant pairs, of which 139,093 (50.7%) also carry a MAVEN-ERE temporal relation.]\n  * v_support(layer_id, property_or_signature, n_pos, n_neg, n_unknown) \u2014 trivalent counts kept SEPARATE so Nhan can reproduce PaTeCon's discard-unknown behaviour exactly, or not, and report both.\n  * eval_pred(layer_id, doc_id, pred, e1, e2) -> {positive, negative, unknown} as a UDF over the closed point graph. One trivalent operator subsuming numeric and purely qualitative cases; this is the clean generalisation of PaTeCon's Table 2.\n  * constraints_frozen \u2014 Nhan writes here ONCE, on layer 0, MINE split only, then the file is hashed and the hash committed. Every later number is reported against that hash.\n\nSIX GUARANTEES (in CONTRACT.md, enforced by contract tests):\nG1 ckey_id is the ONLY sanctioned cross-layer join key. Joining on event_id across layers is a bug; the test asserts no such join appears in either consumer's SQL.\nG2 stmt_conf \u2208 (0,1], and in layer 0 it is EXACTLY 1.0 for every row. Therefore any Phase-4 code that ignores confidence reproduces the single-version baseline bit-for-bit, and the confidence-weighted variant is a strict, auditable add-on rather than a rewrite.\nG3 interval4 rows satisfy b_lo<=b_hi, f_lo<=f_hi, b_lo<=f_lo, b_hi<=f_hi; unresolved time is NULL, never guessed. NULL means UNKNOWN, and UNKNOWN never produces CONFLICT.\nG4 tempekg_split is document-disjoint. Entity leakage is impossible by construction (measured: 0 of 68,348 entities appear in >1 document), but the split is still enforced at document level because TIMEX surfaces and (T1,T2) type signatures do leak.\nG5 Schema is append-only within v1. Adding layer 3 never rewrites a layer-0 row. Every layer's git_sha, model checkpoint and calibration parameters are recorded in `layers`, so any number in the paper is traceable to a build.\nG6 Injector firewall: whoever writes the perturbation code has no read access to frozen\\constraints_L0_MINE.jsonl, and the injected-error manifest stays sealed until detection results exist. A \"blindness audit\" is reported: fraction of injected conflicts that any single mined constraint fires on, versus fraction reachable only through multi-hop closure.\n\nHANDOFF DATES (45 days from 28 Aug 2026, deadline 12 Oct 2026): day 10 \u2014 layers 0 and 1 frozen, v_projection_input live, contract tests green, Vinh and Nhan unblocked on real data. Day 18 \u2014 layer 2 (+3) landed, layer_diff populated, conflict tables live. Day 20 onward Phase 2 is maintenance only; anything not done by day 20 gets cut, not extended.\n\nNOVELTY CLAIM: CLAIM (artefact + method, deliberately not a formal claim): the first event-centric knowledge graph in which the SAME documents are materialised simultaneously at three measured extraction-noise levels inside one store, with a per-statement (valid interval, precision code, derivation layer, calibrated confidence) tuple, so that (a) transfer of mined temporal constraints from a gold layer to a model-extracted layer of the same graph and (b) provenance-attributed ordering and granularity conflict become differential queries over co-resident layers rather than results on an injected benchmark.\n\nDefended against named prior work, one line each:\n- PaTeCon (AAAI 2023) / PaTeCon+ (arXiv 2312.11053): single-version graph over globally-recurring Wikidata/Freebase entities; their own need to construct WD-Confl / YG-Confl is documentary evidence that real conflicts are scarce. No provenance axis, no precision family, no gold-vs-predicted co-residence. We keep their reified sVertex store and trivalent logic and add the axis they lack; PaTeCon+ fact-level confidence produced ~38x more conflicts than entity-level, which is exactly why our conflict rows carry weight and culprit and are reported at Precision@k.\n- Fan & Strube (Findings of EMNLP 2025): Allen-algebra consistency over PREDICTED MAVEN-ERE relations \u2014 same dataset, same relation family, so this is the live novelty threat and must be cited. Difference: instance-level logical closure inside one prediction set, versus a differential query between co-resident gold and predicted layers with per-statement provenance, which is what lets a violation be attributed to a named model and checkpoint.\n- EventKG (Gottschalk & Demidova, ESWC 2018 / SWJ 2019): the closest provenance precedent \u2014 named graphs per SOURCE, reified event-entity relations, time on the event resource via SEM. But provenance there means which external KG the fact came from; it carries no extraction confidence, no precision code, and never holds two noise-level variants of the same document.\n- HTKG / Wiki-hy / YAGO-hy (Ding et al., Findings of EMNLP 2024) and VITA (2025): per-fact timestamps and a time-invariant / time-dependent qualifier split, but distilled from Wikidata/YAGO and provenance-free. No HTKG benchmark is grounded in raw text with gold n-ary argument roles; MAVEN-Arg + MAVEN-ERE on identical documents makes this the first, which is the resource the ECTKG survey (Mathematics 2023, 11(23), 4852) explicitly names as missing.\n- Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959, 2026): names \"information granularity differences\" and measures it at 18.6%, then recommends normalising it away before labelling contradictions. We invert the stance and add what it has no mechanism for \u2014 attributing the mismatch to a derivation layer and a confidence.\n- Soulard, Sa\u00efs & Raad (ESWC 2025) and When Facts Expire (CIKM 2025): entity-timeline temporal constraints on Wikidata, Allen-extended, single-version, entity-attribute facts. Differentiated on event level (168 types, 143 roles, 652 properties), document-local entities, and the multi-version axis.\n- Bitemporal Property Graphs (ADBIS 2025), AeonG (PVLDB 17(6), 2024), Gradoop TPGM (VLDBJ 2021), BiTRDF (2025): bitemporal models exist and are mature, but the second axis is system/transaction time. Repurposing it as a DERIVATION/noise axis and instantiating it on an NLP extraction pipeline has no prior instance. Cite ADBIS 2025 for the model; do not adopt AeonG as an engine.\n- DEC (arXiv 2606.15246, June 2026): supplies cognitive worlds with a distinguished factual core and is explicitly agnostic to named graphs / RDF-star / reification \u2014 quoted as the licence to choose reification on engineering grounds. Our layers instantiate its worlds; it provides semantics, not an artefact.\n- WikiConflict (K-CAP 2025) and Wikidata-edit-history refinement (arXiv 2210.15495): the accepted precedent for errors from an independent error process, but entity-centric and not co-resident. Our L2-vs-L0 diff is the event-graph analogue, with the extractors as the error process.\n- Euzenat (Comp. Intelligence 17(4), 2001) and Bettini/Wang/Jajodia (AMAI 1998; AI 140, 2002): they own the formal ground for cross-granularity inconsistency. We claim operationalisation-plus-attribution inside a KG pipeline, and say so; any sentence of the form \"first to formally define granularity conflict\" would be false and a TIME-literate reviewer would catch it.\n\nTwo cheap secondary claims that come free with this design: (i) the first soundness-tested temporal constraint miner, if Nhan bolts on the atomicity / conditional-independence test of Martin et al. (PVLDB 18, 2025) \u2014 they report >95% of discovered DCs are false and give a Beta-Bernoulli log-odds test at alpha\u22480.01 that plugs into a counting pass; (ii) the first temporal-KG conflict paper to state which tractable subalgebra its relation language occupies and to exploit it (disjoint-free sublanguage \u2192 complete O(n^3) detector; `disjoint` \u2192 MaxSAT).\n\nEFFORT (person-days): 25\n\nBUILD STEPS: D1-2 (2d) OFFSET BRIDGE + CROSS-LAYER SPINE. Build tokmap(doc_id, sent_id, tok_idx -> char_start, char_end) by re-tokenising MAVEN-ERE `sentences` against the MAVEN-Arg `document` string; assert 100% coverage per doc or fail loudly. Populate docs, ckey, node_align. ckey_kind='gold_event' for the 3,623 train+valid docs (event ids and mention ids verified identical across the two corpora on sampled docs: 20/20, 15/15, 39/42 events; 20/20, 20/20, 40/43 mentions) and ckey_kind='ere_mention' for the 857 blind-test docs (MAVEN-Arg event_mentions verified a strict subset of MAVEN-ERE test mention ids: 30/30, 16/16, 31/31). Assign tempekg_split MINE/EVAL document-disjoint, seed 20261012, write manifests. THIS IS THE CRITICAL PATH: nothing else can start. | D3-4 (2d) INGEST LAYER 0. events, event_mentions, entities, entity_mentions, stmt (236,937 rows, property pre-materialised as ev_type||'#'||role, all conf = 1.0), timex spans, anchor (67,075 event-TIMEX), trel (~1.22M), crel, subevent (12,019 with implied_contains_present). Re-verify the audit numbers as regression tests: 0 BEFORE cycles, 0 symmetric violations, 1,638 subevent gaps, ~72 total contradictions. If a number moves, ingestion is wrong. | D5-6 (2d) TIMEX NORMALISER STAGE 1 (context-independent representation). Emit cir_value with ISO-TimeML X placeholders and HeidelTime UNDEF markers ('1815', 'XXXX-08-29', 'UNDEF-next-day', 'P14Y'), precision_s (Wikibase 6-11), is_periodic, is_anchorable (FALSE for DURATION), anchor_timex_id/temporal_fn. Regex covers the measured 62.1%. PERSIST STAGE 1 OUTPUT: it is the only place granularity is observable, and the conflict detector needs it. | D7-8 (2d) TIMEX NORMALISER STAGE 2 (disambiguation). Narrative reference time = last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE (HeidelTime narrative-domain strategy; MAVEN is Wikipedia and has no document creation time, so DCT propagation is unavailable). ~50-entry gazetteer for named periods ('World War II' x70, 'the Second World War' x32, 'World War I' x30). Fill val_lo/val_hi, mps_period_days/mps_offset, tlayer_id, norm_conf. Target ~85-90% coverage; leave the tail at tlayer_id=4 with NULL values, never guessed. Frame it in the paper as gold-span value-only normalisation (arXiv 2205.10399), a reimplementation of an established recipe, with EventTempEx (FIRE 2025) as prior art for the relative-date family. | D9-10 (2d) INTERVAL4 + PERIODIC-SET LIBRARY. Materialise the quadruple per (layer, event) with precision_eff, precision_set, anchor_tier, width_days, time_conf, tlayer_mask. Implement minimal-periodic-set canonical form, intersection and emptiness test (Bettini & Mascetti). Regression-assert the L0 tier split: tight 44.9%, loose 27.2%, none 27.9%. MILESTONE: v_projection_input goes live for layer 0; Vinh and Nhan are unblocked. | D11-12 (1.5d) PSEUDO-ENTITY PASS. Cluster the 59.9% bare {content, offset} argument spans within each document by normalised string / head-noun match; mint PSEUDO_* entity ids with cluster_conf; leave unlinkable singletons as 'SPAN:<stmt_id>'. REPORT THE RECOVERED JOIN EDGES before/after \u2014 this number directly bounds how much evidence Nhan's SP(a) star sees. Baseline to beat: 16,284 entities in >=2 events, mean 3.15, max 59. | D12-13 (1.5d) POINT GRAPH + D1/D2 DETECTORS. Translate every trel and anchor into pointrel with weights; Floyd-Warshall closure per (doc, layer); trivalent eval_pred UDF; ORD_NEGCYCLE, ORD_SYM, ORD_TRANS. Do NOT recompute closure on layer 0 (already 100% transitively closed; recomputing double-counts support) \u2014 compute it only for layers 2 and 3. | D14-16 (2.5d) LAYER 1 (gold trigger + predicted argument). Run the already-trained PAIE (C:\\Reseach_Quang\\PAIE_Maven) and/or DEEIA (C:\\Reseach_Quang\\DEEIA_Maven) in inference over train+valid with gold triggers; map decoder outputs onto the 143-role vocabulary; temperature-scale on the MINE split ONLY; ingest as layer_id=1. Mostly wall-clock and role-mapping glue, not new code. Note the asymmetry that makes this layer clean: entities AND TIMEX spans are given in every split, and layer 1 keeps gold triggers and gold ERE relations, so the noise is isolated to arguments alone. | D17-20 (3.5d) LAYER 2 + LAYER 3 (fully predicted). ED predictions from MAVEN_Arg_Pipeline for triggers; PAIE args on predicted triggers; a temporal-relation classifier trained on the MINE split ONLY, applied to within-document pairs (~254 pairs/doc, ~1.1M corpus-wide \u2014 same order as gold's 1.22M, so the layer is genuinely dense). Align predicted events to ckey by trigger char-offset (exact, then overlap) via node_align. Layer 3 = the same pipeline over the 857 blind-test docs, where no gold exists and only gold-free consistency metrics are reportable \u2014 the one place a circularity objection is not even formulable. | D21-22 (2d) DIFFERENTIAL ENGINE. Populate layer_diff by FULL OUTER JOIN of v_projection_input at layers 0 and L on (ckey_id, property, subject_id): MATCH / SPURIOUS / MISSING / ROLE_SWAP / TYPE_SWAP / TIME_SHIFT (delta_days) / GRAN_DROP / GRAN_REFINE / ANCHOR_LOST / TREL_FLIP. Populate conflict with scope, ref_layer_id and gold_status ('gold_clean' | 'gold_conflict' | 'no_gold'). gold_status IS the free label; this table is the paper's primary error track. | D22-23 (1.5d) GRANULARITY DETECTORS. GRAN_EMPTY via periodic-set intersection emptiness over each event's anchor set; GRAN_NONNEST via subset-chain test; GRAN_DRIFT and GRAN_DROP as differential queries on layer_diff. Assert on the 17,102 multi-anchor L0 events that the overwhelming majority return CONSISTENT (nesting is the normal case) and report the exact benign/conflict/unknown split as a headline number. | D24 (1d) MaxSAT CULPRIT LOCALISER. Partial weighted MaxSAT per (doc, layer) in Z3: hard clauses = point/Allen axioms, soft clauses = extracted relations and anchors weighted by pointrel.w. Falsified soft clauses fill culprit_stmt_id / culprit_anchor / culprit_tlayer_id. Enables culprit-accuracy@1 alongside detection F1, and handles `disjoint`, which sits outside ORD-Horn. | D25 (1d) INTERFACE HARDENING AND RELEASE. Write CONTRACT.md with the six guarantees; ship the pytest contract-test file; export Parquet partitioned by layer_id; freeze and SHA-256 constraints_L0_MINE.jsonl; write the datasheet, split manifests and a 'Claims Supported / Claims Not Supported' subsection. Engine story stated with measured scale: Parquet as canonical release format + DuckDB for aggregation + DuckPGQ (SQL/PGQ, SQL:2023) only for BEFORE-closure reachability; optional pyoxigraph quad store if an RDF 1.2 release is wanted, since named graphs then carry the layers for free. Explicitly NOT Kuzu (repository archived 10 Oct 2025) and NOT rdflib at low-millions triples. | CUT LINE, if behind on day 18: drop layer 2's trel_clf and ship layers 0 + 1 + 3 only. That costs GRAN_DRIFT and TREL_FLIP and reduces the ordering track to a single-layer STP baseline, but keeps the noise dial (gold vs predicted arguments), the differential argument diff, GRAN_EMPTY, and the whole Phase-3/4 interface. Salvage cost ~17 person-days.\n\nRISKS: The offset bridge fails on some documents. MAVEN-Arg gives character offsets into `document`; MAVEN-ERE gives (sent_id, token) offsets. If detokenisation does not reproduce the document string byte-for-byte on some docs, those documents cannot join arguments to temporal anchors at all and silently vanish from every layer. Mitigation: build tokmap on day 1, assert per-document 100% coverage, and publish the exact count of dropped documents rather than discovering it in October. A corpus-level char-alignment mismatch is the single failure that could sink Phase 2. | Layer 2 depends on inference runs completing on someone else's checkpoints. The repos are 3-9 GB zips and there is a 1.4 GB pytorch_model.bin loose in the working directory; if a checkpoint is missing, mismatched, or unrunnable, layer 2 evaporates and with it the headline gold-to-predicted transfer experiment. Mitigation: smoke-run PAIE and DEEIA inference on 20 documents in week 1, before writing any layer-2 ingest code, and treat the cut line as a real plan rather than a fallback fiction. | Confidence is claimed but uncalibrated. PAIE/DEEIA/SCPRG softmax scores are not probabilities, and 'confidence-weighted conflict detection' with uncalibrated numbers is a soft claim a reviewer can dismiss in one sentence. Mitigation: temperature-scale on the MINE split, report reliability diagrams and ECE per layer, and if calibration fails, demote to rank-only weights and say so explicitly. | Alignment of predicted to gold events is itself noisy, and the noise propagates into the labels. Trigger-offset matching produces partial overlaps, boundary disagreements and many-to-one collisions. Every 'free' label in layer_diff inherits that error. Mitigation: store align_method and align_conf, report all headline differential numbers restricted to align_method='trigger_offset_exact', and give the overlap-matched rows as a sensitivity analysis rather than folding them in. | TIMEX normalisation has zero gold supervision, so the granularity track rests on an unevaluable component. Every GRAN_EMPTY could be a normalisation error rather than a data conflict \u2014 precisely the 7.2% bucket Kontrast measured. Mitigation: hard-enforce trivalence (UNKNOWN never becomes CONFLICT), hand-label ~200 TIMEX values for a normalisation accuracy figure with a confidence interval, and always report culprit_tlayer_id so a reviewer can see how much of the conflict mass is blamed on refprop rather than regex. | Gold DATE/DURATION typing errors manufacture false granularity conflicts in bulk. Verified instance: doc 0371bbf116422f8e3a0a853bdd1962aa has '14 years' typed DATE, and ten events in that one document share the identical anchor pair with '1996'. One annotation slip becomes ten false conflicts. Mitigation: is_anchorable on the timex row, a duration-surface regex independent of tx_type, and report the count of mistyped TIMEX as a finding. | Three layers means three chances to leak. Any statistic fitted on EVAL documents \u2014 the temperature scale, the reference-time propagator's tuning, the trel classifier, the pseudo-entity threshold \u2014 silently poisons the evaluation, and unlike entity leakage (impossible by construction: 0 of 68,348 entities appear in >1 document) this leakage is invisible. Mitigation: every fitting step reads split_MINE.txt and only split_MINE.txt, and a contract test greps the build scripts for EVAL doc ids. | The differential design measures errors, not conflicts, and a reviewer may say so. Most layer_diff rows are extraction mistakes (a wrong Victim span) rather than temporal contradictions. If the paper conflates the two, the contribution reads as an extraction error analysis wearing a conflict-detection costume. Mitigation: report contradiction and incompleteness in separate columns everywhere (the audit already justifies it: ~72 contradictions versus 1,638 subevent gaps), and gate the conflict tables on the temporal detectors, never on the argument diff. | Scope creep across four detectors and four conflict subtypes with 25 days of work in a 45-day window shared with two dependent phases. The realistic failure is not a wrong design, it is layer 2 landing on day 30 and Vinh and Nhan having built against a moving schema. Mitigation: freeze v_projection_input and the six guarantees on day 10 and treat the schema as append-only thereafter, even at the cost of an ugly column. | Base rate makes any careless metric meaningless. ~72 true conflicts in 843,808 pairs is ~8.5e-5, so ROC-AUC will look near-perfect and mean nothing. Mitigation: never report accuracy or AUROC; report the base rate as the random-baseline precision, Precision@{50,100,500,1000}, R-Precision, AUPRC, and two-tier recall (exact on injected/system-error sets, stratified-sampling interval estimate with confidence intervals on the natural graph). | Positioning risk that is fatal if missed rather than merely costly. Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959) and Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025) must both be cited and differentiated in the submitted draft, not discovered at review time; and Euzenat plus Bettini/Wang/Jajodia mean no formal-novelty claim about granularity can be made at all. Mitigation: write the related-work paragraph in week 1, while there is still time for the design to move if the differentiation turns out to be thin.\n\nGIVES UP: Versus a GRANULARITY-FIRST angle: this design stores everything granularity needs (precision codes, periodic sets, four endpoints, per-anchor derivation) but spends its remaining budget on layers rather than on temporal semantics. It stays in resolve-then-STP and never enters multi-granularity TCSP, so no contribution is made to the Bettini/Wang/Jajodia line, no granularity-conversion operators in Euzenat's sense are implemented, and non-convex or fuzzy interval treatments are deliberately refused. Concretely, GRAN_EMPTY on ~17,102 multi-anchor events is one detector plus one baseline here, whereas a granularity-first angle would ship the human-verified precision study and the three-way benign/conflict/unknown benchmark that is plausibly a larger contribution than any detector.\n\nVersus a PURE-GOLD, deeper-mining angle: three layers cost roughly 8 of the 25 person-days (layer 1 at 2.5, layer 2/3 at 3.5, differential engine at 2) and buy no better constraints on gold. If Nhan's mining underperforms, this design has no fallback story of the form 'but the gold graph is richer than anything published'. It also multiplies the ingest surface threefold, and every schema mistake is now paid for three times.\n\nVersus a BENCHMARK-FIRST angle: the differential labels are free but they are model-error labels, not human-adjudicated conflict labels. No pooled blinded adjudication, no Krippendorff's alpha, no four-way {genuine / constraint-wrong / incomplete / undecidable} annotation, no released guidelines are produced by Phase 2 as scoped here. Those are the things that make a resource paper reviewer-proof, and this design hands them to Vinh's annotation tooling as a dependency rather than delivering them.\n\nHard scope exclusions, stated so nobody expects them: no cross-document entity or event coreference (entities are strictly document-local \u2014 0 of 68,348 recur \u2014 so the ECKG is a union of document-local graphs and constraints must be typed at the (EventType, Role) level; xCoRe and the 2025 CD-ECR line are cited as considered-and-deferred). No new extraction model and no improvement to Phase 1's numbers; the extractors are re-purposed as an error generator, which is a legitimate role but not a modelling contribution. No differential anything on the 857 blind-test documents, because there is no gold to diff against \u2014 layer 3 supports only gold-free consistency metrics (uniqueness, symmetry, transitivity violation rates in the Kougia et al. / Joint Constrained Learning vocabulary). No transaction time in the database sense, and therefore no claim on the bitemporal literature beyond borrowing its second axis. No RDF release on the critical path; the reified view and the optional RDF 1.2 / qualifier serialisation are day-25 nice-to-haves that get cut first. And no formal-novelty claim about granularity conflict, ordering conflict, or Allen-algebra consistency \u2014 all three are occupied ground, and the claim here is confined to the multi-version artefact and the differential, provenance-attributed evaluation built on it.\"", "B-nary-hypergraph": "DESIGN: HEDGE \u2014 Hyperedge Event\u2013Datum Graph with Explicit Granularity, mined with HyPaL (Hyperedge Pattern Language)\nStore every MAVEN event as ONE time-bearing hyperedge over its declared typed role frame plus an anchor MULTISET with per-anchor precision codes, never project to binary triples as the store, and mine constraints in HyPaL \u2014 an n-ary pattern language with slot-nullity, slot-cardinality, set-relational and k-wise-anchor atoms that PaTeCon's SP(a)/SP(b) binary language provably cannot express and that any one-interval-per-statement projection provably destroys.\n\nCORE IDEA: MAVEN-Arg's native on-disk format is ALREADY a hyperedge, and Phase 2 has been planning to throw that away. Verified on C:\\Reseach_Quang\\EE\\MAVEN-Arg\\train.jsonl: each event record is {id, type, type_id, mention[], argument{ROLE -> [filler,...]}} \u2014 argument is a DICT keyed by role whose values are LISTS. That is exactly a typed role-slot hyperedge: one edge, k labelled slots, each slot set-valued. Three further measured properties make the hyperedge the right primitive rather than a stylistic preference:\n\n(1) ROLE FRAMES ARE DECLARED, NOT EMERGENT. Role key sets are fixed per event type. Measured over the first 400 train docs: every Killing carries exactly {Killer, Cause, Victim, Location, Instrument} (297/297 each key present); every Attack carries {Agent, Patient, Location, Consequence} (398 each); Process_start {Agent, Patient, Location, Symbol, Purpose} (378 each); Military_operation {Agent, Patient, Location} (426 each). So MAVEN-Arg ships a per-type ARITY SIGNATURE \u2014 a frame catalogue \u2014 that a triple store has no slot for.\n\n(2) 37.7% OF DECLARED SLOTS ARE EMPTY (17,463 of 46,303 in the 400-doc sample). Filled-arity for Killing is distributed {0:3, 1:9, 2:45, 3:161, 4:74, 5:5}. A declared-but-empty slot is a POSITIVE fact (\"the annotators saw no Victim\") and it is the correct denominator for any confidence over that role. Under projection an empty slot and an unannotated slot are both \"no triple\" \u2014 the information is destroyed, not merely awkward. This is the single largest measured loss.\n\n(3) 5.8% OF FILLED SLOTS ARE SET-VALUED (2,679/46,303 have >1 filler), and role slots co-depend: Location_original and Location_final occur 698 and 698 times \u2014 always jointly. Set identity and joint slot occupancy are hyperedge properties.\n\n(4) ARITY-0 HYPEREDGES EXIST AND MATTER. Hostile_encounter has 2,024 mentions in MAVEN_ERE/train.jsonl and ZERO events in MAVEN-Arg train; 9 event types are absent from MAVEN-Arg entirely; 3,649 of 67,984 ERE train events (5.4%) have no MAVEN-Arg counterpart at all. These are time-bearing, relation-bearing hyperedges with an EMPTY slot set. A projection whose only vertex-of-interest is an argument filler makes 5.4% of the temporal graph disappear from the mining index; HEDGE keeps them as arity-0 edges that still participate in ordering constraints.\n\n(5) TIME IS A PROPERTY OF THE EDGE, AND THE ANCHOR SET IS ITSELF N-ARY. Following EventKG/SEM (sem:hasBeginTimeStamp on the event resource), time hangs on the hyperedge \u2014 the team already diagnosed that event-as-subject projection makes all predicates share one interval. But HEDGE goes one step past EventKG: the event carries its ANCHOR MULTISET with per-anchor precision codes, not just a resolved interval. 17,102 events have >=2 CONTAINS anchors. Any projection that writes one (start, finish) pair per statement collapses that multiset and the per-anchor precisions with it, which is precisely the machinery the granularity conflict type needs.\n\nThe payoff is a THEOREM, not an aesthetic. One-dimensional intervals have Helly number 2: pairwise non-empty intersection implies k-wise non-empty. So if every anchor were an interval, binary testing would suffice and Angle B would buy nothing for granularity. Periodic sets and metric width (DURATION) constraints break Helly. I measured the prevalence on the multi-anchor events in MAVEN_ERE train+valid (17,274 events with >=2 anchors of any anchoring type, consistent with the stated 17,102 CONTAINS-only figure): 41.9% (7,235 events) carry at least one DURATION anchor, giving a width constraint f-b in [d_lo,d_hi] that is not an interval on the timeline; 15.9% (2,742 events) carry a yearless-date or weekday anchor, which is a minimal periodic set, not an interval; the ('YEAR','YEARLESS') co-anchoring family alone is 909 events and ('OTHER','YEAR','YEARLESS') is 448. So the non-Helly families are the MAJORITY of multi-anchor events, and pairwise granularity testing is provably insufficient on the majority of the substrate. That is a defensible, measured, representation-level result that holds whatever the mining numbers turn out to be.\n\nHEDGE therefore inverts the project's Phase 2/3 relationship: the hypergraph is the store, and PaTeCon's reified eVertex/sVertex graph becomes a MATERIALISED VIEW that Phase 2 generates, shipped alongside a machine-readable projection_loss table quantifying exactly which HyPaL atoms the view cannot carry. Phase 3 stops being a lossy conversion step and becomes serialisation-plus-baseline-generation; PaTeCon runs unchanged as the baseline; the loss table is a paper table.\n\nNODE/EDGE MODEL: FORMAL OBJECT. HEDGE is a 6-tuple H = (V, E, I, E2, A, T) with a layer function L over every tuple.\n\nV = V_ent \u222a V_span \u222a V_timex \u222a {\u22a5}\n  V_ent    : 68,348 document-local entities, 7 types (Location, Other, Organization, Person, Product, Building, Art). Provided in EVERY split including blind test. 16,284 participate in >=2 events (mean 3.15, max 59). ZERO cross-document entities (0/68,348) \u2014 every vertex is document-local by construction, so every pattern match is intra-document.\n  V_span   : bare {content, offset} argument fillers with no entity_id \u2014 59.9% of the 236,937 arguments. First-class vertices, flagged vkind='SPAN', deliberately NOT coreferenced.\n  V_timex  : 20,827 gold TIMEX spans (9,287 distinct surfaces), types DATE/DURATION/TIME/PREPOSTEXP. Provided in every split.\n  \u22a5        : the single null vertex, incident to every DECLARED-BUT-EMPTY slot. This is the vertex a triple store cannot have.\n\nE = 80,479 event hyperedges (79,740 carry both an ERE id and an Arg frame; 3,649/67,984 = 5.4% of ERE train events are frameless arity-0 edges). An event is ONE edge, not a node with edges hanging off it. Event coreference is already resolved inside the edge: a hyperedge carries trigger_n mentions (the sample Motion event has 5: \"Expedition\"/\"venture\"/\"expedition\"/\"Expedition\"/\"expedition\").\n\nI \u2286 E \u00d7 Role \u00d7 \u2115 \u00d7 V is a FOUR-PLACE POSITIONED LABELLED INCIDENCE RELATION, not a two-place incidence. I(e, r, p, v) reads \"vertex v occupies position p of slot r of hyperedge e\". The 4-placeness is the whole design: role labels give typed slots (143 roles), positions make slots ordered multisets, and \u22a5 in the v position encodes nullity. A conventional hypergraph (2-place incidence, edges are vertex sets) would already lose the roles; a bipartite \"incidence graph\" encoding of it would already be the projection we are refusing.\n\nFRAME. F : EventType \u2192 2^Role, induced from data (not from an ontology): F(Killing) = {Killer, Cause, Victim, Location, Instrument}; F(Attack) = {Agent, Patient, Location, Consequence}; F(Process_start) = {Agent, Patient, Location, Symbol, Purpose}; F(Military_operation) = {Agent, Patient, Location}; F(Hostile_encounter) = \u2205. 162 types have a frame in MAVEN-Arg train; 9 ERE types (incl. Hostile_encounter) have none. Every hyperedge is required to be frame-complete: for every r in F(type(e)) there is at least one I-tuple, with v = \u22a5 if the slot is empty. This is the invariant that makes null_signature computable and confidence denominators correct.\n\nE2 \u2286 E \u00d7 RelType \u00d7 E \u2014 RELATIONS BETWEEN HYPEREDGES, i.e. relations between relations (the Equivalence Theorem preprint's fourth capability; cite as motivation only). RelType = {BEFORE 1.04M, CONTAINS 152K, SIMULTANEOUS 9.9K, OVERLAP 9.9K, BEGINS-ON 639, ENDS-ON 380, CAUSE 46,014, PRECONDITION, SUBEVENT 12,019}. In a triple store these need a statement node; in a hypergraph they are simply edges whose endpoints are edges \u2014 a second-order edge layer, no reification required.\n\nA \u2286 E \u00d7 AnchorType \u00d7 V_timex is a MULTISET-valued anchoring, AnchorType = {CONTAINS 66,418 (~99% of anchoring mass), SIMULTANEOUS 496, BEGINS-ON 122, ENDS-ON 39}. |A(e)| >= 2 for 17,102 events (2 anchors: 10,653; 3: 3,707; >=4: 2,742). Keeping A as a multiset ON THE EDGE, rather than resolving it eagerly to one interval, is what keeps granularity decidable. Theoretical backing: Stubbs & Pustejovsky's narrative containers \u2014 a multi-anchor event is an event placed in NESTED containers, so nesting is the normal case and the conflict test must be emptiness, never precision-difference.\n\nT : E \u2192 TBox (see interval_representation). Derived from A, never a substitute for it \u2014 both are stored.\n\nL : every I-, E2-, A-, T-tuple carries layer \u2208 {gold, rule:<version>, pred:<model>@<ckpt>} and conf. This is the bitemporal second axis (ADBIS 2025 \"Bitemporal Property Graphs\"), repurposed as a DERIVATION axis rather than transaction time, with DEC's cognitive-worlds semantics (arXiv 2606.15246) for a predicted layer that can be inconsistent without destabilising the gold factual core. It is the mechanism that de-orphans Phase 1: the same hypergraph holds gold and PAIE/DEEIA/SCPRG-predicted layers, and \"do HyPaL constraints mined on gold transfer to the predicted layer?\" is a WHERE clause, not a new pipeline.\n\nWHAT PROJECTION DESTROYS \u2014 stated honestly in two tiers, because the strong version of the claim is only true of one tier.\nTIER 1, GENUINELY DESTROYED (unrecoverable from the projected graph at any cost, because the target format has no place to put it):\n  a. Slot nullity. 37.7% of declared slots. Projection emits no triple; \u22a5 is gone; the frame is gone, so you cannot even enumerate which roles were declared.\n  b. Frame arity / filled arity. Killing's {0:3,1:9,2:45,3:161,4:74,5:5} distribution is a per-edge property with no triple-level home.\n  c. The anchor multiset and per-anchor precision. The mandated projection (entity_x, \"EventType#Role\", event_e, T_e) writes ONE interval T_e per statement. All per-anchor precision codes and the DURATION width constraints vanish. 17,102 events affected; 7,235 of them carry a width constraint that is not an interval and therefore has no representation at all.\n  d. Arity-0 hyperedges. 5.4% of ERE events have no argument filler, so they generate no (entity, property, event) statement and are absent from the projected mining index while still carrying BEFORE/CONTAINS edges.\nTIER 2, RECOVERABLE BY AGGREGATION BUT NOT EXPRESSIBLE IN PaTeCon'S LANGUAGE: slot cardinality, set-level disjointness/subsumption between two slots, and any pattern binding >=3 argument positions simultaneously. PaTeCon's SP(a) is \"one subject, two temporal statements\" and SP(b) is \"two subjects joined by an atemporal property\": the pattern language is conjunctive, purely binary, aggregation-free, and admits at most two non-time entity variables. card(), sdisj() and 3-position patterns are outside it by construction, not by oversight.\n\nBeing explicit about the two tiers is what makes the claim survive review. The Tier-1 items are the ones we put in the projection_loss table with row counts.\n\nSCHEMA: Canonical format: Parquet (columnar, projection pushdown, zero server, standard dataset-release format) under eckg/v1/, attached read-only in DuckDB; DuckPGQ (SQL/PGQ, SQL:2023, PVLDB 2023) used ONLY for BEFORE-closure reachability. NOT Kuzu \u2014 repository archived 10 Oct 2025, a reproducibility liability a reviewer can check in ten seconds. Total store ~2.5M rows / well under 1 GB, so the ~4x standard-reification blow-up (Hernandez, Hogan & Kr\u00f6tzsch, SSWS 2015) is irrelevant here and we say so: representation fidelity is free at this scale, therefore choose the most expressive model.\n\n-- T1  event_frame : the induced frame catalogue (162 typed frames + 9 frameless types)\nCREATE TABLE event_frame (\n  event_type      VARCHAR NOT NULL,   -- 'Killing'\n  type_id         SMALLINT,\n  role            VARCHAR NOT NULL,   -- 'Victim'\n  role_id         SMALLINT,           -- 0..142 over the 143 MAVEN-Arg roles\n  role_ordinal    TINYINT NOT NULL,   -- canonical slot index within the frame (bit position)\n  declared_freq   INTEGER,            -- #events of this type where the key is present\n  filled_freq     INTEGER,            -- #events where the slot is non-empty\n  null_rate       FLOAT,              -- 1 - filled_freq/declared_freq\n  is_set_valued   BOOLEAN,            -- observed max_card > 1\n  max_card        TINYINT,\n  paired_with     VARCHAR,            -- 'Location_final' for 'Location_original' (698/698)\n  PRIMARY KEY (event_type, role));\n\n-- T2  event_hyperedge : ONE ROW PER EVENT = one hyperedge\nCREATE TABLE event_hyperedge (\n  eid             VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY,  -- 'EVENT_f535eb21f8f958a04cbc69c1d1521186' (shared Arg/ERE id)\n  doc_id          VARCHAR NOT NULL,\n  split           VARCHAR NOT NULL,     -- 'mine' | 'eval'  (DOCUMENT-disjoint; exact, since 0/68,348 entities cross docs)\n  event_type      VARCHAR NOT NULL,\n  frame_arity     TINYINT NOT NULL,     -- |F(type)|; 0 for Hostile_encounter and the other 8 frameless types\n  filled_arity    TINYINT NOT NULL,     -- # non-empty slots\n  slot_signature  BIGINT NOT NULL,      -- bitmask over role_ordinal of FILLED slots\n  null_signature  BIGINT NOT NULL,      -- bitmask of DECLARED-BUT-EMPTY slots  <-- Tier-1 information\n  trigger_n       TINYINT,              -- event-coref cluster size (sample Motion event: 5)\n  head_trigger    VARCHAR,\n  head_char_lo    INTEGER, head_char_hi INTEGER,   -- MAVEN-Arg CHARACTER offsets\n  sent_id         INTEGER, tok_lo INTEGER, tok_hi INTEGER,  -- MAVEN-ERE token coords\n  has_arg_frame   BOOLEAN NOT NULL,     -- FALSE for the 5.4% ERE-only events\n  layer           VARCHAR NOT NULL, conf FLOAT);\n\n-- T3  slot_incidence : THE 4-PLACE INCIDENCE RELATION. This is not a triple table.\nCREATE TABLE slot_incidence (\n  eid           VARCHAR NOT NULL,\n  role          VARCHAR NOT NULL,\n  role_ordinal  TINYINT NOT NULL,\n  pos           TINYINT NOT NULL,   -- position WITHIN the slot, 0..slot_card-1\n  slot_card     TINYINT NOT NULL,   -- 0 => is_null\n  is_null       BOOLEAN NOT NULL,   -- TRUE <=> declared slot with no filler (vid = '_BOT_')\n  vid           VARCHAR NOT NULL,   -- '_BOT_' for the null vertex\n  vkind         VARCHAR NOT NULL,   -- 'ENT' | 'SPAN' | 'TIMEX'\n  ent_type      VARCHAR,            -- Person | Organization | Location | ...\n  char_lo INTEGER, char_hi INTEGER, surface VARCHAR,\n  layer VARCHAR NOT NULL, conf FLOAT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (eid, role, pos));\n-- ~236,937 filler rows + ~143,000 null rows ~= 380K rows.\n\n-- T4  vertex\nCREATE TABLE vertex (\n  vid VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR, vkind VARCHAR,\n  ent_type VARCHAR, canonical_surface VARCHAR,\n  n_mentions SMALLINT, n_events SMALLINT,   -- n_events>=2 for 16,284 entities, max 59\n  timex_type VARCHAR, is_cross_doc BOOLEAN); -- always FALSE; asserted, not assumed\n\n-- T5  timex_norm : two-stage HeidelTime-style normalisation, TIMEX3-conformant output\nCREATE TABLE timex_norm (\n  tid VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR, surface VARCHAR, timex_type VARCHAR,\n  cir           VARCHAR,   -- STAGE 1 context-independent rep: 'UNDEF-year-11-11', 'UNDEF-REF-next-day', 'P10Y'\n  value_t3      VARCHAR,   -- STAGE 2 ISO-8601 profile w/ placeholders: '1778', 'XXXX-11-11', 'P10Y', 'XXXX-WXX-7'\n  prec          TINYINT,   -- Wikibase-style: 6 millennium, 7 century, 8 decade, 9 year, 10 month, 11 day\n  kind          VARCHAR,   -- 'INTERVAL' | 'PERIODIC' | 'DURATION' | 'UNRESOLVED'\n  jd_lo INTEGER, jd_hi INTEGER,             -- day-chronon Julian bounds (INTERVAL only)\n  pset_period INTEGER, pset_offsets BLOB,   -- minimal periodic set (Bettini & Mascetti), PERIODIC only\n  dur_lo INTEGER, dur_hi INTEGER,           -- width bounds in days, DURATION only\n  anchor_tid VARCHAR, temporal_fn BOOLEAN,  -- TIMEX3 anchorTimeID / temporalFunction\n  norm_method VARCHAR,  -- 'regex:<rule_id>' | 'refprop' | 'gazetteer' | 'unresolved'\n  layer VARCHAR, conf FLOAT);\n\n-- T6  anchor : event -> timex, a MULTISET per event. THE granularity substrate.\nCREATE TABLE anchor (\n  eid VARCHAR, tid VARCHAR, anchor_type VARCHAR,  -- CONTAINS | SIMULTANEOUS | BEGINS-ON | ENDS-ON\n  prec TINYINT, kind VARCHAR, jd_lo INTEGER, jd_hi INTEGER,\n  width_days INTEGER, anchor_rank TINYINT,        -- rank by width, 0 = narrowest\n  layer VARCHAR, PRIMARY KEY (eid, tid, anchor_type));\n\n-- T7  tbox : the resolved k-wise solution of the anchor set, one row per hyperedge\nCREATE TABLE tbox (\n  eid VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY,\n  b_lo INTEGER, b_hi INTEGER, f_lo INTEGER, f_hi INTEGER,  -- 4-endpoint quadruple, Julian days\n  w_lo INTEGER, w_hi INTEGER,                              -- width bounds\n  prec_min TINYINT, prec_max TINYINT, n_anchors TINYINT,\n  status VARCHAR,      -- 'TIGHT' (44.9%) | 'LOOSE' (27.2%) | 'UNANCHORED' (27.9%) | 'PERIODIC_UNRESOLVED'\n  chain_ok BOOLEAN,    -- anchor multiset is a subset-chain => benign nesting\n  kwise_sat VARCHAR,   -- 'SAT' | 'UNSAT' | 'UNKNOWN'   (trivalent, per PaTeCon)\n  unsat_core VARCHAR[],-- minimal set of tids witnessing UNSAT (the explanation)\n  core_kind VARCHAR);  -- 'INTERVAL' | 'PERIODIC' | 'METRIC_WIDTH' | 'MIXED'\n\n-- T8  event_relation : E2, edges over hyperedges\nCREATE TABLE event_relation (\n  eid_l VARCHAR, eid_r VARCHAR, rel VARCHAR,  -- 9 types incl. CAUSE/PRECONDITION/SUBEVENT\n  doc_id VARCHAR, allen_mask SMALLINT,        -- 13-bit Allen relation set\n  from_closure BOOLEAN,                       -- TRUE if added by transitive closure, not gold\n  layer VARCHAR, conf FLOAT,\n  PRIMARY KEY (eid_l, eid_r, rel, layer));\n\n-- T9  hyperedge_overlap : the n-ary join unit. 274,525 rows. Set-level atoms PRECOMPUTED.\nCREATE TABLE hyperedge_overlap (\n  eid_l VARCHAR, eid_r VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  type_l VARCHAR, type_r VARCHAR,\n  shared_vids VARCHAR[], n_shared TINYINT,\n  role_pairs VARCHAR[],           -- ['Killing.Killer~Military_operation.Agent', ...]\n  role_pair_mask BLOB,\n  slot_disjoint_pairs VARCHAR[],  -- role pairs whose filler SETS are disjoint  <-- set-level atom\n  slot_subsume_pairs VARCHAR[],   -- role pairs where set(l) subset-of set(r)\n  jaccard_all FLOAT,\n  gold_rel VARCHAR,               -- one of the 9, or NULL. Non-null for 139,093 (50.7%).\n  PRIMARY KEY (eid_l, eid_r));\n\n-- T10 hypattern : the constraint table. Schema owned by Phase 2, rows written by Phase 4.\nCREATE TABLE hypattern (\n  pat_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, body_atoms VARCHAR,   -- HyPaL s-expression\n  head_kind VARCHAR,     -- 'ALLEN' | 'MUTEX' | 'REQUIRE' | 'PREC' | 'WIDTH'\n  head_allen_mask SMALLINT, head_arg VARCHAR,\n  n_vars TINYINT, max_slot_positions TINYINT,\n  uses_null BOOLEAN, uses_card BOOLEAN, uses_setrel BOOLEAN, uses_kwise BOOLEAN, -- the 4 non-PaTeCon families\n  expressible_in_patecon BOOLEAN,     -- computed, not asserted\n  ordhorn BOOLEAN,                    -- head inside ORD-Horn?\n  supp_event INTEGER, supp_doc INTEGER, supp_activation INTEGER,\n  n_pos INTEGER, n_neg INTEGER, n_unknown INTEGER,\n  conf FLOAT, p_value FLOAT, q_value FLOAT,          -- Benjamini-Hochberg\n  atomic BOOLEAN, atomicity_p FLOAT,                 -- Martin et al. PVLDB 2025 test\n  mine_split VARCHAR);\n\n-- T11 token_index : offset reconciliation. MANDATORY and easy to get wrong.\nCREATE TABLE token_index (doc_id VARCHAR, tok_i INTEGER, sent_id INTEGER,\n  sent_tok_i INTEGER, char_lo INTEGER, char_hi INTEGER, surface VARCHAR,\n  PRIMARY KEY (doc_id, tok_i));\n-- MAVEN-Arg offsets are CHARACTER offsets over 'document' (verified: trigger \"Expedition\" at [4,14]);\n-- MAVEN-ERE offsets are (sent_id, sentence-local TOKEN indices) over 'tokens'/'sentences'\n-- (verified: TIMEX \"1860\" at sent_id 0, offset [24,25]). Nothing joins without this table.\n\n-- T12 projection_loss : the paper table. One row per Tier-1 loss incident.\nCREATE TABLE projection_loss (\n  eid VARCHAR, loss_kind VARCHAR,  -- 'NULL_SLOT' | 'FRAME_ARITY' | 'ANCHOR_MULTISET'\n                                   -- | 'PRECISION_CODE' | 'WIDTH_CONSTRAINT' | 'ARITY0_EDGE'\n  detail VARCHAR, n_items SMALLINT);\n\nWORKED INSTANCE (schematic, real types and role names):\n  event_hyperedge: eid=EVENT_a1..., type='Killing', frame_arity=5, filled_arity=3,\n    slot_signature=0b10101 (Killer, Victim, Location), null_signature=0b01010 (Cause, Instrument declared+empty),\n    has_arg_frame=TRUE, layer='gold'\n  slot_incidence:\n    (EVENT_a1, Killer,     ord 0, pos 0, card 1, is_null F, vid=ENT_org7,  vkind ENT, ent_type Organization)\n    (EVENT_a1, Cause,      ord 1, pos 0, card 0, is_null T, vid=_BOT_)          <-- destroyed by projection\n    (EVENT_a1, Victim,     ord 2, pos 0, card 2, is_null F, vid=ENT_per3,  ENT, Person)\n    (EVENT_a1, Victim,     ord 2, pos 1, card 2, is_null F, vid=SPAN_5512, SPAN)  <-- set-valued slot\n    (EVENT_a1, Location,   ord 3, pos 0, card 1, is_null F, vid=ENT_loc2,  ENT, Location)\n    (EVENT_a1, Instrument, ord 4, pos 0, card 0, is_null T, vid=_BOT_)\n  anchor: (EVENT_a1, TIME_x, CONTAINS, prec 9,  INTERVAL, 1778-01-01..1778-12-31, width 364, rank 1)\n          (EVENT_a1, TIME_y, CONTAINS, prec 10, PERIODIC, XXXX-11-11, period 365.25d,   rank 0)\n  tbox:   b=f=1778-11-11, prec_min 9, prec_max 10, n_anchors 2, status TIGHT,\n          chain_ok TRUE (periodic \u2229 interval = singleton refinement), kwise_sat SAT\n  hyperedge_overlap: (EVENT_a1, EVENT_b2 [Military_operation]) shared_vids=[ENT_org7],\n          role_pairs=['Killing.Killer~Military_operation.Agent'],\n          slot_disjoint_pairs=['Killing.Victim~Military_operation.Patient'], gold_rel='CONTAINS'\n\nINTERVALS: Three-layer time, all three persisted. Layer 1 is per-TIMEX and is the ONLY place granularity is observable; layer 2 is the anchor multiset on the edge; layer 3 is the derived solution. Eager collapse to layer 3 is what kills the granularity conflict type, so layers 1 and 2 are never discarded.\n\nLAYER 1 \u2014 PER-TIMEX NORMALISED VALUE (timex_norm). Two-stage, HeidelTime-style, TIMEX3-conformant.\n  Stage 1 (context-independent representation, `cir`): \"1778\" -> '1778'; \"November 11\" -> 'UNDEF-year-11-11'; \"the next day\" -> 'UNDEF-REF-plus-1-day'; \"ten years\" -> 'P10Y'; \"Sunday\" -> 'UNDEF-REF-weekday-7'; \"World War II\" -> 'GAZ:WWII'.\n  Stage 2 disambiguation. MAVEN is Wikipedia = NARRATIVE domain with NO document creation time, so DCT propagation is unavailable and the citable default is HeidelTime's narrative rule: reference time = last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE. This resolves the four measured tractable families in the 37.9% context-dependent bucket: relative dates (\"the next day\" x91, \"the following day\" x62, \"the same day\" x21), yearless dates (\"September 11\" x24, \"August 31\" x17), named periods (\"World War II\" x70, typed DURATION), weekdays (\"Sunday\" x21). Regex covers the 62.1% deterministic bucket (bare year 29.9%, day-month-year 9.1%, month+year 6.9%, N+unit 6.1%, month+day+year 4.6%, ranges/decades/centuries/deictic 5.6%).\n  Output is a tagged union over FOUR kinds, not one interval type:\n    INTERVAL : (jd_lo, jd_hi) + prec.        '1778' -> (JD(1778-01-01), JD(1778-12-31)), prec 9\n    PERIODIC : minimal periodic set (period, offsets) + prec. 'XXXX-11-11' -> {y-11-11 : y in Z}, period 1 year, prec 10. 'XXXX-WXX-7' -> every Sunday, period 7 days.\n    DURATION : (dur_lo, dur_hi) in days, prec NULL, NO position on the timeline. 'P10Y' -> [3650, 3653]. This constrains WIDTH, not location \u2014 it is 6.1% of normalisation mass and 33 of the 143 roles' 'Duration' instances.\n    UNRESOLVED : nothing resolved; propagates trivalent UNKNOWN and can NEVER produce a conflict. This is the guard against Kontrast's measured 7.2% normalisation-error bucket.\n  Representing PERIODIC as a minimal periodic set rather than an interval is the Bettini & Mascetti move, with canonical form + set operations + an emptiness test. Yearless dates and weekdays become first-class instead of discarded.\n\nLAYER 2 \u2014 ANCHOR MULTISET ON THE HYPEREDGE (anchor). A(e) is a multiset of (anchor_type, prec, kind, bounds) with an anchor_rank by width. Kept because granularity conflict is a property of the SET, not of any pair, and because the projection cannot hold it.\n\nLAYER 3 \u2014 TBox, THE FOUR-ENDPOINT QUADRUPLE PLUS WIDTH (tbox).\n  TBox(e) = <[b_lo, b_hi], [f_lo, f_hi], [w_lo, w_hi], prec_min, prec_max, status, kwise_sat, unsat_core>\n  over a DAY chronon in Julian day numbers, with +/-INF sentinels. Semantics from Dyreson & Snodgrass (TODS 1998) indeterminate instants; IE-side precedent from Reimers et al. (ACL 2016) day-granularity anchoring and the unified quadruple time-anchor representation (arXiv 2008.06452). The quadruple uniformly subsumes the measured three cases instead of special-casing them: TIGHT (44.9%, 37,852 events) = narrow b and f boxes; LOOSE (27.2%) = one side unbounded; UNANCHORED (27.9%) = both boxes (-INF, +INF) with only b_lo <= f_hi. The explicit width bounds [w_lo, w_hi] are the addition that no projection can carry, and they are needed by 7,235 events (41.9% of multi-anchor events) that have a DURATION anchor.\n  CRITICAL DESIGN RULE: down-cast then STP. Every RESOLVED anchor is converted to day-granularity constraints of the form b >= x, f <= y, f - b in [w_lo, w_hi], b <= f. That is a Simple Temporal Problem (Dechter, Meiri & Pearl 1991), decided COMPLETELY by Floyd-Warshall in O(n^3) with conflict witnessed by a negative cycle. Keeping unresolved TIMEXes as periodic sets inside the constraint network instead would put us in multi-granularity TCSP, which Bettini, Wang & Jajodia prove NP-hard. So: periodic sets are resolved by set intersection FIRST (a local, per-event operation over |A(e)| <= 6 anchors), and only the resolved result enters the STP. With 45 days, resolve-then-STP is the only viable path; periodic sets inside the network is future work. This is a stated, cited complexity decision, and almost no KG-conflict paper makes one.\n  GRANULARITY IS A PRECISION CODE, NEVER AN INFERRED WIDTH. prec is stored Wikibase-style (6 millennium ... 9 year, 10 month, 11 day) on every anchor. If '1778' were stored only as the interval [1778-01-01, 1778-12-31], granularity conflict would be indistinguishable from ordinary interval containment and the second conflict type would collapse into the first.\n\nORDERING CONFLICT: Ordering conflict is detected NATIVELY over hyperedges: bodies are n-ary HyPaL patterns, heads are Allen relation sets, and checking is point-algebra path consistency over the document's endpoint graph.\n\nHyPaL BODY ATOMS (12 constructors; the last four are the ones PaTeCon has no counterpart for):\n  1  type(x, T)                          -- hyperedge type\n  2  rel(x, rho, y)                      -- E2 atom over the 9 relation types\n  3  slot(x, r) contains v               -- membership\n  4  slotset(x, r) = S                   -- bind a SET variable (set-valued slot)\n  5  etype(x, r, tau)                    -- entity type on a slot (7 types)\n  6  allen(x, y) subset A                -- 13-bit Allen mask\n  7  width(x) in [lo, hi]                -- metric atom on TBox width\n  8  prec(x) op p / prec_gap(x) op g     -- precision atoms\n  9  null(x, r)                          -- DECLARED-BUT-EMPTY slot        [not expressible in PaTeCon; Tier-1 destroyed by projection]\n 10  card(x, r) op k                     -- slot cardinality               [not expressible in PaTeCon]\n 11  sdisj(x,r1,y,r2) / ssub(x,r1,y,r2) / seq(x,r1,y,r2)  -- SET-LEVEL relations between two slots  [not expressible in PaTeCon]\n 12  kwise(x) in {SAT,UNSAT,UNKNOWN}     -- k-wise anchor satisfiability   [Tier-1 destroyed by projection]\n\nHEAD PREDICATES: an Allen relation set (13 relations, OWL-Time vocabulary) for ordering; MUTEX (falsity) for mutual exclusion; REQUIRE(rho) for EXISTENCE heads (GGD-style, arXiv 2403.17082), which is how the 1,638/12,019 = 13.6% SUBEVENT pairs lacking an implied CONTAINS get reported as INCOMPLETENESS rather than contradiction \u2014 a distinction denial constraints and GEDs structurally cannot make. Default heads are restricted to the ORD-Horn subalgebra (Nebel & Burckert, JACM 1995) so path consistency is a COMPLETE cubic decision procedure; the `ordhorn` column records membership per pattern. PaTeCon's {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} is NOT inside ORD-Horn, because interval disjointness is (end1 < start2) OR (end2 < start1), a genuine disjunction of strict inequalities. We therefore ship two head vocabularies: ORD-Horn-only (complete, O(n^3), our default) and with-disjoint (routed to Z3 partial-weighted MaxSAT). Stating which of the 18 maximal tractable subalgebras (Krokhin, Jeavons & Jonsson, JACM 2003) the language occupies is one paragraph of free rigor.\n\nWORKED CONSTRAINT that PaTeCon structurally cannot state:\n  R1 (co-agent containment, with a set-level atom and three co-bound positions):\n    type(x, Killing) AND type(y, Military_operation)\n    AND NOT sdisj(x, Killer, y, Agent)          -- the killer set and the operation's agent set intersect\n    AND slot(x, Victim) contains v AND slot(y, Patient) contains v\n    AND card(x, Victim) >= 1\n    ==> allen(x, y) subset {d, s, f, e}          -- the killing is temporally inside the operation\n  This body binds THREE argument positions of two hyperedges plus a set-level atom. PaTeCon's SP(a) admits one subject with two temporal statements; SP(b) admits two subjects joined by one atemporal property. Neither can bind three positions, and neither has a set-level atom at all.\n  R2 (nullity-conditioned ordering \u2014 Tier-1, impossible after projection):\n    type(x, Process_start) AND null(x, Purpose) AND type(y, Attack)\n    AND slot(x, Agent) contains v AND slot(y, Agent) contains v\n    ==> allen(x, y) subset {<, m}                -- an unmotivated process start precedes the attack\n  After projection, null(x, Purpose) has no witness: no triple was emitted, the frame was not exported, and an unfilled slot is indistinguishable from an unannotated one across 37.7% of declared slots.\n  R3 (metric ordering head): type(x, Hostile_encounter) ==> width(x) in [0, 3650]. Note x is an ARITY-0 hyperedge (Hostile_encounter has an empty frame; 2,024 mentions in ERE train, 0 in MAVEN-Arg), so it generates no (entity, \"EventType#Role\", event) statement and is INVISIBLE to a projected miner while still carrying BEFORE and CONTAINS edges.\n\nMINING (what Phase 4 runs, over the interface Phase 2 hands it): candidate bodies are enumerated from hyperedge_overlap (274,525 rows, of which 139,093 = 50.7% carry a gold relation) stratified by the measured signature layers \u2014 2,708 (T1,T2) signatures at support >=10 covering 81% of instances; 2,688 (T1,r1,T2,r2) signatures at >=10 covering 59%; then extended with the four new atom families. Support is counted with THREE explicit denominators (supp_event, supp_doc, supp_activation) because entities are strictly document-local: 0 of 68,348 entities recur across documents, so support can never be counted over recurring individuals the way PaTeCon counts it over Wikidata entities. supp_activation is the DECLARE/MINERful activation denominator \u2014 fulfilments divided by occurrences of the activation \u2014 and the null_signature is what makes that denominator computable. Trivalent counts (n_pos/n_neg/n_unknown) never mix. Thresholds are replaced by Benjamini-Hochberg FDR over the candidate space plus the Martin et al. (PVLDB 2025) Beta-Bernoulli atomicity test at alpha=0.01; both operate on precomputed columns so Phase 4 never rescans the corpus.\n\nDETECTION (Phase 5, using the checker Phase 2 ships): per document, build the point graph over 2|E| endpoints from (i) tbox bounds, (ii) E2 Allen atoms decomposed into start/end point comparisons (Fan & Strube's reduction), (iii) mined HyPaL heads instantiated on matched bodies. Floyd-Warshall; a negative cycle is a conflict; the minimal negative cycle plus a hitting-set pass over the involved atoms gives the MINIMAL EXPLANATION and hence culprit localisation, which is a strictly better deliverable than a binary conflict flag. Cost is trivial: 80,479/4,480 = ~18 events per document and max 59 events per entity, so |E| per document is tiny. This is the engineering point that makes Angle B affordable: because MAVEN entities are strictly document-local, native n-ary pattern matching and complete consistency checking are BOTH intra-document and therefore cheap. The property that looks like a limitation is what pays for the expressiveness.\n\nHonest note on the target: gold is essentially conflict-free (0 BEFORE cycles over 843,808 pairs, 100% transitive closure, ~72 genuine contradictions corpus-wide), by construction of MAVEN-ERE's timeline annotation scheme. So the ordering detector's evaluation must live on the PREDICTED layer (`layer LIKE 'pred:%'`), which is a WHERE clause thanks to the derivation axis, and on the independently-calibrated normaliser-level perturbation set \u2014 never on constraint-negating injection.\n\nGRANULARITY CONFLICT: This is where Angle B earns its cost, and the argument is a theorem plus a prevalence measurement rather than an intuition.\n\nTHE HELLY ARGUMENT. One-dimensional intervals have Helly number 2: if a family of intervals intersects pairwise, it intersects k-wise. Consequence: IF every anchor were a plain interval, the k-wise granularity test would be exactly equivalent to all pairwise tests, and the native n-ary representation would buy nothing. Two anchor kinds break Helly:\n  (a) PERIODIC SETS (yearless dates, weekdays) \u2014 not convex, so pairwise non-empty does not imply k-wise non-empty.\n  (b) METRIC WIDTH constraints (DURATION) \u2014 a constraint on f - b, not a subset of the timeline at all, so it has no interval representation to intersect pairwise.\nMEASURED PREVALENCE on the multi-anchor events of MAVEN_ERE train+valid (17,274 events with >=2 anchors of any anchoring type, consistent with the stated 17,102 CONTAINS-only figure): 41.9% (7,235 events) carry at least one DURATION anchor; 15.9% (2,742 events) carry a yearless-date or weekday anchor; the year+yearless co-anchoring family alone is 909 events and the three-kind family (other + year + yearless) is 448. So the non-Helly families are the MAJORITY of multi-anchor events. Pairwise granularity testing \u2014 which is all any binary-triple projection with one interval per statement can do \u2014 is provably insufficient on the majority of the substrate. That result stands independently of whether any constraint mining succeeds.\n\nTHE WORKED WITNESS (arithmetically checkable, and the figure for the paper). An event with three anchors:\n  a1 = \"1778\"        -> INTERVAL [1778-01-01, 1778-12-31], prec 9\n  a2 = \"November 11\" -> PERIODIC {y-11-11 : y in Z}, period 1 year, prec 10\n  a3 = \"Sunday\"      -> PERIODIC every-7-days, prec 11\nPairwise: a1 \u2229 a2 = {1778-11-11}, non-empty. a1 \u2229 a3 = the 52 Sundays of 1778, non-empty. a2 \u2229 a3 = {1770-11-11, 1781-11-11, 1787-11-11, ...}, non-empty. All three pairwise tests PASS. But 1778-11-11 was a WEDNESDAY (verified), so the three-way intersection is EMPTY. A pairwise detector reports no conflict; the k-wise detector reports UNSAT with core {a1, a2, a3} and core_kind='PERIODIC'. No amount of binary-triple machinery finds this.\nSECOND WITNESS, the commoner family (7,235 events): a1 = \"1778\" gives f - b <= 364; a2 = \"ten years\" gives f - b in [3650, 3653]. Both individually satisfiable, jointly UNSAT, core_kind='METRIC_WIDTH'. Under the mandated projection (entity, \"EventType#Role\", event, T_e) the width constraint has NO representation whatsoever \u2014 it is Tier-1 destroyed, not merely inexpressible.\n\nTHE DETECTOR (native, k-wise, trivalent).\n  Input: A(e), the anchor multiset of one hyperedge, |A(e)| in 2..6.\n  Build the constraint system over (b, f) in day chronons:\n    CONTAINS anchor a:      b >= a.jd_lo AND f <= a.jd_hi      (event inside its container)\n    SIMULTANEOUS anchor a:  b >= a.jd_lo AND f <= a.jd_hi AND width match\n    BEGINS-ON a:            b in a;   ENDS-ON a: f in a        (rare: 122 and 39 \u2014 reported, not learned)\n    PERIODIC anchor a:      b in pset(a) AND f in pset(a)      (resolved by periodic-set intersection FIRST)\n    DURATION anchor a:      f - b in [a.dur_lo, a.dur_hi]\n  Solve: canonical-form periodic-set intersection (Bettini & Mascetti) to reduce PERIODIC anchors, then the residual interval + width system as an STP (Floyd-Warshall, complete, O(n^3), n <= 12 endpoints/constraints per event \u2014 microseconds).\n  Return kwise_sat in {SAT, UNSAT, UNKNOWN} with a minimal unsat_core and core_kind.\n  Any anchor with norm_method='unresolved' forces UNKNOWN. An unresolved yearless date must NEVER produce a conflict \u2014 that is exactly Kontrast's measured 7.2% normalisation-error bucket.\n\nTHREE-WAY ADJUDICATION, which is the actual contribution (Kontrast inverted).\n  BENIGN REFINEMENT \u2014 chain_ok = TRUE: the anchor multiset is a subset-chain, or periodic \u2229 interval yields a strictly narrower singleton. \"in 1778\" + \"on November 11\" -> {1778-11-11}. This is the NORMAL case: multi-anchor events are Stubbs & Pustejovsky narrative containers, nested by design. A detector that flagged \"event has >=2 anchors at differing precision\" would be almost entirely false positives across all 17,102 events \u2014 the exact failure mode Kontrast quantified at 18.6% of its flagged conflicts. The conflict definition is INTERSECTION EMPTINESS (or provable non-chain structure), never precision difference. This rule is non-negotiable.\n  GENUINE GRANULARITY CONFLICT \u2014 kwise_sat = UNSAT with a minimal core of size >=2 whose members carry DIFFERENT prec codes, within ONE document (conjunctive incompatibility: all anchors of one event in one document must hold jointly). Distinguish this from DISJUNCTIVE disagreement (independent sources giving rival dates), which Wikidata explicitly permits as separate statements and which would push the problem into NP-hard disjunctive TCSP. Since MAVEN entities and TIMEXes are strictly document-local, our setting is purely conjunctive \u2014 a scoping gift, and it must be said out loud or the conflict class reads as ill-posed.\n  UNKNOWN \u2014 any unresolved anchor in the system. Reported as a rate, never hidden by forced choice; this operationalises PaTeCon's trivalent logic on the granularity side.\n\nMINED GRANULARITY CONSTRAINTS (Phase 4's second family, which PaTeCon has no head predicate for). Per (event_type, anchor_type, prec) learn the admissible width distribution as a conformance constraint (SIGMOD 2021) on log-width, e.g. Military_operation admits w up to years while Killing at prec 11 admits w <= 1 day; head_kind='WIDTH' or 'PREC'. Bodies may use the kwise(x) atom, so constraints can be conditioned on the anchor set's own satisfiability status \u2014 a body atom that literally cannot be evaluated on a projected graph.\n\nNON-CIRCULAR EVALUATION, which is the strongest practical reason to prioritise granularity over ordering under 45 days. Perturb at the NORMALISER level, not the constraint level: take a nested (benign) anchor pair and shift a granule at ONE precision level (1778 -> 1779 while leaving the day anchor; collapse a nested anchor to the coarser one; resolve \"the following day\" from the wrong reference DATE; read a DURATION as a DATE). Detection is then interval/periodic-set arithmetic that was never fitted to any mined constraint, so the circularity the team already diagnosed does not arise. The 17,102 multi-anchor events are additionally a naturally-occurring negative set: a correct detector must ACCEPT all nested ones. Report per-perturbation-family recall so the injection distribution is auditable and demonstrably does not mirror the head vocabulary.\n\nINTERFACE TO P3/P4: Phase 2 (Nhut) hands over ONE frozen, hashed release plus ONE Python package plus TWO generated views. The architectural move is that Phase 3 no longer performs a lossy conversion: Phase 2 generates the projected view itself, so Vinh's projection work becomes serialisation, release engineering and annotation tooling on top of a stable contract.\n\nDELIVERABLE A \u2014 FROZEN RELEASE (day 21 for the MVP subset, day 38 complete)\n  eckg/v1/{event_frame, event_hyperedge, slot_incidence, vertex, timex_norm, anchor, tbox, event_relation, hyperedge_overlap, token_index, projection_loss}.parquet\n  eckg/v1/MANIFEST.json : schema_version, per-file SHA-256, row counts, split assignment (document-disjoint), layer inventory, git commit, and the reproduced values of every hard fact used as a regression assertion.\n  eckg/v1/attach.sql : DuckDB CREATE VIEW script + a DuckPGQ PROPERTY GRAPH declaration used ONLY for BEFORE-closure reachability.\n  CONTRACT GUARANTEES, tested in CI: (i) `split` is document-disjoint \u2014 exact, not approximate, since 0 of 68,348 entities cross documents; (ii) every table carries `layer` and `conf`, so gold-vs-predicted experiments are a WHERE clause; (iii) frame-completeness \u2014 for every eid and every r in F(type), a slot_incidence row exists, with vid='_BOT_' when empty; (iv) trivalent counts never mix; (v) ids are the MAVEN shared ids (79,740 shared event ids) and every row is traceable to text via token_index; (vi) schema hash frozen and committed BEFORE any conflict injection exists (anti-circularity step 0).\n\nDELIVERABLE B \u2014 FOR VINH (PHASE 3): two generated serialisations plus the loss table\n  B1 `view_patecon_evertex` / `view_patecon_svertex` : PaTeCon's exact reified statement-node schema, generated by one SQL view, entity-as-subject per the team's own diagnosis that event-as-subject makes every predicate degenerate:\n      sVertex(sid, subject_vid, property = event_type || '#' || role, object_eid,\n              start = tbox.b_lo, finish = tbox.f_hi, weight = conf, truth)\n      eVertexList = index subject_vid -> sid;  sVertexList = index property -> sid;\n      temporalRelationList from event_relation; entityType from vertex.ent_type.\n    Property vocabulary is the measured 652 distinct \"EventType#Role\" (623 with >=10); the |R|^2 index is 425,104 entries ~0.1 GB. So PaTeCon and PaTeCon+ run UNCHANGED on our own output as the baseline track, and \"n-ary mining vs projected mining\" is a config flag rather than two codebases. This is what makes Angle B a superset rather than a rival: the projection is a deliverable OF the hypergraph.\n  B2 `projection_loss.parquet` : per-event Tier-1 loss incidents with row counts by loss_kind (NULL_SLOT, FRAME_ARITY, ANCHOR_MULTISET, PRECISION_CODE, WIDTH_CONSTRAINT, ARITY0_EDGE). Expected magnitudes from the measured data: ~143K null slots (37.7% of declared), 17,102 events losing their anchor multiset, 7,235 losing a width constraint, 3,649 arity-0 edges absent from the projected index. This table is the empirical form of the whole novelty claim and it is generated automatically by the view builder.\n  B3 Public serialisations: `htkg.jsonl` in Wiki-hy/YAGO-hy shape (primary triple + qualifier key-values + fact-level timestamp, TI vs time-dependent qualifiers separated) so external hyper-relational TKG models can consume the artifact; and `eckg.nq` using RDF 1.2 vocabulary (triple terms, rdf:reifies, reifier, triple annotation) with named graphs per derivation layer, loaded via pyoxigraph, NOT rdflib. Two-serialisation architecture licensed by Iglesias-Molina et al. (ISWC 2023): reification for the mining/inference consumer, qualifier-style for the exploration/SPARQL consumer, and no need to argue either is universally superior.\n  B4 For the annotation tooling: `tbox.unsat_core` and the ordering detector's minimal explanations are the adjudication queue, one row per candidate with the four-way label set {GENUINE | SPURIOUS: constraint wrong | SPURIOUS: incompleteness not contradiction | UNDECIDABLE} plus a culprit-localisation field. Vinh's tool reads this queue directly; no format negotiation.\n\nDELIVERABLE C \u2014 FOR NHAN (PHASE 4): the `hedge` Python package, pinned API\n  hedge.open(\"eckg/v1\")                                  -> Store (DuckDB-backed, read-only)\n  hedge.atoms.{type,rel,slot,slotset,etype,allen,width,prec,null,card,sdisj,ssub,seq,kwise}   -- the 12 constructors\n  Store.match(pattern: HyPaL) -> Iterator[Binding]        -- intra-document hypergraph matching\n  Store.support(pattern, denom in {\"event\",\"doc\",\"activation\"}) -> (n_pos, n_neg, n_unknown)\n  Store.stp(doc_id, extra_atoms=[]) -> (\"SAT\"|\"UNSAT\"|\"UNKNOWN\", minimal_core)   -- Floyd-Warshall, complete on ORD-Horn\n  Store.maxsat(doc_id, weights) -> ranked falsified atoms                        -- Z3 fallback when `disjoint` is needed\n  Store.ordhorn(head_mask) -> bool                        -- subalgebra membership check\n  Plus `candidate_signatures.parquet`, precomputed so Phase 4 never rescans the corpus: (sig_id, atom_list, supp_event, supp_doc, supp_activation, n_pos, n_neg, n_unknown, marginals_for_null_model). Seeded from the measured signature layers \u2014 2,708 (T1,T2) at support >=10 (81% instance coverage) and 2,688 (T1,role1,T2,role2) at >=10 (59%) \u2014 then extended with the null/card/setrel/kwise families. Marginal columns are supplied specifically so Nhan can run Benjamini-Hochberg FDR and the Martin et al. Beta-Bernoulli atomicity test from the table alone.\n  Phase 2 also owns and ships the `hypattern` table schema (T10) that Phase 4 writes into, including `expressible_in_patecon` and `ordhorn` as COMPUTED columns \u2014 so the headline comparison \"X% of HyPaL constraints have no PaTeCon-expressible equivalent\" is produced by the pipeline, not asserted in prose.\n\nSEQUENCING COMMITMENT (this is the part that must not slip): Vinh and Nhan are unblocked on day 21 by the MVP release plus B1 (PaTeCon view), which gives Nhan a fully working baseline track with zero dependence on HyPaL. The `hedge` matcher lands with a stub on day 26 and complete on day 32. Phase 4's novelty track therefore has 6 days of slack before the 12 Oct deadline, which is thin and is the schedule risk stated below.\n\nNOVELTY CLAIM: HEADLINE, stated so it is falsifiable: HEDGE is the first event-centric temporal KG in which the event is a first-class n-ary hyperedge over a DECLARED typed role frame carrying an ANCHOR MULTISET with per-anchor precision codes; and HyPaL is the first temporal-constraint language with slot-nullity, slot-cardinality, set-relational and k-wise-anchor atoms. We prove by a Helly-number argument, and measure on MAVEN, that no binary-triple projection with one interval per statement can express the granularity satisfiability test that the MAJORITY of MAVEN's multi-anchor events require (41.9% of the 17,274 multi-anchor events carry a non-interval DURATION width constraint; 15.9% carry a non-convex periodic anchor), and we ship a machine-generated projection_loss table quantifying the loss per event.\n\nDIFFERENTIATION AGAINST NAMED PRIOR WORK:\n - PaTeCon (AAAI 2023) / PaTeCon+ (arXiv 2312.11053). Their pattern language is two hand-fixed structural patterns: SP(a) one subject with two temporal statements, SP(b) two subjects joined by an atemporal property. It is conjunctive, purely binary, aggregation-free, and admits at most two non-time entity variables. null(), card(), sdisj() and kwise() are outside it by construction. Two of those four (null, kwise) are additionally Tier-1 DESTROYED by projection into their sVertex format, because a statement carries one interval and no frame. We run their code unchanged on our own generated view as the baseline, so the comparison is ours rather than a reviewer's. We also close a gap PaTeCon itself names: their text diagnoses that hand-enumerated constraints \"may have granularity issues\" and that property-only constraints \"may be too coarse\"; they have no precision constraint family. We add one with a violation semantics.\n - Soulard, Sa\u00efs & Raad (ESWC 2025), the nearest 2025 competitor. Entity-timeline constraints over Wikidata binary facts with an Allen-sequence extension. No role frames, no n-ary events, no precision atoms, no slot nullity, and globally recurring entities rather than a document-local graph. Our differentiator is the event level (168 types, 143 roles, declared frames) and the precision family.\n - Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025), on our exact dataset. They enforce instance-level Allen consistency over PREDICTED pairwise relations as a decoding/training constraint. We mine TYPE-level statistical constraints over event-type/role-slot signatures, and we REUSE their start/end point decomposition as our checker. We must and will report both, so the contrast (pairwise logical closure vs type-level n-ary statistical constraints) is stated by us in one sentence rather than by a reviewer.\n - HTKG / Ding et al. (Findings EMNLP 2024), Wiki-hy, YAGO-hy, and StarE (EMNLP 2020). Hyper-relational means a distinguished PRIMARY TRIPLE plus qualifier key-values plus one fact-level timestamp. HEDGE has no distinguished primary triple \u2014 all 143 roles are peers of one hyperedge \u2014 and carries an anchor multiset rather than a single timestamp. Moreover no HTKG benchmark is text-grounded: Wiki-hy and YAGO-hy are distilled from Wikidata/YAGO, ChronoGrapher (Semantic Web Journal 2025) traverses DBpedia/Wikidata, EventKG integrates existing KGs, ECS-KG (DKE 2025) uses unlabelled news. A document-grounded hyper-relational temporal KG with gold n-ary argument roles and 1.2M gold temporal relations would be the first, and the ECTKG survey (Mathematics 2023, 11(23), 4852) explicitly names the lack of annotation supporting end-to-end models as its concluding gap. StarE's measured +25 MRR for qualifiers over binarised representations of the same facts is our quantitative licence for refusing projection as the STORE.\n - Euzenat (Computational Intelligence 2001) and Bettini, Wang & Jajodia (AMAI 1998; AI 140, 2002). We explicitly do NOT claim to formalise cross-granularity conflict \u2014 Euzenat's upward/downward conversion operators and multi-granularity TCSP already are formal theories of it, and a blanket first-to-formalise claim would be false and would be caught by any TIME/temporal-DB reviewer. Our claim is narrower and defensible: the k-wise, non-Helly OPERATIONALISATION inside an event KG, with measured prevalence showing pairwise tests are insufficient on the majority of the substrate, plus the benign/conflict/unknown adjudication, plus a stated restriction (resolve-then-STP) that keeps us in polynomial single-granularity STP rather than NP-hard multi-granularity TCSP.\n - Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959, 2026), the mandatory citation and the biggest novelty risk if missed. It names \"information granularity differences\" as the first category of its inconsistency taxonomy and measures it at 18.6% of flagged conflicts, then RECOMMENDS normalising granularity away before labelling. We invert its stance: granularity divergence is a signal to be ADJUDICATED into {benign refinement, genuine conflict, unknown}, and we cite its own 18.6% as the prevalence justification. Our chain_ok test is precisely the rule that keeps their 18.6% out of the false-positive bucket rather than out of the benchmark.\n - Iglesias-Molina et al. (ISWC 2023) and Hernandez, Hogan & Kr\u00f6tzsch (SSWS 2015). These license a two-serialisation design and quantify reification's ~4x cost. We go one step further: reification is a VIEW, not the store, and the ~4x factor is irrelevant at 2.5M rows \u2014 which is the point worth making, that representation fidelity is free at this scale so the most expressive model should be chosen.\n - Martin, de Almeida, Romero & Queralt (PVLDB 18, 2025) and MiniAMIE (WWW 2026). We adopt the atomicity/soundness test rather than bare support/confidence, and we note the irony that MiniAMIE's independence approximation of support and confidence is the same independence assumption that produces false constraints. At event-KG scale (425,104-entry index, ~0.1 GB) efficiency is not the bottleneck; validity is. Exact counts plus atomicity plus FDR are affordable, and saying so is a clean positioning sentence.\n - The Equivalence Theorem (arXiv 2603.13603) is a single-author unrefereed preprint; cited as motivation only, never as a result, for its observation that n-ary relations with attributes, temporal validity, uncertainty and relations-between-relations are mutually entailing \u2014 MAVEN-ERE's CAUSE and SUBEVENT are literally relations between relations, which is why they are second-order edges here.\n\nCHEAP ADJACENT CREDIBILITY that most comparable papers lack: we state which tractable subalgebra our head language occupies and why (ORD-Horn by default; `disjoint` excluded because it is a genuine disjunction of strict inequalities), we state and justify our engine choice against measured scale (Parquet canonical, DuckDB, DuckPGQ only for reachability; Kuzu explicitly rejected as archived 10 Oct 2025), and we separate CONTRADICTION from INCOMPLETENESS in the output taxonomy with a GGD-style REQUIRE head \u2014 which the measured data demands, since incompleteness (13.6% of SUBEVENT pairs lack an implied CONTAINS) dwarfs contradiction (~72 corpus-wide).\n\nEFFORT (person-days): 38\n\nBUILD STEPS: D1-D3 (2.5 pd) OFFSET RECONCILIATION AND JOIN. Build token_index (T11): MAVEN-Arg uses CHARACTER offsets over 'document' (verified: trigger \"Expedition\" at [4,14]), MAVEN-ERE uses (sent_id, sentence-local TOKEN indices) over 'tokens'/'sentences' (verified: TIMEX \"1860\" at sent_id 0, offset [24,25]). Nothing joins without this. Assert doc-id overlap 4,480/4,480 and shared event ids 79,740 as CI regression tests. Assign the document-disjoint mine/eval split and commit its hash. | D3-D4 (1.0 pd) FRAME CATALOGUE INDUCTION. Build event_frame (T1) by scanning role key sets per event type: 162 typed frames from MAVEN-Arg train (Killing = {Killer,Cause,Victim,Location,Instrument}, Attack = {Agent,Patient,Location,Consequence}, Process_start = {Agent,Patient,Location,Symbol,Purpose}, Military_operation = {Agent,Patient,Location}), plus 9 frameless ERE types including Hostile_encounter (2,024 ERE train mentions, 0 MAVEN-Arg events). Derive is_set_valued, max_card, null_rate, and paired_with (Location_original <-> Location_final, 698/698). | D5-D7 (2.5 pd) HYPEREDGE AND INCIDENCE BUILDERS. Emit event_hyperedge (T2) with slot_signature and null_signature bitmasks, and slot_incidence (T3) with EXPLICIT '_BOT_' rows for all declared-but-empty slots (~143K rows, 37.7% of declared). Emit vertex (T4) with n_events and an asserted is_cross_doc=FALSE for all 68,348. CI test: frame-completeness for every hyperedge; arity-0 edges present for all 3,649 frameless ERE events. | D8-D9 (2.0 pd) TIMEX NORMALISER STAGE 1. Regex rule set over the 9,287 distinct surfaces covering the 62.1% deterministic families (bare year 29.9%, day-month-year 9.1%, month+year 6.9%, N+unit 6.1% as DURATION, month+day+year 4.6%, ranges/decades/centuries/deictic 5.6%). Emit HeidelTime-style context-independent representations in timex_norm.cir with UNDEF markers, and TIMEX3-conformant value_t3 with X placeholders. PERSIST stage-1 output: it is the only place granularity is observable. | D10-D12 (2.5 pd) TIMEX NORMALISER STAGE 2. Narrative-domain reference-time propagation (reference = last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE, since MAVEN is Wikipedia with no document creation time) plus a ~50-entry gazetteer. Targets the four tractable context-dependent families: relative dates (next day x91, following day x62, same day x21), yearless dates (September 11 x24, August 31 x17), named periods (World War II x70, typed DURATION), weekdays (Sunday x21). Set norm_method and force kind='UNRESOLVED' where resolution fails. Human-validate ~200 resolved anchors and report Krippendorff alpha, since there is zero gold normalisation supervision. | D13-D15 (3.0 pd) PERIODIC-SET AND DURATION LIBRARY. Minimal periodic sets with canonical form, equivalence, intersection and an emptiness test (Bettini & Mascetti); day-chronon Julian arithmetic; DURATION width bounds. HIGHEST-VARIANCE ITEM. Gate: if incomplete by D30, fall back to interval + width only, which still breaks Helly for 41.9% of multi-anchor events and preserves the core claim. Unit test must include the verified witness that {1778} , {XXXX-11-11} , {Sunday} intersect pairwise but not three-way (1778-11-11 was a Wednesday; Nov 11 falls on a Sunday in 1770, 1781, 1787). | D16-D18 (3.0 pd) ANCHOR MULTISET AND TBOX WITH K-WISE SOLVER. Build anchor (T6) from the 66,418 CONTAINS + 496 SIMULTANEOUS + 122 BEGINS-ON + 39 ENDS-ON links with per-anchor prec and width. Build tbox (T7): resolve periodic sets locally, then solve the residual interval-plus-width system as an STP by Floyd-Warshall. Emit the four-endpoint quadruple, w_lo/w_hi, status (must reproduce 44.9% TIGHT / 27.2% LOOSE / 27.9% UNANCHORED), kwise_sat trivalent, and unsat_core with core_kind. | D19-D20 (2.0 pd) CHAIN TEST AND THREE-WAY GRANULARITY ADJUDICATION. Implement chain_ok (subset-chain / refinement detection) so that nested anchors are NEVER flagged, and minimal-core extraction for UNSAT. Verify on the 17,102 events with >=2 CONTAINS anchors (2 anchors 10,653; 3 anchors 3,707; >=4 anchors 2,742) that the benign rate is overwhelming, and report the {benign, conflict, unknown} distribution with core_kind breakdown. | D21 (1.5 pd, overlaps) MVP RELEASE v0 + PaTeCon VIEW. Ship Parquet for T1-T7, T11 with MANIFEST and hashes, plus view_patecon_evertex/svertex (entity-as-subject, property = 'EventType#Role', 652 distinct / 623 with >=10, |R|^2 index 425,104 entries) and the first projection_loss table. THIS IS THE HARD GATE: Vinh and Nhan are unblocked here with a fully working baseline track that does not depend on HyPaL. | D22-D23 (1.5 pd) HYPEREDGE_OVERLAP WITH PRECOMPUTED SET ATOMS. Build T9 over the 274,525 shared-participant pairs, materialising shared_vids, role_pairs, slot_disjoint_pairs, slot_subsume_pairs, jaccard, and gold_rel (non-null for 139,093 = 50.7%). This is the candidate-body table Phase 4 enumerates from. | D24-D27 (4.0 pd) HyPaL: LANGUAGE, MATCHER, SUPPORT. Implement the 12 atom constructors, the intra-document hypergraph matcher (cheap: ~18 events/doc, max 59 events/entity, no cross-doc vertices), and support with THREE denominators (event / doc / activation, the DECLARE-style activation denominator being computable only because null slots are stored). Trivalent counting throughout. Ship candidate_signatures.parquet with marginals for the null model, seeded from the 2,708 (T1,T2) and 2,688 (T1,r1,T2,r2) signatures at support >=10. | D28-D30 (2.5 pd) ORDERING CHECKER. Point-algebra decomposition of Allen atoms (Fan & Strube reduction), Floyd-Warshall path consistency over each document's endpoint graph, ORD-Horn membership check per head, minimal negative-cycle plus hitting-set extraction for culprit localisation, and a Z3 partial-weighted MaxSAT fallback for head vocabularies containing `disjoint`. Regression assertions: 0 BEFORE cycles over gold, 0 symmetry violations, and detection of the 11 CAUSE-contradicted and 61 subevent-contradicted pairs. | D31-D33 (3.0 pd) PHASE-3 SERIALISATIONS. Complete projection_loss (all six loss kinds with per-event rows), htkg.jsonl in Wiki-hy/YAGO-hy shape with TI vs time-dependent qualifiers separated, and eckg.nq using RDF 1.2 vocabulary (triple terms, rdf:reifies, reifier, triple annotation) with one named graph per derivation layer, loaded via pyoxigraph rather than rdflib. | D34-D35 (2.0 pd) RELEASE ENGINEERING. Full Parquet v1 with MANIFEST/SHA-256, DuckDB attach.sql, DuckPGQ property-graph declaration for BEFORE reachability, Datasheet for Datasets, HuggingFace hosting with Croissant metadata, per-split document-id lists proving the entity-leak-free split, and the frozen schema hash committed before any injection code exists. | D36-D38 (3.0 pd) TESTS, AUDITS, HANDOVER DOCS, INTEGRATION. Convert every hard measured fact into a CI assertion; write the `hedge` API reference and two worked notebooks (one HyPaL rule with a null atom, one k-wise granularity core); pair-debug with Vinh on the PaTeCon view and with Nhan on candidate_signatures. Reserve the tail for the integration failures that always appear at this boundary.\n\nRISKS: SCHEDULE IS THE PRIMARY RISK. 38 person-days inside 45 calendar days, for one person who must also write paper sections, leaves essentially no slack. Mitigation is the D21 MVP gate: Vinh and Nhan get a working release plus the PaTeCon view on day 21 regardless, so a slip in the HyPaL half degrades the novelty track rather than blocking two teammates. If day 21 slips, the correct move is to cut D24-D27 HyPaL down to the null-atom family only and ship it as a single constraint family rather than a language. | THE PERIODIC-SET LIBRARY (D13-D15) IS THE HIGHEST-VARIANCE ITEM and 3 days could become 6. Explicit gate: if incomplete by D30, drop PERIODIC handling entirely and keep INTERVAL + DURATION width. The Helly-breaking claim survives on width alone (41.9% of multi-anchor events, 7,235 events) and the headline result is unchanged; only the three-anchor calendar witness becomes illustrative rather than detected. | NOVELTY IS REPRESENTATIONAL, SO WEAK MINING RESULTS COULD MAKE THE HYPERGRAPH LOOK LIKE ENGINEERING. Mitigation: the projection_loss table, the Helly argument, and the measured non-Helly prevalence are results that hold independently of whether any HyPaL constraint reaches significance. Sequence the paper so those land in Section 3-4, before any mining number. | GRANULARITY CONFLICT RATE IS A FUNCTION OF OUR OWN UNSUPERVISED NORMALISER (zero gold normalisation exists). A normaliser bug manufactures conflicts. Mitigations: trivalent UNKNOWN that can never yield a conflict, per-family error reporting, ~200 human-validated anchors with Krippendorff alpha, and framing the component as a reimplementation of the gold-span/value-only setting (arXiv 2205.10399) plus HeidelTime's narrative rule rather than an ad-hoc hack. | ARITY-0 HYPEREDGES ARE INVISIBLE TO ROLE-BEARING PATTERNS. 3,649 ERE events (5.4%) and 9 event types including Hostile_encounter (2,024 mentions) have no argument frame, so HyPaL bodies using slot atoms cannot bind them even though they carry BEFORE and CONTAINS edges. This must be reported as a coverage figure per constraint family, not quietly dropped, or a reviewer will find the hole. | HyPaL'S LARGER PATTERN SPACE INFLATES THE MULTIPLE-TESTING BURDEN. Adding four atom families pushes the candidate space from ~10^5-10^6 to plausibly 10^7, weakening FDR power exactly where the novelty lives. Mitigations: stratified Benjamini-Hochberg WITHIN atom family, the Martin et al. atomicity test as a pre-filter, and honest reporting of candidate-space size. Do not hide the inflation; a family-stratified table is a better result than an unstratified q-value. | REVIEWER OBJECTION 'N-ARY IS JUST REIFICATION WITH EXTRA STEPS', backed by Iglesias-Molina et al. finding reification best for embedding/inference consumers. Answer must be pre-written: we SHIP reification as a generated view, the store is the hypergraph, and the difference is measured in projection_loss rather than argued. Anything less concrete loses this exchange. | TWO-PERSON DOWNSTREAM DEPENDENCY. Nhan's novelty track cannot begin before ~D27 and Phase 5 detection depends on D28-D30. Six days of slack before 12 Oct is thin. Mitigation: the D21 baseline track means Nhan has publishable comparison numbers even if the HyPaL track produces only one constraint family. | ENGINE AND TOOLING RISK. DuckPGQ is documented as incomplete and under active development. Mitigation: Parquet is canonical, DuckDB does the relational work, DuckPGQ is used ONLY for BEFORE-closure reachability and is replaceable by a hand-written closure in an afternoon. Kuzu is explicitly not used (archived 10 Oct 2025); rdflib is explicitly not used at this triple count. | GOLD OFFERS ALMOST NO ORDERING TARGET (~72 genuine contradictions corpus-wide, 0 BEFORE cycles over 843,808 pairs, 100% transitive closure), because MAVEN-ERE's timeline annotation enforces global consistency by construction. If Phase 5 evaluates the ordering detector on gold it will measure nothing. Mitigation is baked into the schema: the derivation-layer axis makes the predicted-layer experiment a WHERE clause, and the normaliser-level perturbation generator is independent of the head vocabulary. But this must be planned now, not in October. | OFFSET RECONCILIATION IS A SILENT-FAILURE ZONE. MAVEN-Arg character offsets versus MAVEN-ERE (sent_id, token) offsets will mis-align on whitespace and tokenisation edge cases, and a mis-aligned join produces a plausible-looking graph with wrong fillers. Mitigation: token_index is a first-class released table with CI assertions on trigger-surface round-tripping for every hyperedge.\n\nGIVES UP: \"VERSUS A PROJECTION-FIRST ANGLE (build the entity-as-subject TKG, run PaTeCon, spend the remaining time on evaluation): that design is roughly 10-12 person-days for Phase 2 rather than 38. Angle B spends ~26 extra person-days to buy representational expressiveness. What those 26 days do NOT buy: any improvement to the evaluation protocol, the fake-conflict circularity, or the annotation budget. If the paper's binding constraint turns out to be reviewer trust in the evaluation rather than reviewer interest in the representation, this is the wrong allocation and it cannot be reversed after ~day 25.\n\nGIVES UP THE FREE RIDE ON PaTeCon'S IMPLEMENTATION for the novelty track. Nhan can run PaTeCon unchanged on our generated view, but HyPaL needs a matcher, a support module with three denominators, and a checker \u2014 ~9.5 of the 38 days \u2014 and that code will be less battle-tested than a published implementation two weeks before a deadline.\n\nGIVES UP DIRECT COMPARABILITY WITH PUBLISHED PaTeCon NUMBERS on the novelty track. Only the baseline track is comparable to WD27M/FB37M results; HyPaL constraint counts and confidences are not commensurable with theirs, so every headline comparison must be internal (our view vs our hypergraph), which is weaker rhetorically than an external comparison even though it is methodologically cleaner.\n\nGIVES UP CROSS-DOCUMENT GENERALISATION ENTIRELY. With 0 of 68,348 entities recurring across documents, HyPaL support can only be counted over events, documents or activations \u2014 never over recurring individuals the way PaTeCon counts over Wikidata entities. All constraints are type-level. This is defensible and even interesting as framing, but it means the artifact cannot support any experiment about individuals, and PaTeCon's headline entity-level support/confidence contribution has no analogue here at all.\n\nGIVES UP OFF-THE-SHELF LEARNED-MODEL RESULTS ON THE NATIVE STORE. No released hyper-relational TKG model consumes a 4-place positioned incidence relation. To report StarE/HTKG-style link prediction we must export to htkg.jsonl, which reintroduces a distinguished primary triple \u2014 and then, per Egami et al. (arXiv 2503.21804), the metadata representation model itself becomes a confound we are obliged to report. So the artifact's expressiveness is not demonstrable through embedding numbers, only through the loss table and the k-wise detector.\n\nGIVES UP DECLARATIVE-STANDARDS BASELINES ON THE NATIVE STORE. SHACL-core cannot compare interval endpoints across nodes and has no notion of a hyperedge, so the SHACL-SPARQL granularity baseline (Robaldo & Batsakis) runs only on the projected view. The comparison is therefore native-detector vs projected-baseline, which is exactly the confound a hostile reviewer will name.\n\nGIVES UP ANNOTATION SIMPLICITY. A k-wise unsat core over 3-6 anchors, or an n-ary pattern binding three argument positions, is harder for an annotator to adjudicate than a conflicting pair. Expect lower inter-annotator agreement and higher per-item cost than the pairwise design, in a project where the whole annotation budget is 3 people and ~2 weeks.\n\nGIVES UP THE OPTION TO PRIORITISE ORDERING CONFLICTS. Angle B's decisive advantage is on granularity (the Helly argument and the 17,102 naturally-occurring multi-anchor events, which are also the only escape from the fake-conflict circularity). Its ordering-side advantage is real but softer \u2014 expressiveness rather than a theorem \u2014 and gold offers ~72 targets. Committing to Angle B is in practice committing to granularity as the headline conflict type and to ordering as the secondary one.\n\nDOES NOT GIVE UP PaTeCon COMPATIBILITY, and that is the one thing worth being explicit about: because the projection is a generated view rather than a discarded step, this design is a strict superset of the projection-first plan at the cost of days, not of options.\""}, "DESIGNS_CONDENSED": {"A-projection-faithful": "DESIGN: StarCast-ECKG (event-node core + frozen entity-star cast)\nA 9-table Parquet/DuckDB event-centric KG in which time lives on the event node as a four-endpoint day-chronon quadruple plus a Wikibase-style precision code, so that Phase 3's entity-as-subject projection to PaTeCon's TSV is a single 12-line SQL CTAS and PaTeCon runs with exactly two files replaced.\n\nCORE IDEA: Keep two representations and make the second one a pure function of the first.\n\nINTERNAL (source of truth, Phase 2 deliverable): a document-grounded hyper-relational temporal KG, stored as flat columnar tables. Event is a node; the event node carries ALL time (EventKG/SEM precedent: sem:hasBeginTimeStamp on the event resource, not on the participation edge). Argument participations are hyper-relational qualifiers on the event (Galkin et al., StarE, EMNLP 2020) and are time-INVARIANT within the event (Ding et al., HTKG, Findings EMNLP 2024, TI vs time-dependent qualifier split). This is exactly why event-as-subject projection is degenerate: every participation of one event shares one interval, so time cannot be a property of the participation.\n\nPROJECTION (Phase 3 consumer view): entity-as-subject reified statements (subject=entity, property=\"EventType#Role\", object=event, T=event's interval), i.e. PaTeCon's eVertex/sVertex store. Licensed by Iglesias-Molina et al. (ISWC 2023): standard reification measured most consistent for embedding/inference consumers, which is precisely the mining consumer; RDF 1.2's own reifier IS PaTeCon's sVertex, so the choice is standards-endorsed, not legacy. Hernandez/Hogan/Krotzsch's ~4x reification blow-up is irrelevant at 425K index entries / ~0.1 GB.\n\nThe whole design decision is: Phase 2 pays for a rich event-node table so that Phase 3 is a projection, not a rewrite, and Phase 4 never has to understand the event model. Phase 2 additionally pre-computes everything the projection would destroy (precision codes, nesting verdicts, indeterminacy bounds, marginal counts) into extra columns that ride alongside the PaTeCon TSV, so Nhan can opt in to them without touching PaTeCon's core loop.\n\nConcrete PaTeCon reuse boundary (measured against github.com/JianhaoChen-nju/PaTeCon): Graph_Structure.py, Constraint_Mining.py and the SP(a)/SP(b) enumeration are UNCHANGED. Only two things change: (1) the TSV loader points at our generated files (same 5-column format S\\tP\\tO\\tstart\\tend plus a separate entity_id\\ttype file \u2014 we emit exactly that); (2) Interval_Relations.py is replaced by tempekg.intervals, a drop-in module with identical function names/signatures that reads two extra columns (b_hi, f_lo) and returns PaTeCon's existing trivalent {True, False, UNKNOWN} instead of guessing. That is ~200 lines of new code and zero changes to the mining loop.\n\nNODE/EDGE MODEL: NODE TYPES (4):\n- Event node. One per MAVEN coreference cluster (EVENT_<hash>). 80,479 total; 79,740 also present in MAVEN-ERE (in_ere=true), 739 ARG-only (in_ere=false, no relations, no anchors, excluded from mining but kept for corpus completeness). Attributes: event_type (168), event_type_id, doc_id, mention count, first-mention position. Time is attached via a 1:1 satellite table event_time.\n- Entity node. ENTITY_<hash>, document-local, 7 types, 68,348 total, 0 cross-document. 16,284 have degree >= 2 in the participation graph (mean 3.15, max 59) and are the ONLY subjects that generate SP(a) pairs.\n- UnlinkedSpan node. Synthetic node for the 59.9% of arguments that are bare {content, offset} with no entity_id (~141.9K of 236,937). id = SPAN_<sha1(doc_id|start|end)>, entity_type='Unlinked'. Kept as first-class nodes so the corpus is lossless and so a future coreference layer can merge them; they are degree-1 by construction (a span is a single mention in a single event) so they contribute nothing to SP(a) \u2014 this is stated as a measured limitation, not hidden.\n- Timex node. TIME_<hash>, 20,827 gold spans in train+valid, 9,287 distinct surfaces, 4 types (DATE/DURATION/TIME/PREPOSTEXP). Provided in every split including blind test; detection is not a task.\n\nEDGE / STATEMENT TYPES (4):\n- Participation statement (the reifiable unit, = PaTeCon sVertex payload): (event_id, role, filler) where role is one of 143 MAVEN-Arg roles. 236,937 rows. Carries NO time of its own \u2014 time is inherited from the event node. This is the single most important modelling decision and the direct fix for the diagnosed degeneracy.\n- Event-Event relation edge: BEFORE (1.04M), CONTAINS (152K), SIMULTANEOUS (9.9K), OVERLAP (9.9K), BEGINS-ON (639), ENDS-ON (380), plus CAUSE (46,014), PRECONDITION, SU ...[truncated]\n\nSCHEMA: Physical layout: eckg/v1/*.parquet + eckg/v1/manifest.json + eckg/v1/eckg.duckdb (views only). Day chronon = proleptic Gregorian ordinal (Python date.toordinal); NEG_INF = -9000000, POS_INF = 9000000 (real ordinals span 1..3652059, so sentinels are unambiguous).\n\n-- 1\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n  doc_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, title VARCHAR, n_tokens INT, n_sent INT,\n  split VARCHAR,              -- 'MINE' | 'EVAL' | 'BLIND'  (document-level; see risks)\n  has_gold_args BOOLEAN,      -- false for the 857 BLIND docs\n  ref_time_chain VARCHAR      -- JSON: [[sent_id, timex_id], ...] last-mentioned-DATE chain used by the normaliser\n);\n\n-- 2\nCREATE TABLE events (\n  event_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR, event_type VARCHAR, event_type_id INT,\n  n_mentions INT, first_sent_id INT, first_tok_start INT, first_tok_end INT,\n  trigger_words VARCHAR,      -- JSON list\n  in_arg BOOLEAN, in_ere BOOLEAN\n);\n\n-- 3\nCREATE TABLE entities (\n  entity_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR,\n  entity_type VARCHAR,        -- Location|Other|Organization|Person|Product|Building|Art\n  n_mentions INT, canonical_mention VARCHAR, mention_offsets VARCHAR,  -- JSON list of [char_start,char_end]\n  n_events INT                -- precomputed degree; 16,284 rows have n_events>=2\n);\n\n-- 4\nCREATE TABLE unlinked_spans (\n  span_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR, content VARCHAR,\n  char_start INT, char_end INT, overlaps_timex BOOLEAN   -- 4.97% true\n);\n\n-- 5  the hyper-relational qualifier set / future sVertex payload\nCREATE TABLE participations (\n  stmt_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY,   -- STMT_<sha1(event_id|role|filler_id)>\n  doc_id VARCHAR, event_id VARCHAR, event_type VARCHAR,\n  role VARCHAR, role_id INT,\n  filler_kind VARCHAR,           -- 'entity' | 'span'\n  filler_id VARCHAR, filler_type VARCHAR,\n  property VARCHAR,              -- event_type || '#' || role   -> 652 distinct, 623 with support>=10\n  layer VARCHAR DEFAULT 'gold',  -- 'gold'|'rule'|'pred'   (derivation axis, ADBIS 2025 bitemporal framing)\n  conf DOUBLE DEFAULT 1.0\n);\n\n-- 6\nCREATE TABLE timex (\n  timex_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR, sent_id INT, tok_start INT, tok_end INT,\n  surface VARCHAR, timex_type VARCHAR,     -- DATE|DUR ...[truncated]\n\nINTERVALS: Four-endpoint quadruple over a day chronon, per event: E(e) = <[b_lo, b_hi], [f_lo, f_hi]>, all int64 day ordinals. Semantics: the event began somewhere in [b_lo, b_hi] and ended somewhere in [f_lo, f_hi]; a fully determinate day-level event has b_lo=b_hi=f_lo=f_hi. Cite Dyreson & Snodgrass (TODS 23(1), 1998) for indeterminate-instant semantics and Reimers et al. (ACL 2016) / the unified-quadruple time-anchor work (arXiv 2008.06452) for the temporal-IE precedent. This single form subsumes the three measured populations without special-casing: tight (44.9%), loose-bound-only (27.2%), unanchored (27.9%, all four endpoints = sentinels).\n\nConstruction rule (deterministic, ~120 lines):\n- CONTAINS(t, e), t a TIMEX with window [lo, hi]: constrain b_lo = max(b_lo, lo), f_hi = min(f_hi, hi). Containment gives an OUTER bound, so it touches b_lo and f_hi only, never b_hi or f_lo. This is why 66,418 CONTAINS anchors (99% of mass) yield 'loose' rather than 'tight' unless the anchor is day-precision.\n- SIMULTANEOUS(t, e): b_lo=b_hi=lo, f_lo=f_hi=hi (496 cases).\n- BEGINS-ON / ENDS-ON: set the b or f pair respectively (122 / 39 cases; recorded, never mined).\n- DURATION anchors ('21 years', 'life'): write width_lo/width_hi, do NOT move endpoints. This is the information the projection later loses.\n- Anchor from a coarse TIMEX after a fine one is intersected, not overwritten \u2014 see the granularity path.\n- Sentinels: b_lo/f_lo default NEG_INF, b_hi/f_hi default POS_INF.\n\nGranularity is stored SEPARATELY from the interval and is never recoverable from it. Every timex row keeps (value with X placeholders, precision code, lo, hi, pset_*). '1778' is (value='1778', precision=9, lo=649037, hi=649401). 'November 11' with no year is (value='XXXX-11-11', precision=11, pset_unit='Y', pset_period=1,  ...[truncated]\n\nORDERING CONFLICT: The projection makes PaTeCon's SP(a) literally the shared-participant event pair, so no new machinery is needed.\n\n1. Subject star. For entity x, projection_star gives S(x) = {(x, EventType#Role, e_i, quad_i)}. PaTeCon's Single_Entity_Temporal_Order iterates eVertexList, skips subjects with fewer than 2 statements, and enumerates statement pairs. On our data that is 16,284 subjects and 274,525 event pairs; cost sum_v d(v)^2 with max d = 59, so the whole pass is seconds.\n2. MANDATORY FILTER, or every constraint is an artefact: skip any statement pair with e_i == e_j. Two participations of the same event share one interval by construction, so before/disjoint/include on them is a tautology and confidence goes to 1.0 for free. This one line is the difference between a working miner and 425K degenerate constraints. It is enforced inside tempekg.project so Vinh cannot forget it, and the count of skipped same-event pairs is reported in the manifest.\n3. Constraint form (unchanged from PaTeCon): pattern SP(a) + property pair (p1, p2) + head predicate in {start, finish, before, disjoint, include}, with support and confidence counted over subjects/pairs. Head predicates are evaluated by tempekg.intervals on the quadruples, returning trivalent; UNKNOWN pairs are excluded from both numerator and denominator, matching PaTeCon.\n4. Worked example on real types. Property p1 = 'Killing#Victim', p2 = 'Military_operation#Agent'. Mined constraint C1: for any subject x, if x fills Killing#Victim in ...[truncated]\n\nGRANULARITY CONFLICT: Granularity is decided ENTIRELY inside Phase 2, on event_time, and never on the projection \u2014 because the projection's (t_start, t_end) provably cannot express it. The output is one column, gran_verdict, plus gran_witness for explainability, so Phase 4/5 need zero periodic-set code.\n\nSubstrate: the 17,102 events with >=2 CONTAINS anchors (2 anchors: 10,653; 3: 3,707; >=4: 2,742). These are naturally occurring, not injected \u2014 which is the strongest available escape from the diagnosed fake-conflict circularity. All anchors of one event are CONJUNCTIVE (same document, same event, all must hold), which is the distinction that keeps the class well-posed; DISJUNCTIVE rival dates from independent sources (Wikidata explicitly permits mutually incompatible dates as separate statements) are a different, benign phenomenon and cannot arise here.\n\nAlgorithm (tempekg.gran, ~150 lines on top of tempekg.pset):\n1. For event e collect A(e) = anchors from CONTAINS/SIMULTANEOUS edges. Represent each as a minimal periodic set M(a) = (unit, period, offset spec, window width) in canonical form. Fully-specified anchors are aperiodic single intervals; X-placeholder anchors are periodic.\n2. Compute the canonical intersection I = M(a_1) ^ ... ^ M(a_k) by periodic-set intersection (Bettini & Mascetti), then test emptiness on the canonical form.\n3. Sort anchors by precision code and test the SUBSET CHAIN condition: does M(a_(i+1)) subset-of M(a_i) for the precision-ascending order (coarse contains fine)?\n ...[truncated]\n\nINTERFACE TO P3/P4: Contract frozen 2026-09-12 (project day 15). Everything below is versioned as eckg/v1 with a manifest carrying per-file SHA-256, row counts, git commit and the split-assignment hash. Within v1 columns are additive only; names and types never change; ids are stable strings; the layer enum is fixed.\n\nDELIVERABLE 1 \u2014 data. eckg/v1/{documents,events,entities,unlinked_spans,participations,timex,event_time,event_relations,property_marginals,pair_signatures}.parquet plus eckg.duckdb registering all of them as views. Parquet as the canonical release format (columnar, projection pushdown, zero server, standard for dataset releases), DuckDB for relational/aggregation work, DuckPGQ only for BEFORE-closure reachability if needed. Explicitly NOT Kuzu \u2014 the repository was archived 10 Oct 2025 and a reviewer can check the banner in ten seconds.\n\nDELIVERABLE 2 \u2014 the projection function, owned by Phase 2, called by Phase 3. Phase 3 does not write the projection; it consumes and extends it. This is the whole point of Angle A.\n\n  tempekg.project.to_patecon(conn, split='MINE', out_dir=..., subject_kinds=('entity',), include_unknown=True, min_subject_degree=2) -> ProjectionReport\n\n  writes exactly three files:\n    {split}.facts.tsv       S \\t P \\t O \\t start \\t end            <- PaTeCon's native format, unchanged\n    {split}.types.tsv       entity_id \\t type                       <- PaTeCon's entityType file, unchanged\n    {split}.facts.ext.tsv   S \\t P \\t O \\t b_lo \\t b_hi \\t f_lo \\t f_hi \\t pre ...[truncated]\n\nNOVELTY CLAIM: Four claims, each pinned to named prior work. None of them is 'our representation is better'.\n\n1. FIRST DOCUMENT-GROUNDED HYPER-RELATIONAL TEMPORAL KG WITH GOLD N-ARY ARGUMENT ROLES. Every existing HTKG benchmark is KG-distilled: Wiki-hy and YAGO-hy (Ding et al., Findings EMNLP 2024) come from Wikidata/YAGO; ChronoGrapher (Blin, Tiddi, van Trijp, ten Teije, Semantic Web Journal 2025) traverses DBpedia/Wikidata with best-first search; EventKG (Gottschalk & Demidova, ESWC 2018 / SWJ 2019) integrates existing KGs; ECS-KG (DKE 2025) uses unlabelled news. None has gold n-ary argument roles or a million gold temporal relations on the same documents. The ECTKG survey (Mathematics 2023, 11(23):4852) names exactly this missing resource \u2014 'a noticeable gap in annotated data that could support comprehensive end-to-end models' \u2014 so our motivation paragraph is written for us by a third party. Placement per the TKDE 2022 taxonomy: event-centric KG, instance-level, document-grounded; not an event-schema KG and not an event logic / eventuality graph (ASER, ATOMIC, GLUCOSE, ELG), which forecloses 'why not ASER' cheaply.\n\n2. FIRST EVENT-KG RELEASE CARRYING A PER-STATEMENT (VALID QUADRUPLE, PRECISION CODE, DERIVATION LAYER, CONFIDENCE) TUPLE. EventKG carries provenance and counts but no precision and no extraction confidence; HTKG carries fact-level timestamps but no provenance; Wikibase carries  ...[truncated]\n\nEFFORT (person-days): 18\n\nBUILD STEPS: D1-D3 (3.0 d) - Loader and core tables. Read EE/MAVEN-Arg/{train,valid}.jsonl (char-offset 'document', 'events[].argument' dict of role -> list of {entity_id} or {content, offset}, 'entities[].mention') and MAVEN_ERE/{train,valid}.jsonl (token-offset 'tokens'/'sentences', 'TIMEX', 'temporal_relations' dict, 'causal_relations' dict, 'subevent_relations' list). Join on doc_id and EVENT_ id only - no offset alignment needed for events, which removes the one real integration hazard (ARG is char-space, ERE is token-space). Emit documents, events, entities, unlinked_spans, participations, event_relations. Set in_ere=false for the 739 ARG-only events. Assign split by DOCUMENT over the 3,623 gold docs (2,900 MINE / 723 EVAL), seeded and hashed into the manifest; the 857 blind docs get split='BLIND', has_gold_args=false. Assert manifest counts against the measured facts: 80,479 events, 236,937 participations, 68,348 entities, 652 distinct properties. | D4-D5 (2.0 d) - TIMEX normaliser stage 1 (context-independent representation). HeidelTime-style CIR with UNDEF/X markers written to timex.cir_value. Regex families covering the measured 62.1% deterministic mass: bare year 29.9%, day-month-year 9.1%, month+year 6.9%, N+unit DURATION 6.1% (writes width_lo/width_hi, not endpoints), month+day+year 4.6%, ranges/decades/centuries/deictic 5.6%. Emit ISO-TimeML TIMEX3 profile values with x/X plac\n\nRISKS: THE BLIND TEST SPLIT CANNOT BE A GRAPH. Verified locally: both EE/MAVEN-Arg/test.jsonl and MAVEN_ERE/test.jsonl contain 'event_mentions' (mention-level, with a mention id and no EVENT_ coreference cluster id) instead of 'events'. No EVENT_ ids means no join between ARG and ERE and no event nodes. The project plan's 'split train/test' therefore CANNOT mean the official test split. The test/EVAL graph must be a document-level split inside the 3,623 gold docs. The 857 blind docs are usable only for gold-free consistency metrics after a coreference + argument extractor is run (which is precisely the Phase-1 layer). If this is discovered in October the evaluation section has no test set. Mitigation: the split is fixed and hashed on day 3 and announced to the team the same day. | ENTITY-STAR REACH IS 16.5% (139,093 of 843,808 temporally-related gold pairs). If support at the entity level turns out too thin after the ev1!=ev2 filter, the ordering family may yield very few constraints above support 10. Mitigation shipped in the same schema: pair_signatures aggregates to the type level so support can be counted over (property_1, property_2) across documents rather than over subjects, and ev\n\nGIVES UP: Honest accounting, angle by angle.\n\nGIVES UP REPRESENTATIONAL NOVELTY, deliberately. The internal store is standard reification / a statement-node graph, which is exactly what RDF 1.2 converged on (rdf:reifies + triple term + reifier == PaTeCon's sVertex) and what Iglesias-Molina et al. (ISWC 2023) measured as best for the inference/embedding consumer. So there is no representation contribution to claim, and we should not try: the paper says 'we chose the standards-endorsed model on engineering grounds' (DEC, arXiv 2606.15246, is explicitly agnostic between named graphs, RDF-star and classical reification, which is the license) and locates novelty in the artifact, the granularity adjudication, and the measured projection loss instead. An angle that invented a new hyper-relational temporal formalism could claim more here; it would also spend its 45 days arguing with reviewers about repres", "E-granularity-lattice": "DESIGN: GLANCE \u2014 Granularity-Lattice ANChored Chronology of Events\nAn event-centric KG in which every event carries a *set* of lattice-indexed temporal anchors (value + granularity node + minimal periodic set + day-chronon quadruple), granularity conflict is decided by periodic-set intersection-emptiness, and every ordering conflict is *factored* through the pair's join granularity into a granularity-attributable component and a granularity-independent residue.\n\nCORE IDEA: PRIMARY MODELLING COMMITMENT: time does not live on an event as an interval. It lives as an ANCHOR SET A(e) = {a_1..a_k}, each anchor being a reified statement node carrying (i) a TIMEX3-conformant value string with x/X placeholders, (ii) a pointer into an explicit GRANULARITY LATTICE table, (iii) a canonical MINIMAL PERIODIC SET (Bettini & Mascetti) denotation, (iv) a four-endpoint quadruple over a day chronon, (v) a derivation layer and confidence. The event's interval is a DERIVED, cached fusion of A(e), never the source of truth.\n\nThree consequences, each of which is a paper section:\n\n(1) GRANULARITY CONFLICT IS DECIDED, NOT INFERRED FROM PRECISION CODES. The naive rule \"two anchors with different precision codes \u21d2 conflict\" is almost pure false positive, because nesting is the normal case (Stubbs & Pustejovsky's narrative containers: a TIMEX is a container, nested containers are well-formed). GLANCE decides by intersection-emptiness of the MPS denotations after down-casting to the meet granularity, and adjudicates the outcome into five verdicts: NESTED_REFINEMENT, CROSS_GRANULAR_REFINED, INCOMPATIBLE, SUSPICIOUS_SPAN, UNDECIDABLE. Only INCOMPATIBLE is a conflict. This is the exact inversion of Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959), which measures granularity mismatch at 18.6% of its flagged conflicts and recommends normalising it away before labelling; we keep it and label it.\n\n(2) ORDERING CONSTRAINTS ARE GRANULARITY-INDEXED. Every ordering edge stores `witness_granularity` \u2014 the finest lattice node at which the relation is entailed by the anchor sets \u2014 and `entailed_at_gran`, the set of lattice nodes at which it survives Euzenat-style upward conversion. A relation is not simply true or false; it is true AT A GRANULARITY.\n\n(3) THE HEADLINE OPERATOR: ORDERING-CONFLICT FACTORISATION. Every detected ordering inconsistency (an STP negative cycle over the day chronon) is re-tested after coarsening all participating events to G* = join of their granularities. If the coarsened network becomes consistent, the conflict is GRANULARITY-ATTRIBUTABLE \u2014 it exists only because we imposed a precision the anchors do not support. If it survives at every level of the lattice up to \u22a4, it is RESIDUE \u2014 a genuine ordering conflict. The dual case (a coarse assertion falsified only after refinement by a finer anchor) is REFINEMENT_INDUCED. The paper's headline table is this three-way split, measured, on the gold layer, the rule-normalised layer, and a Phase-1-predicted layer.\n\nWhy this is the right angle for THIS corpus: the gold ordering graph is essentially conflict-free by annota ...[truncated]\n\nNODE/EDGE MODEL: NODE CLASSES (all document-scoped; entities are strictly document-local, so the ECKG is a disjoint union of 4,480 document graphs and cross-document generalisation happens only through TYPES):\n\n- Document \u2014 4,480 nodes. Carries `ref_time_chain`, the ordered list of DATE-typed TIMEX ids used as narrative reference times (HeidelTime narrative-domain rule: MAVEN is Wikipedia, there is no document creation time, so reference time = last mentioned DATE).\n- Event \u2014 80,479 nodes (79,740 shared with MAVEN-ERE). Type from the 168-type inventory. NO interval field; only a cached `event_temporal_profile` row.\n- EventMention \u2014 trigger occurrences; needed because MAVEN-Arg gives character offsets into `document` while MAVEN-ERE gives token offsets into `tokens[sent_id]`. Both are stored and reconciled here.\n- Entity \u2014 68,348 nodes, 7 types. 16,284 participate in \u22652 events (mean 3.15, max 59).\n- Timex \u2014 20,827 gold spans in train+valid, 9,287 distinct surfaces; types DATE, DURATION, TIME, PREPOSTEXP. Provided in every split including blind test.\n- Granularity \u2014 the lattice nodes. ~22 rows. This is a first-class node class, not a lookup enum.\n- ArgumentStatement (sVertex analogue) \u2014 236,937 reified nodes, one per (event, role, filler). 40.1% carry `entity_id`; 59.9% are bare {content, offset} spans with `entity_id = NULL` and `arg_kind = 'span'`.\n- TemporalAnchor (the core new node class) \u2014 one per (event, timex, link_relation) pair. Gold layer: 66,418 from CONTAINS + 496 SIMULTANEOUS + 122 BEGINS-ON + 39 ENDS-ON = 67,075.\n- Constraint \u2014 written by Phase 4, loaded/validated by Phase 2's loader.\n- Conflict \u2014 GranConflict and OrderConflict, each carrying a minimal hitting set (the exact-detection branch of the inconsistency survey, arXiv 2502.19023: output the minimal set of statements  ...[truncated]\n\nSCHEMA: Canonical release: Parquet, one directory per table, partitioned by `layer` then `split`. Path root `C:\\Reseach_Quang\\tempekg\\out\\v1\\`. DuckDB over the Parquet for all relational work; DuckPGQ (SQL/PGQ, SQL:2023) only for BEFORE-closure reachability. Kuzu is not used (repo archived 10 Oct 2025 \u2014 a reproducibility liability a reviewer can check in ten seconds). Optional secondary serialisation via pyoxigraph using RDF 1.2 vocabulary (triple term / rdf:reifies / reifier / triple annotation), justified by the two-serialisation argument from Iglesias-Molina et al. (ISWC 2023): reification measured best for embedding/inference consumers, qualifier-style for human/SPARQL consumers.\n\n-- ============ LATTICE (22 rows; hand-authored, version-controlled CSV) ============\nCREATE TABLE granularity (\n  gran_id            SMALLINT PRIMARY KEY,\n  label              TEXT NOT NULL,          -- 'DAY','MONTH','YEAR','DECADE','CENTURY','DAY_OF_YEAR',...\n  kind               TEXT NOT NULL,          -- anchored_partition | periodic_subgranularity\n                                             -- | unanchored_duration | named_period | top\n  precision_code     SMALLINT,               -- Wikibase-compatible: 6 mill,7 cent,8 dec,9 yr,10 mo,11 day\n                                             -- NULL for periodic subgranularities (NOT linearly ordered)\n  wikibase_precision SMALLINT,               -- NULL where Wikibase has no code (e.g. WEEK, QUARTER)\n  period_days        INTEGER,                -- 0 = aperiodic; 365.2425-cycle handled via 146097-day Gregorian cycle\n  nominal_width_days INTEGER,                -- 1, 30, 91, 365, 3652, 36524 ... used only for log_width_ratio\n  timex3_pattern     TEXT                    -- 'YYYY','YYYY-MM','YYYY-MM-DD','XXXX-MM-DD','XXXX-WXX-N',...\n);\nCREATE TABLE granularity_edge (\n  finer_id SMALLINT, coarser_id SMALLINT,\n  rel TEXT NOT NULL                          -- groups_into | groups_periodically_into | covers\n                                             -- | partitions | finer_than\n);\nCREATE TABLE gran_meet (g SMALLINT, h SMALLINT, meet_id SMALLINT);   -- 484 rows, precomputed\nCREATE TABLE gran_join (g SMALLINT, h SMALLINT, join_id SMALLINT);   -- 484 ...[truncated]\n\nINTERVALS: FOUR LAYERS, all stored, none derivable-away:\n\n(1) SURFACE + CIR. `surface` is the raw TIMEX string; `cir_value` is a HeidelTime-style context-independent representation with explicit UNDEF markers (UNDEF-year-11-11 for a year-less date, UNDEF-last-day for \"yesterday\", UNDEF-REF for \"the same day\"). The CIR is PERSISTED, not thrown away after disambiguation, because it is the only place granularity is directly observable and the conflict detector needs to know what was underspecified.\n\n(2) TIMEX3 VALUE + GRANULARITY POINTER. `timex3_value` is an ISO-8601 profile with x/X placeholders exactly as ISO-TimeML specifies ('1778', '1778-11', 'XXXX-11-11', 'XXXX-WXX-1'), plus `temporal_function`, `anchor_timex_id`, `valueFromFunction`-equivalent `resolution_rule`. Granularity is a FOREIGN KEY into the lattice table, and `precision_code` is a Wikibase-compatible integer for anchored partitions only. Storing (value, precision) Wikidata-style rather than collapsing to an interval is the make-or-break decision: if 'in 1778' becomes [1778-01-01, 1778-12-31] and nothing else is kept, granularity conflict becomes indistinguishable from ordinary interval containment and the second conflict type collapses into the first.\n\n(3) MINIMAL PERIODIC SET (the denotation). Canonical JSON:\n  {\"P\": <period in days; 0 = aperiodic single interval>,\n   \"phase\": [[o_lo, o_hi], ...],        // day offsets within [0,P), sorted, pairwise disjoint\n   \"window\": [w_lo, w_hi],              // day-chronon bounds on the whole set; \u00b1SENTINEL if unbounded\n   \"gran_id\": <int>}\nCanonicalisation: P minimal, phases normalised into [0,P), merged and sorted. Examples:\n  '1778'        \u2192 {\"P\":0, \"phase\":[[0,364]], \"window\":[D(1778-01-01), D(1778-12-31)], \"gran_id\":YEAR}\n  'November 11' \u2192 {\"P\":146097, \"phase\":[Nov-11 off ...[truncated]\n\nORDERING CONFLICT: STAGE 1 \u2014 DOWN-CAST TO A SIMPLE TEMPORAL PROBLEM. Per document, create two integer point variables per event (b_e, f_e) and per TIMEX (b_t, f_t). Emit only difference constraints:\n  profile bounds:  b_e \u2265 b\u207b,  b_e \u2264 b\u207a,  f_e \u2265 f\u207b,  f_e \u2264 f\u207a,  b_e \u2264 f_e\n  BEFORE(i,j):     f_i \u2212 b_j \u2264 \u22121\n  CONTAINS(i,j):   b_i \u2212 b_j \u2264 0  \u2227  f_j \u2212 f_i \u2264 0\n  SIMULTANEOUS:    b_i = b_j \u2227 f_i = f_j  (four inequalities)\n  OVERLAP(i,j):    b_i \u2212 f_j \u2264 0 \u2227 b_j \u2212 f_i \u2264 0   (intersection non-empty ONLY \u2014 MAVEN's OVERLAP is vaguer than Allen `o`, and over-committing here would manufacture conflicts)\n  BEGINS-ON(e,t):  b_e = b_t;  ENDS-ON(e,t): f_e = f_t\n  CONTAINS(t,e):   b_t \u2212 b_e \u2264 0 \u2227 f_e \u2212 f_t \u2264 0\nEvery constraint is a difference constraint, so the network is an STP (Dechter, Meiri & Pearl, AI 49, 1991): Floyd\u2013Warshall on the distance graph decides consistency COMPLETELY in O(n\u00b3), and a conflict is witnessed by a negative cycle. Not a heuristic, not an approximation \u2014 this is the exact, explainable, non-mined baseline detector, and it costs ~4.4\u00d710\u2078 integer ops corpus-wide.\n\nWHY `disjoint` IS EXCLUDED (one paragraph, and almost no KG-conflict paper writes it): interval disjointness is (f_i < b_j) \u2228 (f_j < b_i), a genuine disjunction of strict inequalities, which is not ORD-Horn. ORD-Horn is a maximal tractable subclass of Allen's algebra on which path consistency is a complete cubic decision procedure (Nebel & B\u00fcrckert, JACM 42(1), 1995), and the full classification into 18 maximal tractable subalgeb ...[truncated]\n\nGRANULARITY CONFLICT: SCOPE (this is what keeps the class well-posed). Only CONJUNCTIVE anchor sets are tested: same event, same document, same layer. MAVEN-ERE annotates one timeline per document, so all of that document's CONTAINS links on a single event must jointly hold, and an empty intersection is a genuine error rather than epistemic disagreement. Cross-layer anchor disagreement (gold vs model_pred) is DISJUNCTIVE and produces a `layer_disagreement` row, never a conflict \u2014 Wikidata explicitly permits mutually incompatible dates as separate statements for one entity+property.\n\nALGORITHM (per event with n_anchors \u2265 2; the target population is 17,102 events \u2192 \u226538,226 anchor pairs: 10,653 events \u00d7 1 pair + 3,707 \u00d7 3 + 2,742 \u00d7 \u22656):\n  1. Canonicalise each anchor to its minimal periodic set.\n  2. Pairwise: meet_gran = gran_meet[g_i][g_j]; compute the Allen-13 bitmask between the two denotations at meet_gran; compute intersect_empty by periodic-set intersection (lcm capped at the exact 146,097-day Gregorian cycle); compute precision_delta = |pc_i \u2212 pc_j|, NULL if either anchor is a periodic subgranularity.\n  3. Fuse: D(e) = \u22c2_i MPS_i.\n  4. Adjudicate into five verdicts:\n     \u2022 NESTED_REFINEMENT \u2014 the anchors form a \u2286-chain and D \u2260 \u2205. BENIGN, and predicted to be the large majority ('usually NESTED granularity'). This is exactly Stubbs & Pustejovsky's narrative containers: a TIMEX is a container, nested containers are well-formed annotation, and the fused profile is simply the innermost container.\n   ...[truncated]\n\nINTERFACE TO P3/P4: Phase 2 ships a Python package `tempekg.glance` (modules: `ingest.py`, `lattice.py`, `mps.py`, `normalise.py`, `anchors.py`, `fuse.py`, `stp.py`, `granconf.py`, `export.py`) plus a versioned Parquet directory and a `MANIFEST.json` carrying SHA-256 per file, row counts, the lattice CSV hash, and the split assignment. Nothing downstream reads the raw MAVEN jsonl. Three contract-test suites (`tests/contract_p3.py`, `tests/contract_p4.py`, `tests/contract_lattice.py`) run in CI and must pass before any handoff; each failure names the violated clause below.\n\n=== TO PHASE 3 (Vinh \u2014 projection ECKG \u2192 Temporal KG for PaTeCon reuse) ===\n\nDELIVERABLE P3-1: `p3_statement_view.parquet`, one row per argument statement, already joined to the event's fused temporal profile so Vinh never touches anchors:\n  stmt_id, doc_id, split, layer,\n  subject_entity_id,            -- NULL for the 59.9% bare spans; see clause C3\n  property,                     -- 'EventType#Role', 652 distinct, e.g. 'Killing#Victim', 'Attack#Attacker'\n  object_event_id, event_type, role,\n  start_lo, start_hi, end_lo, end_hi,     -- day-chronon ints copied from event_temporal_profile\n  gran_id, precision_code, meet_gran, fusion_status, anchor_class,\n  truth,                        -- POS | UNK   (UNK where anchor_class = UNANCHORED or fusion_status = UNDECIDABLE)\n  time_provenance TEXT[],       -- the anchor_ids that produced the interval\n  confidence\nCLAUSES: (C1) Direction is ENTITY-AS-SUBJECT \u2014 (entity_x, 'EventType#Rol ...[truncated]\n\nNOVELTY CLAIM: Stated as four claims, each pinned against named prior work, and one explicit non-claim.\n\nNON-CLAIM, made first and loudly so it cannot be used against us: GLANCE does NOT claim to be the first to formalise inconsistency across temporal granularities. That was done by Bettini, Wang & Jajodia (Annals of Mathematics and AI 1998; Artificial Intelligence 140:107-152, 2002), who define multi-granularity temporal constraint networks, prove consistency NP-hard in general, and give sound-and-complete plus approximate algorithms; and by Euzenat (Computational Intelligence 17(4), 2001), who defines upward/downward granularity-conversion operators over relation algebras with six proved independence constraints and explicitly says merging representations of different granularity raises problems. Any sentence of the form \"we are the first to formally define granularity conflict\" is false and a TIME/temporal-DB reviewer will catch it. We also do NOT claim that granularity mismatch is un-noticed empirically: Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959) names 'information granularity differences' as the first category of its inconsistency taxonomy and measures it at 18.6% of flagged conflicts (against 74.2% genuine, 7.2% normalisation error). That paper is cited in the first paragraph of related work; discovering it at review time would be fatal.\n\nCLAIM 1 (the inversion, against Kontrast). Kontrast's explicit  ...[truncated]\n\nEFFORT (person-days): 26\n\nBUILD STEPS: S1 (2.0 d) \u2014 Ingest and offset reconciliation. Load MAVEN-Arg (id, title, document, events[{id,type,type_id,mention[{trigger_word,offset}],argument{role:[{content,offset}|{entity_id}]}}], entities, negative_triggers) and MAVEN-ERE (id, tokens, sentences, events[{mention[{sent_id,offset}]}], TIMEX, temporal_relations, causal_relations, subevent_relations). CRITICAL AND ALREADY VERIFIED IN THE DATA: MAVEN-Arg offsets are CHARACTER offsets into the flat `document` string; MAVEN-ERE offsets are TOKEN offsets into `tokens[sent_id]`. Build a per-document char\u2194(sent_id, tok) alignment table by re-tokenising `sentences` and cumulative-summing, assert round-trip on every one of the 80,479 events, and store both offset systems on event_mention. Join events on event_id (79,740 shared) and log the 739 Arg-only events. Emit document, event, event_mention, entity, entity_mention, argument_statement, timex, split. Contract test: no NULL char offsets, no unaligned TIMEX. | S2 (3.0 d) \u2014 Lattice and periodic-set engine. Hand-author granularity.csv (22 rows) and granularity_edge.csv with the glossary edge types; compute the transitive closure of \u2291_c, verify it is a bounded lattice with \u22a5 = DAY and \u22a4 = ALL_TIME, and materialise gran_meet / gran_join (484 rows each). Implement mps.py: canonical form, normalisation, union, intersection with the 146,097-day Gregorian-cycle lcm cap, emptiness test, wi\n\nRISKS: THE WHOLE HEADLINE RESTS ON AN UNSUPERVISED NORMALISER. TIMEX has no normalised value field and there is zero gold supervision, yet INCOMPATIBLE verdicts are exactly normaliser disagreements. If the reference-time propagation is wrong at, say, a 15% rate, then a large share of detected granularity conflicts are our own bugs \u2014 which is precisely the 7.2% normalisation-error bucket Kontrast measured, scaled up. MITIGATION: the UNDECIDABLE verdict is mandatory and its rate is reported; a 200-item stratified manual audit with per-family accuracy is a deliverable, not optional; and every conflict carries a JSON proof a human can check in ten seconds. | THE VERDICT DISTRIBUTION MAY BE ALL-BENIGN. Multi-anchor events are described as usually NESTED. If the measured distribution comes out at, e.g., 96% NESTED_REFINEMENT / 3.5% CROSS_GRANULAR_REFINED / 0.4% INCOMPATIBLE, the natural INCOMPATIBLE population is ~70 events \u2014 the same order as the ~72 genuine gold contradictions, i.e. no better a detection target than ordering. MITIGATION: the paper's primary claims must be (a) the characterisation itself, which is publishable as a resource finding regardless of the split, and (b) performance o\n\nGIVES UP: VERSUS AN ORDERING-CENTRIC ANGLE (maximum PaTeCon fidelity). We deprioritise the conflict type with the overwhelming data mass: 1.04M BEFORE plus 152K CONTAINS relations versus ~38,226 anchor pairs. A reviewer who wants a clean head-to-head against PaTeCon on its own three families gets a thinner story, and Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025) already occupy Allen-consistency-on-MAVEN-ERE, so the ordering half is defensive positioning rather than contribution. We also cannot claim completeness over PaTeCon's head language, because we drop `disjoint` to stay in ORD-Horn and route mutual exclusion to a MaxSAT path with no completeness guarantee.\n\nVERSUS AN ENTITY/PROJECTION-CENTRIC ANGLE. Making time a lattice-indexed property of the event node, faithfully inherited by statements, means the projected entity timelines have heterogeneous precision. An angle that force-normalises every anchor t", "C-constraint-network": "DESIGN: ECN \u2014 the Event Constraint Network (ECKG whose temporal core is a lexicographically-weighted Simple Temporal Network over event endpoints)\nPhase 2 stores the ECKG's temporal layer not as labelled relation edges but as a per-document Simple Temporal Network over event ENDPOINTS with weights in the lexicographically ordered group Z x Z = (days, epsilon), so that MAVEN-ERE relations are binary difference constraints, TIMEX anchors are unary difference constraints against a global day origin, and BOTH ordering conflict and granularity conflict are the SAME decision procedure \u2014 a negative cycle in the distance graph \u2014 distinguished only by the support and the weight component of the witnessing cycle.\n\nCORE IDEA: Every event e in the ECKG is compiled into exactly two constraint variables, b_e (begin point) and f_e (end point), living in a day chronon indexed from a single global origin Z = 0001-01-01 (proleptic Gregorian, day 0). Every temporal assertion the corpus supplies \u2014 1.04M BEFORE, 152K CONTAINS, 9.9K SIMULTANEOUS, 9.9K OVERLAP, 639 BEGINS-ON, 380 ENDS-ON event-event relations; 66,418 CONTAINS + 496 SIMULTANEOUS + 122 BEGINS-ON + 39 ENDS-ON event-TIMEX anchors \u2014 translates without loss into a CONJUNCTION of difference constraints of the form (y - x <= w). No disjunction is required for any of them. Therefore the whole gold temporal layer is a Simple Temporal Problem in the sense of Dechter, Meiri & Pearl (AI 1991): consistency is decided COMPLETELY in O(n^3) by Floyd-Warshall on the distance graph, the minimal network is the all-pairs shortest-path matrix, and inconsistency is witnessed by a negative cycle. Relative to Allen's algebra the Tier-1 language is a set of basic pointisable relations, hence inside ORD-Horn (Nebel & Burckert, JACM 1995), hence path consistency is a complete decision procedure \u2014 a positional claim in the Krokhin/Jeavons/Jonsson (JACM 2003) classification of the 18 maximal tractable subalgebras that essentially no KG conflict-detection paper makes.\n\nThe one non-obvious move that makes this work on real data: weights are pairs (d, k) in Z x Z ordered LEXICOGRAPHICALLY, where d counts days and k counts infinitesimal epsilon units. Qualitative strictness lives entirely in the epsilon component and calendar granularity lives entirely in the day component. BEFORE(e1,e2) compiles to f_e1 - b_e2 <= (0, -1): strictly before at sub-chronon resolution, but ZERO days, so a BEFORE pair both of whose events are CONTAINS-anchored to the same day is satisfiable rather than spuriously unsatisfiable. This matters at scale: naive strict-at-day-resolution encoding would make a large fraction of the 1.04M BEFORE edges collide with the 66,418 day-level CONTAINS anchors and manufacture false conflicts; naive non-strict encoding makes a BEFORE cycle sum to exactly (0,0) and be satisfied by collapsing all points to one instant, i.e. a systematic FALSE NEGATIVE on precisely the ordering-conflict class the project targets. The lexicographic group removes both failure modes at once and Floyd-Warshall is unchanged because it only needs an ordered abelian group. Implementation is a single packed int64: w_packed = d * 2^21 + k, with |k| bounded by the per-document edge count (max ~10^4), so integer comparison IS lexicographic comparison and the closure runs as  ...[truncated]\n\nNODE/EDGE MODEL: TWO LAYERS, ONE STORE. The ECKG is a union of 4,480 document-local subgraphs (justified, not apologised for: 0 of 68,348 entities appear in more than one document, so there is no cross-document component to reason over; cross-doc coreference \u2014 xCoRe EMNLP 2025 and the CD-ECR line \u2014 is explicitly deferred).\n\nLAYER A \u2014 the hyper-relational content layer (a document-grounded HTKG in the sense of Ding et al., Findings EMNLP 2024, but the first one grounded in raw text with gold n-ary argument roles rather than distilled from Wikidata/YAGO):\n  - Event node: one per MAVEN event mention, carrying event_type (168 values), trigger span, doc_id. Time lives HERE as the source of truth (EventKG/SEM precedent: sem:hasBeginTimeStamp on the event resource, not on the participation edge). This is why edge-labelled TKG quadruples (s,r,o,t) are refused: replicating one interval across an event's up-to-many argument edges is exactly the degenerate-predicate problem already diagnosed.\n  - Entity node: one per document-local entity_id (7 types). 40.1% of arguments attach to one; the other 59.9% attach to a BareSpan node keyed by (doc_id, char offsets) so the primary triple is never dropped.\n  - Argument statement (reified, PaTeCon sVertex-shaped, which is also RDF 1.2's own reifier design \u2014 triple term + rdf:reifies + reifier): (event_id, role, arg_span | entity_id) with a time_invariance flag (HTKG's TI vs time-dependent split: event_type and role-fillers are TI within the event; the anchoring TIMEX is time-dependent). Reification is chosen on the ISWC 2023 (Iglesias-Molina et al.) finding that standard reification is most consistent for embedding/inference consumers, plus DEC (arXiv 2606.15246) explicitly licensing an engineering-grounds choice; the ~4x triple blow-up (Hernandez, Hogan &  ...[truncated]\n\nSCHEMA: -- All tables Parquet, partitioned by layer_id then doc_id. DuckDB DDL shown.\n-- Sources: C:\\Reseach_Quang\\EE\\MAVEN-Arg\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl\n--          C:\\Reseach_Quang\\MAVEN_ERE\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl\n\nCREATE TABLE layer (\n  layer_id      SMALLINT PRIMARY KEY,      -- 0 gold, 1 rule_norm_timex, 2 model_pred, 3+ perturbed_*\n  name          VARCHAR, derivation VARCHAR,  -- 'gold' | 'regex+refprop+gaz' | 'PAIE+DEEIA+ERE-clf' | 'perturb:year_shift'\n  parent_layer  SMALLINT, created_at TIMESTAMP, code_sha VARCHAR, manifest_sha256 VARCHAR);\n\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n  doc_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, split VARCHAR,      -- 'mine' | 'eval' | 'blind'  (DOCUMENT-disjoint)\n  n_events INT, n_timex INT, n_points INT, n_cons INT,\n  grounded BOOLEAN, origin_kind VARCHAR,          -- 'GLOBAL_Z' | 'FREE_Zd'\n  ref_time_chain_len INT);                        -- length of the narrative reference-time chain used in normalisation\n\nCREATE TABLE events (\n  event_id VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR, layer_id SMALLINT,\n  event_type VARCHAR,                             -- 168 values: 'Killing','Attack','Process_start','Military_operation','Hostile_encounter',...\n  trigger_text VARCHAR, sent_id INT, tok_start INT, tok_end INT,\n  b_point_id BIGINT, f_point_id BIGINT, confidence DOUBLE);\n\nCREATE TABLE entities (\n  entity_id VARCHAR, doc_id VARCHAR, layer_id SMALLINT,\n  entity_type VARCHAR,                            -- Location|Other|Organization|Person|Product|Building|Art\n  canonical_mention VARCHAR, n_events INT,        -- 16,284 entities have n_events>=2, mean 3.15, max 59\n  PRIMARY KEY (entity_id, layer_id));\n\nCREATE TABLE arg_statements (                     -- reified sVertex-shaped; the hyper-relational qualifier set\n  stmt_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY, doc_id VARCHAR, layer_id SMALLINT,\n  event_id VARCHAR, role VARCHAR,                 -- 143 roles; NONE of them is Time/Date/When\n  entity_id VARCHAR,                              -- NULL for the 59.9% bare spans\n  bare_span_id BIGINT, arg_text VARCHAR, char_start INT, char_end INT,\n  property VARCHAR,                               -- materialised 'EventType#Role', 652 distinct: 'Killing#Victim','Attack#Attacker',...\n  time_invariant  ...[truncated]\n\nINTERVALS: FOUR representations, each with a distinct job. Nothing is fuzzy (Schockaert & De Cock / Ohlbach are cited as the deliberately rejected alternative: no supervision exists for membership functions and fuzziness destroys the crisp emptiness test that makes granularity conflict decidable).\n\n1. DAY CHRONON, absolute. All positions are int64 day indices from a single origin Z = 0001-01-01 proleptic Gregorian. One resolution for the whole corpus. Documents with no resolvable anchor get a free origin Z_d and are marked grounded=FALSE. Day is the right chronon: MAVEN's TIMEX mass is DATE-dominated, TIME is rare, and Reimers et al. (ACL 2016) already established day-granularity event anchoring with coarser-than-day tags allowed.\n\n2. (value, precision) PAIR, Wikibase-style, on every anchor. precision in {8 decade, 9 year, 10 month, 11 day}. '1778' is stored as (resolved_value='1778', precision=9, lo_day=D(1778-01-01), hi_day=D(1778-12-31)) and '11 November 1778' as (precision=11, lo_day=hi_day=D(1778-11-11)). The interval is DERIVED from (value, precision); the precision code is never inferred back from the interval. Without this, granularity conflict is literally indistinguishable from ordinary interval containment and the second conflict type collapses into the first.\n\n3. MINIMAL PERIODIC SET for underspecified TIMEXes (Bettini & Mascetti), keyed to the ISO-TimeML X-placeholder syntax. 'November 11' with no year -> cir_value='XXXX-11-11', is_periodic=TRUE, period_num=1, period_unit='YEAR', period_offsets=[314] (day-of-year offset). 'Sunday' -> period_num=7, period_unit='DAY'. These NEVER enter Tier 1 as intervals, because a periodic set is a disjunctive unary constraint and would push the network out of STP into NP-hard multi-granularity TCSP (Bettini, Wang & Jajodia, AMAI 1998 ...[truncated]\n\nORDERING CONFLICT: COMPILATION TABLE (reading: dst - src <= (ub_days, ub_eps); every row is one tconstraint tuple):\n  AXIOM(e)                : (f_e -> b_e, (0,0))                                          [b_e <= f_e]\n  BEFORE(e1,e2)           : (b_2 -> f_1, (0,-1))                                         [f_1 < b_2 strictly, 0 days]\n  CONTAINS(e1,e2)         : (b_2 -> b_1, (0,0)) ; (f_1 -> f_2, (0,0))                    [b_1<=b_2, f_2<=f_1]\n  SIMULTANEOUS(e1,e2)     : (b_2->b_1,(0,0)) ; (b_1->b_2,(0,0)) ; (f_2->f_1,(0,0)) ; (f_1->f_2,(0,0))\n  OVERLAP(e1,e2)          : (b_2->b_1,(0,-1)) ; (f_1->b_2,(0,-1)) ; (f_2->f_1,(0,-1))    [b_1<b_2<f_1<f_2]\n  BEGINS-ON(e1,e2)        : (b_2->b_1,(0,0)) ; (b_1->b_2,(0,0))\n  ENDS-ON(e1,e2)          : (f_2->f_1,(0,0)) ; (f_1->f_2,(0,0))\n  ANCHOR CONTAINS(t,e)    : (b_e -> Z, (-lo_day,0)) ; (Z -> f_e, (hi_day,0))             [b_e>=lo, f_e<=hi]\n  ANCHOR BEGINS-ON(t,e)   : (b_e -> Z, (-lo_day,0)) ; (Z -> b_e, (hi_day,0))\n  ANCHOR WIDTH (DURATION) : (b_e -> f_e, (dur_hi,0)) ; (f_e -> b_e, (-dur_lo,0))\n  SUBEVENT-implied CONTAINS: same rows as CONTAINS but source_kind='subevent_implied', in_core=FALSE \u2014 the 1,638/12,019 (13.6%) subevent pairs lacking an annotated CONTAINS are INCOMPLETENESS, and compiling them into the core would manufacture the very edges that make the graph look consistent. They are a separately ablatable family, and the contradiction-vs-incompleteness split (Kontrast's 4th category; GGD-style existence heads) becomes reportable.\n\nDETECTION. Per ...[truncated]\n\nGRANULARITY CONFLICT: SAME NETWORK, SAME ALGORITHM, SAME NEGATIVE-CYCLE TEST. The only thing that changes is the support of the witnessing cycle: a granularity conflict is a negative cycle whose edge support touches the two endpoints of exactly ONE event plus unary edges incident on the origin Z. That is the design's unification claim, stated operationally.\n\nG1 \u2014 GENUINE GRANULARITY CONFLICT (unsatisfiable anchor set on one event).\nWorked example: e3 Killing (\"killed\") in a document about an 18th-century engagement. Gold anchors after normalisation: CONTAINS(t2='11 November 1778', e3) with precision=11, lo=hi=D(1778-11-11); and (L3 perturbation, or an L1 normaliser reference-time error) CONTAINS(t3='1779', e3) with precision=9, lo=D(1779-01-01), hi=D(1779-12-31). Let Z' = D(1778-01-01) for readability, so D(1778-11-11) = Z'+314, D(1778-12-31) = Z'+364, D(1779-01-01) = Z'+365.\nEdges: (Z -> f_3, (314,0)) from t2's hi ; (b_3 -> Z, (-365,0)) from t3's lo ; (f_3 -> b_3, (0,0)) AXIOM.\nCycle Z -> f_3 -> b_3 -> Z: (314,0) + (0,0) + (-365,0) = (-51,0). w_days = -51 < 0 => UNSAT.\nReported: conflict_type=G1_ANCHOR_UNSAT, cycle_len=3, n_distinct_events=1, support_timex_ids=[t2,t3], precision_pair=[11,9], min_hitting_set = {anchor(t3,e3)} because t2 is layer L0 gold (+inf cost) and t3 came from L1/L3. Note the conflict is FOUND by the identical Floyd-Warshall pass that finds O1/O2 \u2014 there is no separate granularity detector to build, tune, or defend.\n\nNESTING IS NEVER FLAGGED, BY CONSTRUCTION. The 17,102 event ...[truncated]\n\nINTERFACE TO P3/P4: Phase 2 (Nhut) publishes exactly two frozen contracts plus one write-back API. Everything else is internal and may change.\n\n=== CONTRACT A: phase3_input_v1 (Vinh, projection ECKG -> Temporal KG for PaTeCon) ===\nFiles (Parquet, partitioned by layer_set then doc_id), plus manifest.json with per-file SHA-256 and row counts:\n  1. event_extent.parquet  \u2014 exactly ONE row per (event_id, layer_set). Guaranteed: no duplicates, no NULL status, status in {SAT, UNSAT, UNKNOWN}, b_lo<=b_hi<=f_hi and b_lo<=f_lo<=f_hi whenever status='SAT', b_lo/b_hi = NULL sentinel (-2^62 / +2^62) for unbounded.\n  2. arg_statements.parquet \u2014 with the `property` column PRE-MATERIALISED as 'EventType#Role' (652 distinct, 623 with support >=10). Vinh does not compute this string; Phase 2 owns the vocabulary file property_vocab_v1.tsv.\n  3. entities.parquet, events.parquet, timex.parquet, documents.parquet (split column: mine | eval | blind, DOCUMENT-disjoint).\n  4. stmt_extent (SQL view, definition frozen in phase3_views_v1.sql) \u2014 materialises event-node time onto each statement so PaTeCon's sVertex can be filled directly:\n       SELECT s.stmt_id, s.entity_id AS subject, s.property, s.event_id AS object,\n              x.b_lo AS start_lo, x.b_hi AS start_hi, x.f_lo AS finish_lo, x.f_hi AS finish_hi,\n              s.confidence AS weight, x.status AS truth\n       FROM arg_statements s JOIN event_extent x USING (event_id, layer_set)\n       WHERE s.entity_id IS NOT NULL;\n     Phase 3's projection is therefore mech ...[truncated]\n\nNOVELTY CLAIM: Specific, and scoped to survive the named prior art.\n\nWHAT IS CLAIMED: the first event-centric knowledge graph whose temporal layer is STORED AND RELEASED as an explicit constraint network over event endpoints, in which (a) TIMEX anchors are unary constraints and MAVEN-ERE relations are binary constraints in one single-granularity Simple Temporal Problem, (b) conflict detection is unsatisfiability rather than pattern matching, and (c) ORDERING and GRANULARITY conflict are the SAME decision procedure \u2014 a negative cycle in a lexicographically weighted difference graph \u2014 distinguished only by the support and the weight component of the witnessing cycle. The enabling technical device, which I believe is genuinely new in this literature, is the LEXICOGRAPHIC (day, epsilon) WEIGHT GROUP: it lets strict qualitative order and day-granularity calendar anchoring coexist in one STP, and it is demonstrably necessary \u2014 non-strict encoding makes every BEFORE cycle sum to zero and be satisfied by point collapse (a systematic false negative on the target conflict class), strict-at-day encoding makes BEFORE pairs sharing a day-level CONTAINS anchor spuriously unsatisfiable. Plus: an explicit declaration of the relation language's position in the Allen tractability classification, which essentially no KG conflict-detection paper provides.\n\nAGAINST NAMED WORK:\n- Dechter, Meiri & Pearl (AI 1991) \u2014 ...[truncated]\n\nEFFORT (person-days): 20\n\nBUILD STEPS: D1-D2 (2d) INGEST + SPLIT. Join C:\\Reseach_Quang\\EE\\MAVEN-Arg\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl with C:\\Reseach_Quang\\MAVEN_ERE\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl on doc_id (100% overlap, 79,740 shared event ids; log and quarantine the 80,479-79,740 = 739 Arg-only events as a named exclusion set). Emit documents/events/entities/arg_statements Parquet with layer_id=0, `property` = 'EventType#Role' pre-materialised, and property_vocab_v1.tsv (652 strings). Build the DOCUMENT-disjoint mine/eval split stratified on n_events and anchor_class; write splits.json. Deliverable: row counts matching the measured facts, committed as a regression test. | D3-D4 (2d) CALENDAR + PERIODIC-SET MODULE. day_index(date) over proleptic Gregorian from 0001-01-01; granule_expand(value, precision) -> (lo_day, hi_day) for precision codes 8/9/10/11; minimal periodic set type with canonical form, intersect(periodic, interval), intersect(periodic, periodic), is_empty, is_singleton. Lexicographic weight type as packed int64 (d*2^21 + k) with add/compare and overflow assertions. Full unit-test suite including leap years, decade/century granules, and the 'XXXX-11-11' / 'XXXX-WXX-7' placeholder forms. This module is the correctness foundation for everything downstream; do not skip the tests. | D5-D8 (4d) TIMEX NORMALISER, two stages, HeidelTime-shaped. Stage 1 -> cir_value with UNDEF/X markers for all 20,827 gold spans, covering\n\nRISKS: THE GOLD NETWORK WILL BE SATISFIABLE ALMOST EVERYWHERE. With 0 BEFORE cycles across 843,808 pairs, 0 symmetry violations, 100% transitive closure and ~72 genuine contradictions corpus-wide, Tier-1 closure over L0 will report essentially nothing. That is a correctness proof for the encoding, not an evaluation. Mitigation: the derivation-layer axis is first-class from D1, and every reported detection number comes from L2 (Phase-1 predicted) or L3 (perturbed) layers. If the layer machinery slips, the paper has no numbers at all \u2014 this is the single largest schedule risk and is why the layer writer is D18, not D40. | THE LEXICOGRAPHIC ENCODING MIGHT MISFIRE AT SCALE. 1.04M BEFORE edges against 66,418 day-level CONTAINS anchors is exactly the collision regime. If any strictness leaks into the day component, thousands of spurious UNSAT appear; if epsilon is dropped, BEFORE cycles become zero-weight and undetectable. Mitigation: the D11 early-validation gate on the full corpus, before the conflict typer is written, with the documented fallback (non-strict plus explicit zero-cycle detection). | NORMALISER COVERAGE CAPS GRANULARITY-CONFLICT RECALL. Every UNRESOLVED or PERIODIC_UNRESOLVED an\n\nGIVES UP: Honest accounting, both against the other candidate angles and in absolute terms.\n\nGIVEN UP RELATIVE TO A REPRESENTATION/RESOURCE ANGLE (build the richest possible document-grounded HTKG and sell the artifact): this design spends 8 of 20 days on machinery (calendar module, closure engine, conflict typer) that produces no new content in the graph. It emits no KG embeddings, no link-prediction numbers, no learned baseline \u2014 so it forecloses the StarE/HTKG-flavoured evaluation entirely, and the Egami et al. warning about representation as a confound becomes moot only because nothing learned is reported. If the reviewers wanted a benchmark paper, this angle looks thin on data-description and heavy on formalism.\n\nGIVEN UP RELATIVE TO A PATTERN-MATCHING / PaTeCon-FAITHFUL ANGLE: pattern-based conflict detection is far easier to present. 'Constraint C says Killing#Victim events with the same vi", "D-provenance-multiversion": "DESIGN: PROV-ECKG: a three-layer, provenance-first multi-version Event-Centric Knowledge Graph over MAVEN-Arg + MAVEN-ERE\nOne physical store holding the SAME 4,480 documents at three measured extraction-noise levels (L0 gold, L1 gold-trigger+predicted-argument, L2 fully-predicted) with a (valid interval, precision code, derivation layer, confidence) tuple on every statement, so that conflict detection is a differential, confidence-weighted query across layers instead of an injected benchmark \u2014 and the noise dial is literally `WHERE layer_id = :L`.\n\nCORE IDEA: Repurpose the transaction-time axis of a bitemporal property graph (Bitemporal Property Graphs, ADBIS 2025; Gradoop TPGM, VLDBJ 2021; AeonG, PVLDB 17(6) 2024 \u2014 cited as models, NOT adopted as engines) as a DERIVATION axis. Valid time stays what it always was (the TIMEX-derived interval of the event in the world); the second axis records HOW the row came to exist: gold annotation, rule-normalised time, or model prediction, each with a calibrated confidence. Because MAVEN-Arg and MAVEN-ERE annotate the SAME 4,480 documents (100% doc overlap, 79,740 shared event ids), and because Phase 1's extractors are already trained, the same document can be materialised three times inside one store and joined row-for-row on a canonical event key.\n\nThat single change buys four things Phase 2 currently cannot deliver:\n\n(1) It de-orphans Phase 1 structurally, not rhetorically. The extractors stop being a dead branch and become the graph's error generator. L0-vs-L2 diff on a shared canonical key yields thousands of naturally distributed, automatically labelled temporal/argument errors (SPURIOUS / MISSING / ROLE_SWAP / TIME_SHIFT / GRAN_DROP) whose generating process \u2014 a PAIE/DEEIA/SCPRG argument decoder with no temporal component whatsoever \u2014 is provably independent of anything Nhan mines. Cite Contrastive KG Error Detection (AAAI 2024 / CIKM 2022) for the measured ~30% recall drop when detectors validated on synthetic noise meet real errors, and WikiConflict (K-CAP 2025) + Wikidata-edit-history refinement (arXiv 2210.15495) as the accepted precedent for sourcing errors from an independent human/system error process.\n\n(2) It kills the circularity as a *measured quantity* rather than an argument. The paper's central table becomes a provenance matrix: rows = error sources (S1 L2-predicted diff, S2 confusion-sampled TIMEX perturbation, S3 human-authored, S4 the ~72 native gold contradictions, S5 rule-negating injection), columns = detectors. \"Circularity gap\" = S5-column-max minus S1-column-max. Constraints are mined on L0 restricted to the MINE split, serialised, SHA-256'd and committed before any error exists (holdout rule mining, arXiv 1110.6652).\n\n(3) Gold's near-emptiness stops being a problem. 0 BEFORE cycles / 0 symmetric violations across 843,808 pairs is not a shortage of targets once L0 is a *reference layer* rather than the mining target: any symmetry violation or BEFORE cycle appearing in L2 is, by construction, an extraction error with a free label, because gold has exactly zero of them.\n\n(4) Granularity becomes attributable. A precision code lives on the TIMEX v ...[truncated]\n\nNODE/EDGE MODEL: NODES (every node row is keyed by (layer_id, node_id) and carries ckey_id, prov_json, conf):\n- Document. Layer-invariant (the text is the text). Carries ere_split and tempekg_split (MINE|EVAL, document-disjoint).\n- Event. Per layer. In L0/L1 the id is the gold `EVENT_*` (event coreference already collapsed by MAVEN-Arg/ERE). In L2 the id is minted by the ED model and aligned to a ckey by trigger character-offset match. Carries ev_type (168), type_conf, trigger_conf. TIME LIVES HERE (interval4), following EventKG/SEM, not on any edge.\n- EventMention. Per layer. The offset-bearing node; the only place char/token offsets are authoritative.\n- Entity. Layer-invariant in practice: entities (ids, 7 types, mention offsets) are PROVIDED as input in every split including blind test, so entity_src = 'gold_given' in all three layers. This is a large gift and the design leans on it: the subject side of the entity-as-subject projection is noise-free even in L2, which isolates the noise dial to arguments/triggers/relations/time.\n- PseudoEntity. Rule-derived (its own derivation code), minted to give the 59.9% of arguments that are bare {content, offset} spans a subject. Without this the entity-as-subject projection discards ~142K of 236,937 argument statements.\n- Timex. Per layer (the SPANS are given in every split, so timex nodes are identical across L0/L1/L2; what differs per layer is the *normalised value*, its precision, and its derivation code). 20,827 gold spans / 9,287 distinct surfaces in train+valid.\n\nSTATEMENT NODES (the reified sVertex analogue; one row = one argument participation):\n- stmt(stmt_id) : (event_id, role, subject) + property = ev_type||'#'||role (652 distinct, 623 with support >=10) + arg_conf + ent_link_conf + truth \u2208 {1 asserted, 0 retracted-by-diff, -1 unknow ...[truncated]\n\nSCHEMA: Root: C:\\Reseach_Quang\\tempekg\\  (new; sibling of EE\\MAVEN-Arg and MAVEN_ERE)\n  build\\eckg_v1\\eckg.duckdb                      -- working store\n  build\\eckg_v1\\parquet\\layer_id=0\\<table>.parquet ... layer_id=3\\   -- canonical release\n  build\\eckg_v1\\frozen\\constraints_L0_MINE.jsonl + .sha256\n  build\\eckg_v1\\manifest\\split_MINE.txt, split_EVAL.txt              -- doc ids, one per line\nSources: C:\\Reseach_Quang\\EE\\MAVEN-Arg\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl ; C:\\Reseach_Quang\\MAVEN_ERE\\{train,valid,test}.jsonl\n\n-- ===================== 0. PROVENANCE DIMENSIONS =====================\nCREATE TABLE layers (                 -- the DERIVATION axis (bitemporal 2nd axis, repurposed)\n  layer_id     SMALLINT PRIMARY KEY,\n  layer_name   VARCHAR,   -- 'L0_gold' | 'L1_trig' | 'L2_pred' | 'L3_blind'\n  trigger_src  VARCHAR,   -- 'gold' | 'ed_model'\n  arg_src      VARCHAR,   -- 'gold' | 'PAIE' | 'DEEIA' | 'SCPRG'\n  entity_src   VARCHAR,   -- 'gold_given' in ALL layers (entities are input in every split)\n  timex_span_src VARCHAR, -- 'gold_given' in ALL layers (TIMEX spans are input in every split)\n  trel_src     VARCHAR,   -- 'gold_ere' | 'trel_clf'\n  model_ckpt   VARCHAR,\n  calib_json   VARCHAR,   -- temperature-scaling params fitted on MINE split ONLY\n  git_sha      VARCHAR,\n  built_at     TIMESTAMP\n);\n-- seed: (0,'L0_gold','gold','gold','gold_given','gold_given','gold_ere',...)\n--       (1,'L1_trig','gold','PAIE','gold_given','gold_given','gold_ere',...)\n--       (2,'L2_pred','ed_model','PAIE','gold_given','gold_given','trel_clf',...)\n--       (3,'L3_blind','ed_model','PAIE','gold_given','gold_given','trel_clf',...)  -- 857 blind docs\n\nCREATE TABLE time_layers (            -- derivation axis for TIME, orthogonal to layers\n  tlayer_id SMALLINT PRIMARY KEY,     -- 0 regex | 1 refprop | 2 gazetteer | 3 llm_tail | 4 unresolved\n  method VARCHAR, expected_cov DOUBLE, notes VARCHAR\n);\n-- 0 regex  -> 62.1% of TIMEX deterministic (bare year 29.9, dmy 9.1, month+year 6.9, N+unit 6.1, mdy 4.6, ranges 5.6)\n-- 1 refprop-> narrative reference time = last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE (HeidelTime narrative domain\n--             strategy; MAVEN is Wikipedia and has NO document creation time, so DCT prop ...[truncated]\n\nINTERVALS: Four things per event, all persisted, none inferred at query time:\n\n1. QUADRUPLE over a day chronon: interval4 = <[b_lo, b_hi], [f_lo, f_hi]>. Semantics from Dyreson & Snodgrass (TODS 23(1), 1998) indeterminate instants; IE-side precedent from Reimers et al. (ACL 2016) and the unified quadruple time-anchor representation (arXiv 2008.06452). This uniformly subsumes L0's three measured tiers instead of special-casing them: tight anchor (37,852 events, 44.9%, 10.4/doc) \u2192 narrow b/f boxes; loose bound only (27.2%) \u2192 one side open; no TIMEX link (27.9%) \u2192 all four NULL with anchor_tier='none'. It also degrades to PaTeCon's exact (start, finish) when b_lo=b_hi and f_lo=f_hi, so Nhan's sVertex fields fill with a straight rename.\n\n2. PRECISION CODE, Wikibase-style, on the TIMEX value AND aggregated onto the event (precision_eff plus the full precision_set multiset). 9=year, 10=month, 11=day. This is the make-or-break decision: if '1815' is normalised straight to [1815-01-01, 1815-12-31] and nothing else is kept, granularity conflict collapses into ordinary interval containment and the project's second conflict type disappears. precision_set='[9,11]' is what survives that collapse.\n\n3. MINIMAL PERIODIC SET for underspecified values (Bettini & Mascetti, arXiv 1110.2213), carried as (cir_value, is_periodic, mps_period_days, mps_offset). '29 August' with no year is \u27e6XXXX-08-29\u27e7 = {y-08-29 : y \u2208 Z}, period 365/366 days \u2014 a periodic subgranularity that COVERS days and GROUPS PERIODICALLY INTO years but does NOT PARTITION the timeline (Bettini/Dyreson/Evans/Snodgrass/Wang glossary, LNCS 1399, 1998 \u2014 use these exact words or a TIME-community reviewer will object to calling it a granularity). Compatibility is periodic-set intersection with a canonical-form emptiness test.\n\n4. is_anchora ...[truncated]\n\nORDERING CONFLICT: Three detectors over pointrel, all confidence-weighted, run per (doc_id, layer_id). Ordering conflict here means: the point-algebra closure of one document-layer entails both p and \u00acp for some pair, i.e. the STP is unsatisfiable.\n\nTRANSLATION (fixed, layer-independent): BEFORE(a,b) \u2192 a.f < b.s ; CONTAINS(a,b) \u2192 a.s <= b.s AND b.f <= a.f ; SIMULTANEOUS \u2192 a.s = b.s AND a.f = b.f ; OVERLAP(a,b) \u2192 a.s < b.s < a.f < b.f ; BEGINS-ON(e,tx) \u2192 e.s = tx.s ; ENDS-ON(e,tx) \u2192 e.f = tx.f. Anchors add absolute bounds b_lo <= e.s <= b_hi, f_lo <= e.f <= f_hi. Every pointrel row carries w = product of the confidences that produced it (rel_conf * norm_conf * type_conf).\n\nD1 \u2014 STP negative-cycle (exact, mining-free, the honest floor). Floyd-Warshall over the document-layer's point graph. Unsatisfiable \u21d2 ctype='ORD_NEGCYCLE', witness = the cycle's pointrel rows. This detector uses ZERO mined constraints, so its numbers are non-circular by construction and it is the fairest baseline for Nhan's mined-constraint claim. Cite Dechter/Meiri/Pearl for completeness and Nebel & B\u00fcrckert (JACM 42(1), 1995) plus Krokhin/Jeavons/Jonsson (JACM 50(5), 2003) for the tractability position. STATE THE POSITION EXPLICITLY, because almost no KG-conflict paper does: PaTeCon's head predicate set {start, finish, before, disjoint, include} is NOT inside ORD-Horn, because interval disjointness is (a.f < b.s) OR (b.f < a.s), a genuine disjunction of strict inequalities. Drop `disjoint` and path consistency is a complete  ...[truncated]\n\nGRANULARITY CONFLICT: Granularity conflict is defined on the ANCHOR SET of one canonical event key, and the definition is INTERSECTION EMPTINESS, never precision difference. That distinction is the single most important guard in the design: 17,102 L0 events carry >=2 CONTAINS anchors (2 anchors: 10,653; 3: 3,707; >=4: 2,742) and they are overwhelmingly NESTED (\"in 1778\" + \"on November 11\"), i.e. narrative containers in the Stubbs & Pustejovsky (LAW V, 2011) sense. A detector that flagged \"event has >=2 anchors at differing precision\" would be almost entirely false positives \u2014 exactly the failure Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959) quantified at 18.6% of its flagged conflicts.\n\nFOUR SUBTYPES, all confidence- and provenance-attributed:\n\nGRAN_EMPTY (intra-layer, conjunctive). Two or more anchors of the same event in the same layer whose precision-expanded / periodic-set-expanded denotations have EMPTY intersection. Verdict CONFLICT only when provably empty; CONSISTENT when non-empty; UNKNOWN when any contributing anchor has tlayer_id=4. Worked example, real: doc 79e2767b814f136745e35123957316b3 (\"1815 North Carolina hurricane\"), EVENT_a0b68c8ac4f38c6bcfafdab518b24bd8 (type Arriving, trigger \"arriving\"), anchors TIME_689b44ba\u2026 surface '29 August' (yearless \u2192 cir_value 'XXXX-08-29', precision_s 11, is_periodic TRUE) and TIME_fe849f87\u2026 surface '1815' (cir_value '1815', precision_s 9). \u27e6XXXX-08-29\u27e7 \u2229 \u27e61815\u27e7 = {1815-08-29}: singleton, non-empty \u21d2 BENIGN REFINEMENT, and interval4 collapses to b_lo=b_hi=f_lo=f_hi=1 ...[truncated]\n\nINTERFACE TO P3/P4: Phase 2 ships ONE artefact directory and ONE contract document. Nobody reads Phase 2's Python.\n\nDELIVERABLE: C:\\Reseach_Quang\\tempekg\\build\\eckg_v1\\ containing eckg.duckdb, parquet\\layer_id={0,1,2,3}\\*.parquet, manifest\\split_{MINE,EVAL}.txt, frozen\\constraints_L0_MINE.jsonl(+.sha256), and CONTRACT.md with the table list, the six guarantees, and a `pytest` contract-test file that both consumers run before filing a bug.\n\nTO PHASE 3 (Vinh \u2014 projection ECKG -> Temporal KG):\nSingle entry point `v_projection_input`. It is already entity-as-subject, which is the direction the audit says is mandatory (event-as-subject makes all predicates degenerate because every statement of one event shares one interval). Vinh selects layer and gets the quadruple form his projection needs:\n    SELECT subject_id, subject_type, property, object_event_id,\n           b_lo, b_hi, f_lo, f_hi, precision_eff, precision_set,\n           stmt_conf, ckey_id, doc_id\n    FROM v_projection_input\n    WHERE layer_id = :L AND tempekg_split = :S;\n  * property is pre-materialised as ev_type||'#'||role, e.g. 'Killing#Victim', 'Attack#Attacker', 'Military_operation#Location', 'Hostile_encounter#Side_A', 'Process_start#Agent' \u2014 652 distinct values, 623 with support >=10. Vinh does not build this string; he consumes it, so the vocabulary can never drift between phases.\n  * The noise dial is the `layer_id` parameter. Nothing else in his code changes between G_gold, G_trig and G_pred. That is the whole point of the angle.\n ...[truncated]\n\nNOVELTY CLAIM: CLAIM (artefact + method, deliberately not a formal claim): the first event-centric knowledge graph in which the SAME documents are materialised simultaneously at three measured extraction-noise levels inside one store, with a per-statement (valid interval, precision code, derivation layer, calibrated confidence) tuple, so that (a) transfer of mined temporal constraints from a gold layer to a model-extracted layer of the same graph and (b) provenance-attributed ordering and granularity conflict become differential queries over co-resident layers rather than results on an injected benchmark.\n\nDefended against named prior work, one line each:\n- PaTeCon (AAAI 2023) / PaTeCon+ (arXiv 2312.11053): single-version graph over globally-recurring Wikidata/Freebase entities; their own need to construct WD-Confl / YG-Confl is documentary evidence that real conflicts are scarce. No provenance axis, no precision family, no gold-vs-predicted co-residence. We keep their reified sVertex store and trivalent logic and add the axis they lack; PaTeCon+ fact-level confidence produced ~38x more conflicts than entity-level, which is exactly why our conflict rows carry weight and culprit and are reported at Precision@k.\n- Fan & Strube (Findings of EMNLP 2025): Allen-algebra consistency over PREDICTED MAVEN-ERE relations \u2014 same dataset, same relation family, so this is the live novelty threat and must b ...[truncated]\n\nEFFORT (person-days): 25\n\nBUILD STEPS: D1-2 (2d) OFFSET BRIDGE + CROSS-LAYER SPINE. Build tokmap(doc_id, sent_id, tok_idx -> char_start, char_end) by re-tokenising MAVEN-ERE `sentences` against the MAVEN-Arg `document` string; assert 100% coverage per doc or fail loudly. Populate docs, ckey, node_align. ckey_kind='gold_event' for the 3,623 train+valid docs (event ids and mention ids verified identical across the two corpora on sampled docs: 20/20, 15/15, 39/42 events; 20/20, 20/20, 40/43 mentions) and ckey_kind='ere_mention' for the 857 blind-test docs (MAVEN-Arg event_mentions verified a strict subset of MAVEN-ERE test mention ids: 30/30, 16/16, 31/31). Assign tempekg_split MINE/EVAL document-disjoint, seed 20261012, write manifests. THIS IS THE CRITICAL PATH: nothing else can start. | D3-4 (2d) INGEST LAYER 0. events, event_mentions, entities, entity_mentions, stmt (236,937 rows, property pre-materialised as ev_type||'#'||role, all conf = 1.0), timex spans, anchor (67,075 event-TIMEX), trel (~1.22M), crel, subevent (12,019 with implied_contains_present). Re-verify the audit numbers as regression tests: 0 BEFORE cycles, 0 symmetric violations, 1,638 subevent gaps, ~72 total contradictions. If a number moves, ingestion is wrong. | D5-6 (2d) TIMEX NORMALISER STAGE 1 (context-independent representation). Emit cir_value with ISO-TimeML X placeholders and HeidelTime UNDEF markers ('1815', 'XXXX-08-29', 'UNDEF-next-day',\n\nRISKS: The offset bridge fails on some documents. MAVEN-Arg gives character offsets into `document`; MAVEN-ERE gives (sent_id, token) offsets. If detokenisation does not reproduce the document string byte-for-byte on some docs, those documents cannot join arguments to temporal anchors at all and silently vanish from every layer. Mitigation: build tokmap on day 1, assert per-document 100% coverage, and publish the exact count of dropped documents rather than discovering it in October. A corpus-level char-alignment mismatch is the single failure that could sink Phase 2. | Layer 2 depends on inference runs completing on someone else's checkpoints. The repos are 3-9 GB zips and there is a 1.4 GB pytorch_model.bin loose in the working directory; if a checkpoint is missing, mismatched, or unrunnable, layer 2 evaporates and with it the headline gold-to-predicted transfer experiment. Mitigation: smoke-run PAIE and DEEIA inference on 20 documents in week 1, before writing any layer-2 ingest code, and treat the cut line as a real plan rather than a fallback fiction. | Confidence is claimed but uncalibrated. PAIE/DEEIA/SCPRG softmax scores are not probabilities, and 'confidence-weighted conflict det\n\nGIVES UP: Versus a GRANULARITY-FIRST angle: this design stores everything granularity needs (precision codes, periodic sets, four endpoints, per-anchor derivation) but spends its remaining budget on layers rather than on temporal semantics. It stays in resolve-then-STP and never enters multi-granularity TCSP, so no contribution is made to the Bettini/Wang/Jajodia line, no granularity-conversion operators in Euzenat's sense are implemented, and non-convex or fuzzy interval treatments are deliberately refused. Concretely, GRAN_EMPTY on ~17,102 multi-anchor events is one detector plus one baseline here, whereas a granularity-first angle would ship the human-verified precision study and the three-way benign/conflict/unknown benchmark that is plausibly a larger contribution than any detector.\n\nVersus a PURE-GOLD, deeper-mining angle: three layers cost roughly 8 of the 25 person-days (layer 1 at 2.5, la", "B-nary-hypergraph": "DESIGN: HEDGE \u2014 Hyperedge Event\u2013Datum Graph with Explicit Granularity, mined with HyPaL (Hyperedge Pattern Language)\nStore every MAVEN event as ONE time-bearing hyperedge over its declared typed role frame plus an anchor MULTISET with per-anchor precision codes, never project to binary triples as the store, and mine constraints in HyPaL \u2014 an n-ary pattern language with slot-nullity, slot-cardinality, set-relational and k-wise-anchor atoms that PaTeCon's SP(a)/SP(b) binary language provably cannot express and that any one-interval-per-statement projection provably destroys.\n\nCORE IDEA: MAVEN-Arg's native on-disk format is ALREADY a hyperedge, and Phase 2 has been planning to throw that away. Verified on C:\\Reseach_Quang\\EE\\MAVEN-Arg\\train.jsonl: each event record is {id, type, type_id, mention[], argument{ROLE -> [filler,...]}} \u2014 argument is a DICT keyed by role whose values are LISTS. That is exactly a typed role-slot hyperedge: one edge, k labelled slots, each slot set-valued. Three further measured properties make the hyperedge the right primitive rather than a stylistic preference:\n\n(1) ROLE FRAMES ARE DECLARED, NOT EMERGENT. Role key sets are fixed per event type. Measured over the first 400 train docs: every Killing carries exactly {Killer, Cause, Victim, Location, Instrument} (297/297 each key present); every Attack carries {Agent, Patient, Location, Consequence} (398 each); Process_start {Agent, Patient, Location, Symbol, Purpose} (378 each); Military_operation {Agent, Patient, Location} (426 each). So MAVEN-Arg ships a per-type ARITY SIGNATURE \u2014 a frame catalogue \u2014 that a triple store has no slot for.\n\n(2) 37.7% OF DECLARED SLOTS ARE EMPTY (17,463 of 46,303 in the 400-doc sample). Filled-arity for Killing is distributed {0:3, 1:9, 2:45, 3:161, 4:74, 5:5}. A declared-but-empty slot is a POSITIVE fact (\"the annotators saw no Victim\") and it is the correct denominator for any confidence over that role. Under projection an empty slot and an unannotated slot are both \"no triple\" \u2014 the information is destroyed, not merely awkward. This is the single largest measured loss.\n\n(3) 5.8% OF FILLED SLOTS ARE SET-VALUED (2,679/46,303 have >1 filler), and role slots co-depend: Location_original and Location_final occur 698 and 698 times \u2014 always jointly. Set identity and joint slot occupancy are hyperedge properties.\n\n(4) ARITY-0 HYPEREDGES EXIST AND MATTER. Hostile_encounter has 2,024 mentions in MAVEN_ERE/train.jsonl and ZERO events in MAVEN-Arg train; 9 event types are absent from MAVEN-Arg entirely; 3,649 of 67,984 ERE train events (5.4%) have no MAVEN-Arg counterpart at all. These are time-bearing, relation-bearing hyperedges with an EMPTY slot set. A projection whose only vertex-of-interest is an argument filler makes 5.4% of the temporal graph disappear from the mining index; HEDGE keeps them as arity-0 edges that still participate in ordering constraints.\n\n(5) TIME IS A PROPERTY OF THE EDGE, AND THE ANCHOR SET IS ITSELF N-ARY. Following EventKG/SEM (sem:hasBeginTimeStamp on the event resource), time hangs on the hyperedge \u2014 the team already diagnosed that event-as-subject projection makes all predicates share one interval. But HEDGE  ...[truncated]\n\nNODE/EDGE MODEL: FORMAL OBJECT. HEDGE is a 6-tuple H = (V, E, I, E2, A, T) with a layer function L over every tuple.\n\nV = V_ent \u222a V_span \u222a V_timex \u222a {\u22a5}\n  V_ent    : 68,348 document-local entities, 7 types (Location, Other, Organization, Person, Product, Building, Art). Provided in EVERY split including blind test. 16,284 participate in >=2 events (mean 3.15, max 59). ZERO cross-document entities (0/68,348) \u2014 every vertex is document-local by construction, so every pattern match is intra-document.\n  V_span   : bare {content, offset} argument fillers with no entity_id \u2014 59.9% of the 236,937 arguments. First-class vertices, flagged vkind='SPAN', deliberately NOT coreferenced.\n  V_timex  : 20,827 gold TIMEX spans (9,287 distinct surfaces), types DATE/DURATION/TIME/PREPOSTEXP. Provided in every split.\n  \u22a5        : the single null vertex, incident to every DECLARED-BUT-EMPTY slot. This is the vertex a triple store cannot have.\n\nE = 80,479 event hyperedges (79,740 carry both an ERE id and an Arg frame; 3,649/67,984 = 5.4% of ERE train events are frameless arity-0 edges). An event is ONE edge, not a node with edges hanging off it. Event coreference is already resolved inside the edge: a hyperedge carries trigger_n mentions (the sample Motion event has 5: \"Expedition\"/\"venture\"/\"expedition\"/\"Expedition\"/\"expedition\").\n\nI \u2286 E \u00d7 Role \u00d7 \u2115 \u00d7 V is a FOUR-PLACE POSITIONED LABELLED INCIDENCE RELATION, not a two-place incidence. I(e, r, p, v) reads \"vertex v occupies position p of slot r of hyperedge e\". The 4-placeness is the whole design: role labels give typed slots (143 roles), positions make slots ordered multisets, and \u22a5 in the v position encodes nullity. A conventional hypergraph (2-place incidence, edges are vertex sets) would already lose the roles; a bipartite \"incidence graph\" encoding of it ...[truncated]\n\nSCHEMA: Canonical format: Parquet (columnar, projection pushdown, zero server, standard dataset-release format) under eckg/v1/, attached read-only in DuckDB; DuckPGQ (SQL/PGQ, SQL:2023, PVLDB 2023) used ONLY for BEFORE-closure reachability. NOT Kuzu \u2014 repository archived 10 Oct 2025, a reproducibility liability a reviewer can check in ten seconds. Total store ~2.5M rows / well under 1 GB, so the ~4x standard-reification blow-up (Hernandez, Hogan & Kr\u00f6tzsch, SSWS 2015) is irrelevant here and we say so: representation fidelity is free at this scale, therefore choose the most expressive model.\n\n-- T1  event_frame : the induced frame catalogue (162 typed frames + 9 frameless types)\nCREATE TABLE event_frame (\n  event_type      VARCHAR NOT NULL,   -- 'Killing'\n  type_id         SMALLINT,\n  role            VARCHAR NOT NULL,   -- 'Victim'\n  role_id         SMALLINT,           -- 0..142 over the 143 MAVEN-Arg roles\n  role_ordinal    TINYINT NOT NULL,   -- canonical slot index within the frame (bit position)\n  declared_freq   INTEGER,            -- #events of this type where the key is present\n  filled_freq     INTEGER,            -- #events where the slot is non-empty\n  null_rate       FLOAT,              -- 1 - filled_freq/declared_freq\n  is_set_valued   BOOLEAN,            -- observed max_card > 1\n  max_card        TINYINT,\n  paired_with     VARCHAR,            -- 'Location_final' for 'Location_original' (698/698)\n  PRIMARY KEY (event_type, role));\n\n-- T2  event_hyperedge : ONE ROW PER EVENT = one hyperedge\nCREATE TABLE event_hyperedge (\n  eid             VARCHAR PRIMARY KEY,  -- 'EVENT_f535eb21f8f958a04cbc69c1d1521186' (shared Arg/ERE id)\n  doc_id          VARCHAR NOT NULL,\n  split           VARCHAR NOT NULL,     -- 'mine' | 'eval'  (DOCUMENT-disjoint; exact, since 0/68,348 entities cross docs)\n  event_type      VARCHAR NOT NULL,\n  frame_arity     TINYINT NOT NULL,     -- |F(type)|; 0 for Hostile_encounter and the other 8 frameless types\n  filled_arity    TINYINT NOT NULL,     -- # non-empty slots\n  slot_signature  BIGINT NOT NULL,      -- bitmask over role_ordinal of FILLED slots\n  null_signature  BIGINT NOT NULL,      -- bitmask of DECLARED-BUT-EMPTY slots  <-- Tier-1 infor ...[truncated]\n\nINTERVALS: Three-layer time, all three persisted. Layer 1 is per-TIMEX and is the ONLY place granularity is observable; layer 2 is the anchor multiset on the edge; layer 3 is the derived solution. Eager collapse to layer 3 is what kills the granularity conflict type, so layers 1 and 2 are never discarded.\n\nLAYER 1 \u2014 PER-TIMEX NORMALISED VALUE (timex_norm). Two-stage, HeidelTime-style, TIMEX3-conformant.\n  Stage 1 (context-independent representation, `cir`): \"1778\" -> '1778'; \"November 11\" -> 'UNDEF-year-11-11'; \"the next day\" -> 'UNDEF-REF-plus-1-day'; \"ten years\" -> 'P10Y'; \"Sunday\" -> 'UNDEF-REF-weekday-7'; \"World War II\" -> 'GAZ:WWII'.\n  Stage 2 disambiguation. MAVEN is Wikipedia = NARRATIVE domain with NO document creation time, so DCT propagation is unavailable and the citable default is HeidelTime's narrative rule: reference time = last mentioned TIMEX of type DATE. This resolves the four measured tractable families in the 37.9% context-dependent bucket: relative dates (\"the next day\" x91, \"the following day\" x62, \"the same day\" x21), yearless dates (\"September 11\" x24, \"August 31\" x17), named periods (\"World War II\" x70, typed DURATION), weekdays (\"Sunday\" x21). Regex covers the 62.1% deterministic bucket (bare year 29.9%, day-month-year 9.1%, month+year 6.9%, N+unit 6.1%, month+day+year 4.6%, ranges/decades/centuries/deictic 5.6%).\n  Output is a tagged union over FOUR kinds, not one interval type:\n    INTERVAL : (jd_lo, jd_hi) + prec.        '1778' -> (JD(1778-01-01), JD(1778-12-31)), prec 9\n    PERIODIC : minimal periodic set (period, offsets) + prec. 'XXXX-11-11' -> {y-11-11 : y in Z}, period 1 year, prec 10. 'XXXX-WXX-7' -> every Sunday, period 7 days.\n    DURATION : (dur_lo, dur_hi) in days, prec NULL, NO position on the timeline. 'P10Y' -> [3650, 3653]. This constrain ...[truncated]\n\nORDERING CONFLICT: Ordering conflict is detected NATIVELY over hyperedges: bodies are n-ary HyPaL patterns, heads are Allen relation sets, and checking is point-algebra path consistency over the document's endpoint graph.\n\nHyPaL BODY ATOMS (12 constructors; the last four are the ones PaTeCon has no counterpart for):\n  1  type(x, T)                          -- hyperedge type\n  2  rel(x, rho, y)                      -- E2 atom over the 9 relation types\n  3  slot(x, r) contains v               -- membership\n  4  slotset(x, r) = S                   -- bind a SET variable (set-valued slot)\n  5  etype(x, r, tau)                    -- entity type on a slot (7 types)\n  6  allen(x, y) subset A                -- 13-bit Allen mask\n  7  width(x) in [lo, hi]                -- metric atom on TBox width\n  8  prec(x) op p / prec_gap(x) op g     -- precision atoms\n  9  null(x, r)                          -- DECLARED-BUT-EMPTY slot        [not expressible in PaTeCon; Tier-1 destroyed by projection]\n 10  card(x, r) op k                     -- slot cardinality               [not expressible in PaTeCon]\n 11  sdisj(x,r1,y,r2) / ssub(x,r1,y,r2) / seq(x,r1,y,r2)  -- SET-LEVEL relations between two slots  [not expressible in PaTeCon]\n 12  kwise(x) in {SAT,UNSAT,UNKNOWN}     -- k-wise anchor satisfiability   [Tier-1 destroyed by projection]\n\nHEAD PREDICATES: an Allen relation set (13 relations, OWL-Time vocabulary) for ordering; MUTEX (falsity) for mutual exclusion; REQUIRE(rho) for EXISTENCE heads (GGD-style, arXiv 240 ...[truncated]\n\nGRANULARITY CONFLICT: This is where Angle B earns its cost, and the argument is a theorem plus a prevalence measurement rather than an intuition.\n\nTHE HELLY ARGUMENT. One-dimensional intervals have Helly number 2: if a family of intervals intersects pairwise, it intersects k-wise. Consequence: IF every anchor were a plain interval, the k-wise granularity test would be exactly equivalent to all pairwise tests, and the native n-ary representation would buy nothing. Two anchor kinds break Helly:\n  (a) PERIODIC SETS (yearless dates, weekdays) \u2014 not convex, so pairwise non-empty does not imply k-wise non-empty.\n  (b) METRIC WIDTH constraints (DURATION) \u2014 a constraint on f - b, not a subset of the timeline at all, so it has no interval representation to intersect pairwise.\nMEASURED PREVALENCE on the multi-anchor events of MAVEN_ERE train+valid (17,274 events with >=2 anchors of any anchoring type, consistent with the stated 17,102 CONTAINS-only figure): 41.9% (7,235 events) carry at least one DURATION anchor; 15.9% (2,742 events) carry a yearless-date or weekday anchor; the year+yearless co-anchoring family alone is 909 events and the three-kind family (other + year + yearless) is 448. So the non-Helly families are the MAJORITY of multi-anchor events. Pairwise granularity testing \u2014 which is all any binary-triple projection with one interval per statement can do \u2014 is provably insufficient on the majority of the substrate. That result stands independently of whether any constraint mining succeeds.\n\nTHE WO ...[truncated]\n\nINTERFACE TO P3/P4: Phase 2 (Nhut) hands over ONE frozen, hashed release plus ONE Python package plus TWO generated views. The architectural move is that Phase 3 no longer performs a lossy conversion: Phase 2 generates the projected view itself, so Vinh's projection work becomes serialisation, release engineering and annotation tooling on top of a stable contract.\n\nDELIVERABLE A \u2014 FROZEN RELEASE (day 21 for the MVP subset, day 38 complete)\n  eckg/v1/{event_frame, event_hyperedge, slot_incidence, vertex, timex_norm, anchor, tbox, event_relation, hyperedge_overlap, token_index, projection_loss}.parquet\n  eckg/v1/MANIFEST.json : schema_version, per-file SHA-256, row counts, split assignment (document-disjoint), layer inventory, git commit, and the reproduced values of every hard fact used as a regression assertion.\n  eckg/v1/attach.sql : DuckDB CREATE VIEW script + a DuckPGQ PROPERTY GRAPH declaration used ONLY for BEFORE-closure reachability.\n  CONTRACT GUARANTEES, tested in CI: (i) `split` is document-disjoint \u2014 exact, not approximate, since 0 of 68,348 entities cross documents; (ii) every table carries `layer` and `conf`, so gold-vs-predicted experiments are a WHERE clause; (iii) frame-completeness \u2014 for every eid and every r in F(type), a slot_incidence row exists, with vid='_BOT_' when empty; (iv) trivalent counts never mix; (v) ids are the MAVEN shared ids (79,740 shared event ids) and every row is traceable to text via token_index; (vi) schema hash frozen and committed BEFORE any conflict in ...[truncated]\n\nNOVELTY CLAIM: HEADLINE, stated so it is falsifiable: HEDGE is the first event-centric temporal KG in which the event is a first-class n-ary hyperedge over a DECLARED typed role frame carrying an ANCHOR MULTISET with per-anchor precision codes; and HyPaL is the first temporal-constraint language with slot-nullity, slot-cardinality, set-relational and k-wise-anchor atoms. We prove by a Helly-number argument, and measure on MAVEN, that no binary-triple projection with one interval per statement can express the granularity satisfiability test that the MAJORITY of MAVEN's multi-anchor events require (41.9% of the 17,274 multi-anchor events carry a non-interval DURATION width constraint; 15.9% carry a non-convex periodic anchor), and we ship a machine-generated projection_loss table quantifying the loss per event.\n\nDIFFERENTIATION AGAINST NAMED PRIOR WORK:\n - PaTeCon (AAAI 2023) / PaTeCon+ (arXiv 2312.11053). Their pattern language is two hand-fixed structural patterns: SP(a) one subject with two temporal statements, SP(b) two subjects joined by an atemporal property. It is conjunctive, purely binary, aggregation-free, and admits at most two non-time entity variables. null(), card(), sdisj() and kwise() are outside it by construction. Two of those four (null, kwise) are additionally Tier-1 DESTROYED by projection into their sVertex format, because a statement carries one interval and no frame. We  ...[truncated]\n\nEFFORT (person-days): 38\n\nBUILD STEPS: D1-D3 (2.5 pd) OFFSET RECONCILIATION AND JOIN. Build token_index (T11): MAVEN-Arg uses CHARACTER offsets over 'document' (verified: trigger \"Expedition\" at [4,14]), MAVEN-ERE uses (sent_id, sentence-local TOKEN indices) over 'tokens'/'sentences' (verified: TIMEX \"1860\" at sent_id 0, offset [24,25]). Nothing joins without this. Assert doc-id overlap 4,480/4,480 and shared event ids 79,740 as CI regression tests. Assign the document-disjoint mine/eval split and commit its hash. | D3-D4 (1.0 pd) FRAME CATALOGUE INDUCTION. Build event_frame (T1) by scanning role key sets per event type: 162 typed frames from MAVEN-Arg train (Killing = {Killer,Cause,Victim,Location,Instrument}, Attack = {Agent,Patient,Location,Consequence}, Process_start = {Agent,Patient,Location,Symbol,Purpose}, Military_operation = {Agent,Patient,Location}), plus 9 frameless ERE types including Hostile_encounter (2,024 ERE train mentions, 0 MAVEN-Arg events). Derive is_set_valued, max_card, null_rate, and paired_with (Location_original <-> Location_final, 698/698). | D5-D7 (2.5 pd) HYPEREDGE AND INCIDENCE BUILDERS. Emit event_hyperedge (T2) with slot_signature and null_signature bitmasks, and slot_incidence (T3) with EXPLICIT '_BOT_' rows for all declared-but-empty slots (~143K rows, 37.7% of declared). Emit vertex (T4) with n_events and an asserted is_cross_doc=FALSE for all 68,348. CI test: frame-completeness fo\n\nRISKS: SCHEDULE IS THE PRIMARY RISK. 38 person-days inside 45 calendar days, for one person who must also write paper sections, leaves essentially no slack. Mitigation is the D21 MVP gate: Vinh and Nhan get a working release plus the PaTeCon view on day 21 regardless, so a slip in the HyPaL half degrades the novelty track rather than blocking two teammates. If day 21 slips, the correct move is to cut D24-D27 HyPaL down to the null-atom family only and ship it as a single constraint family rather than a language. | THE PERIODIC-SET LIBRARY (D13-D15) IS THE HIGHEST-VARIANCE ITEM and 3 days could become 6. Explicit gate: if incomplete by D30, drop PERIODIC handling entirely and keep INTERVAL + DURATION width. The Helly-breaking claim survives on width alone (41.9% of multi-anchor events, 7,235 events) and the headline result is unchanged; only the three-anchor calendar witness becomes illustrative rather than detected. | NOVELTY IS REPRESENTATIONAL, SO WEAK MINING RESULTS COULD MAKE THE HYPERGRAPH LOOK LIKE ENGINEERING. Mitigation: the projection_loss table, the Helly argument, and the measured non-Helly prevalence are results that hold independently of whether any HyPaL constraint reaches s\n\nGIVES UP: \"VERSUS A PROJECTION-FIRST ANGLE (build the entity-as-subject TKG, run PaTeCon, spend the remaining time on evaluation): that design is roughly 10-12 person-days for Phase 2 rather than 38. Angle B spends ~26 extra person-days to buy representational expressiveness. What those 26 days do NOT buy: any improvement to the evaluation protocol, the fake-conflict circularity, or the annotation budget. If the paper's binding constraint turns out to be reviewer trust in the evaluation rather than reviewer interest in the representation, this is the wrong allocation and it cannot be reversed after ~day 25.\n\nGIVES UP THE FREE RIDE ON PaTeCon'S IMPLEMENTATION for the novelty track. Nhan can run PaTeCon unchanged on our generated view, but HyPaL needs a matcher, a support module with three denominators, and a checker \u2014 ~9.5 of the 38 days \u2014 and that code will be less battle-tested than a published i"}, "VERDICTS_DONE": {"A-projection-faithful": "HOSTILE VERDICT (feasibility 5/10, novelty 4/10, effectiveness 4/10, OVERALL 4/10)\nREJECT AS FRAMED; SALVAGEABLE AS A NEGATIVE-RESULT / AUDIT PAPER. The engineering is the best-reasoned part of the submission \u2014 the event-node-carries-time fix for the projection degeneracy, the ev1!=ev2 filter, resolve-then-STP to stay out of NP-hard multi-granularity TCSP, the ORD-Horn split, and the loss list are all correct and unusually self-aware. But the design's value proposition is that the granularity arm buys a non-circular evaluation, and it does not: the natural substrate is predicted by the design itself to yield almost no CONFLICT, the perturbation substrate shares its decision procedure with the detector, and the residue that survives cannot be separated from unvalidated normalisation error because MAVEN has zero gold TIMEX values. Meanwhile the novelty stack is one repackaging claim (Claim 1, undercut by MAVEN-ERE/MAVEN-Arg and RED), one schema-assembly claim (Claim 2, near zero for an AC), a disclaimed-formal-novelty position (Claim 3), and one genuine measurement (Claim 4). Only Claim 4 earns a COLING slot, and it does not need 9 tables, Z3, SHACL, or an RDF 1.2 dump to be made. Schedule is 26-30 person-days sold as 18, with a hard day-15 freeze that three other phases block on and 1 day of buffer; the normaliser, tempekg.pset, and the D16-D17 grab-bag will all slip. Concrete recommendation: cut MaxSAT, SHACL, RDF, unlinked_spans-as-nodes and pair_signatures' full relation matrix; spend the recovered week on a blind normaliser validation set and 100% manual adjudication of the flagged conflict set; retitle around Claim 4; keep the freeze date and let the paper be smaller and true.\n  FATAL: THE NON-CIRCULAR ARM PROBABLY HAS ZERO POSITIVES. The design's escape from circularity rests entirely on 'naturally occurring' granularity conflicts among the 17,102 multi-anchor events. But the gold graph is essentially conflict-free (0 BEFORE cycles / 843,808 pairs, ~72 genuine contradictions corpus-wide), MAVEN-ERE CONTAINS anchors were annotated by the same annotator on the same document, and the design itself says REFINEMENT is 'the NORMAL case' and that if REFINEMENT is not the overwhelming majority 'the implementation is wrong'. That is a pre-committed prediction that CONFLICT is near-empty. Expected yield after excluding UNKNOWN (10-15% unresolved plus 27.9% unanchored propagation) is plausibly single-to-low-double digits, and every one of those must then be argued not to be a normalisation or coreference artefact. The headline result of the whole angle is a number the design predicts will be ~0. | THE GRANULARITY PERTURBATION ARM IS CIRCULAR BY A DIFFERENT ROUTE, and the design's own worked example proves it. tempekg.perturb is 'interval-arithmetic only, imports nothing from the miner' \u2014 but the detector IS interval/periodic-set arithmetic (emptiness of I plus chain failure). The example explicitly discards the 1778->1779 perturbation because it does not empty I, and adopts 'November 1778 + XXXX-03-03' because it does. Injector and detector share one decision procedure, so recall is 1.0 by construction and precision is undefined. Firewalling the module boundary firewalls the wrong thing: the leak is semantic, not an import. | CONFLICT VERDICTS WILL BE DOMINATED BY UPSTREAM ERROR, NOT CONTRADICTION, AND THE DESIGN'S GUARD DOES NOT COVER IT. The guard only handles resolver='unresolved' (precision=0 -> UNKNOWN). It does nothing about confidently WRONG resolutions, which is the actual failure mode of narrative reference-time propagation on Wikipedia with no DCT: 37.9% of surfaces need document context, the 'next day / following day / same day / Sunday' families are exactly where last-mentioned-DATE chaining silently mis-anchors, and there is ZERO gold normalisation to measure that error rate against. A CONFLICT set of tens of items produced on top of an unvalidated normaliser cannot be distinguished from Kontrast's 7.2% normalisation-error bucket \u2014 which is the very bucket the paper claims to have escaped. The stance-inversion claim collapses without a measured normalisation accuracy number, and none is budgeted. | THE CHAIN-FAILURE RULE HAS NO ARTEFACT MODEL. 'Two distinct day-precision anchors on one event => CONFLICT' fires on (a) durative events legitimately mentioned with two different day containers, (b) over-merged MAVEN coreference clusters, and (c) CONTAINS edges annotated as loose narrative-container attachment rather than strict temporal containment. Those three populations are almost certainly larger than the genuine-contradiction population, and the design contains no estimate, no held-out audit, and no test that separates them. Under a hostile review this converts the flagship family from a detector into an annotation-error finder \u2014 publishable, but not the paper being written.\n  FIXABLE: THE BUILD STEPS DO NOT SUM TO THE COMMITTED DELIVERABLES. Listed steps total 16.5 d + 1 d buffer = 17.5, but D16-D17 (2.0 d) is asked to deliver manifest + property_marginals + pair_signatures (7 relation counts per property pair over 1.39M edges) + a complete Floyd-Warshall STP checker + a Z3 partial-weighted MaxSAT encoder with culprit localisation + make smoke on 20 hand-checked docs + a 3-page INTERFACE.md. That is 5-6 days of work in a 2-day box. The 'constraint-free exact baseline (~1 day)' and the 'declarative second detector, ~1 day' SHACL-SPARQL shape appear in prose and in the promised deliverable list but are NOT in the schedule at all. Real effort is 26-30 person-days, not 18. Cut MaxSAT and SHACL to future work, or move them behind the freeze. | SLIP LIST, NAMED. (1) D4-D8 normaliser: 4.5 d for regex + TimeML reference-time propagation + gazetteer + hand-labelling 200 of 9,287 distinct surfaces, on the one component with zero supervision \u2014 this is where 2-4 days go. (2) D9-D10 tempekg.pset at 1.5 d: canonical form, intersection, subset and emptiness over periodic sets with X placeholders is the least-familiar code in the plan and has no reference implementation to copy; budget 3 d. (3) D16-D17 as above. Net effect: the day-15 PaTeCon go/no-go gate and the eckg-v1.0.0 freeze land day 20-24, and Vinh and Nhan block on it, in a 45-day window that must also contain the paper. There is exactly 1 day of declared buffer against three independent slip sources. | THE PLAN ASSERTS A COUNT ITS OWN PIPELINE CANNOT PRODUCE. D11-D13 says 'assert 44.9% tight / 27.2% loose / 27.9% none'. Those are properties of the gold anchor graph; after a normaliser at 85-90% coverage, 10-15% of contributing anchors resolve to precision=0 and their events cannot be 'tight'. The assertion fails on day 13 and will be 'fixed' by loosening it, which is the moment the manifest stops being a contract. Restate the assertions as (structural counts, asserted) vs (post-normalisation counts, reported). | NO NORMALISER VALIDATION SET IS BUDGETED BEYOND 200 UNIT-TEST SURFACES USED FOR DEVELOPMENT. Reserve ~150 surfaces stratified by family as a blind held-out set, and report value-accuracy per family. This is ~0.5 d and it is the difference between the granularity claim being measurable and being asserted. | MANUAL ADJUDICATION OF THE FLAGGED SET IS NOT SCHEDULED, and it is the single highest-value day in the whole plan. If CONFLICT yield is small (it will be), hand-label 100% of it into {genuine, normalisation error, coreference error, durative-event artefact} and report precision with a witness table. That is the only defensible effectiveness number this design can produce, and Vinh already owns annotation tooling. | MINEABLE SUPPORT AFTER SPLITTING AND UNKNOWN-DROPPING IS NOT ESTIMATED ANYWHERE. Rough arithmetic: 274,525 SP(a) pairs -> ~80% in MINE (~220K) -> both endpoints usefully bounded at roughly 0.45-0.7 each, so ~20-40% of pairs are non-UNKNOWN for an ordering predicate (~45-90K), spread over 2,688 signatures. Many of the 2,688 will drop below support 10, and PaTeCon's conf>=0.9 will then be estimated off 10-20 observations. Compute this projection BEFORE day 15 from pair_signatures.n_both_timed / n_either_unknown (the columns already exist) and make it the go/no-go criterion instead of 'PaTeCon runs to completion'. | SUPPORT IS COUNTED OVER SUBJECTS THAT ARE DOCUMENT-LOCAL, so 'distinct subjects' is very nearly 'distinct documents x topic'. A war article yields dozens of Attack/Killing stars whose orderings are all correlated. Confidence estimates are pseudo-replicated; the Beta-Bernoulli atomicity test does not fix clustering. Cluster at the document level or report both. | 'assert 652 distinct properties' on the MINE split will fail: 652 is the full-corpus vocabulary; an 80% document split loses rare (EventType,Role) pairs. Same class of error as the 44.9% assertion. | OFFSET-SPACE ALIGNMENT IS DISMISSED AS OFF-CRITICAL-PATH, but the paper's own limitation numbers (4.97% / 13.8% overlap) and any future coreference merge of the 141,925 unlinked spans need it. Half a day, but say so in the schedule rather than in a risk note. | PHASE 3 IS HOLLOWED OUT. Defining Vinh's deliverable as 'variants of a function whose reference implementation already exists and already passes tests' plus annotation tooling is a real reduction in his phase's content, and Nhan is handed two tasks not in his plan (Beta-Bernoulli atomicity, BH-FDR). Neither is a change of method, so this is recoverable \u2014 but it needs to be negotiated in week 1, not presented as a fait accompli on day 15.\n  PRIOR WORK UNDERCUTTING NOVELTY: MAVEN-ERE (Wang et al., EMNLP 2022) and MAVEN-Arg (Wang et al., ACL 2024) \u2014 the 'first document-grounded event KG with gold n-ary roles and a million gold temporal relations on the same documents' IS these two releases, on the same 4,480 documents, joined by event id. Converting them to 10 Parquet tables is a format change, not a resource contribution. An AC will read Claim 1 as 'we joined two public files on doc_id'. This is the most dangerous single line in the submission. | Richer Event Description / RED (O'Gorman, Wright-Bettner & Palmer, 2016) and THYME/TimeBank-Dense/TempEval-3 \u2014 gold event arguments, coreference, temporal relations and TIMEX all annotated over the same documents, with normalised TIMEX3 values, which MAVEN lacks. 'First document-grounded' does not survive RED. Neither does the implicit claim that a document-grounded event+time+argument corpus is a missing resource. | Bettini, Wang & Jajodia (AMAI 1998; AI 140, 2002), Euzenat (Computational Intelligence 17(4), 2001), and the Bettini et al. Glossary (LNCS 1399, 1998) \u2014 own multi-granularity inconsistency, granularity conversion with proved consistency conditions, and the periodic-subgranularity machinery. The design concedes this. What remains is an implementation of a known intersection test on a new corpus. | PaTeCon / PaTeCon+ (AAAI 2023; arXiv 2312.11053) \u2014 the mining loop, the trivalent logic, the SP(a)/SP(b) patterns, the support/confidence protocol, the defaults. With Graph_Structure.py and Constraint_Mining.py unchanged, the ordering arm reduces exactly to 'we applied PaTeCon to events', which is the review sentence this design is trying to avoid and does not. | Soulard, Sais & Raad (ESWC 2025) \u2014 already occupy entity-as-subject temporal constraint discovery over an Allen-algebra extension. The stated difference (document-local entities, no normalised times, type-level properties) is a harder setting, not a different problem; 'harder setting, weaker results' is a familiar and weak novelty argument. | Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025) \u2014 Allen-algebra logical closure on MAVEN-ERE. Same dataset, temporal consistency as the object. The 'orthogonal object' rebuttal is correct but it also establishes that temporal-consistency work on MAVEN-ERE is already a Findings-level contribution, which caps this one. | EventKG (Gottschalk & Demidova, ESWC 2018 / SWJ 2019), Wiki-hy/YAGO-hy (Ding et al., Findings EMNLP 2024), StarE (Galkin et al., EMNLP 2020), Wikibase precision codes, Dyreson & Snodgrass (TODS 1998), the four-endpoint time-anchor line (Reimers et al. ACL 2016; arXiv 2008.06452), ADBIS 2025 bitemporal property graphs \u2014 every element of Claim 2's four-way tuple is off the shelf. 'First release to carry all four columns at once' is a schema-assembly claim; ACs discount these to near zero. | Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959) \u2014 the design's chosen foil already names granularity divergence as taxonomy category #1 and measures it at 18.6%. Inverting a recommendation in an existing taxonomy is a position, not a finding, unless the adjudication is shown to be accurate. See the effectiveness flaws: accuracy is exactly what is unmeasurable here.\n  STRONGEST SURVIVING CLAIM: A measurement paper, not a method paper: \"What PaTeCon-style temporal constraint mining costs when ported from a globally-linked entity KG to a document-local event graph.\" Three defensible, already-measured or cheaply-measurable results: (1) the entity-star projection structurally reaches only 139,093 of 843,808 temporally-related event pairs (16.5%), and 59.9% of arguments are degree-1 unlinked spans that generate no mineable subject at all; (2) with 0 of 68,348 entities recurring across documents, PaTeCon's headline entity-level confidence provably degenerates to fact-level confidence \u2014 a negative result about their central contribution, demonstrated rather than argued; (3) on the 17,102 naturally occurring multi-anchor events, granularity divergence adjudicates overwhelmingly to benign REFINEMENT, with the small CONFLICT residue hand-labelled 100% into {genuine, normalisation error, coreference error, durative artefact} and reported with witnesses. That is a short/Findings-strength empirical contribution and it is honest. It is NOT a conflict-detection paper, and the ordering arm and both novelty Claims 1 and 2 should be demoted to a resource-release appendix.", "E-granularity-lattice": "HOSTILE VERDICT (feasibility 4/10, novelty 5/10, effectiveness 4/10, OVERALL 4/10)\nREVISE-AND-RESCOPE, do not build as written. The design is the most intellectually honest of its class - the non-claims section, the ORD-Horn positioning, the assertion_mode split, the UNDECIDABLE discipline, entity-as-subject, document-level split, and the frozen-constraint/injector-firewall artefacts are all correct and several are things reviewers rarely see. But it has bet the paper on a number it cannot obtain in 45 days. The chain is: headline = INCOMPATIBLE and factorisation verdicts -> gold layer is conflict-free by annotation construction (~72 contradictions corpus-wide, 0 BEFORE cycles) -> so the headline must run on rule_norm or model_pred -> rule_norm conflicts ARE our own unsupervised normaliser's errors (a taxonomy of our bugs, ~1,450 expected spurious INCOMPATIBLE vs ~70 genuine, S/N ~1:20) -> and model_pred, the only escape, has no build step, no owner, and no days, and cannot be derived from Phase 1 because EAE cannot supply event time (4.97% span-TIMEX overlap, no Time role among the 143). Meanwhile the perturbation track's recall is tautological because F1/F5 are defined by the detector's own emptiness predicate. Net: the arithmetic is sound, the representation is defensible, and the empirical payoff is structurally absent. THREE CHANGES MAKE IT SUBMITTABLE. (1) Reframe now, in writing, as resource + characterisation + released normaliser, with detection as a secondary section; stop calling the factorisation table the headline, and never pre-commit an abstract to a natural conflict rate. (2) Reclaim ~10 days by cutting the Z3 MaxSAT path, the SHACL baseline, entailed_at_gran on all 1.22M edges (compute it only on conflict-participating edges), WEEK/WEEK_OF_YEAR/TIME_OF_DAY from the lattice, and the RDF secondary serialisation - then spend 6 of those days on a minimal event-TIMEX CONTAINS classifier so a genuine predicted layer exists, and 2 on a real two-annotator audit with an IAA number. (3) Rebuild the normaliser audit as the paper's central measurement rather than a mitigation: 400+ items, two annotators, per-family accuracy with CIs, and report the INCOMPATIBLE set as 'flagged anomalies, N of which human adjudication confirms as annotation errors' - that number, even at N=30, is a publishable finding, whereas an unaudited count of 700 is not. As written, the most likely outcome is a technically immaculate schema, a working periodic-set engine, an empty headline table on day 40, and a paper positioned on a claim the data cannot support.\n  FATAL: THE PREDICTED LAYER HAS NO BUILD STEP, NO OWNER, AND NO DAYS - yet the design's own risk section makes it 'a hard requirement of the paper, not a nice-to-have'. Both headline degeneracy risks are mitigated ONLY by the model_pred layer: (a) the verdict distribution coming out all-benign, (b) the factorisation table being empty on gold (0 BEFORE cycles / 843,808 pairs). S1-S12 contains no step that produces model_pred. It cannot come from Phase 1: EAE outputs arguments, and only 4.97% of argument spans overlap any TIMEX while MAVEN-Arg's 143 roles contain no Time/Date/When role - EAE structurally cannot supply event time. Producing a predicted anchor layer requires training an event-TIMEX CONTAINS classifier and an ERE temporal-relation classifier on MAVEN-ERE. That is new model training, ~8-12 person-days minimum with GPU contention, and it is not in the 26. Consequence: if the gold verdict distribution is benign (which the design itself predicts), the paper has no headline table and no fallback. | THE NATURAL INCOMPATIBLE POPULATION IS SWAMPED BY THE AUTHORS' OWN NORMALISER ERROR, AND THE DESIGN NEVER DOES THIS ARITHMETIC. Gold is 100% transitively closed with ~72 genuine contradictions corpus-wide across ALL relation types; anchors are gold CONTAINS links onto gold TIMEX spans annotated on one timeline per document, so genuine empty-intersection events plausibly number in the tens (the design's own estimate: ~70). Now the contamination: 37.9% of surfaces need document context, and reference-time propagation errs. At a generous 5% error on that family, per-anchor error is 0.379*0.05 = 1.9%; a pair is contaminated at ~3.8%; over >=38,226 anchor pairs that is ~1,450 spurious INCOMPATIBLE verdicts against a genuine population of ~70. Signal-to-noise ~1:20. At the design's own worst-case 15% propagation error rate it is ~1:60. The natural-conflict track is therefore not a conflict-detection result at all - it is an error analysis of an unsupervised normaliser wearing a conflict-detection costume, and every INCOMPATIBLE row's most likely explanation is our bug. | THE 200-ITEM AUDIT CANNOT ADJUDICATE THE VERY QUESTION IT IS BUILT TO ANSWER. A 200-item stratified sample estimating ~90% accuracy carries a 95% CI of roughly +/-4 points; stratified across four context-dependent families it is ~50 items per family, CI +/-8-13 points. The precision of the natural INCOMPATIBLE set hinges on whether the propagation error rate is 2% or 6% - a distinction 200 items provably cannot make. It is also audited by one person (Nhut) against his own normaliser, with no second annotator and no IAA, on the deliverable that is load-bearing for the paper's central number. | THE PERTURBATION TRACK REPLACES MINING-CIRCULARITY WITH DETECTOR-DEFINITION CIRCULARITY. Claim 4 is that conflicts are 'generated at the NORMALISER level ... and detected by arithmetic that was never fitted to any mined constraint'. True but irrelevant: F5 is defined as 'coarsen one anchor by one lattice step then shift by one granule (produces INCOMPATIBLE with minimum surface change)' and F1 shifts a reference year by +/-1 - i.e. the injector's success criterion IS the detector's predicate (intersection-emptiness). Recall will be ~100% by construction and carries zero information. The same holds for the headline factorisation split: a one-granule shift is absorbed by one coarsening step, so GRANULARITY_ATTRIBUTABLE will dominate as an arithmetic consequence of the perturbation family, not as a discovery. After stripping the tautological parts, the only informative numbers left are the F3 negative-control false-positive rate and precision on the natural graph - and the latter is contaminated per above. | THE PAPER SPLITS INTO TWO PAPERS AND DEMOTES TWO OF THREE AUTHORS TO APPENDIX. The design explicitly deprioritises the conflict type with 99% of the data mass (1.04M BEFORE + 152K CONTAINS vs >=38,226 anchor pairs), concedes Fan & Strube own Allen-consistency-on-MAVEN-ERE, and makes granularity the headline. But granularity adjudication uses almost none of PaTeCon: Vinh's projection becomes a to_patecon(mode) export and Nhan's mined constraints appear only as a reported ablation. A COLING reviewer reads a granularity-representation paper with a bolted-on constraint-mining section and writes the standard reject: 'two loosely connected contributions, neither fully evaluated.' There is no single experiment in which the granularity lattice improves a mined-constraint or conflict-detection number.\n  FIXABLE: S7's cost model is wrong by ~1.5 orders of magnitude and its 3.0 d is the most likely slip. The 4.4e8-op figure is ONE Floyd-Warshall pass per document. But entailed_at_gran requires re-testing each edge at each surviving lattice node (~9 anchored-partition nodes minimum, up to 22), across >=2 layers, i.e. ~1e10 integer ops plus per-edge bookkeeping over 1.22M edges. Minimal hitting set by iterative removal is O(|edges| * n^3) per conflict on documents averaging ~272 order edges. And 4,480 per-document Z3 MaxSAT calls are buried as a sub-bullet in the same 3 days - that alone is 2 d. Realistic S7: 6-7 d. FIX: compute entailed_at_gran only for edges that participate in a conflict or in the eval split; defer Z3 entirely to a stretch goal. | S2 (3.0 d) is under-scoped for what it must be right about. Bounded-lattice verification, MPS canonicalisation, 146,097-day Gregorian-cycle lcm intersection, Euzenat upcast/downcast, ISO-week vs calendar-week, leap-day 'February 29', BCE dates in historical Wikipedia articles, plus hypothesis property tests - the design's own risk section says a wrong intersection 'silently manufactures or hides conflicts'. Realistic 4.5-5 d. FIX: drop WEEK/WEEK_OF_YEAR/TIME_OF_DAY from the v1 lattice (they are a tiny share of surfaces and the main source of week-numbering bugs), shrinking 22 nodes to ~16 and removing the ambiguity class outright. | S4 (2.5 d) bundles implementation AND the 200-item manual audit AND per-family accuracy reporting. The audit is a full day on its own. Realistic 4 d. FIX: split the audit into its own step and fund a second annotator from Vinh's tooling time for a 100-item overlap so an IAA number exists. | S1 (2.0 d) requires char<->token round-trip assertion on all 80,479 events via re-tokenising `sentences` and cumulative-summing. Whitespace/punctuation/unicode mismatches between MAVEN-Arg's flat `document` string and MAVEN-ERE's `tokens[sent_id]` are the classic silent-failure zone; a strict round-trip assert will fail on a long tail and each failure family costs hours. Realistic 3.5 d. The design correctly flags this and S4 as the two overrun candidates, then funds both with a single shared 2 d buffer (S12) - that buffer is already spent twice over. | No paper-writing days exist anywhere in the 45. Deadline 12 Oct means camera-ready prose, the headline table, and figures must be done by ~day 40, so the real build window is ~35 calendar days, not 45. At a realistic grad-student duty cycle of 0.6-0.7 that is ~22-25 effective person-days against a 26-day nominal plan that is itself underestimated by ~8 d. FIX: cut S7's Z3 path, S6's SHACL baseline, and S11's Datasheet to reclaim ~4 d; move S10 to Nhan as already contemplated. | THE HEADLINE POPULATION FIGURES ARE CORPUS-WIDE BUT THE EVALUATION IS SPLIT-RESTRICTED. 17,102 multi-anchor events and 67,075 anchors are whole-corpus counts. The design's eval split is 725 documents = ~16% of 4,480. If the eval-split natural INCOMPATIBLE population is ~16% of an already-~70-event population, it is ~11 events - not a number any table can rest on. FIX: state clearly that the verdict-distribution characterisation is computed corpus-wide (it needs no gold arguments, only MAVEN-ERE relations, which cover all 4,480 docs) and that mine/eval exists solely for Nhan's constraint mining. Also verify whether MAVEN-ERE's own test split ships gold temporal_relations before counting those documents in. | P4-2 asks Nhan to mine a GRANULARITY constraint family (pattern GRAN_a) for which PaTeCon supplies no algorithm. That is new scope on another member's phase, mid-project. It must be declared optional in writing or it becomes an unowned blocker. Similarly gran_scope forces a field into Nhan's schema he may never populate. | PaTeCon's headline contribution is unusable here and Phase 2 does not surface this to the team. Entity-level support/confidence needs an entity with several independent subgraphs; entities are strictly document-local with mean 3.15 events (max 59), so entity-level confidence is computed over ~3 statements - statistically vacuous. The PaTeCon reuse therefore collapses to fact-level support over a 652-property vocabulary. Better to say so explicitly and reposition Phase 3/4 as a type-level baseline than to let Nhan discover it at day 30. | SUSPICIOUS_SPAN is a threshold fitted on the mine split and then reported as a finding. It has no ground truth at all - 'consistent but almost certainly an annotation or normalisation error' is an assertion, not a measurement. Either hand-adjudicate a sample of flagged spans (50 items, half a day) or demote the verdict to a descriptive column like precision_delta.\n  PRIOR WORK UNDERCUTTING NOVELTY: Bettini, Wang & Jajodia (AMAI 1998; AI 140:107-152, 2002) - multi-granularity temporal constraint networks, granularity conversion, consistency complexity, sound-and-complete plus approximate algorithms. The design concedes this, but concedes it away too cheaply: 'we restrict to the polynomial fragment' is a scoping decision, not a contribution. | Euzenat, Computational Intelligence 17(4), 2001 - proved upward/downward cross-granularity relation conversion operators. The factorisation operator (Claim 2) is upward conversion applied to a conflict set followed by a consistency re-check. An AC will read it as a two-line application of a 25-year-old operator, and 'he does not make this use of it' is a weak defence when the use is the obvious one. | Kontrast (arXiv 2607.25959) - already names information-granularity difference as taxonomy category #1 and measures it at 18.6%. 'They normalise it away, we adjudicate it' is a stance, not a result; the paper needs the adjudication to change an outcome somewhere, and currently it changes nothing measurable. | Stubbs & Pustejovsky narrative containers (LAW V, 2011) and TimeML/ISO-TimeML - the well-formedness of nested containers is already the annotation scheme's premise, so NESTED_REFINEMENT vs INCOMPATIBLE is implicit in TimeML rather than newly discovered. | Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025) - Allen consistency over predicted MAVEN-ERE relations. Same corpus, same relation inventory, consistency as the object. The one-sentence differentiation ('they use a single implicit granularity') is exactly the kind of delta an AC downgrades to incremental. | CAEVO (Chambers et al., TACL 2014) and CATENA - sieve-based temporal graph construction that already enforces global consistency via transitive closure and surfaces conflicts; Do, Lu & Roth (EMNLP 2012) and Chambers & Jurafsky (2008) - global/ILP inference for temporal consistency in text. 'Complete polynomial consistency decision over a document temporal graph' is not new to NLP. | Leeuwenberg & Moens - structured prediction of temporal bounds for events (interval endpoints with uncertainty), which is very close to the day-chronon quadruple as an event time representation, and Reimers et al. (ACL 2016) day-granularity anchoring, cited here as precedent rather than treated as competition. | Dyreson & Snodgrass (TODS 23(1), 1998) indeterminate instants - the quadruple representation is theirs. | UzZaman & Allen / TempEval-3 value-based TIMEX normalisation scoring, HeidelTime (Strotgen & Gertz) narrative-domain reference-time rules, SUTime - the entire S3/S4 normaliser is a reimplementation, and the design says so; a reviewer will note that the paper's central number depends on a component with no novelty and no supervision. | PaTeCon / PaTeCon+ (AAAI 2023; arXiv 2312.11053), TISCO (JWS 2019), TeCre, Soulard/Sais/Raad (ESWC 2025) - temporal constraint mining and conflict detection over KGs; Wiki-hy / YAGO-hy (HTKG, Findings EMNLP 2024), ChronoGrapher (SWJ 2025), ECS-KG (DKE 2025), EventKG/SEM - the event-KG-with-time resource lineage against which 'first text-grounded hyper-relational temporal event KG' must be argued, and which reviewers routinely treat as already crowded.\n  STRONGEST SURVIVING CLAIM: A resource-plus-analysis claim, not a detection claim: 'We give the first quantitative characterisation of multi-granularity temporal anchoring in a text-grounded event corpus - the distribution of nesting, cross-granular refinement, and incompatibility over the 17,102 MAVEN events carrying >=2 gold CONTAINS anchors (>=38,226 anchor pairs) - decided by a complete polynomial procedure (resolve-then-STP inside the disjoint-free ORD-Horn fragment) over an explicit, released granularity lattice and minimal-periodic-set denotations, together with a released rule-based TIMEX normaliser for MAVEN's value-less TIMEX layer with per-family accuracy from a human audit.' That survives review because it is descriptive: it is true whether the INCOMPATIBLE bucket holds 700 events or 7, it fills a gap the ECTKG survey names, and its contribution (the normaliser + lattice + verdict distribution + anchor-set representation on a corpus where 4,480/4,480 documents join MAVEN-Arg to MAVEN-ERE) does not depend on the corpus containing conflicts - which, at ~72 genuine contradictions corpus-wide, it does not. Everything framed as conflict DETECTION performance must be demoted to a secondary section, and the factorisation operator must be presented as a diagnostic instrument with a worked-example proof, not as a headline table with numbers.", "C-constraint-network": "HOSTILE VERDICT (feasibility 5/10, novelty 3/10, effectiveness 6/10, OVERALL 4.5/10)\nREVISE AND NARROW - not acceptable as written. The design is engineering-sound and the schema is the best-specified artifact of the candidate set, but its two advertised novelties both fail: the lexicographic (day, epsilon) weight group is the delta-rational construction from SMT difference-logic solving (Dutertre & de Moura, CAV 2006), and the ordering/granularity 'unification' is a post-hoc label on an arbitrarily chosen negative cycle, with two of five conflict classes detected by scans outside the cycle test at all. Endpoint-based global temporal consistency over text is a 2006-2018 NLP line (Bramsen et al., Chambers & Jurafsky, Denis & Muller, Do/Lu/Roth, Leeuwenberg & Moens, Ning et al., Han et al.) plus a 2025 same-dataset competitor (Fan & Strube), so as a method paper this reduces to 'we applied STP to MAVEN and released it'. Feasibility: the 20 person-day budget is ~28-32 real days once the normaliser (measured ~1 week, budgeted 4d and on the critical path), the typer/fixpoint/localiser bundle (2d for four subsystems, one of which - 'exact minimal hitting set over all negative cycles' - is NP-hard as specified), and the L2 temporal-relation classifier (new model training, 1d, at the very end, and the sole non-circular error source) are priced honestly. It also silently reassigns Nhan's work: mined mutex/disjoint are quarantined in a completeness-free Tier 2 while Tier-1 mining-free numbers are the headline, so Phase 4 contributes to no reported result and PaTeCon's detection path is discarded. Recommended narrowing: drop the epsilon-novelty and the unification claim, keep the strict encoding as an implementation note with the citation, reframe as resource + anchor-coverage diagnosis + non-circular perturbation protocol, start the normaliser on D1, publish the contracts by D5, cut Tier-2/Z3, and move the L2 classifier to a named owner with two weeks of runway or excise it and accept a perturbation-only evaluation reported as such.\n  FATAL: The headline novelty device is a textbook SMT construction, not new. Handling strict inequalities in difference logic by lexicographically ordered (rational, infinitesimal) pairs is the delta-rational / epsilon-reduction of Dutertre & de Moura 2006 (Simplex for DPLL(T)), standard in every QF_RDL/QF_IDL solver and already how Z3 decides the Tier-2 fragment the design itself delegates to Z3. Calling Z x Z lexicographic weights 'genuinely new in this literature' while simultaneously calling Z3 QF_IDL in Tier 1's overlay is a contradiction a temporal-reasoning reviewer will spot in one paragraph, and the necessity argument (non-strict collapses cycles to zero) is the exact motivation given in that SMT literature. Remove this as the enabling claim and the technical core is Floyd-Warshall over a corpus. | The conflict typology is not well-defined, so the central formal sentence ('conflict TYPE is a property of the cycle's support') is false as stated. Minimal negative cycles are not unique and the predecessor-walk recovers one arbitrary cycle, so (a) an incompatible-anchor set on ONE event whose contradiction also closes through a neighbour's BEFORE chain will be typed O2 rather than G1 depending purely on FW's pivot order, and (b) a document containing both an ordering and a granularity conflict yields one cycle and one label, hiding the other until the first is repaired. Typing is therefore order-dependent and non-deterministic across numpy pivot orders. Worse, G2 is detected by a one-pass scan over event_extent (precision_min != precision_max AND anchor_chain=FALSE) and G3 by bookkeeping - neither is a negative cycle - so two of the five conflict classes are outside the 'one graph, one algorithm' unification that is the paper's selling point. | Every headline number depends on the L2 model-predicted layer, and L2 requires a MAVEN-ERE temporal-relation classifier that does not exist in any of the team's repos (PAIE_Maven, DEEIA_Maven, SCPRG, TSAR_Maven, MAVEN_Arg_Pipeline contain no temporal component) and is budgeted at half of D19-D20. Phase 1's argument extractors cannot supply temporal relations (only 4.97% of argument spans overlap any TIMEX, 13.8% of TIMEX are covered by an argument, and none of the 143 roles is Time/Date/When), so L2 is new model training on ~1.2M relation pairs squeezed into one day at the end of the schedule with no slack behind it. Gold yields ~0 O1 and ~72 total contradictions corpus-wide, so if L2 slips the paper is perturbation-only and the design's own risk section concedes the circularity critique returns. | Phase 5 is redefined as 're-run detect() with include_mined=TRUE' and mined mutual-exclusion/disjoint constraints are quarantined in Tier 2 with no completeness guarantee and timeouts reported as UNKNOWN, while the *headline* numbers are explicitly the Tier-1 mining-free detector. That structurally guarantees Nhan's Phase 4 contributes nothing to any reported result, and it removes his PaTeCon detection code path (baseline must now be constructed, not inherited). This forces a teammate to change approach mid-project, which the schedule does not price.\n  FIXABLE: 'Exact minimal hitting set over all negative cycles in a document' is ill-posed and not polynomial: enumerating all negative cycles is exponential in general, and minimum hitting set is NP-hard. What is wanted is a minimal correction subset / MUS over the difference constraints (or partial-weighted MaxSMT, which the design already has a Z3 dependency for). Restate as MaxSMT-based minimal correction set with L0 cost +inf; drop the word 'exact'. | TIMEX normaliser is budgeted 4d (D5-D8) against a measured realistic build of ~1 week (regex 2d + reference-time propagation 2d + gazetteer 0.5d + integration/tests). It blocks D9 onward. Move to 5-6d and start it on D1 in parallel with ingest, since it has zero dependency on the Parquet schema. | D13-D14 (2d) bundles cycle-support typing, the periodic-set fixpoint loop with determinism testing, the G2 scan, G3 bookkeeping, and the localiser. Realistically 4d. D15 (Z3 Tier-2 in 1d) is speculative and low-value given Tier-1-only headline numbers; cut it to buy the typer time and report disjoint/mutex as future work. | The int64 packing needs a stated invariant, not an 'overflow assertion': d*2^21 + k is order-isomorphic to lexicographic (d,k) only for |k| < 2^20 with sign handling; with per-document edge counts ~10^4 this holds, but cycle-sum accumulation across a 5-layer union must be bounded explicitly in the paper or a reviewer will ask. | OVERLAP compiled as b1<b2<f1<f2 assumes MAVEN-ERE OVERLAP is asymmetric and non-containing; SIMULTANEOUS compiled as four equalities makes large forced-equality classes that interact with 1.04M eps-strict BEFORE edges. Validate both readings against the annotation guidelines before D11, or the 'reproduce ~72 contradictions' regression test will fail for encoding reasons and be misread as a data problem. | The perturbation evaluation's detection rate is a monotone function of anchor coverage, so report it stratified by anchor_class and by 'number of independent constraints on the perturbed event' - otherwise the number is uninterpretable. | Contract freeze on D17 leaves Vinh and Nhan only 28 calendar days downstream with the paper due 12 Oct; publish a draft phase3_input_v1 / phase4_input_v1 column contract (even with synthetic rows) by D5 so their code can be written against it.\n  PRIOR WORK UNDERCUTTING NOVELTY: Dutertre & de Moura, 'A Fast Linear-Arithmetic Solver for DPLL(T)' (CAV 2006) - delta-rationals: strict inequalities encoded as lexicographically ordered (value, infinitesimal) pairs. This is the lexicographic (day, epsilon) weight group, already standard in SMT difference-logic solvers. | Denis & Muller, 'Predicting Globally-Coherent Temporal Structures from Texts via Endpoint Inference and Graph Decomposition' (IJCAI 2011) and Muller & Tannier - endpoint/point-algebra encoding of text temporal graphs with global coherence. Not cited in the design; directly pre-empts 'compile events into begin/end point variables and decide consistency on the point graph'. | Bramsen, Deshpande, Lee & Barzilay, 'Inducing Temporal Graphs' (EMNLP 2006) - global temporal graph induction with transitivity/consistency constraints. | Chambers & Jurafsky, 'Jointly Combining Implicit Constraints Improves Temporal Ordering' (EMNLP 2008) and Do, Lu & Roth, 'Joint Inference for Event Timeline Construction' (EMNLP 2012) - ILP global consistency over temporal relations; consistency-as-optimisation over a whole document is 15+ years old in NLP. | Leeuwenberg & Moens, 'Temporal Information Extraction by Predicting Relative Time-lines' (EMNLP 2018) and their metric-constraint / TIMEX-anchored timeline work - events placed on a metric timeline with TIMEX anchors and endpoint bounds. This is the closest thing to the 'four-endpoint quadruple derived from anchors + relations' output and it undercuts the claim that unary calendar constraints alongside binary relations is outside prior formulations. | Ning, Feng, Wu & Roth (2018) CogCompTime / structured learning with transitivity constraints, and Han, Ning & Peng, 'Joint Constrained Learning for Event-Event Relation Extraction' (EMNLP 2020) - logical-consistency enforcement over temporal+subevent relations. | Fan & Strube (Findings EMNLP 2025) - Allen-consistency decoding on the same MAVEN-ERE data, with the same start/end-point decomposition. The design concedes this is the live threat; the surviving difference (persisted store + unary anchors) is an engineering difference a reviewer may not credit as a contribution. | Verhagen's TimeML temporal closure / SputLink and Stubbs & Pustejovsky narrative containers - closure over TimeML graphs including TIMEX anchoring, i.e. 'the stored temporal layer is closed and consistency-checked' as a pre-existing practice. | PaTeCon / PaTeCon+ (AAAI 2023; arXiv 2312.11053) - after removing the epsilon claim, 'we replaced two hand-fixed patterns with global unsatisfiability and applied it to events' is exactly the reduction the area chair will write. | Bettini, Wang & Jajodia (AMAI 1998 / AI 2002) and Euzenat (Comp. Intelligence 2001) - the design already concedes granularity conflict is formally theirs, which leaves only 'we down-cast to a day chronon', an engineering decision.\n  STRONGEST SURVIVING CLAIM: A released, text-grounded event knowledge graph over MAVEN-Arg/MAVEN-ERE (4,480 documents, 80,479 events, 236,937 role-typed arguments) whose temporal layer is published not as relation labels but as a compiled per-document difference-constraint network over 160,958 event endpoints, with TIMEX anchors carried as unary constraints bearing explicit Wikibase-style precision codes and expanded day granules, an explicit derivation-layer axis (gold / rule-normalised / model-predicted / perturbed) so that every temporal fact's provenance is queryable, and a frozen consumer contract (event_extent's four-endpoint quadruple with a trivalent SAT/UNSAT/UNKNOWN status, entity-as-subject statement view, pair_features with transitively-derived Allen labels). The accompanying empirical claim that survives: the gold temporal layer of MAVEN-ERE is essentially conflict-free (~72 contradictions corpus-wide, 0 BEFORE cycles, 100% transitive closure), only 44.9% of events admit a tight calendar anchor, and consequently conflict-detection recall on this resource is bounded by anchor coverage rather than by detector strength - an anchor-coverage-stratified measurement no prior event-KG conflict paper reports. That is a resource-plus-diagnosis paper, not a formalism or a detection-method paper."}}

const FACTS = DATA.FACTS
const RESEARCH_DIGEST = DATA.RESEARCH_DIGEST

const VERDICT_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    feasibility_45d: { type: 'number' },
    novelty_coling: { type: 'number' },
    effectiveness: { type: 'number' },
    overall: { type: 'number' },
    fatal_flaws: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    fixable_issues: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    prior_work_that_undercuts_novelty: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    strongest_defensible_claim: { type: 'string' },
    verdict: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['feasibility_45d', 'novelty_coling', 'effectiveness', 'overall', 'fatal_flaws', 'fixable_issues', 'prior_work_that_undercuts_novelty', 'strongest_defensible_claim', 'verdict'],
}

const JUDGE_PREAMBLE =
  'You are a hostile reviewer judging one candidate design on THREE lenses. Score each 1-10; a score above 7 must be earned. Default to skepticism.\n\n' +
  'LENS 1 FEASIBILITY IN 45 DAYS: 3 grad students; the user (Nhut) owns Phase 2 alone; TIMEX normalisation is unbuilt and blocks everything (measured at ~1 week, see facts). Penalise unbuilt infrastructure, new model training, scale annotation, and anything forcing the other two members to change approach. Name which build steps will slip.\n\n' +
  'LENS 2 NOVELTY FOR COLING 2027 (CORE B): you are a skeptical area chair. Attack the novelty claim directly and name specific prior work that undercuts it. Would this survive review, or does it reduce to "we applied PaTeCon to events" / "yet another event KG"? State the strongest defensible claim that remains.\n\n' +
  'LENS 3 EFFECTIVENESS AND SOUNDNESS: gold is 100% transitively closed with ~72 genuine contradictions; only 44.9% of events have a tight TIMEX anchor; ~55% will evaluate to unknown; entities are document-local; 59.9% of arguments are unlinked spans. Will this design produce enough evaluable instances? Will its conflicts be real or artefacts of TIMEX normalisation error? Is the evaluation non-circular? Be quantitative.\n\n'

// ---------------- JUDGE the 2 missing ----------------
phase('Judge')

const MISSING = ['B-nary-hypergraph', 'D-provenance-multiversion']

const fresh = await parallel(MISSING.map(function (angle) {
  return function () {
    return agent(
      JUDGE_PREAMBLE + FACTS + '\n\nTHE DESIGN UNDER REVIEW:\n' + DATA.DESIGNS_FULL[angle],
      { label: 'judge:' + angle, phase: 'Judge', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA, effort: 'medium' }
    )
  }
}))

const freshMap = {}
MISSING.forEach(function (a, i) { if (fresh[i]) freshMap[a] = fresh[i] })
log('New verdicts: ' + Object.keys(freshMap).join(', '))

function verdictText(v) {
  return 'HOSTILE VERDICT (feasibility ' + v.feasibility_45d + '/10, novelty ' + v.novelty_coling +
    '/10, effectiveness ' + v.effectiveness + '/10, OVERALL ' + v.overall + '/10)\n' + v.verdict +
    '\n  FATAL: ' + (v.fatal_flaws.join(' | ') || 'none') +
    '\n  FIXABLE: ' + (v.fixable_issues.join(' | ') || 'none') +
    '\n  PRIOR WORK UNDERCUTTING NOVELTY: ' + (v.prior_work_that_undercuts_novelty.join(' | ') || 'none named') +
    '\n  STRONGEST SURVIVING CLAIM: ' + v.strongest_defensible_claim
}

const ANGLES = ['A-projection-faithful', 'B-nary-hypergraph', 'C-constraint-network', 'D-provenance-multiversion', 'E-granularity-lattice']
const allText = ANGLES.map(function (a) {
  const v = DATA.VERDICTS_DONE[a] || (freshMap[a] ? verdictText(freshMap[a]) : 'NOT JUDGED')
  return '===== ' + a + ' =====\n' + DATA.DESIGNS_CONDENSED[a] + '\n\n--- ' + v
}).join('\n\n')

// ---------------- SYNTHESIZE ----------------
phase('Synthesize')

const plan = await agent(
  'You are the senior advisor to the TempEKG team. Produce the FINAL DECISION DOCUMENT for Nhut, who owns Phase 2 (Graph Construction), with 45 days to the COLING 2027 deadline.\n\n' +
  FACTS +
  '\n\nRESEARCH GATHERED (4 sweeps):\n' + RESEARCH_DIGEST +
  '\n\nFIVE CANDIDATE DESIGNS WITH HOSTILE VERDICTS:\n' + allText +
  '\n\nNote: every design scored 4-4.5/10 overall from the hostile judges. Do NOT simply pick the highest scorer and declare victory - the judges are telling you the whole framing needs repair. Your job is to build the best achievable plan from these parts, including rejecting a design outright if warranted.\n\n' +
  'Before writing, do your own hostile novelty pass: across all five designs, identify which single novelty claim you could NOT undercut with named prior work, and why it survives.\n\n' +
  'Then write the document with these sections:\n' +
  '1. COMPARISON TABLE of the five designs: feasibility(45d) / novelty / effectiveness / effort-days / what it gives up.\n' +
  '2. RECOMMENDED DESIGN - pick ONE as primary, justify against the hostile verdicts, and explicitly graft the best surviving ideas from the runners-up into it. Say exactly what is grafted and why.\n' +
  '3. CONCRETE PHASE-2 SPEC for Nhut: exact schema with field names, exact interval representation, exact interface handed to Phase 3 (Vinh) and Phase 4 (Nhan), with worked examples using real MAVEN event types.\n' +
  '4. THE NOVELTY CLAIM that survives hostile review, written as it would appear in the paper abstract, plus two backup claims.\n' +
  '5. NON-CIRCULAR EVALUATION PROTOCOL replacing the broken fake-conflict plan.\n' +
  '6. 45-DAY SCHEDULE week by week, naming who does what across the 3 people, with explicit go/no-go gates.\n' +
  '7. ALTERNATIVE OPTIONS if the user disagrees with the primary recommendation - at least 2 fully-specified fallbacks with trade-offs.\n' +
  '8. WHAT TO CUT - ruthless and explicit.\n\n' +
  'Be concrete, quantitative and opinionated. This is a decision document, not a survey. Use the measured numbers throughout. Do not hedge.',
  { label: 'final-plan', phase: 'Synthesize', effort: 'high' }
)

return { new_verdicts: freshMap, plan: plan }
